{"parentUuid":"63324ec0-f298-4b09-9868-ef95b62991a8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfukEXsKmdV7DaR73xJCL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":873,"cache_read_input_tokens":539878,"output_tokens":3766,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1918},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":873,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3766,"cache_read_input_tokens":539878,"cache_creation_input_tokens":873,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":873},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":27368,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"34c12337-65bd-4cd2-9f06-d01def1dc697","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfukEXKpvvfEBUE7iaL3m","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:learn","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"9d33fe32-2e5f-46fa-8036-1353b4066e93","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:33:45.177Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"9d33fe32-2e5f-46fa-8036-1353b4066e93","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfukEXsKmdV7DaR73xJCL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These are the points since the last learn session where the flow changed course, in the order they happened. This part of the session wasn't compacted, so all of it is still in context.\n\n1. **Canvas port collision.** `canvas.sh start` tried to stop the canvas running for another project (filadd), failed with \"port taken\", and I restarted on `CANVAS_PORT=5191`. The script keeps one PID file for every project in its cache, so starting a document in one project tries to stop a server another project owns. The canvas skill says \"one server runs at a time\" and says nothing about other projects or the port variable.\n\n2. **Font of prose and notes.** I wrote the write-up prose and the Claude notes in tldraw's sans font. You asked why they differed from the canvas font, and I switched them to the hand font. Neither design-with-canvas nor canvas names a font for prose or Claude notes; canvas only says mockups use the hand font.\n\n3. **Where questions sit.** design-with-canvas puts question cards at the top of the right side, above the write-up. You asked for them under the summary, so I moved them below the prose and kept that layout in every branch after.\n\n4. **Explaining the opening tree.** The opening tree was nine areas with titles only, as design-with-canvas prescribes. You asked for a short explanation of each before approving.\n\n5. **Questions the settled design already answered.**\n   - **Q6:** asked about the job index. You said you didn't understand why I asked, since the new process doesn't need it.\n   - **Q10:** asked about the Modal payloads. You said \"do you need to ask this? why don't we just choose what we already did on the previous implementation\".\n\n   In branch 6 I read the existing notes and carried today's rules over as ✓ \"as today\" leaves instead of asking. You had also asked at the start not to raise anything already decided. design-tree has no rule for a question that today's implementation or the settled design already answers. This correction came up twice, plus the opening instruction.\n\n6. **Representation for contracts.** Branch 3 went through many rounds:\n   - monospace tables, then tables drawn with shapes\n   - one class diagram for everything (\"pretty bad\")\n   - two candidates, then a map per boundary\n   - a diagram per method with a class diagram beside it, plus a context diagram per sub-branch, which you first cut down to the two interacting components and then dropped\n   - \"understandable on its own\", which became tldraw call and return arrows, then mermaid\n   - interface boxes inside the flowchart, then braces shapes, colours, and a different shape per kind\n\n   The final form is one mermaid flowchart per method. The call is a solid arrow through a braces box holding the interface sent, with each field, its type and its meaning. The return is a dashed arrow through a braces box with the interface returned. design's contract type prescribes tables plus a class diagram, which is the form you rejected.\n\n7. **Colours and shapes in mermaid.** You asked for colours and a shape per kind in the contract diagrams, then for branch 2's diagram to match. I kept one palette for every later diagram:\n   - blue rectangle for the API Worker\n   - green for the pipeline, with the Workflow as a framed rectangle\n   - orange cylinders for stores\n   - violet clouds for outside services\n   - grey pill for the client\n   - amber braces for interfaces\n\n   canvas's \"draw each kind of part with its own shape\" covers tldraw drawings only, and no skill says it for mermaid.\n\n8. **Mermaid rendering problems the validator missed.** Each of these passed validation but drew wrong in the canvas's mermaid shape:\n   - a sequence diagram drawn with its labels out of place\n   - a `;` in a state-diagram note, which split the note into stray states (you asked about the \"leftover squares\")\n   - `**bold**` inside an `@{ label }`, drawn as plain text\n   - an LR flowchart with a TB subgraph, which came out over 3,000 wide\n\n   The mermaid-validation skill lists two rendering rules, and none of these.\n\n9. **Splitting a branch.** You asked to split branch 3 into one sub-branch per boundary, API to pipeline and pipeline to Modal.\n\n10. **Facts about Workflows the wiki didn't have.** The researcher and the restart-resolve spike found:\n    - `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts it, in about 0.4 s, with the status queued. The restarted step starts about 3.4 s later.\n    - `restart()` without `from` runs every step again.\n    - No binding member names the failed step. The REST API does, and it needs a token.\n    - No step hook exists, and code outside a step runs again on resume.\n    - `wrangler delete` leaves the Workflow behind until `wrangler workflows delete` removes it.\n\n    `cloudflare/workflows.md` already had the step-names fact and `restart({from})`. It didn't have the rest.\n\n11. **The `common/` package.** You noticed late that the tree had no branch for the shared package and asked for one. design's opening lists areas from the shape tree, and nothing prompted a branch for a package several deployables share.\n\n12. **Canvas layout mechanics.** After I set a geo shape's `h`, its `growY` stayed stale, so cards overlapped their children several times. I worked around it by measuring the label's DOM height. The canvas skill doesn't mention it.\n\n13. **Recording many notes at once.** write-documentation is written around one note shown whole. Recording touched 13 notes plus 2 new ones and a rename, so I improvised: five drafters running in parallel with one shared brief, each writing the full note and an edits file in the show format.\n\nDo these match what you remember?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":873,"cache_read_input_tokens":539878,"output_tokens":3766,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1918},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":873,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3766,"cache_read_input_tokens":539878,"cache_creation_input_tokens":873,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":873},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"34c12337-65bd-4cd2-9f06-d01def1dc697","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfukEXKpvvfEBUE7iaL3m","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:learn","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"4fb85a81-ca95-43cc-b9b7-6f8ab2de46bc","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:33:56.505Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
