{"parentUuid":"9bc6111f-94c3-4e70-8864-c940ade1c123","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfukwSu5HHwn7w8JazepH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FqfaNat1KhqRZHBxzYwJCL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace/plugins/experimental-dev/skills/mermaid; ls -R | head; wc -l SKILL.md; cat SKILL.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1852,"cache_read_input_tokens":562647,"output_tokens":119,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1852,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":119,"cache_read_input_tokens":562647,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1852,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1852},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FqfaNat1KhqRZHBxzYwJCL":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace/plugins/experimental-dev/skills/mermaid; ls -R | head; wc -l SKILL.md; cat SKILL.md"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"a6f2f9a6-63cc-4d55-9e95-641dc901c1c7","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfukwSF8ggVc8Wz42trGD","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"ea126d5e-92d2-4f37-941a-b283fd10764d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:42:35.019Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace/plugins/dev/skills/mermaid","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"ea126d5e-92d2-4f37-941a-b283fd10764d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"93123f5c-2014-4df8-8796-cbf57f61f75a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FqfaNat1KhqRZHBxzYwJCL","type":"tool_result","content":".:\nreferences\nscripts\nSKILL.md\n\n./references:\nclass-diagram.md\ner-diagram.md\nflowchart.md\nsequence-diagram.md\n72 SKILL.md\n---\nname: mermaid\ndescription: Loaded by other skills to author and validate mermaid diagrams (flows, models, state machines, sequences). Encodes common syntax, a chart-selection decision table, UX/styling guidance, and a bundled validation script.\nuser-invocable: false\nallowed-tools: Read, Write, Bash\n---\n\n# Mermaid Diagrams\n\nKnowledge base for producing **descriptive, valid** mermaid diagrams. Other skills load this when they want to illustrate something as a picture — a data model, a flow across services or APIs, a state lifecycle, an object structure — instead of describing it in prose alone.\n\nA diagram earns its place when it makes a relationship clearer than words would: many moving parts, branching, ordering over time, or structure. Don't diagram the trivial.\n\n## How to use this skill\n\n1. Pick the chart type from the **decision table** below.\n2. Open the matching `references/*.md` for that chart's syntax, a worked example, and UX/styling guidance. Load only the reference(s) you need.\n3. Author the diagram following the common syntax rules. In a flowchart, pick each node's shape from the Shapes section of [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md).\n4. **Validate it** (mandatory — see below) before embedding it anywhere.\n5. Embed the validated diagram **inline, next to the prose it illustrates** — not collected in a separate \"diagrams\" section.\n\n## Chart-selection decision table\n\n| You're documenting | Chart | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data model / schema / DB changes (entities, fields, relationships) | `erDiagram` | [references/er-diagram.md](references/er-diagram.md) |\n| Branching logic, request routing, a flow across a repo's services | `flowchart` | [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md) |\n| Interaction over time across actors/services/APIs (who calls whom, in what order) | `sequenceDiagram` | [references/sequence-diagram.md](references/sequence-diagram.md) |\n| Lifecycle / status transitions of an entity (how something changes state) | `stateDiagram-v2` | [references/state-diagram.md](references/state-diagram.md) |\n| Object/type structure & relationships (classes, DTO hierarchies, interfaces) | `classDiagram` | [references/class-diagram.md](references/class-diagram.md) |\n\nWhen two fit (e.g. a flow could be a `flowchart` or a `sequenceDiagram`): use `sequenceDiagram` when **who** acts and the **order** of messages matter; use `flowchart` when **branching/decisions** matter more than ordering.\n\n## Common syntax\n\nThese rules apply across all chart types:\n\n- **Every diagram starts with a type declaration** — `flowchart TD`, `sequenceDiagram`, `stateDiagram-v2`, `erDiagram`, `classDiagram`. Without it the block won't render.\n- **Direction** (flowchart/state): `TD`/`TB` top-down, `LR` left-right, `BT`, `RL`. Prefer `LR` for pipelines/flows, `TD` for trees/decisions.\n- **Quote labels with special characters** — any label containing `()`, `[]`, `{}`, `:`, `,`, or quotes must be quoted: `A[\"Process (step 1)\"]`. Unquoted special characters are the most common parse failure.\n- **Define a node once, reference by id afterward** — give a node its shape/label on first use (`A[Start]`), then refer to it as just `A`. Re-declaring the shape on every line is noisy and error-prone.\n- **Comments** start with `%%` on their own line.\n- **Break container text every 24 characters with `<br/>`.** A flowchart node, a subgraph title, a note in a sequence or state diagram, a class box or an entity box clips any line that runs past about 24 characters, and the validator does not catch it because the parser accepts the line. Insert `<br/>` at a word boundary before the 24th character and again every 24 characters after that. Text that is not boxed is unaffected: flowchart edge labels and sequence messages. State transition labels and notes clip like boxed text and follow the same rule.\n- **Keep it implementation-targeted** — diagrams here are explanatory aids, not polished design comps. Show what clarifies the decision; omit decoration.\n\n## Validation (mandatory)\n\nMermaid has strict syntax and invalid diagrams **silently fail to render**, leaving a broken code block. Always validate before embedding. Validate via a **temp file**, never by piping an inline string — multi-line diagrams break shell quoting and produce false errors.\n\n1. **Write** the raw diagram body (everything you'd put between the ` ```mermaid ` fences, *without* the fences) to a temp file using the `Write` tool, e.g. `/tmp/diagram.mmd`.\n2. **Run** the validator against the file:\n   ```bash\n   bash ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/mermaid/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh /tmp/diagram.mmd\n   ```\n3. **Read the result.** It prints JSON; check `isValid` and `errors[]`. Exit codes: `0` = valid, `1` = invalid, `2` = setup/usage error.\n4. If invalid, **fix** using the `line` / `column` / `message` in each error, then re-run until `isValid` is `true`.\n\nThe script parses with [mermaid-ast](https://www.npmjs.com/package/mermaid-ast), which runs mermaid's own grammars. The script installs mermaid-ast once into `~/.cache/claude-plugins/mermaid-ast`. Then it checks every expanded node shape (`@{ shape: … }`) against `scripts/notion-shapes.txt`, because the grammar accepts any shape name, including shapes Notion does not render.\n\n### Validator limitations\n\n- **ER diagrams**: a `%%` comment inside an `erDiagram` fails to parse. Leave comments out of ER diagrams.\n\n### Fixing common errors\n\n- **Unquoted special-char labels** — wrap in quotes: `A[\"Total (USD)\"]`, `B{\"valid?\"}`.\n- **Wrong arrow for the diagram type** — `-->` in flowcharts, `->>`/`-->>` in sequence diagrams, `-->` in state/class. Mixing them fails.\n- **Missing type declaration** — the first non-comment line must declare the diagram type.\n- **`subgraph` / `state` block without `end`** — every `subgraph` (flowchart) and every composite `state X { … }` opener needs its closing `end` / `}`.\n- **Re-declaring a node's shape** — define shape once; later references use the bare id.\n- **Reserved word as a bare node id** — `end`, `class`, etc. as ids break parsing; rename or quote.\n- **Shape not rendered by Notion**. Replace it with a shape from the Shapes section of [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6a8a2dd6-d63a-4a5a-94b3-ff5d5653a502","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:42:35.520Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":".:\nreferences\nscripts\nSKILL.md\n\n./references:\nclass-diagram.md\ner-diagram.md\nflowchart.md\nsequence-diagram.md\n72 SKILL.md\n---\nname: mermaid\ndescription: Loaded by other skills to author and validate mermaid diagrams (flows, models, state machines, sequences). Encodes common syntax, a chart-selection decision table, UX/styling guidance, and a bundled validation script.\nuser-invocable: false\nallowed-tools: Read, Write, Bash\n---\n\n# Mermaid Diagrams\n\nKnowledge base for producing **descriptive, valid** mermaid diagrams. Other skills load this when they want to illustrate something as a picture — a data model, a flow across services or APIs, a state lifecycle, an object structure — instead of describing it in prose alone.\n\nA diagram earns its place when it makes a relationship clearer than words would: many moving parts, branching, ordering over time, or structure. Don't diagram the trivial.\n\n## How to use this skill\n\n1. Pick the chart type from the **decision table** below.\n2. Open the matching `references/*.md` for that chart's syntax, a worked example, and UX/styling guidance. Load only the reference(s) you need.\n3. Author the diagram following the common syntax rules. In a flowchart, pick each node's shape from the Shapes section of [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md).\n4. **Validate it** (mandatory — see below) before embedding it anywhere.\n5. Embed the validated diagram **inline, next to the prose it illustrates** — not collected in a separate \"diagrams\" section.\n\n## Chart-selection decision table\n\n| You're documenting | Chart | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data model / schema / DB changes (entities, fields, relationships) | `erDiagram` | [references/er-diagram.md](references/er-diagram.md) |\n| Branching logic, request routing, a flow across a repo's services | `flowchart` | [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md) |\n| Interaction over time across actors/services/APIs (who calls whom, in what order) | `sequenceDiagram` | [references/sequence-diagram.md](references/sequence-diagram.md) |\n| Lifecycle / status transitions of an entity (how something changes state) | `stateDiagram-v2` | [references/state-diagram.md](references/state-diagram.md) |\n| Object/type structure & relationships (classes, DTO hierarchies, interfaces) | `classDiagram` | [references/class-diagram.md](references/class-diagram.md) |\n\nWhen two fit (e.g. a flow could be a `flowchart` or a `sequenceDiagram`): use `sequenceDiagram` when **who** acts and the **order** of messages matter; use `flowchart` when **branching/decisions** matter more than ordering.\n\n## Common syntax\n\nThese rules apply across all chart types:\n\n- **Every diagram starts with a type declaration** — `flowchart TD`, `sequenceDiagram`, `stateDiagram-v2`, `erDiagram`, `classDiagram`. Without it the block won't render.\n- **Direction** (flowchart/state): `TD`/`TB` top-down, `LR` left-right, `BT`, `RL`. Prefer `LR` for pipelines/flows, `TD` for trees/decisions.\n- **Quote labels with special characters** — any label containing `()`, `[]`, `{}`, `:`, `,`, or quotes must be quoted: `A[\"Process (step 1)\"]`. Unquoted special characters are the most common parse failure.\n- **Define a node once, reference by id afterward** — give a node its shape/label on first use (`A[Start]`), then refer to it as just `A`. Re-declaring the shape on every line is noisy and error-prone.\n- **Comments** start with `%%` on their own line.\n- **Break container text every 24 characters with `<br/>`.** A flowchart node, a subgraph title, a note in a sequence or state diagram, a class box or an entity box clips any line that runs past about 24 characters, and the validator does not catch it because the parser accepts the line. Insert `<br/>` at a word boundary before the 24th character and again every 24 characters after that. Text that is not boxed is unaffected: flowchart edge labels and sequence messages. State transition labels and notes clip like boxed text and follow the same rule.\n- **Keep it implementation-targeted** — diagrams here are explanatory aids, not polished design comps. Show what clarifies the decision; omit decoration.\n\n## Validation (mandatory)\n\nMermaid has strict syntax and invalid diagrams **silently fail to render**, leaving a broken code block. Always validate before embedding. Validate via a **temp file**, never by piping an inline string — multi-line diagrams break shell quoting and produce false errors.\n\n1. **Write** the raw diagram body (everything you'd put between the ` ```mermaid ` fences, *without* the fences) to a temp file using the `Write` tool, e.g. `/tmp/diagram.mmd`.\n2. **Run** the validator against the file:\n   ```bash\n   bash ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/mermaid/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh /tmp/diagram.mmd\n   ```\n3. **Read the result.** It prints JSON; check `isValid` and `errors[]`. Exit codes: `0` = valid, `1` = invalid, `2` = setup/usage error.\n4. If invalid, **fix** using the `line` / `column` / `message` in each error, then re-run until `isValid` is `true`.\n\nThe script parses with [mermaid-ast](https://www.npmjs.com/package/mermaid-ast), which runs mermaid's own grammars. The script installs mermaid-ast once into `~/.cache/claude-plugins/mermaid-ast`. Then it checks every expanded node shape (`@{ shape: … }`) against `scripts/notion-shapes.txt`, because the grammar accepts any shape name, including shapes Notion does not render.\n\n### Validator limitations\n\n- **ER diagrams**: a `%%` comment inside an `erDiagram` fails to parse. Leave comments out of ER diagrams.\n\n### Fixing common errors\n\n- **Unquoted special-char labels** — wrap in quotes: `A[\"Total (USD)\"]`, `B{\"valid?\"}`.\n- **Wrong arrow for the diagram type** — `-->` in flowcharts, `->>`/`-->>` in sequence diagrams, `-->` in state/class. Mixing them fails.\n- **Missing type declaration** — the first non-comment line must declare the diagram type.\n- **`subgraph` / `state` block without `end`** — every `subgraph` (flowchart) and every composite `state X { … }` opener needs its closing `end` / `}`.\n- **Re-declaring a node's shape** — define shape once; later references use the bare id.\n- **Reserved word as a bare node id** — `end`, `class`, etc. as ids break parsing; rename or quote.\n- **Shape not rendered by Notion**. Replace it with a shape from the Shapes section of [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"a6f2f9a6-63cc-4d55-9e95-641dc901c1c7","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace/plugins","root":"/home/agus/workspace/filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","branch":"master","default_branch":"master","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}],"visibilit