{"parentUuid":"4d9a0dca-7afe-4b83-82e7-e3fb48455c10","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfunsEL9LnRUX3idTJzU4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015L7EiCSNo6JLCMmdrTJ7Y3","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2; cat > 01-canvas-multi-server.md <<'EOF'\n# 01 Several canvases at once\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/canvas/scripts/canvas.sh` (full draft and diff in `canvas/`, written by a subagent) and `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`.\n\n## canvas/SKILL.md, Running it, the `start` bullet\n\nOld:\n\n- `start` installs the app on first run, starts the server detached and prints the URL to give the user. One server runs at a time: starting another document stops the server for the first, whose document is already on disk. It refuses a document saved in an older format and names the `migrate` command.\n\nNew:\n\n- `start` installs the app on first run, starts a server for the document on the first free port from 5190, detached, and prints the URL to give the user. A document that already has a server gets its URL back. Each document has its own server, so several canvases run at once, in one project or in several; `CANVAS_PORT` picks the port instead. It refuses a document saved in an older format and names the `migrate` command.\n\n## canvas/SKILL.md, Running it, the `stop` bullet\n\nOld:\n\n- `stop` stops the server.\n\nNew:\n\n- `stop` stops the document's own server and leaves every other canvas running.\nEOF\ncat > 02-canvas-font.md <<'EOF'\n# 02 Every text in tldraw's default font\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, Drawing.\n\nOld:\n\n- Text is black, arrow labels included.\n\nNew:\n\n- Text is black, arrow labels included, and written in tldraw's default font, the hand font (`font: 'draw'`): prose, notes, labels and cards alike. Use the monospace font only where the loading skill asks for it, such as a tree of leaves.\nEOF\ncat > 03-canvas-growy.md <<'EOF'\n# 03 Sizing a geo shape set from a script\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, Drawing, after \"Resize an area whenever what it holds grows or moves\".\n\nNew bullet:\n\n- **Set `growY: 0` whenever a script sets a geo shape's `h`.** tldraw keeps the `growY` it computed for the earlier size, and the shape stays taller than what it holds. To stack shapes inside a geo shape under its label, read the label's height first: the height of the shape's `.tl-rich-text` element divided by the zoom level.\nEOF\ncat > 04-questions-under-write-up.md <<'EOF'\n# 04 Questions under the write-up\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/SKILL.md` and `mahou/skills/shape-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, Questions.\n\n## design-with-canvas\n\nOld:\n\nQuestions go in the open branch, at the top of its right side, above its write-up.\n\nNew:\n\nQuestions go in the open branch, on its right side, under its write-up. The branch's Claude note goes under the questions.\n\n## shape-with-canvas\n\nOld:\n\nQuestions go in the open branch, at the top of its right side, above its prose.\n\nNew:\n\nQuestions go in the open branch, on its right side, under its prose. The branch's Claude note goes under the questions.\nEOF\ncat > 05-opening-tree-lines.md <<'EOF'\n# 05 What each branch of the opening tree settles\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/SKILL.md` and `mahou/skills/shape-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, the paragraph on the opening tree.\n\n## design-with-canvas\n\nOld:\n\nThe opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with no leaves yet.\n\nNew:\n\nThe opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with no leaves yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles.\n\n## shape-with-canvas\n\nOld:\n\nThe opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with the ✓ leaves the user brought under their areas and no prose yet.\n\nNew:\n\nThe opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with the ✓ leaves the user brought under their areas and no prose yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles.\nEOF\ncat > 06-propose-the-obvious.md <<'EOF'\n# 06 Proposing an answer when only one is plausible\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/basics/SKILL.md` rule 2, and `mahou/skills/design-tree/SKILL.md`, the paragraph on decisions already made and \"What a question carries\".\n\n## basics, rule 2\n\nOld:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is the one exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal.\n\nNew:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. Two exceptions. mahou:program-design proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal. And when a decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions already settled or from how the code already works, propose that answer with the fact it follows from and ask the user to confirm or correct it, instead of asking the question open.\n\n## design-tree, after \"State a decision the user already made…\"\n\nNew paragraph:\n\nWhen an open decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions the tree already settled or from how the code works today, do not ask it as a question. List it as a proposal, with the decision or the behaviour it follows from, and ask the user to confirm or correct each proposal. A confirmed proposal becomes a ✓ leaf; a corrected one becomes a ✓ leaf with the user's answer. A decision that more than one answer could reasonably settle stays a question.\n\n## design-tree, What a question carries, after the list of things that propose an answer\n\nNew sentence:\n\nA proposal under the paragraph on decisions with one plausible answer is not a question, and these rules do not apply to it.\nEOF\ncat > 10-wiki-workflows.md <<'EOF'\n# 10 Workflows: restart, fan-out and step facts\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/wiki/references/cloudflare/workflows.md` (global). The index line stays as it is.\n\n## Replace the Recovery bullet\n\nOld:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n\nNew:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results; `from` takes `name`, plus `count` for the n-th step of that name and `type` (`do`, `sleep`, `waitForEvent`), and throws when no step matches. `restart()` with no argument erases the instance's state and runs every step again. No option restarts from the failed step without naming it, and no binding member names it: only the REST get-instance endpoint does, and it needs an API token. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n- `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts the restart, before any step runs: 0.4 to 0.6 s through a cross-script binding, with `status()` reading `queued` right after and `error` null. The restarted step started 3.1 to 3.8 s later (median 3.4 s) whether it restarted from a named step or from the start, so the delay is scheduling, not replay. A caller that wants the step's own effect, such as a status write, waits for it after `restart()` returns.\n\n## New bullets after \"Steps run once durably via step.do…\"\n\n- Workflows has no hook that runs before each step or on restart. Logic common to every step goes in a wrapper function of your own around `step.do`. Code in `run()` outside a step runs again each time the instance resumes, so a write there has to give the same result when repeated.\n- Steps run concurrently: 25 `step.do` calls awaited together started within 96 ms and ran at once. Outbound `fetch` calls share the Workers limit of 6 open connections per instance, so 25 five-second fetches finished in batches of 6, the last at 26 s. Awaited with `Promise.allSettled`, steps that fail after their retries leave the instance running, even when all of them fail. A `catch` around `Promise.all` that does not wait for the other steps lets the instance report `complete` while they still run.\n- A `step.do` callback can call `step.do`: the children run concurrently and appear in the step list as ordinary top-level steps beside the parent. A parent that throws fails as one step and the instance continues if the error is caught. A retry of the parent runs every child again as new steps, none reused. A restart from a step after a fan-out replays the finished children from cache, and a failed child is cached like a successful one, so the restart does not retry it.\n\n## Append to the Deploys bullet\n\n`wrangler delete` on the Worker leaves its Workflow in place; `wrangler workflows delete <name>` removes it.\nEOF\nls"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2891,"cache_read_input_tokens":617998,"output_tokens":3266,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":18},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2891,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"