as, each one pending, with the ✓ leaves the user brought under their areas and no prose yet.\n\nNew:\n\nThe opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with the ✓ leaves the user brought under their areas and no prose yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles.\nEOF\ncat > 06-propose-the-obvious.md <<'EOF'\n# 06 Proposing an answer when only one is plausible\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/basics/SKILL.md` rule 2, and `mahou/skills/design-tree/SKILL.md`, the paragraph on decisions already made and \"What a question carries\".\n\n## basics, rule 2\n\nOld:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is the one exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal.\n\nNew:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. Two exceptions. mahou:program-design proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal. And when a decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions already settled or from how the code already works, propose that answer with the fact it follows from and ask the user to confirm or correct it, instead of asking the question open.\n\n## design-tree, after \"State a decision the user already made…\"\n\nNew paragraph:\n\nWhen an open decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions the tree already settled or from how the code works today, do not ask it as a question. List it as a proposal, with the decision or the behaviour it follows from, and ask the user to confirm or correct each proposal. A confirmed proposal becomes a ✓ leaf; a corrected one becomes a ✓ leaf with the user's answer. A decision that more than one answer could reasonably settle stays a question.\n\n## design-tree, What a question carries, after the list of things that propose an answer\n\nNew sentence:\n\nA proposal under the paragraph on decisions with one plausible answer is not a question, and these rules do not apply to it.\nEOF\ncat > 10-wiki-workflows.md <<'EOF'\n# 10 Workflows: restart, fan-out and step facts\n\nLands in: `mahou/skills/wiki/references/cloudflare/workflows.md` (global). The index line stays as it is.\n\n## Replace the Recovery bullet\n\nOld:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n\nNew:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results; `from` takes `name`, plus `count` for the n-th step of that name and `type` (`do`, `sleep`, `waitForEvent`), and throws when no step matches. `restart()` with no argument erases the instance's state and runs every step again. No option restarts from the failed step without naming it, and no binding member names it: only the REST get-instance endpoint does, and it needs an API token. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n- `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts the restart, before any step runs: 0.4 to 0.6 s through a cross-script binding, with `status()` reading `queued` right after and `error` null. The restarted step started 3.1 to 3.8 s later (median 3.4 s) whether it restarted from a named step or from the start, so the delay is scheduling, not replay