ort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"e41b20ec-411a-405b-a206-069bc696be24","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2d630d20-4960-4dbc-866d-3ab4253829fe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017mMP73VWDbj5akYdQn1X7G","type":"tool_result","content":"=== w30:p14\nI added the end-to-end diagram to `item-lifecycle.md` and `item-lifecycle.edits.md`, in a new section \"🗺️ How an item moves through the system\" after the Overview. The validator reports no errors.\n\nIt covers everything you listed: enqueue, all nine steps with the describe fan-out, the services and stores, the status moves, the retry path and the client reading the item and audio. A short paragraph under it walks the path. No node text runs past 24 characters per line, and the palette matches the design.\n\nOne departure: mermaid cannot give a subgraph a shape, so the item Workflow is a subgraph frame, not a subproc node.\n=== w30:p15\nI applied the corrected D4 to `persistence-and-storage.md` and its `.edits.md`. The other two notes needed no change.\n\n- **Status paragraph:** the step wrapper writes every status after `queued`, together with `current_step`, as each step starts. I deleted the separate write that ran once before the describe fan-out.\n- **Units paragraph:** the describe step writes every described unit's spoken form, with its degradations and ledger rows, in one write at its end. Its child steps, one per description and run concurrently, only return their results to it.\n- **Callout:** it is now titled \"Why the describe children write nothing to the row\". A child that wrote the whole list back would overwrite its siblings' results, so the parent collects every result and makes the one write.\n- **Ledger table:** the `describer` row is written by \"the describe step, one row per child's call\".\n\n`item-contract.md` still holds under the new D4. Its only describe statement says the images and describe steps rewrite the units before `ready`, which is true either way. `authentication.md` says nothing about describe.\n\nNo mermaid block changed, and I applied every decision.\n=== w30:p16\nI applied the corrected D4. The earlier D4 had reached only one place in my drafts: the item-lifecycle row of the technical-design index. That row is fixed in both `README.md` and `README.edits.md` (the index draft, not the charter). It now says all nine steps run inside one step wrapper, with describe running one concurrent child step per description. The rest of the row is unchanged.\n\nNone of my other drafts (`deployment-and-ci`, `invariants`, `queue`, `CLAUDE`) mention describe or its fan-out, so they needed no change.\n\nI still have not applied P13 (the charter's \"without opening `api/app` or `tts/app.py`\"), because `docs/README.md` is not among my assigned files. The `api/` and `common/` command lines in CLAUDE.md are also still my guess, copied from the pipeline's scripts.\n=== w30:p17\nI revised all four drafts with recording-decisions.md, including the corrected D4, and the parallel-steps spike findings. I updated each `.edits.md` to match. All mermaid blocks pass the validator, and the editing pass is done.\n\n- **image-and-code-describer:** describe is now one wrapped parent step. It runs one child step per description with `Promise.allSettled`, the children only return results, and the parent writes spoken forms, degradations and ledger rows in one write at its end. Gemini calls run at most 6 at a time. A new warning says that waiting for a connection counts toward a child's timeout. A retry of describe runs every child again, and the gateway cache can answer the repeats. The rule that the item fails when every code block failed is gone; the diagram follows suit.\n- **article-extraction:** the spoken form and the drop run at the end of the recipe step (D3). Units and degradations go into the row as soon as a step produces them (D2), so the \"persisted rather than re-extracted\" callout is back. The image hash is taken over the stored bytes (P4). The download limits stay (P7), with a sentence that at most 6 downloads run at once.\n- **tts-service:** the store step writes the tts ledger row (P3). The call id and the result pass between steps as step results (D2).\n- **recipes:** adds the 15-minute recipe-step timeout and the 97 s measured authoring run (D8). A not-an-article verdict fails the item without retrying the step (P2).\n\nDecisions I could not apply:\n- Neither the decisions nor design.md say how many attempts and what timeout a describe child step gets, so the drafts name none.\n- They also don't say how the title reaches the row, so I didn't describe it.\n=== w30:p18\nI applied the new D4 in `common-package.md` and in its `.edits.md`. My revision had added \"the item Workflow's run() before the describe fan-out\" as a writer of the status. Under the new D4, describe runs inside the step wrapper like every other step, so I removed that clause. The item row reads-and-writes row again says \"the API Worker for queued, the step wrapper for every later status\".\n\n`local-development.md` needed no change: the only place it mentions describe is a diagram label, and nothing about the label changes.\n\nOne question is still open from my last reply. Under P5, the retry route only claims an item whose status is failed. An item stuck in queued, or stranded by a dev-server reload, never reaches failed, so the retry route would refuse it. P12 and design branch 8 still say to restart those items through the retry route. Which way should the notes go?","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bce49875-f1bb-4338-bc3e-cbba43689135","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:08:53.786Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== w30:p14\nI added the end-to-end diagram to `item-lifecycle.md` and `item-lifecycle.edits.md`, in a new section \"🗺️ How an item moves through the system\" after the Overview. The validator reports no errors.\n\nIt covers everything you listed: enqueue, all nine steps with the describe fan-out, the services and stores, the status moves, the retry path and the client reading the item and audio. A short paragraph under it walks the path. No node text runs past 24 characters per line, and the palette matches the design.\n\nOne departure: mermaid cannot give a subgraph a shape, so the item Workflow is a subgraph frame, not a subproc node.\n=== w30:p15\nI applied the corrected D4 to `persistence-and-storage.md` and its `.edits.md`. The other two notes needed no change.\n\n- **Status paragraph:** the step wrapper writes every status after `queued`, together with `current_step`, as each step starts. I deleted the separate write that ran once before the describe fan-out.\n- **Units paragraph:** the describe step writes every described unit's spoken form, with its degradations and ledger rows, in one write at its end. Its child steps, one per description and run concurrently, only return their results to it.\n- **Callout:** it is now titled \"Why the describe children write nothing to the row\". A child that wrote the whole list back would overwrite its siblings' results, so the parent collects every result and makes the one write.\n- **Ledger table:** the `describer` row is written by \"the describe step, one row per child's call\".\n\n`item-contract.md` still holds under the new D4. Its only describe statement says the images and describe steps rewrite the units before `ready`, which is true either way. `authentication.md` says nothing about describe.\n\nNo mermaid block changed, and I applied every decision.\n=== w30:p16\nI applied the corrected D4. The earlier D4 had reached only one place in my drafts: the item-lifecycle row of the technical-design index. That row is fixed in both `README.md` and `README.edits.md` (the index draft, not the charter). It now says all nine steps run inside one step wrapper, with describe running one concurrent child step per description. The rest of the row is unchanged.\n\nNone of my other drafts (`deployment-and-ci`, `invariants`, `queue`, `CLAUDE`) mention describe or its fan-out, so they needed no change.\n\nI still have not applied P13 (the charter's \"without opening `api/app` or `tts/app.py`\"), because `docs/README.md` is not among my assigned files. The `api/` and `common/` command lines in CLAUDE.md are also still my guess, copied from the pipeline's scripts.\n=== w30:p17\nI revised all four drafts with recording-decisions.md, including the corrected D4, and the parallel-steps spike findings. I updated each `.edits.md` to match. All mermaid blocks pass the validator, and the editing pass is done.\n\n- **image-and-code-describer:** describe is now one wrapped parent step. It runs one child step per description with `Promise.allSettled`, the children only return results, and the parent writes spoken forms, degradations and ledger rows in one write at its end. Gemini calls run at most 6 at a time. A new warning says that waiting for a connection counts toward a child's timeout. A retry of describe runs every child again, and the gateway cache can answer the repeats. The rule that the item fails when every code block failed is gone; the diagram follows suit.\n- **article-extraction:** the spoken form and the drop run at the end of the recipe step (D3). Units and degradations go into the row as soon as a step produces them (D2), so the \"persisted rather than re-extracted\" callout is back. The image hash is taken over the stored bytes (P4). The download limits stay (P7), with a sentence that at most 6 downloads run at once.\n- **tts-service:** the store step writes the tts ledger row (P3). The call id and the result pass between steps as step results (D2).\n- **recipes:** adds the 15-minute recipe-step timeout and the 97 s measured authoring run (D8). A not-an-article verdict fails the item without retrying the step (P2).\n\nDecisions I could not apply:\n- Neither the decisions nor design.md say how many attempts and what timeout a describe child step gets, so the drafts name none.\n- They also don't say how the title reaches the row, so I didn't describe it.\n=== w30:p18\nI applied the new D4 in `common-package.md` and in its `.edits.md`. My revision had added \"the item Workflow's run() before the describe fan-out\" as a writer of the status. Under the new D4, describe runs inside the step wrapper like every other step, so I removed that clause. The item row reads-and-writes row again says \"the API Worker for queued, the step wrapper for every later status\".\n\n`local-development.md` needed no change: the only place it mentions describe is a diagram label, and nothing about the label changes.\n\nOne question is still open from my last reply. Under P5, the retry route only claims an item whose status is failed. An item stuck in queued, or stranded by a dev-server reload, never reaches failed, so the retry route would refuse it. P12 and design branch 8 still say to restart those items through the retry route. Which way should the notes go?","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"efe3a4d7-f235-47d5-8ac5-12c0ec36bdb9","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":1},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e41b20ec-411a-405b-a206-069bc696be24","permissionD