{"parentUuid":"56ae6f7b-f323-4e8f-b000-780e1b2e4bb0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuoYzckHKTPyFdPwuX8E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LN3rnFn8UUZCvmytpQ3pib","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2; cat mermaid/SKILL.md; echo \"=====FLOW\"; cat mermaid/references/flowchart.md; echo \"=====STATE\"; cat mermaid/references/state-diagram.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":515,"cache_read_input_tokens":643217,"output_tokens":427,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":237},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":515,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":427,"cache_read_input_tokens":643217,"cache_creation_input_tokens":515,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":515},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LN3rnFn8UUZCvmytpQ3pib":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2; cat mermaid/SKILL.md; echo \"=====FLOW\"; cat mermaid/references/flowchart.md; echo \"=====STATE\"; cat mermaid/references/state-diagram.md"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"99cc8573-0f70-4b62-b38f-d53bfd325e50","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CfuoYz1nkGvGF5rYg85UE","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"ad04baa7-f089-465d-8904-59c886c1e1fa","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:16:53.143Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"ad04baa7-f089-465d-8904-59c886c1e1fa","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c89c9508-3a14-466d-a396-c3993d3d1069","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LN3rnFn8UUZCvmytpQ3pib","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: mermaid\ndescription: Loaded to choose, write, style and validate mermaid diagrams (flowcharts, sequence, state, ER and class diagrams). Carries a chart-selection table, one reference per chart type with a default shape and colour palette, the common syntax rules, a bundled validator and the rendering rules the validator cannot catch. Use whenever you generate, write or edit a mermaid code block, and validate every diagram before showing it, even one that looks correct.\n---\n\n# Mermaid Diagrams\n\nUse a diagram to show something that prose would explain worse: a data model, a flow across services or APIs, a state lifecycle, an object structure. A diagram is worth drawing when the subject has many parts, branches, an order over time or a structure. Do not diagram something one sentence explains.\n\n## How to use this skill\n\n1. Pick the chart type from the chart-selection table below.\n2. Open the matching `references/*.md` for that chart's syntax, a worked example and its styling guidance. Load only the references you need.\n3. Write the diagram following the common syntax rules. In a flowchart or a state diagram, take each element's shape and colour from the palette in [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md).\n4. Validate it, then render it and look at the image (see Validation). Both steps are mandatory.\n5. Place the diagram next to the prose it illustrates, not in a separate \"diagrams\" section.\n\n## Chart selection\n\n| You're documenting | Chart | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| A data model, a schema, database changes (entities, fields, relationships) | `erDiagram` | [references/er-diagram.md](references/er-diagram.md) |\n| Branching logic, request routing, a flow across a repository's services | `flowchart` | [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md) |\n| Interaction over time across actors, services or APIs (who calls whom, in what order) | `sequenceDiagram` | [references/sequence-diagram.md](references/sequence-diagram.md) |\n| The lifecycle or status transitions of an entity | `stateDiagram-v2` | [references/state-diagram.md](references/state-diagram.md) |\n| Object and type structure and relationships (classes, DTO hierarchies, interfaces) | `classDiagram` | [references/class-diagram.md](references/class-diagram.md) |\n\nWhen two charts fit, such as a flow that could be a `flowchart` or a `sequenceDiagram`, use `sequenceDiagram` when who acts and the order of messages matter, and `flowchart` when branches and decisions matter more than order.\n\n## Common syntax\n\n\n- **Start every diagram with a type declaration**: `flowchart TD`, `sequenceDiagram`, `stateDiagram-v2`, `erDiagram`, `classDiagram`. A block without one does not render.\n- **Direction** (flowchart and state diagram): `TD` or `TB` runs top-down, `LR` left to right, `BT` bottom-up and `RL` right to left. Use `LR` for pipelines and flows, `TD` for trees and decisions.\n- **Quote labels with special characters.** Quote any label that contains `()`, `[]`, `{}`, `:`, `,` or quotes: `A[\"Process (step 1)\"]`. Unquoted special characters cause most parse failures.\n- **Define a node once, then refer to it by id.** Give a node its shape and label on first use (`A[Start]`), then write only `A`.\n- **Comments** start with `%%` on their own line.\n- **Leave out decoration and any detail the reader does not need.**\n\n## Validation (mandatory)\n\nMermaid's syntax is strict, and an invalid diagram renders as a broken code block with no warning. Validate every diagram before you show it. Write the diagram to a temp file and feed the file to the validator; do not pass a multi-line diagram as an inline shell string, because shell quoting breaks it and the validator reports errors that are not in the diagram.\n\n1. **Write** the raw diagram body (the text between the ` ```mermaid ` fences, without the fences) to a temp file with the `Write` tool, such as `/tmp/diagram.mmd`.\n2. **Run** the validator, which reads the diagram from stdin:\n   ```bash\n   bash ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/mermaid/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh < /tmp/diagram.mmd\n   ```\n3. **Read the result.** The script prints JSON; check `isValid` and `errors[]`:\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"isValid\": false,\n     \"diagramType\": \"flowchart\",\n     \"errors\": [\n       {\n         \"line\": 2,\n         \"message\": \"Failed to parse flowchart: Parse error on line 2: ... Expecting 'SQE', ...\"\n       }\n     ]\n   }\n   ```\n   Exit codes: `0` valid, `1` invalid, `2` setup error.\n4. If the diagram is invalid, **fix** it using the `line` and `message` of each error, then run the validator again until `isValid` is `true`.\n5. **Render** the valid diagram and look at the image before you show the diagram. A diagram that parses can still draw wrong (see Rendering rules the validator cannot catch):\n   ```bash\n   npx -y -p @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli mmdc -i /tmp/diagram.mmd -o /tmp/diagram.png\n   ```\n   If mmdc reports that it cannot find `chrome-headless-shell`, install one and point mmdc at it:\n   ```bash\n   npx -y puppeteer browsers install chrome-headless-shell\n   export PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=$(ls -d ~/.cache/puppeteer/chrome-headless-shell/*/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell | tail -1)\n   ```\n   The installer fetches the newest version, and mmdc rejects any version other than its own unless `PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH` points at a browser. Open `/tmp/diagram.png` with the `Read` tool. Check for clipped text, stray nodes or states, and a layout far wider or taller than the page.\n\nThe script parses with [mermaid-ast](https://github.com/neongreen/mermaid-ast), which runs the grammars vendored from mermaid itself, so a diagram it accepts is one mermaid parses. It covers 18 diagram types and installs itself on first run into `~/.cache/claude-plugins/mermaid-ast/`. The script accepts a leading frontmatter block and strips it before parsing. An error carries a `line` when the grammar reports one, and never a column.\n\n### Validator limitations\n\n- **ER diagrams**: the validator rejects a `%%` comment inside an `erDiagram`, although mermaid accepts it. Leave comments out of ER diagrams.\n\n### Fixing common errors\n\n- **Unquoted labels with special characters**: wrap them in quotes, `A[\"Total (USD)\"]`, `B{\"valid?\"}`.\n- **Wrong arrow for the diagram type**: `-->` in flowcharts, `->>`/`-->>` in sequence diagrams, `-->` in state and class diagrams. Mixing them fails.\n- **Missing type declaration**: the first line that is not a comment must declare the diagram type.\n- **`subgraph` or `state` block without its end**: every `subgraph` needs an `end`, and every composite `state X {` needs its `}`.\n- **A node's shape declared twice**: declare the shape once; later references use the bare id.\n- **Reserved word as a bare node id**: `end`, `class` and similar ids break parsing; rename the node or quote it.\n\n## Rendering rules the validator cannot catch\n\nThe parser accepts these, and the renderer draws them wrong. Check for them in the rendered image after validation passes.\n\n- **Long lines inside a container are cut.** A flowchart node, a subgraph title, a note in a sequence or state diagram, a class box, an entity box: the renderer clips any line in these that runs past about 24 characters. Break the text with `<br/>` at a word boundary before the 24th character, and again every 24 characters after that. Text outside a box is unaffected: edge labels, state transition labels and sequence messages wrap or extend on their own.\n- **Curly braces and `<br/>` in sequence messages break some renderers.** A sequenceDiagram message carrying `{id}`-style braces or a `<br/>` tag parses, so the validator passes it, but some renderers (Obsidian's, observed) fail at the first such message and report the parse error on a later line. Keep sequence messages plain text, and put text that must be split into a note. `<br/>` inside flowchart node labels and sequence notes renders correctly, and so do commas, slashes and parentheses in messages.\n- **A `;` inside state-diagram text ends the statement.** In a note or a transition label, the text after a `;` renders as stray states. Use a comma instead.\n- **Markdown inside an `@{ shape, label }` label renders as plain text.** Leave markdown out of expanded-shape labels.\n- **Mermaid ignores a subgraph's `direction` when edges cross its border.** Set the layout direction on the whole flowchart (`flowchart LR` or `flowchart TD`). When a flowchart grows too wide in one direction, redraw it in the other.\n=====FLOW\n# Flowchart\n\n## When to use\n\nBranching logic, decision trees, request routing, and flows across a repository's services or modules, where decisions and paths matter more than the order of messages. For who calls whom and in what order, use a [sequence diagram](sequence-diagram.md).\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type and direction: `flowchart TD` (top-down) or `flowchart LR` (left-right).\n- Take each node's shape from the palette below and declare it as an expanded shape: `A@{ shape: cyl, label: \"…\" }`. The short forms `A[Rectangle]` and `B{Diamond}` draw the same `rect` and `diam`.\n- Edges: `-->` solid, `==>` thick, `-.->` dotted. Labels go on the edge: `A -->|yes| B` or `A -- text --> B`.\n- Fan-out shorthand: `A --> B & C` points A to both B and C.\n- **Grouping**: wrap related nodes in `subgraph Name ... end` to show a boundary, such as a service, a layer or a repository. Quote the title if it has spaces: `subgraph s[\"Consumer API\"]`.\n\n## Worked example\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    User@{ shape: stadium, label: \"User\" }\n    Gateway@{ shape: rect, label: \"Consumer API\" }\n    Auth@{ shape: diam, label: \"auth valid?\" }\n    Reject@{ shape: rect, label: \"Return 401\" }\n    Courses@{ shape: rect, label: \"Fetch courses\" }\n    Rules@{ shape: hex, label: \"Pricing rules\" }\n    DB@{ shape: cyl, label: \"Postgres\" }\n    Mail@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Email provider\" }\n\n    User --> Gateway --> Auth\n    Auth -->|no| Reject\n    Auth -->|yes| Courses\n\n    subgraph data[\"Data API\"]\n        Courses --> Rules\n    end\n\n    Courses --> DB\n    Courses -.-> Mail\n\n    classDef grey fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n    classDef blue fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n    classDef green fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef amber fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n    classDef orange fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n    classDef violet fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n    classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n\n    class User grey\n    class Gateway,Auth blue\n    class Courses green\n    class Rules amber\n    class DB orange\n    class Mail violet\n    class Reject red\n```\n\n## Palette\n\nGive each kind of element the same shape and colour in every diagram, so a reader who has seen one diagram reads the next one without a legend. This palette is the default; use another shape or colour only when the diagram needs a kind of element the table does not list.\n\n### Syntax\n\nDeclare an expanded shape on the node's first use, with the label inside the braces:\n\n```\nDB@{ shape: cyl, label: \"Database<br/>of the service\" }\n```\n\nLater references use the bare id. The 24-character line rule from `SKILL.md` applies to the label.\n\n### Which shape and colour for each element\n\n| Element | Shape | Colour |\n|---|---|---|\n| A person or a client: a user, an admin, a frontend | `stadium` | grey |\n| An API or service the user reaches | `rect` | blue |\n| A module, a step, a part of a worker | `rect` | green |\n| A durable job or a workflow | `subproc` | green |\n| A background job, a scheduled task | `st-rect` | green |\n| A shared piece several components use: a library, a set of rules | `hex` | amber |\n| An interface or a payload crossing a boundary | `braces` | amber |\n| A store: a database, a table, stored state, a warehouse you query | `cyl` | orange |\n| An external service: a SaaS, a third-party API | `cloud` | violet |\n| A decision | `diam` | blue |\n| A wait: a timeout, a period without activity | `delay` | grey |\n| A failure or a dropped outcome | `rect` | red |\n\nA service's boundary is a `subgraph`, and its storage sits outside the subgraph.\n\n### Colours\n\nCopy this block into the diagram and assign each node with `class <ids> <colour>`. Declare only the colours the diagram uses.\n\n```\nclassDef grey fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\nclassDef blue fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\nclassDef green fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\nclassDef amber fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\nclassDef orange fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\nclassDef violet fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\nclassDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n```\n\nA state diagram takes the same colours through `classDef`; see [state-diagram.md](state-diagram.md).\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Colour through the palette's classes**, not inline styles. Apply one class to several nodes with a comma list: `class B,C,F green`.\n- **One-off node styling**, for a node the palette does not cover: `style C fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:3px`.\n- **Highlight a path**: colour edges by their declaration order (0-indexed): `linkStyle 0,1,4 stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:3px`. `linkStyle default` styles every edge.\n- **Use `subgraph` to group** by service, layer or repository. It is the main way to show architecture boundaries in a flow.\n- **Line breaks** inside labels: `A[\"Line one<br/>Line two\"]`.\n- **Match the direction to the content**: `LR` reads as a pipeline, `TD` as a decision tree. Set it on the whole flowchart, not on a subgraph.\n=====STATE\n# State Diagram\n\n## When to use\n\nThe lifecycle of an entity: how an order, a payment, a subscription or a job moves between statuses, and which event triggers each move. If you are writing \"when X happens it goes from pending to active\", draw a state diagram.\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type: `stateDiagram-v2`.\n- **Start and end**: `[*] --> First` marks the entry; `Last --> [*]` marks the end.\n- **Transitions** carry the triggering event as a label: `Pending --> Active : payment_confirmed`.\n- **Named states with descriptions**: `state \"Waiting for payment\" as Pending`.\n- **Composite states** group substates inside a parent, for a state that has its own internal lifecycle:\n  ```\n  state Active {\n      [*] --> Running\n      Running --> Paused : pause\n      Paused --> Running : resume\n  }\n  ```\n- **Choice, fork and join**: `state c <<choice>>` for a branch, `state f <<fork>>` and `state j <<join>>` for parallel paths.\n- **Notes**: `note right of StateName : text`.\n- **Colour**: `classDef` and `class` work as in a flowchart: `classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626` and `class Cancelled red`.\n\n## Worked example\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Draft\n\n    Draft --> Submitted : submit\n    Submitted --> Cancelled : cancel\n\n    state Submitted {\n        [*] --> AwaitingPayment\n        AwaitingPayment --> Paid : payment_confirmed\n        AwaitingPayment --> AwaitingPayment : retry\n    }\n\n    Submitted --> Fulfilled : ship\n    Fulfilled --> [*]\n    Cancelled --> [*]\n\n    note right of Submitted : Holds the payment flow\n\n    classDef green fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n\n    class Fulfilled green\n    class Cancelled red\n```\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Colour with the palette** in [flowchart.md](flowchart.md). Use the same colours for the same meaning: red for a failed or dropped state, grey for a wait, green for a state where work runs.\n- **Use composite states for substates** when a status has its own internal lifecycle, as `Submitted` holds the payment steps above. The top level then shows only the statuses, and the substates appear inside the composite state.\n- **Label every transition** with the event or condition that causes it. An unlabeled transition leaves the reader guessing what triggers the change.\n- **Use `<<choice>>`** for one decision point with mutually exclusive outgoing transitions, and **`<<fork>>` / `<<join>>`** only when the entity does things in parallel.\n- **A self-transition** (`AwaitingPayment --> AwaitingPayment : retry`) shows a retry that does not leave the state.\n- **Add a `note`** to explain a state or an invariant the diagram cannot express in its structure.\n- **Never put a `;` in a note or a transition label.** It ends the statement and the rest renders as stray states; use a comma.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8675481f-2d0e-48df-9d02-01f490bb7a47","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:16:53.217Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nname: mermaid\ndescription: Loaded to choose, write, style and validate mermaid diagrams (flowcharts, sequence, state, ER and class diagrams). Carries a chart-selection table, one reference per chart type with a default shape and colour palette, the common syntax rules, a bundled validator and the rendering rules the validator cannot catch. Use whenever you generate, write or edit a mermaid code block, and validate every diagram before showing it, even one that looks correct.\n---\n\n# Mermaid Diagrams\n\nUse a diagram to show something that prose would explain worse: a data model, a flow across services or APIs, a state lifecycle, an object structure. A diagram is worth drawing when the subject has many parts, branches, an order over time or a structure. Do not diagram something one sentence explains.\n\n## How to use this skill\n\n1. Pick the chart type from the chart-selection table below.\n2. Open the matching `references/*.md` for that chart's syntax, a worked example and its styling guidance. Load only the references you need.\n3. Write the diagram following the common syntax rules. In a flowchart or a state diagram, take each element's shape and colour from the palette in [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md).\n4. Validate it, then render it and look at the image (see Validation). Both steps are mandatory.\n5. Place the diagram next to the prose it illustrates, not in a separate \"diagrams\" section.\n\n## Chart selection\n\n| You're documenting | Chart | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| A data model, a schema, database changes (entities, fields, relationships) | `erDiagram` | [references/er-diagram.md](references/er-diagram.md) |\n| Branching logic, request routing, a flow across a repository's services | `flowchart` | [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md) |\n| Interaction over time across actors, services or APIs (who calls whom, in what order) | `sequenceDiagram` | [references/sequence-diagram.md](references/sequence-diagram.md) |\n| The lifecycle or status transitions of an entity | `stateDiagram-v2` | [references/state-diagram.md](references/state-diagram.md) |\n| Object and type structure and relationships (classes, DTO hierarchies, interfaces) | `classDiagram` | [references/class-diagram.md](references/class-diagram.md) |\n\nWhen two charts fit, such as a flow that could be a `flowchart` or a `sequenceDiagram`, use `sequenceDiagram` when who acts and the order of messages matter, and `flowchart` when branches and decisions matter more than order.\n\n## Common syntax\n\n\n- **Start every diagram with a type declaration**: `flowchart TD`, `sequenceDiagram`, `stateDiagram-v2`, `erDiagram`, `classDiagram`. A block without one does not render.\n- **Direction** (flowchart and state diagram): `TD` or `TB` runs top-down, `LR` left to right, `BT` bottom-up and `RL` right to left. Use `LR` for pipelines and flows, `TD` for trees and decisions.\n- **Quote labels with special characters.** Quote any label that contains `()`, `[]`, `{}`, `:`, `,` or quotes: `A[\"Process (step 1)\"]`. Unquoted special characters cause most parse failures.\n- **Define a node once, then refer to it by id.** Give a node its shape and label on first use (`A[Start]`), then write only `A`.\n- **Comments** start with `%%` on their own line.\n- **Leave out decoration and any detail the reader does not need.**\n\n## Validation (mandatory)\n\nMermaid's syntax is strict, and an invalid diagram renders as a broken code block with no warning. Validate every diagram before you show it. Write the diagram to a temp file and feed the file to the validator; do not pass a multi-line diagram as an inline shell string, because shell quoting breaks it and the validator reports errors that are not in the diagram.\n\n1. **Write** the raw diagram body (the text between the ` ```mermaid ` fences, without the fences) to a temp file with the `Write` tool, such as `/tmp/diagram.mmd`.\n2. **Run** the validator, which reads the diagram from stdin:\n   ```bash\n   bash ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/mermaid/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh < /tmp/diagram.mmd\n   ```\n3. **Read the result.** The script prints JSON; check `isValid` and `errors[]`:\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"isValid\": false,\n     \"diagramType\": \"flowchart\",\n     \"errors\": [\n       {\n         \"line\": 2,\n         \"message\": \"Failed to parse flowchart: Parse error on line 2: ... Expecting 'SQE', ...\"\n       }\n     ]\n   }\n   ```\n   Exit codes: `0` valid, `1` invalid, `2` setup error.\n4. If the diagram is invalid, **fix** it using the `line` and `message` of each error, then run the validator again until `isValid` is `true`.\n5. **Render** the valid diagram and look at the image before you show the diagram. A diagram that parses can still draw wrong (see Rendering rules the validator cannot catch):\n   ```bash\n   npx -y -p @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli mmdc -i /tmp/diagram.mmd -o /tmp/diagram.png\n   ```\n   If mmdc reports that it cannot find `chrome-headless-shell`, install one and point mmdc at it:\n   ```bash\n   npx -y puppeteer browsers install chrome-headless-shell\n   export PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=$(ls -d ~/.cache/puppeteer/chrome-headless-shell/*/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell | tail -1)\n   ```\n   The installer fetches the newest version, and mmdc rejects any version other than its own unless `PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH` points at a browser. Open `/tmp/diagram.png` with the `Read` tool. Check for clipped text, stray nodes or states, and a layout far wider or taller than the page.\n\nThe script parses with [mermaid-ast](https://github.com/neongreen/mermaid-ast), which runs the grammars vendored from mermaid itself, so a diagram it accepts is one mermaid parses. It covers 18 diagram types and installs itself on first run into `~/.cache/claude-plugins/mermaid-ast/`. The script accepts a leading frontmatter block and strips it before parsing. An error carries a `line` when the grammar reports one, and never a column.\n\n### Validator limitations\n\n- **ER diagrams**: the validator rejects a `%%` comment inside an `erDiagram`, although mermaid accepts it. Leave comments out of ER diagrams.\n\n### Fixing common errors\n\n- **Unquoted labels with special characters**: wrap them in quotes, `A[\"Total (USD)\"]`, `B{\"valid?\"}`.\n- **Wrong arrow for the diagram type**: `-->` in flowcharts, `->>`/`-->>` in sequence diagrams, `-->` in state and class diagrams. Mixing them fails.\n- **Missing type declaration**: the first line that is not a comment must declare the diagram type.\n- **`subgraph` or `state` block without its end**: every `subgraph` needs an `end`, and every composite `state X {` needs its `}`.\n- **A node's shape declared twice**: declare the shape once; later references use the bare id.\n- **Reserved word as a bare node id**: `end`, `class` and similar ids break parsing; rename the node or quote it.\n\n## Rendering rules the validator cannot catch\n\nThe parser accepts these, and the renderer draws them wrong. Check for them in the rendered image after validation passes.\n\n- **Long lines inside a container are cut.** A flowchart node, a subgraph title, a note in a sequence or state diagram, a class box, an entity box: the renderer clips any line in these that runs past about 24 characters. Break the text with `<br/>` at a word boundary before the 24th character, and again every 24 characters after that. Text outside a box is unaffected: edge labels, state transition labels and sequence messages wrap or extend on their own.\n- **Curly braces and `<br/>` in sequence messages break some renderers.** A sequenceDiagram message carrying `{id}`-style braces or a `<br/>` tag parses, so the validator passes it, but some renderers (Obsidian's, observed) fail at the first such message and report the parse error on a later line. Keep sequence messages plain text, and put text that must be split into a note. `<br/>` inside flowchart node labels and sequence notes renders correctly, and so do commas, slashes and parentheses in messages.\n- **A `;` inside state-diagram text ends the statement.** In a note or a transition label, the text after a `;` renders as stray states. Use a comma instead.\n- **Markdown inside an `@{ shape, label }` label renders as plain text.** Leave markdown out of expanded-shape labels.\n- **Mermaid ignores a subgraph's `direction` when edges cross its border.** Set the layout direction on the whole flowchart (`flowchart LR` or `flowchart TD`). When a flowchart grows too wide in one direction, redraw it in the other.\n=====FLOW\n# Flowchart\n\n## When to use\n\nBranching logic, decision trees, request routing, and flows across a repository's services or modules, where decisions and paths matter more than the order of messages. For who calls whom and in what order, use a [sequence diagram](sequence-diagram.md).\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type and direction: `flowchart TD` (top-down) or `flowchart LR` (left-right).\n- Take each node's shape from the palette below and declare it as an expanded shape: `A@{ shape: cyl, label: \"…\" }`. The short forms `A[Rectangle]` and `B{Diamond}` draw the same `rect` and `diam`.\n- Edges: `-->` solid, `==>` thick, `-.->` dotted. Labels go on the edge: `A -->|yes| B` or `A -- text --> B`.\n- Fan-out shorthand: `A --> B & C` points A to both B and C.\n- **Grouping**: wrap related nodes in `subgraph Name ... end` to show a boundary, such as a service, a layer or a repository. Quote the title if it has spaces: `subgraph s[\"Consumer API\"]`.\n\n## Worked example\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    User@{ shape: stadium, label: \"User\" }\n    Gateway@{ shape: rect, label: \"Consumer API\" }\n    Auth@{ shape: diam, label: \"auth valid?\" }\n    Reject@{ shape: rect, label: \"Return 401\" }\n    Courses@{ shape: rect, label: \"Fetch courses\" }\n    Rules@{ shape: hex, label: \"Pricing rules\" }\n    DB@{ shape: cyl, label: \"Postgres\" }\n    Mail@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Email provider\" }\n\n    User --> Gateway --> Auth\n    Auth -->|no| Reject\n    Auth -->|yes| Courses\n\n    subgraph data[\"Data API\"]\n        Courses --> Rules\n    end\n\n    Courses --> DB\n    Courses -.-> Mail\n\n    classDef grey fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n    classDef blue fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n    classDef green fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef amber fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n    classDef orange fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n    classDef violet fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n    classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n\n    class User grey\n    class Gateway,Auth blue\n    class Courses green\n    class Rules amber\n    class DB orange\n    class Mail violet\n    class Reject red\n```\n\n## Palette\n\nGive each kind of element the same shape and colour in every diagram, so a reader who has seen one diagram reads the next one without a legend. This palette is the default; use another shape or colour only when the diagram needs a kind of element the table does not list.\n\n### Syntax\n\nDeclare an expanded shape on the node's first use, with the label inside the braces:\n\n```\nDB@{ shape: cyl, label: \"Database<br/>of the service\" }\n```\n\nLater references use the bare id. The 24-character line rule from `SKILL.md` applies to the label.\n\n### Which shape and colour for each element\n\n| Element | Shape | Colour |\n|---|---|---|\n| A person or a client: a user, an admin, a frontend | `stadium` | grey |\n| An API or service the user reaches | `rect` | blue |\n| A module, a step, a part of a worker | `rect` | green |\n| A durable job or a workflow | `subproc` | green |\n| A background job, a scheduled task | `st-rect` | green |\n| A shared piece several components use: a library, a set of rules | `hex` | amber |\n| An interface or a payload crossing a boundary | `braces` | amber |\n| A store: a database, a table, stored state, a warehouse you query | `cyl` | orange |\n| An external service: a SaaS, a third-party API | `cloud` | violet |\n| A decision | `diam` | blue |\n| A wait: a timeout, a period without activity | `delay` | grey |\n| A failure or a dropped outcome | `rect` | red |\n\nA service's boundary is a `subgraph`, and its storage sits outside the subgraph.\n\n### Colours\n\nCopy this block into the diagram and assign each node with `class <ids> <colour>`. Declare only the colours the diagram uses.\n\n```\nclassDef grey fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\nclassDef blue fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\nclassDef green fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\nclassDef amber fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\nclassDef orange fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\nclassDef violet fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\nclassDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n```\n\nA state diagram takes the same colours through `classDef`; see [state-diagram.md](state-diagram.md).\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Colour through the palette's classes**, not inline styles. Apply one class to several nodes with a comma list: `class B,C,F green`.\n- **One-off node styling**, for a node the palette does not cover: `style C fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:3px`.\n- **Highlight a path**: colour edges by their declaration order (0-indexed): `linkStyle 0,1,4 stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:3px`. `linkStyle default` styles every edge.\n- **Use `subgraph` to group** by service, layer or repository. It is the main way to show architecture boundaries in a flow.\n- **Line breaks** inside labels: `A[\"Line one<br/>Line two\"]`.\n- **Match the direction to the content**: `LR` reads as a pipeline, `TD` as a decision tree. Set it on the whole flowchart, not on a subgraph.\n=====STATE\n# State Diagram\n\n## When to use\n\nThe lifecycle of an entity: how an order, a payment, a subscription or a job moves between statuses, and which event triggers each move. If you are writing \"when X happens it goes from pending to active\", draw a state diagram.\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type: `stateDiagram-v2`.\n- **Start and end**: `[*] --> First` marks the entry; `Last --> [*]` marks the end.\n- **Transitions** carry the triggering event as a label: `Pending --> Active : payment_confirmed`.\n- **Named states with descriptions**: `state \"Waiting for payment\" as Pending`.\n- **Composite states** group substates inside a parent, for a state that has its own internal lifecycle:\n  ```\n  state Active {\n      [*] --> Running\n      Running --> Paused : pause\n      Paused --> Running : resume\n  }\n  ```\n- **Choice, fork and join**: `state c <<choice>>` for a branch, `state f <<fork>>` and `state j <<join>>` for parallel paths.\n- **Notes**: `note right of StateName : text`.\n- **Colour**: `classDef` and `class` work as in a flowchart: `classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626` and `class Cancelled red`.\n\n## Worked example\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Draft\n\n    Draft --> Submitted : submit\n    Submitted --> Cancelled : cancel\n\n    state Submitted {\n        [*] --> AwaitingPayment\n        AwaitingPayment --> Paid : payment_confirmed\n        AwaitingPayment --> AwaitingPayment : retry\n    }\n\n    Submitted --> Fulfilled : ship\n    Fulfilled --> [*]\n    Cancelled --> [*]\n\n    note right of Submitted : Holds the payment flow\n\n    classDef green fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n\n    class Fulfilled green\n    class Cancelled red\n```\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Colour with the palette** in [flowchart.md](flowchart.md). Use the same colours for the same meaning: red for a failed or dropped state, grey for a wait, green for a state where work runs.\n- **Use composite states for substates** when a status has its own internal lifecycle, as `Submitted` holds the payment steps above. The top level then shows only the statuses, and the substates appear inside the composite state.\n- **Label every transition** with the event or condition that causes it. An unlabeled transition leaves the reader guessing what triggers the change.\n- **Use `<<choice>>`** for one decision point with mutually exclusive outgoing transitions, and **`<<fork>>` / `<<join>>`** only when the entity does things in parallel.\n- **A self-transition** (`AwaitingPayment --> AwaitingPayment : retry`) shows a retry that does not leave the state.\n- **Add a `note`** to explain a state or an invariant the diagram cannot express in its structure.\n- **Never put a `;` in a note or a transition label.** It ends the statement and the rest renders as stray states; use a comma.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ad04baa7-f089-465d-8904-59c886c1e1fa","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"subcommandResults"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
