{"parentUuid":"3aa4e08e-7f70-4b94-bb97-69785f5b8f0a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuoZyrdox2TE5ENhnBRd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018q43FfLHm1N7Ko7MbUJdVk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2; for f in mermaid/scripts/README.md mermaid/references/sequence-diagram.md mermaid/references/class-diagram.md mermaid/references/er-diagram.md; do echo \"----- $f\"; cat $f; done"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2416,"cache_read_input_tokens":650421,"output_tokens":162,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2416,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":162,"cache_read_input_tokens":650421,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2416,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2416},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018q43FfLHm1N7Ko7MbUJdVk":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2; for f in mermaid/scripts/README.md mermaid/references/sequence-diagram.md mermaid/references/class-diagram.md mermaid/references/er-diagram.md; do echo \"----- $f\"; cat $f; done"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"d5052be2-e0f3-434e-9c81-5f0c84a13751","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfuoZyDwsUjyKRtmRj5PB","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"de8bbe85-85a4-4e28-ba8a-7ccbd7b4cf7d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:17:04.815Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"de8bbe85-85a4-4e28-ba8a-7ccbd7b4cf7d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c89c9508-3a14-466d-a396-c3993d3d1069","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018q43FfLHm1N7Ko7MbUJdVk","type":"tool_result","content":"----- mermaid/scripts/README.md\n# scripts/validate-mermaid.sh\n\nMove `superpowers/skills/mermaid-validation/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh` to `superpowers/skills/mermaid/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh` unchanged. It reads the diagram from stdin, which `SKILL.md` feeds with `< /tmp/diagram.mmd`.\n\nDo not port Filadd's `validate-mermaid.mjs` or `notion-shapes.txt`: they carry the Notion shape check, which superpowers does not have.\n\nThe script names its cache package `mermaid-validation-cache` (line 17). That name only labels the shared cache in `~/.cache/claude-plugins/mermaid-ast/`, and renaming it would not reinstall anything, so leave it.\n----- mermaid/references/sequence-diagram.md\n# Sequence Diagram\n\n## When to use\n\nInteractions over time across actors, services or APIs: who calls whom, in what order, and what comes back. Use it for a request flow that spans a frontend, a BFF or consumer API, and one or more data APIs. For branching logic with no order in time, use a [flowchart](flowchart.md).\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type: `sequenceDiagram`.\n- **Participants**: `actor U as User` for people (a stick figure); `participant S as Service` for systems (a box). Declaring them first fixes their left-to-right order.\n- **Messages**: `->>` solid call, `-->>` dashed return, `-x` lost message. Always give a label after the colon: `U->>S: GET /courses`.\n- **Activations** (the bar that shows a participant is working): append `+` to the arrow to activate the target and `-` to deactivate it on return, `Alice->>+John: request` then `John-->>-Alice: response`. Or write `activate John` and `deactivate John`.\n- **Fragments**: `alt`/`else` (conditional), `opt` (optional), `loop` (repeat), `par`/`and` (parallel). Each closes with `end`.\n- **Notes**: `Note over A,B: text`, `Note right of A: text`.\n- **`autonumber`** numbers every message.\n\n## Worked example\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor U as User\n    participant BFF as Consumer API\n    participant API as Courses API\n    participant DB as Postgres\n\n    Note over U,API: Authenticated fetch\n    U->>+BFF: GET /courses\n    BFF->>+API: GET /internal/courses\n    API->>DB: SELECT ...\n    DB-->>API: rows\n    API-->>-BFF: courses[]\n    BFF-->>-U: 200 courses[]\n\n    alt no enrollment\n        U->>BFF: POST /enroll\n        BFF-->>U: 201 created\n    else already enrolled\n        BFF-->>U: 409 conflict\n    end\n```\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Draw people as `actor` and systems as `participant`.**\n- **Use activations** to show which participant is working and when control returns. Write them with the `+`/`-` shorthand on the messages so each activation has its end.\n- **Group a phase with `rect`**: wrap the messages of one phase in a coloured background, with a `Note over` that names it:\n  ```\n  rect rgba(37, 99, 235, 0.15)\n      Note over U,API: Phase 1, request\n      ...\n  end\n  ```\n  Use a different translucent colour per phase; an alpha of about 0.15 to 0.25 keeps the messages readable. `rect` blocks can nest.\n- **Use `autonumber`** when the prose refers to steps by number.\n- **Declare participants in the order they should appear** so message lines cross as little as possible.\n- **Keep messages plain text.** Braces and `<br/>` in a message break some renderers; see the rendering rules in `SKILL.md`.\n----- mermaid/references/class-diagram.md\n# Class Diagram\n\n## When to use\n\nThe structure of objects and types and their relationships: domain classes, DTO hierarchies, service interfaces, and how they inherit from or compose one another. Use it when a plan introduces a set of related types and you want to show their shape and dependencies. For persistence models (tables and fields), use an [ER diagram](er-diagram.md); use a class diagram for code structure.\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type: `classDiagram`.\n- **Members** with visibility: `+` public, `-` private, `#` protected, `~` package. Attributes are `+Type name`; methods are `+name(args) ReturnType`.\n  ```\n  class Course {\n      +UUID id\n      +String title\n      +publish() void\n  }\n  ```\n- **Stereotypes**: `<<interface>>`, `<<service>>`, `<<abstract>>` inside a class.\n- **Relationships**:\n\n  | Notation | Meaning |\n  |---|---|\n  | `A <|-- B` | B inherits from A |\n  | `A *-- B` | composition (B is part of A) |\n  | `A o-- B` | aggregation |\n  | `A --> B` | association, \"uses\" |\n  | `A ..> B` | dependency |\n  | `A ..|> B` | B implements interface A |\n\n  Add multiplicities and a label: `Course \"1\" *-- \"0..n\" Lesson : contains`.\n- **`namespace Name { ... }`** groups related classes into a labeled box.\n\n## Worked example\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Enrollable {\n        <<interface>>\n        +enroll(userId) Enrollment\n    }\n\n    class Course {\n        +UUID id\n        +String title\n        -bool published\n        +publish() void\n    }\n\n    class Lesson {\n        +UUID id\n        +String title\n        +int order\n    }\n\n    class Enrollment {\n        +UUID id\n        +UUID userId\n        +Date enrolledAt\n    }\n\n    Enrollable <|.. Course : implements\n    Course \"1\" *-- \"0..n\" Lesson : contains\n    Course \"1\" --> \"0..n\" Enrollment : produces\n```\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Pick the arrow that matches the relationship.** Inheritance (`<|--`), composition (`*--`, a part that shares its owner's lifecycle), aggregation (`o--`, a part that outlives its owner) and association (`-->`) mean different things, and the wrong arrow misleads the reader.\n- **Add multiplicities** (`\"1\"`, `\"0..n\"`) on the ends where cardinality matters, as in an ER diagram.\n- **Use stereotypes** (`<<interface>>`, `<<service>>`) to mark interfaces and service objects.\n- **Use `namespace`** to group classes by module or package when the diagram spans more than one area. It shows the same boundary a `subgraph` shows in a flowchart.\n- **List only the members the change touches.** A class box that lists every field hides the ones the reader needs.\n----- mermaid/references/er-diagram.md\n# Entity-Relationship Diagram\n\n## When to use\n\nData models, database schemas and model changes: entities, their fields and the relationships between them. Use it when a plan introduces or alters tables or models.\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type: `erDiagram`.\n- **Relationship line**: `ENTITY_A <cardinality>--<cardinality> ENTITY_B : label`. The label names the relationship (`allows`, `places`, `contains`).\n- **Cardinality** is written on each end of the line:\n\n  | Notation | Meaning |\n  |---|---|\n  | `\\|o` / `o\\|` | zero or one |\n  | `\\|\\|` | exactly one |\n  | `}o` / `o{` | zero or more |\n  | `}\\|` / `\\|{` | one or more |\n\n  A solid line (`--`) is an identifying relationship; a dashed line (`..`) is a non-identifying one.\n- **Attributes** go in a block after the entity: `type name key \"comment\"`. Keys are `PK`, `FK`, `UK`, combined with a comma: `PK, FK`. Comments are double-quoted and cannot contain double quotes.\n- **No `%%` comments.** The validator rejects them in an ER diagram.\n\n## Worked example\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    USER ||--o{ ENROLLMENT : has\n    COURSE ||--o{ ENROLLMENT : \"is taken in\"\n    COURSE ||--o{ PREREQUISITE : requires\n\n    USER {\n        uuid id PK\n        string email UK\n        string name\n    }\n    COURSE {\n        uuid id PK\n        string title\n        bool published\n    }\n    ENROLLMENT {\n        uuid id PK\n        uuid user_id FK\n        uuid course_id FK\n        timestamp enrolled_at\n    }\n    PREREQUISITE {\n        uuid course_id PK, FK\n        uuid requires_course_id PK, FK\n    }\n```\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Check every cardinality.** `USER ||--o{ ENROLLMENT` reads \"one user has zero or more enrollments\". Read each line aloud to check it.\n- **Mark keys** (`PK`, `FK`, `UK`) on attributes. They show the schema's integrity rules and the join paths.\n- **Draw junction tables as entities** (like `ENROLLMENT` and `PREREQUISITE`) instead of a direct many-to-many line, the way the schema is built.\n- **List only the keys and the fields the plan touches**, not every column.\n- **Use the relationship label for the verb** (`requires`, `has`); quote it if it contains spaces.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b41bc3b5-9bc0-40da-914e-94149d2584f9","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:17:05.296Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"----- mermaid/scripts/README.md\n# scripts/validate-mermaid.sh\n\nMove `superpowers/skills/mermaid-validation/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh` to `superpowers/skills/mermaid/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh` unchanged. It reads the diagram from stdin, which `SKILL.md` feeds with `< /tmp/diagram.mmd`.\n\nDo not port Filadd's `validate-mermaid.mjs` or `notion-shapes.txt`: they carry the Notion shape check, which superpowers does not have.\n\nThe script names its cache package `mermaid-validation-cache` (line 17). That name only labels the shared cache in `~/.cache/claude-plugins/mermaid-ast/`, and renaming it would not reinstall anything, so leave it.\n----- mermaid/references/sequence-diagram.md\n# Sequence Diagram\n\n## When to use\n\nInteractions over time across actors, services or APIs: who calls whom, in what order, and what comes back. Use it for a request flow that spans a frontend, a BFF or consumer API, and one or more data APIs. For branching logic with no order in time, use a [flowchart](flowchart.md).\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type: `sequenceDiagram`.\n- **Participants**: `actor U as User` for people (a stick figure); `participant S as Service` for systems (a box). Declaring them first fixes their left-to-right order.\n- **Messages**: `->>` solid call, `-->>` dashed return, `-x`