,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"addbaf8e-a6eb-4f98-aca6-93f81301f82f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c89c9508-3a14-466d-a396-c3993d3d1069","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CojzpFeAnaBQC3TZtEKNdQ","type":"tool_result","content":"2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is the one exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal.\n\nNew:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is an exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal. When a decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions already settled or from how the code already works, propose that answer with the fact it follows from and ask the user to confirm or correct it, instead of asking the question open.\n\n## design-tree, after \"State a decision the user already made…\"\n\nNew paragraph:\n\nWhen an open decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions the tree already settled or from how the code works today, do not ask it as a question. List it as a proposal, with the decision or the behaviour it follows from, and ask the user to confirm or correct each proposal. A confirmed proposal becomes a ✓ leaf; a corrected one becomes a ✓ leaf with the user's answer. A decision that more than one answer could reasonably settle stays a question.\n\n## design-tree, What a question carries, after the list of things that propose an answer\n\nNew sentence:\n\nThese rules do not apply to a proposal made under the paragraph on decisions with one plausible answer.\n\nNew:\n\nInterface and outputs: one flowchart per member, captioned `<member> · <when it is called>`. The caller and the component sit at the two ends. The call is a solid arrow from the caller, through a braces box holding what the call sends, to the component. The return is a dashed arrow labelled `returns` from the component, through a braces box holding what comes back, to the caller. A member that returns nothing has no return arrow. Each braces box lists its interface's name, then one line per field with its type, then in parentheses what the values mean. A value nested in another gets its own braces box, joined to its parent by a plain line. Style each flowchart with superpowers:mermaid's palette.\n\n\nNew:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results; `from` takes `name`, plus `count` for the n-th step of that name and `type` (`do`, `sleep`, `waitForEvent`), and throws when no step matches. `restart()` with no argument erases the instance's state and runs every step again. No option restarts from the failed step without naming that step, and no binding member returns its name. Only the REST get-instance endpoint returns it, and that endpoint needs an API token. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n- `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts the restart, before any step runs. Through a cross-script binding it took 0.4 to 0.6 s, and right after it `status()` read `queued` with `error` null. The restarted step started 3.1 to 3.8 s later, with a median of 3.4 s, whether it restarted from a named step or from the start, so the delay is scheduling, not replay. A caller that wants the step's own effect, such as a status write, waits for it after `restart()` returns.\n\n## New bullets after \"Steps run once durably via step.do…\"\n\n- Workflows has no hook that runs before each step or on restart. Logic common to every step goes in a wrapper function of your own around `step.do`. Code in `run()` outside a step runs again each time the instance resumes, so a write there has to give the same result when repeated.\n- Steps run concurrently: 25 `step.do` calls awaited together started within 96 ms and ran at once. Outbound `fetch` calls share the Workers limit of 6 open connections per instance, so 25 five-second fetches finished in batches of 6, the last at 26 s. Awaited with `Promise.allSettled`, steps that fail after their retries leave the instance running, even when all of them fail. A `catch` around `Promise.all` that does not wait for the other steps lets the instance report `complete` while they still run.\n- A `step.do` callback can call `step.do`. The children run concurrently and appear in the step list as ordinary top-level steps beside the parent. A parent that throws fails as one step and the instance continues if `run()` catches the error. When the parent retries, every child runs again as a new step, and the retry reuses none of the earlier children. A restart from a step after a fan-out replays the finished children from cache, and the cache keeps a failed child like a successful one, so the restart does not retry it.\n\n## Append to the Deploys bullet","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f4e76fcf-65c2-4286-a36b-a014f9dfa438","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:17:16.207Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is the one exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal.\n\nNew:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is an exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal. When a decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions already settled or from how the code already works, propose that answer with the fact it follows from and ask the user to confirm or correct it, instead of asking the question open.\n\n## design-tree, after \"State a decision the user already made…\"\n\nNew paragraph:\n\nWhen an open decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions the tree already settled or from how the code works today, do not ask it as a question. List it as a proposal, with the decision or the behaviour it follows from, and ask the user to confirm or correct each proposal. A confirmed proposal becomes a ✓ leaf; a corrected one becomes a ✓ leaf with the user's answer. A decision that more than one answer could reasonably settle stays a question.\n\n## design-tree, What a question carries, after the list of things that propose an answer\n\nNew sentence:\n\nThese rules do not apply to a proposal made under the paragraph on decisions with one plausible answer.\n\nNew:\n\nInterface and outputs: one flowchart per member, captioned `<member> · <when it is called>`. The caller and the component sit at the two ends. The call is a solid arrow from the caller, through a braces box holding what the call sends, to the component. The return is a dashed arrow labelled `returns` from the component, through a braces box holding what comes back, to the caller. A member that returns nothing has no return arrow. Each braces box lists its interface's name, then one line per field with its type, then in parentheses what the values mean. A value nested in another gets its own braces box, joined to its parent by a plain line. Style each flowchart with superpowers:mermaid's palette.\n\n\nNew:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results; `from` takes `name`, plus `count` for the n-th step of that name and `type` (`do`, `sleep`, `waitForEvent`), and throws when no step matches. `restart()` with no argument erases the instance's state and runs every step again. No option restarts from the failed step without naming that step, and no binding member returns its name. Only the REST get-instance endpoint returns it, and that endpoint needs an API token. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n- `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts the restart, before any step runs. Through a cross-script binding it took 0.4 to 0.6 s, and right after it `status()` read `queued` with `error` null. The restarted step started 3.1 to 3.8 s later, with a median of 3.4 s, whether it restarted from a named step or from the start, so the delay is scheduling, not replay. A caller that wants the step's own effect, such as a status write, waits for it after `restart()` returns.\n\n## New bullets after \"Steps run once durably via step.do…\"\n\n- Workflows has no hook that runs before each step or on restart. Logic common to every step goes in a wrapper function of your own around `step.do`. Code in `run()` outside a step runs again each time the instance resumes, so a write there has to give the same result when repeated.\n- Steps run concurrently: 25 `step.do` calls awaited together started within 96 ms and ran at once. Outbound `fetch` calls share the Workers limit of 6 open connections per instance, so 25 five-second fetches finished in batches of 6, the last at 26 s. Awaited with `Promise.allSettled`, steps that fail after their retries leave the instance running, even when all of them fail. A `catch` around `Promise.all` that does not wait for the other steps lets the instance report `complete` while they still run.\n- A `step.do` callback can call `step.do`. The children run concurrently and appear in the step list as ordinary top-level steps beside the parent. A parent that throws fails as one step and the instance continues if `run()` catches the error. When the parent retries, every child runs again as a new step, and the retry reuses none of the earlier children. A restart from a step after a fan-out replays the finished children from cache, and the cache keeps a failed child like a successful one, so the restart does not retry it.\n\n## Append to the Deploys bullet","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"adcd1fe5-f69e-4e3a-9ae7-746badd0aae9","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":1},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"addbaf8e-a6eb-4f98-aca6-93f81301f82f","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-