{"parentUuid":"b3553b73-d7ad-401d-8b9e-f9ef64f78daa","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuobBApFZerwa8kTxXsH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1898,"cache_read_input_tokens":659804,"output_tokens":14792,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":95},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1898,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":14792,"cache_read_input_tokens":659804,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1898,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1898},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":5972,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"ed69da55-7b8e-411e-a7f3-331648ebe29a","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfuobAda1Hq7CfoHpchXX","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"f9467549-175b-4e2e-bddc-05c94fa57d30","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:17:25.732Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"f9467549-175b-4e2e-bddc-05c94fa57d30","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuobBApFZerwa8kTxXsH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All the learn drafts are below, each written to `.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round2/` and through both unslop rounds. Approve, correct or drop each one separately.\n\n## 1. Every text in tldraw's default font\n\n**`mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, Drawing**\n\nOld:\n\n- Text is black, arrow labels included.\n\nNew:\n\n- Text is black, arrow labels included, and written in tldraw's default font, the hand font (`font: 'draw'`): prose, notes, labels and cards alike. Use the monospace font only where the loading skill asks for it, such as a tree of leaves.\n\n## 2. Sizing a geo shape set from a script\n\n**`mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, Drawing, a new bullet after \"Resize an area whenever what it holds grows or moves\"**\n\n- **Set `growY: 0` whenever a script sets a geo shape's `h`.** tldraw keeps the `growY` it computed for the earlier size, and the shape stays taller than what it holds. To stack shapes inside a geo shape under its label, read the label's height first: the height of the shape's `.tl-rich-text` element divided by the zoom level.\n\n## 3. Questions under the write-up\n\n**`mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, Questions**\n\nOld: Questions go in the open branch, at the top of its right side, above its write-up.\n\nNew: Questions go in the open branch, on its right side, under its write-up. The branch's Claude note goes under the questions.\n\n**`mahou/skills/shape-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, Questions**\n\nOld: Questions go in the open branch, at the top of its right side, above its prose.\n\nNew: Questions go in the open branch, on its right side, under its prose. The branch's Claude note goes under the questions.\n\n## 4. What each branch of the opening tree settles\n\n**`mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, the opening-tree paragraph**\n\nOld: The opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with no leaves yet.\n\nNew: The opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with no leaves yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles.\n\n**`mahou/skills/shape-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, the opening-tree paragraph**\n\nOld: The opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with the ✓ leaves the user brought under their areas and no prose yet.\n\nNew: The opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with the ✓ leaves the user brought under their areas and no prose yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles.\n\n## 5. Workflows: restart, fan-out and step facts\n\n**`mahou/skills/wiki/references/cloudflare/workflows.md`**: global. Its index line stays as it is.\n\nReplace the Recovery bullet. Old:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n\nNew:\n\n- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results; `from` takes `name`, plus `count` for the n-th step of that name and `type` (`do`, `sleep`, `waitForEvent`), and throws when no step matches. `restart()` with no argument erases the instance's state and runs every step again. No option restarts from the failed step without naming that step, and no binding member returns its name. Only the REST get-instance endpoint returns it, and that endpoint needs an API token. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n- `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts the restart, before any step runs. Through a cross-script binding it took 0.4 to 0.6 s, and right after it `status()` read `queued` with `error` null. The restarted step started 3.1 to 3.8 s later, with a median of 3.4 s, whether it restarted from a named step or from the start, so the delay is scheduling, not replay. A caller that wants the step's own effect, such as a status write, waits for it after `restart()` returns.\n\nNew bullets after \"Steps run once durably via `step.do`…\":\n\n- Workflows has no hook that runs before each step or on restart. Logic common to every step goes in a wrapper function of your own around `step.do`. Code in `run()` outside a step runs again each time the instance resumes, so a write there has to give the same result when repeated.\n- Steps run concurrently: 25 `step.do` calls awaited together started within 96 ms and ran at once. Outbound `fetch` calls share the Workers limit of 6 open connections per instance, so 25 five-second fetches finished in batches of 6, the last at 26 s. Awaited with `Promise.allSettled`, steps that fail after their retries leave the instance running, even when all of them fail. A `catch` around `Promise.all` that does not wait for the other steps lets the instance report `complete` while they still run.\n- A `step.do` callback can call `step.do`. The children run concurrently and appear in the step list as ordinary top-level steps beside the parent. A parent that throws fails as one step and the instance continues if `run()` catches the error. When the parent retries, every child runs again as a new step, and the retry reuses none of the earlier children. A restart from a step after a fan-out replays the finished children from cache, and the cache keeps a failed child like a successful one, so the restart does not retry it.\n\nAppend to the Deploys bullet: `wrangler delete` on the Worker leaves its Workflow in place; `wrangler workflows delete <name>` removes it.\n\n## 6. Several canvases at once\n\n**`mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, Running it**\n\nOld:\n\n- `start` installs the app on first run, starts the server detached and prints the URL to give the user. One server runs at a time: starting another document stops the server for the first, whose document is already on disk. It refuses a document saved in an older format and names the `migrate` command.\n\nNew:\n\n- `start` installs the app on first run, starts a detached server for the document on the first free port from 5190 and prints the URL to give the user. A document that already has a server gets its URL back. Each document has its own server, so several canvases run at once, in one project or in several; `CANVAS_PORT` picks the port instead. It refuses a document saved in an older format and names the `migrate` command.\n\nOld:\n\n- `stop` stops the server.\n\nNew:\n\n- `stop` stops the document's own server and leaves every other canvas running.\n\n**`mahou/skills/canvas/scripts/canvas.sh`**: the diff against the current script. The drafter tested it with two documents running at once on 5191 and 5192, while your filadd canvas kept running on 5190. It flagged two risks:\n- Servers starting at the same time share vite's dependency cache, so two first-time starts could collide.\n- A canvas started by the current script has no state file. The new `start` reuses that server, but the new `stop` can't stop it.\n\n```diff\n--- canvas.sh (current)\n+++ canvas.sh (draft)\n@@ -6,7 +6,10 @@\n #   canvas.sh export  <document>  write <folder>/<name>.svg from the open document\n #   canvas.sh texts   <document>  print every canvas text and arrow label, one per line\n #   canvas.sh migrate <document>  bring a document saved by an older version of the app to the current format\n-#   canvas.sh stop    <document>  stop the server\n+#   canvas.sh stop    <document>  stop the document's server\n+#\n+# Each document gets its own server on the first free port from 5190 up, or on CANVAS_PORT when it is set. Its\n+# document, port and PID are kept in a state file in the cache, keyed by a hash of the document's path.\n #\n # The app and its dependencies live in a cache folder outside the plugin, because plugin updates replace the\n # plugin's directory. Every document in a folder shares the folder's mockups/.\n@@ -25,18 +28,63 @@\n SKILL_APP=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")/app\" && pwd)\"\n CACHE=\"${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/mahou-canvas\"\n APP=\"$CACHE/app\"\n-PORT=\"${CANVAS_PORT:-5190}\"\n-URL=\"http://localhost:$PORT\"\n-PID_FILE=\"$CACHE/server.pid\"\n+SERVERS=\"$CACHE/servers\"\n+FIRST_PORT=5190\n+LAST_PORT=5289\n+\n+# One server per document, so the state, the log and the port belong to that document alone\n+KEY=\"$(printf '%s' \"$DOC\" | { sha256sum 2>/dev/null || shasum -a 256; } | cut -c1-16)\"\n+STATE=\"$SERVERS/$KEY.state\"\n+LOG=\"$SERVERS/$KEY.log\"\n \n-# The document the canvas on the port serves, or nothing. Any other server on the port answers with something else.\n+# The document the canvas on a port serves, or nothing. Any other server on the port answers with something else.\n serving() {\n   local answer\n-  answer=\"$(curl -sf \"$URL/api/health\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n+  answer=\"$(curl -sf --max-time 2 \"http://localhost:$1/api/health\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n \n   [[ \"$answer\" == /* && \"$answer\" != *$'\\n'* && \"$answer\" != *'<'* ]] && echo \"$answer\" || true\n }\n \n+# curl exits with 7 only when nothing accepts the connection; any other result means something listens there\n+port_free() {\n+  local code=0\n+  curl -s -o /dev/null --max-time 2 \"http://localhost:$1\" || code=$?\n+\n+  [[ \"$code\" == 7 ]]\n+}\n+\n+# A state file is stale once its process is gone or its port serves something other than its document\n+state_alive() {\n+  local doc port pid\n+  { read -r doc; read -r port; read -r pid; } <\"$1\" || return 1\n+\n+  kill -0 \"$pid\" 2>/dev/null && [[ \"$(serving \"$port\")\" == \"$doc\" ]]\n+}\n+\n+sweep() {\n+  local state\n+\n+  for state in \"$SERVERS\"/*.state; do\n+    [[ -f \"$state\" ]] || continue\n+\n+    if ! state_alive \"$state\"; then\n+      rm -f \"$state\"\n+    fi\n+  done\n+}\n+\n+# Sets PORT and PID from this document's state file when its server is up and confirmed by the health check\n+find_server() {\n+  [[ -f \"$STATE\" ]] || return 1\n+\n+  if ! state_alive \"$STATE\"; then\n+    rm -f \"$STATE\"\n+    return 1\n+  fi\n+\n+  { read -r _; read -r PORT; read -r PID; } <\"$STATE\"\n+}\n+\n install() {\n   mkdir -p \"$APP\"\n   rsync -a --delete --exclude node_modules --exclude package-lock.json \"$SKILL_APP/\" \"$APP/\"\n@@ -58,33 +106,48 @@\n }\n \n stop() {\n-  [[ -f \"$PID_FILE\" ]] && kill \"$(cat \"$PID_FILE\")\" 2>/dev/null || true\n-  rm -f \"$PID_FILE\"\n+  find_server || return 0\n+\n+  kill \"$PID\" 2>/dev/null || true\n+  rm -f \"$STATE\"\n \n   for _ in $(seq 20); do\n-    [[ -z \"$(serving)\" ]] && return\n+    kill -0 \"$PID\" 2>/dev/null || return 0\n     sleep 0.25\n   done\n }\n \n-start() {\n-  local current\n-  current=\"$(serving)\"\n+# Starts the server on one port and waits for it; fails when the process exits, as vite does when the port is taken\n+launch() {\n+  local port=\"$1\" pid\n \n-  if [[ \"$current\" == \"$DOC\" ]]; then\n-    echo \"$URL\"\n-    return\n-  fi\n+  # Detached, so the server outlives the shell that started it and any background time limit\n+  CANVAS_DOC=\"$DOC\" setsid nohup \"$APP/node_modules/.bin/vite\" --config \"$APP/vite.config.ts\" --port \"$port\" --strictPort \\\n+    >\"$LOG\" 2>&1 </dev/null &\n+  pid=$!\n \n-  # One server serves one document at a time, and the other document is already on disk\n-  if [[ -n \"$current\" ]]; then\n-    echo \"stopping the canvas for $current\" >&2\n-    stop\n-  fi\n+  for _ in $(seq 60); do\n+    if [[ \"$(serving \"$port\")\" == \"$DOC\" ]]; then\n+      printf '%s\\n%s\\n%s\\n' \"$DOC\" \"$port\" \"$pid\" >\"$STATE\"\n+      echo \"http://localhost:$port\"\n+      return 0\n+    fi\n \n-  if curl -s -o /dev/null \"$URL\"; then\n-    echo \"port $PORT is taken by another server; set CANVAS_PORT to use another port\" >&2\n-    exit 1\n+    kill -0 \"$pid\" 2>/dev/null || return 1\n+    sleep 0.5\n+  done\n+\n+  kill \"$pid\" 2>/dev/null || true\n+  return 1\n+}\n+\n+start() {\n+  mkdir -p \"$SERVERS\"\n+  sweep\n+\n+  if find_server; then\n+    echo \"http://localhost:$PORT\"\n+    return\n   fi\n \n   # An older document would be served in a format the app no longer reads, and its edits saved over it\n@@ -94,17 +157,42 @@\n \n   scaffold\n \n-  # Detached, so the server outlives the shell that started it and any background time limit\n-  CANVAS_DOC=\"$DOC\" setsid nohup \"$APP/node_modules/.bin/vite\" --config \"$APP/vite.config.ts\" --port \"$PORT\" --strictPort \\\n-    >\"$CACHE/server.log\" 2>&1 </dev/null &\n-  echo $! >\"$PID_FILE\"\n+  if [[ -n \"${CANVAS_PORT:-}\" ]]; then\n+    if ! port_free \"$CANVAS_PORT\"; then\n+      echo \"port $CANVAS_PORT is taken by another server; unset CANVAS_PORT to pick a free port\" >&2\n+      exit 1\n+    fi\n \n-  for _ in $(seq 60); do\n-    [[ \"$(serving)\" == \"$DOC\" ]] && { echo \"$URL\"; return; }\n-    sleep 0.5\n+    launch \"$CANVAS_PORT\" && return\n+    echo \"the server did not come up; its log is $LOG\" >&2\n+    exit 1\n+  fi\n+\n+  local port\n+  for port in $(seq \"$FIRST_PORT\" \"$LAST_PORT\"); do\n+    # A server started before the state files existed has none, and a second one would write the same document\n+    if [[ \"$(serving \"$port\")\" == \"$DOC\" ]]; then\n+      echo \"http://localhost:$port\"\n+      return\n+    fi\n+\n+    port_free \"$port\" || continue\n+\n+    # Another start may take the port between the check and the launch; vite then exits and the next port is tried\n+    launch \"$port\" && return\n+\n+    if [[ \"$(serving \"$port\")\" == \"$DOC\" ]]; then\n+      echo \"http://localhost:$port\"\n+      return\n+    fi\n+\n+    if port_free \"$port\"; then\n+      echo \"the server did not come up; its log is $LOG\" >&2\n+      exit 1\n+    fi\n   done\n \n-  echo \"the server did not come up; its log is $CACHE/server.log\" >&2\n+  echo \"no free port between $FIRST_PORT and $LAST_PORT\" >&2\n   exit 1\n }\n \n@@ -113,11 +201,11 @@\n     start\n     ;;\n   watch)\n-    start >/dev/null\n+    URL=\"$(start)\"\n     curl -sN \"$URL/api/events\" | sed -u -n 's/^data: //p'\n     ;;\n   export)\n-    start >/dev/null\n+    URL=\"$(start)\"\n     # Its own browser session, so the export leaves Claude's open tab alone\n     agent-browser --session canvas-export open \"$URL?user=export\" >/dev/null\n     agent-browser --session canvas-export wait --fn \"window.editor != null\" >/dev/null\n@@ -128,7 +216,7 @@\n     document_cli texts\n     ;;\n   migrate)\n-    if [[ \"$(serving)\" == \"$DOC\" ]]; then\n+    if find_server; then\n       echo \"stop the canvas before migrating its document\" >&2\n       exit 1\n     fi\n```\n\n## 7. Proposing an answer when only one is plausible\n\n**`mahou/skills/basics/SKILL.md`, rule 2**\n\nOld:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is the one exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal.\n\nNew:\n\n2. **Never decide for the user.** Map the state, then ask. No recommendations, no leaning toward an option, no answering for them. Give a recommendation only when the user asks for one. mahou:program-design is an exception: it proposes the details of a design whose decisions are already settled, grounded in the code and the conventions, and the user corrects each proposal. When a decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions already settled or from how the code already works, propose that answer with the fact it follows from and ask the user to confirm or correct it, instead of asking the question open.\n\n**`mahou/skills/design-tree/SKILL.md`, a new paragraph after \"State a decision the user already made…\"**\n\nWhen an open decision has only one plausible answer, because it follows from decisions the tree already settled or from how the code works today, do not ask it as a question. List it as a proposal, with the decision or the behaviour it follows from, and ask the user to confirm or correct each proposal. A confirmed proposal becomes a ✓ leaf; a corrected one becomes a ✓ leaf with the user's answer. A decision that more than one answer could reasonably settle stays a question.\n\n**`mahou/skills/design-tree/SKILL.md`, What a question carries, a new sentence after the list of things that propose an answer**\n\nThese rules do not apply to a proposal made under the paragraph on decisions with one plausible answer.\n\n## 8. Design types in the palette, contract per member, canvas references\n\n**`mahou/skills/design/references/types/contract.md`, Representation**: replace the Interface paragraph and its table, and the Outputs paragraph and its class diagram, with:\n\nInterface and outputs: one flowchart per member, captioned `<member> · <when it is called>`. The caller and the component sit at the two ends. The call is a solid arrow from the caller, through a braces box holding what the call sends, to the component. The return is a dashed arrow labelled `returns` from the component, through a braces box holding what comes back, to the caller. A member that returns nothing has no return arrow. Each braces box lists its interface's name, then one line per field with its type, then in parentheses what the values mean. A value nested in another gets its own braces box, joined to its parent by a plain line. Style each flowchart with superpowers:mermaid's palette.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  caller@{ shape: rect, label: \"Caller\" } -->|\"create\"| inp@{ shape: braces, label: \"CreateParams\n  resourceId: string\n  (the row the caller just wrote)\" }\n  inp --> comp@{ shape: subproc, label: \"Component\" }\n  comp -.-> out@{ shape: braces, label: \"Instance\n  id: string\n  (stored on the row)\" }\n  out -.->|\"returns\"| caller\n  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef payload fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n  class caller worker\n  class comp flow\n  class inp,out payload\n```\n\nThe prose for each member states what the diagram cannot: when the member is called, what an absent field means, who validates the value and what an invalid one does to the caller.\n\n**`component-architecture.md`, Representation, append:** Draw each component, store and outside service with its kind's shape and colour from superpowers:mermaid's palette, so the diagram reads without a legend.\n\n**`data-flow.md`, Representation, append:** Style the participants and nodes with superpowers:mermaid's palette.\n\n**`lifecycle.md`, Representation, append:** Colour each state by what it means for whoever reads it, with `classDef`: a waiting state amber, a working state blue, a done state green, a failed state red. Keep `;` out of notes and transition labels.\n\n**`rules.md`, Representation, append:** Colour the flowchart with superpowers:mermaid's palette: steps green, decisions blue, an outcome that drops or fails red, what gets stored orange.\n\n**`mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, a new paragraph and table after \"Use `design`'s types for the representations…\"**\n\nDraw a representation as its canvas reference under `references/types/` describes, when one exists; otherwise as `design`'s type describes. Each canvas reference has the same file name as the type it adapts.\n\n| The branch is about | `design` type | Canvas reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| What a component agrees with the rest of the system | `contract.md` | `references/types/contract.md` |\n| How a value moves between components | `data-flow.md` | `references/types/data-flow.md` |\n\n**New file `mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/references/types/contract.md`**\n\n# Contract on the canvas\n\nDraw one `mermaid` shape per member, in the form `design`'s contract type gives, each under a caption `<member> · <when it is called>` in the hand font, stacked in the order the members are called. A branch with several boundaries draws each boundary's members under that boundary's sub-branch heading. Draw no table and no separate class diagram: the braces boxes inside each flowchart carry the interfaces.\n\n**New file `mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/references/types/data-flow.md`**\n\n# Data flow on the canvas\n\nThe canvas's `mermaid` shape draws sequence diagrams with their labels out of place. Draw a data flow as a left-to-right flowchart instead: the components as nodes in the palette's shapes, each arrow numbered in the order of the path (`1 GET the audio`, `2 the item exists?`), and the prose naming the steps by number. Use a sequence diagram in the final document only when the document's renderer draws it correctly.\n\n## 9. `superpowers:mermaid` replaces `superpowers:mermaid-validation`\n\nA new skill, ported from `filadd-experimental-dev:mermaid` without its Notion parts. It adds the palette as the default, the missing state-diagram reference, a render-and-look step, and the rendering rules this session hit. The validator script moves over unchanged.\n\n**`superpowers/skills/mermaid/SKILL.md`**\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: mermaid\ndescription: Loaded to choose, write, style and validate mermaid diagrams (flowcharts, sequence, state, ER and class diagrams). Carries a chart-selection table, one reference per chart type with a default shape and colour palette, the common syntax rules, a bundled validator and the rendering rules the validator cannot catch. Use whenever you generate, write or edit a mermaid code block, and validate every diagram before showing it, even one that looks correct.\n---\n\n# Mermaid Diagrams\n\nUse a diagram to show something that prose would explain worse: a data model, a flow across services or APIs, a state lifecycle, an object structure. A diagram is worth drawing when the subject has many parts, branches, an order over time or a structure. Do not diagram something one sentence explains.\n\n## How to use this skill\n\n1. Pick the chart type from the chart-selection table below.\n2. Open the matching `references/*.md` for that chart's syntax, a worked example and its styling guidance. Load only the references you need.\n3. Write the diagram following the common syntax rules. In a flowchart or a state diagram, take each element's shape and colour from the palette in [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md).\n4. Validate it, then render it and look at the image (see Validation). Both steps are mandatory.\n5. Place the diagram next to the prose it illustrates, not in a separate \"diagrams\" section.\n\n## Chart selection\n\n| You're documenting | Chart | Reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| A data model, a schema, database changes (entities, fields, relationships) | `erDiagram` | [references/er-diagram.md](references/er-diagram.md) |\n| Branching logic, request routing, a flow across a repository's services | `flowchart` | [references/flowchart.md](references/flowchart.md) |\n| Interaction over time across actors, services or APIs (who calls whom, in what order) | `sequenceDiagram` | [references/sequence-diagram.md](references/sequence-diagram.md) |\n| The lifecycle or status transitions of an entity | `stateDiagram-v2` | [references/state-diagram.md](references/state-diagram.md) |\n| Object and type structure and relationships (classes, DTO hierarchies, interfaces) | `classDiagram` | [references/class-diagram.md](references/class-diagram.md) |\n\nWhen two charts fit, such as a flow that could be a `flowchart` or a `sequenceDiagram`, use `sequenceDiagram` when who acts and the order of messages matter, and `flowchart` when branches and decisions matter more than order.\n\n## Common syntax\n\n- **Start every diagram with a type declaration**: `flowchart TD`, `sequenceDiagram`, `stateDiagram-v2`, `erDiagram`, `classDiagram`. A block without one does not render.\n- **Direction** (flowchart and state diagram): `TD` or `TB` runs top-down, `LR` left to right, `BT` bottom-up and `RL` right to left. Use `LR` for pipelines and flows, `TD` for trees and decisions.\n- **Quote labels with special characters.** Quote any label that contains `()`, `[]`, `{}`, `:`, `,` or quotes: `A[\"Process (step 1)\"]`. Unquoted special characters cause most parse failures.\n- **Define a node once, then refer to it by id.** Give a node its shape and label on first use (`A[Start]`), then write only `A`.\n- **Comments** start with `%%` on their own line.\n- **Leave out decoration and any detail the reader does not need.**\n\n## Validation (mandatory)\n\nMermaid's syntax is strict, and an invalid diagram renders as a broken code block with no warning. Validate every diagram before you show it. Write the diagram to a temp file and feed the file to the validator; do not pass a multi-line diagram as an inline shell string, because shell quoting breaks it and the validator reports errors that are not in the diagram.\n\n1. **Write** the raw diagram body (the text between the ```mermaid fences, without the fences) to a temp file with the `Write` tool, such as `/tmp/diagram.mmd`.\n2. **Run** the validator, which reads the diagram from stdin:\n   bash ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/mermaid/scripts/validate-mermaid.sh < /tmp/diagram.mmd\n3. **Read the result.** The script prints JSON; check `isValid` and `errors[]` (each with a `line` and a `message`). Exit codes: `0` valid, `1` invalid, `2` setup error.\n4. If the diagram is invalid, **fix** it using the `line` and `message` of each error, then run the validator again until `isValid` is `true`.\n5. **Render** the valid diagram and look at the image before you show the diagram. A diagram that parses can still draw wrong (see Rendering rules the validator cannot catch):\n   npx -y -p @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli mmdc -i /tmp/diagram.mmd -o /tmp/diagram.png\n   If mmdc reports that it cannot find `chrome-headless-shell`, install one and point mmdc at it:\n   npx -y puppeteer browsers install chrome-headless-shell\n   export PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=$(ls -d ~/.cache/puppeteer/chrome-headless-shell/*/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell | tail -1)\n   The installer fetches the newest version, and mmdc rejects any version other than its own unless `PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH` points at a browser. Open `/tmp/diagram.png` with the `Read` tool. Check for clipped text, stray nodes or states, and a layout far wider or taller than the page.\n\nThe script parses with mermaid-ast, which runs the grammars vendored from mermaid itself, so a diagram it accepts is one mermaid parses. It covers 18 diagram types and installs itself on first run into `~/.cache/claude-plugins/mermaid-ast/`. The script accepts a leading frontmatter block and strips it before parsing. An error carries a `line` when the grammar reports one, and never a column.\n\n### Validator limitations\n\n- **ER diagrams**: the validator rejects a `%%` comment inside an `erDiagram`, although mermaid accepts it. Leave comments out of ER diagrams.\n\n### Fixing common errors\n\n- **Unquoted labels with special characters**: wrap them in quotes, `A[\"Total (USD)\"]`, `B{\"valid?\"}`.\n- **Wrong arrow for the diagram type**: `-->` in flowcharts, `->>`/`-->>` in sequence diagrams, `-->` in state and class diagrams. Mixing them fails.\n- **Missing type declaration**: the first line that is not a comment must declare the diagram type.\n- **`subgraph` or `state` block without its end**: every `subgraph` needs an `end`, and every composite `state X {` needs its `}`.\n- **A node's shape declared twice**: declare the shape once; later references use the bare id.\n- **Reserved word as a bare node id**: `end`, `class` and similar ids break parsing; rename the node or quote it.\n\n## Rendering rules the validator cannot catch\n\nThe parser accepts these, and the renderer draws them wrong. Check for them in the rendered image after validation passes.\n\n- **Long lines inside a container are cut.** A flowchart node, a subgraph title, a note in a sequence or state diagram, a class box, an entity box: the renderer clips any line in these that runs past about 24 characters. Break the text with `<br/>` at a word boundary before the 24th character, and again every 24 characters after that. Text outside a box is unaffected: edge labels, state transition labels and sequence messages wrap or extend on their own.\n- **Curly braces and `<br/>` in sequence messages break some renderers.** A sequenceDiagram message carrying `{id}`-style braces or a `<br/>` tag parses, so the validator passes it, but some renderers (Obsidian's, observed) fail at the first such message and report the parse error on a later line. Keep sequence messages plain text, and put text that must be split into a note. `<br/>` inside flowchart node labels and sequence notes renders correctly, and so do commas, slashes and parentheses in messages.\n- **A `;` inside state-diagram text ends the statement.** In a note or a transition label, the text after a `;` renders as stray states. Use a comma instead.\n- **Markdown inside an `@{ shape, label }` label renders as plain text.** Leave markdown out of expanded-shape labels.\n- **Mermaid ignores a subgraph's `direction` when edges cross its border.** Set the layout direction on the whole flowchart (`flowchart LR` or `flowchart TD`). When a flowchart grows too wide in one direction, redraw it in the other.\n```\n\nThe real file keeps the fenced bash and JSON blocks in the Validation steps. I flattened them here only so the outer block shows.\n\n**`superpowers/skills/mermaid/references/flowchart.md`** (the palette)\n\n```markdown\n# Flowchart\n\n## When to use\n\nBranching logic, decision trees, request routing, and flows across a repository's services or modules, where decisions and paths matter more than the order of messages. For who calls whom and in what order, use a [sequence diagram](sequence-diagram.md).\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type and direction: `flowchart TD` (top-down) or `flowchart LR` (left-right).\n- Take each node's shape from the palette below and declare it as an expanded shape: `A@{ shape: cyl, label: \"…\" }`. The short forms `A[Rectangle]` and `B{Diamond}` draw the same `rect` and `diam`.\n- Edges: `-->` solid, `==>` thick, `-.->` dotted. Labels go on the edge: `A -->|yes| B` or `A -- text --> B`.\n- Fan-out shorthand: `A --> B & C` points A to both B and C.\n- **Grouping**: wrap related nodes in `subgraph Name ... end` to show a boundary, such as a service, a layer or a repository. Quote the title if it has spaces: `subgraph s[\"Consumer API\"]`.\n\n## Worked example\n\n(a flowchart using every palette colour: a User stadium, a Consumer API rect, an \"auth valid?\" diamond, a \"Return 401\" red rect, a \"Fetch courses\" step inside a \"Data API\" subgraph, a \"Pricing rules\" hex, a Postgres cylinder and an \"Email provider\" cloud, with the classDef block below applied)\n\n## Palette\n\nGive each kind of element the same shape and colour in every diagram, so a reader who has seen one diagram reads the next one without a legend. This palette is the default; use another shape or colour only when the diagram needs a kind of element the table does not list.\n\n### Syntax\n\nDeclare an expanded shape on the node's first use, with the label inside the braces:\n\n    DB@{ shape: cyl, label: \"Database<br/>of the service\" }\n\nLater references use the bare id. The 24-character line rule from `SKILL.md` applies to the label.\n\n### Which shape and colour for each element\n\n| Element | Shape | Colour |\n|---|---|---|\n| A person or a client: a user, an admin, a frontend | `stadium` | grey |\n| An API or service the user reaches | `rect` | blue |\n| A module, a step, a part of a worker | `rect` | green |\n| A durable job or a workflow | `subproc` | green |\n| A background job, a scheduled task | `st-rect` | green |\n| A shared piece several components use: a library, a set of rules | `hex` | amber |\n| An interface or a payload crossing a boundary | `braces` | amber |\n| A store: a database, a table, stored state, a warehouse you query | `cyl` | orange |\n| An external service: a SaaS, a third-party API | `cloud` | violet |\n| A decision | `diam` | blue |\n| A wait: a timeout, a period without activity | `delay` | grey |\n| A failure or a dropped outcome | `rect` | red |\n\nA service's boundary is a `subgraph`, and its storage sits outside the subgraph.\n\n### Colours\n\nCopy this block into the diagram and assign each node with `class <ids> <colour>`. Declare only the colours the diagram uses.\n\n    classDef grey fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n    classDef blue fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n    classDef green fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef amber fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n    classDef orange fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n    classDef violet fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n    classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n\nA state diagram takes the same colours through `classDef`; see [state-diagram.md](state-diagram.md).\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Colour through the palette's classes**, not inline styles. Apply one class to several nodes with a comma list: `class B,C,F green`.\n- **One-off node styling**, for a node the palette does not cover: `style C fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:3px`.\n- **Highlight a path**: colour edges by their declaration order (0-indexed): `linkStyle 0,1,4 stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:3px`. `linkStyle default` styles every edge.\n- **Use `subgraph` to group** by service, layer or repository. It is the main way to show architecture boundaries in a flow.\n- **Line breaks** inside labels: `A[\"Line one<br/>Line two\"]`.\n- **Match the direction to the content**: `LR` reads as a pipeline, `TD` as a decision tree. Set it on the whole flowchart, not on a subgraph.\n```\n\n**`superpowers/skills/mermaid/references/state-diagram.md`** (new)\n\n```markdown\n# State Diagram\n\n## When to use\n\nThe lifecycle of an entity: how an order, a payment, a subscription or a job moves between statuses, and which event triggers each move. If you are writing \"when X happens it goes from pending to active\", draw a state diagram.\n\n## Key syntax\n\n- Declare the type: `stateDiagram-v2`.\n- **Start and end**: `[*] --> First` marks the entry; `Last --> [*]` marks the end.\n- **Transitions** carry the triggering event as a label: `Pending --> Active : payment_confirmed`.\n- **Named states with descriptions**: `state \"Waiting for payment\" as Pending`.\n- **Composite states** group substates inside a parent, for a state that has its own internal lifecycle (`state Active { [*] --> Running ... }`).\n- **Choice, fork and join**: `state c <<choice>>` for a branch, `state f <<fork>>` and `state j <<join>>` for parallel paths.\n- **Notes**: `note right of StateName : text`.\n- **Colour**: `classDef` and `class` work as in a flowchart: `classDef red fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626` and `class Cancelled red`.\n\n## Worked example\n\n(an order lifecycle: Draft, Submitted with an AwaitingPayment and Paid composite inside it, Fulfilled in green and Cancelled in red, with a note on Submitted)\n\n## Styling guidance\n\n- **Colour with the palette** in [flowchart.md](flowchart.md). Use the same colours for the same meaning: red for a failed or dropped state, grey for a wait, green for a state where work runs.\n- **Use composite states for substates** when a status has its own internal lifecycle, as `Submitted` holds the payment steps above. The top level then shows only the statuses, and the substates appear inside the composite state.\n- **Label every transition** with the event or condition that causes it. An unlabeled transition leaves the reader guessing what triggers the change.\n- **Use `<<choice>>`** for one decision point with mutually exclusive outgoing transitions, and **`<<fork>>` / `<<join>>`** only when the entity does things in parallel.\n- **A self-transition** (`AwaitingPayment --> AwaitingPayment : retry`) shows a retry that does not leave the state.\n- **Add a `note`** to explain a state or an invariant the diagram cannot express in its structure.\n- **Never put a `;` in a note or a transition label.** It ends the statement and the rest renders as stray states; use a comma.\n```\n\nI've summarized the worked examples in the two references above. The full examples are in the draft files, and both pass the validator and rendered correctly.\n\n**`references/sequence-diagram.md`, `class-diagram.md`, `er-diagram.md`**: ported from Filadd with plain-language edits and no change to what they teach. `sequence-diagram.md` adds a \"Keep messages plain text\" rule pointing at `SKILL.md`. The full text is in `learn/round2/mermaid/references/`.\n\n**`scripts/validate-mermaid.sh`**: moves unchanged from `mermaid-validation/scripts/`. Its cache label, `mermaid-validation-cache`, stays, because renaming it reinstalls nothing. Filadd's `validate-mermaid.mjs` and `notion-shapes.txt` are not ported.\n\n**References to update**, since this replaces a skill:\n- **The old directory:** `superpowers/skills/mermaid-validation/` is removed, and the drafts take its place as `superpowers/skills/mermaid/`.\n- **READMEs and CLAUDE.md:**\n  - `README.md:27`: `mermaid-validation` → `mermaid`.\n  - `superpowers/README.md:23`: becomes \"**mermaid**: Choose, write, style and validate mermaid diagrams, with a chart-selection table, a default shape and colour palette, and a bundled validator\".\n  - `CLAUDE.md:81`: the same new description. `CLAUDE.md:307`, `:370` and `:398`: `mermaid-validation` → `mermaid`.\n- **mahou:**\n  - `mahou/README.md:103`: `superpowers:mermaid`, \"diagram validation\" → \"diagrams\".\n  - `report/SKILL.md:21`, `program-design/SKILL.md:7`, `write-documentation/SKILL.md:35`, `design/SKILL.md:7` and `lookup/SKILL.md:22`: `superpowers:mermaid-validation` → `superpowers:mermaid`, with \"validated\" → \"written and validated\".\n- **Sync skills:**\n  - `.claude/skills/sync-upstream/SKILL.md:73`: `mermaid-validation` → `mermaid`.\n  - `.claude/skills/sync-to-filadd/SKILL.md`, the substitution table: the shared row splits into `superpowers:unslop` → `filadd-docs:unslop`, `filadd-docs:unslop-pass` and `superpowers:mermaid` → `filadd-experimental-dev:mermaid`.\n  - Also in `sync-to-filadd`: a renamed-pairs row `superpowers/skills/mermaid/` ↔ `experimental-dev/skills/mermaid/`, and `superpowers/skills/mermaid` added to step 1's `git log` paths.\n  - A new \"Deliberate divergences\" bullet: \"When syncing `superpowers:mermaid` to `filadd-experimental-dev:mermaid`, keep Filadd's Notion shape whitelist (`scripts/notion-shapes.txt`), its check in `scripts/validate-mermaid.mjs`, the \"Shapes Notion renders\" section of `references/flowchart.md` and the Notion fix in `SKILL.md`. superpowers carries none of them. superpowers' validator reads stdin and Filadd's takes a file path, so the validation steps differ too.\"\n\nThe drafter also installed a headless Chrome into `~/.cache/puppeteer` so it could render the worked examples."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1898,"cache_read_input_tokens":659804,"output_tokens":14792,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":95},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1898,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":14792,"cache_read_input_tokens":659804,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1898,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_token