{"parentUuid":"e8f01904-a4a0-45b5-b18b-21c40d3a109b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"100ff23d-76c2-456a-9c05-6aa0096c8be8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T5nRKhd5ZdHGy4CT8euvpd","type":"tool_result","content":"- **Use `design`'s types for the representations.** While a branch is open, draw its diagrams with tldraw shapes, which are easier to change as answers come in. Once the branch settles, replace each diagram with a `mermaid` shape that holds the source the final document uses. Size the shape to the diagram's own proportions, at one canvas unit per diagram unit. Draw a screen as a mockup copied from its prototype.\n- **Prose is plain paragraphs, all of them the same width**, 1160 units, under the representation they describe.\n- When a branch's sub-branches make it much taller than the others, lay its sub-branch sections side by side under its leaves.\n\nFor a branch whose type has a canvas reference under `references/types/`, read both the type in `design` and the canvas reference: draw the canvas as the canvas reference describes, and write `design.md` and the final documentation in the type's own form. A type with no canvas reference is drawn in its own form. Each canvas reference has the same file name as the type it adapts.\n\n| The branch is about | `design` type | Canvas reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| What a component agrees with the rest of the system | `contract.md` | `references/types/contract.md` |\n| How a value moves between components | `data-flow.md` | `references/types/data-flow.md` |\n\nLay the branches out in tree order, read down each column and then across. Use three columns, split where the tallest column stays as short as possible, and make each branch as wide as its column.\n\nThe opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with no leaves yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles. A branch's opening statement goes on the canvas as its leaves and its prose. When a branch settles, draw its representation and prose in its section and ask the user to approve it there.\n\n## Questions\n mahou/skills/basics/SKILL.md                       |   2 +-\n mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md                       |   7 +-\n mahou/skills/canvas/scripts/canvas.sh              | 158 ++++++++++++++++-----\n mahou/skills/design-tree/SKILL.md                  |   2 +\n mahou/skills/design-with-canvas/SKILL.md           |  11 +-\n .../references/types/component-architecture.md     |   2 +\n mahou/skills/design/references/types/contract.md   |  52 +++----\n mahou/skills/design/references/types/data-flow.md  |   2 +\n mahou/skills/design/references/types/lifecycle.md  |   2 +\n mahou/skills/design/references/types/rules.md      |   2 +\n mahou/skills/program-design/SKILL.md               |   2 +-\n mahou/skills/shape-with-canvas/SKILL.md            |   4 +-\n .../skills/wiki/references/cloudflare/workflows.md |   8 +-\n 13 files changed, 173 insertions(+), 81 deletions(-)\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"766df0c4-58fe-4b4e-bb8b-1294dc3b512d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:26:37.633Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"- **Use `design`'s types for the representations.** While a branch is open, draw its diagrams with tldraw shapes, which are easier to change as answers come in. Once the branch settles, replace each diagram with a `mermaid` shape that holds the source the final document uses. Size the shape to the diagram's own proportions, at one canvas unit per diagram unit. Draw a screen as a mockup copied from its prototype.\n- **Prose is plain paragraphs, all of them the same width**, 1160 units, under the representation they describe.\n- When a branch's sub-branches make it much taller than the others, lay its sub-branch sections side by side under its leaves.\n\nFor a branch whose type has a canvas reference under `references/types/`, read both the type in `design` and