port\")\" == \"$DOC\" ]]; then\n187\t+      echo \"http://localhost:$port\"\n188\t+      return\n189\t+    fi\n190\t+\n191\t+    if port_free \"$port\"; then\n192\t+      echo \"the server did not come up; its log is $LOG\" >&2\n193\t+      exit 1\n194\t+    fi\n195\t   done\n196\t \n197\t-  echo \"the server did not come up; its log is $CACHE/server.log\" >&2\n198\t+  echo \"no free port between $FIRST_PORT and $LAST_PORT\" >&2\n199\t   exit 1\n200\t }\n201\t \n202\t@@ -113,11 +201,11 @@\n203\t     start\n204\t     ;;\n205\t   watch)\n206\t-    start >/dev/null\n207\t+    URL=\"$(start)\"\n208\t     curl -sN \"$URL/api/events\" | sed -u -n 's/^data: //p'\n209\t     ;;\n210\t   export)\n211\t-    start >/dev/null\n212\t+    URL=\"$(start)\"\n213\t     # Its own browser session, so the export leaves Claude's open tab alone\n214\t     agent-browser --session canvas-export open \"$URL?user=export\" >/dev/null\n215\t     agent-browser --session canvas-export wait --fn \"window.editor != null\" >/dev/null\n216\t@@ -128,7 +216,7 @@\n217\t     document_cli texts\n218\t     ;;\n219\t   migrate)\n220\t-    if [[ \"$(serving)\" == \"$DOC\" ]]; then\n221\t+    if find_server; then\n222\t       echo \"stop the canvas before migrating its document\" >&2\n223\t       exit 1\n224\t     fi\n225\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"007bea0b-29a2-4a2a-b0dd-eaf3b761950a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/README.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/README.md","content":"# Cloudflare migration: working folder\n\nWork for the Linear milestone \"Cloudflare migration\" (project nagara). Git ignores this folder; nothing here is committed.\n\nRead `shape.md` first. It holds the settled design tree, and every later step (design, tasks, implementation) builds on it.\n\n## Contents\n\n| Path | What it holds |\n|---|---|\n| `shape.md` | The settled design tree from `mahou:shape`: every decision, grouped by area, plus what is out of scope |\n| `spikes/REPORT.md` | The combined report of the first nine spikes, with answers, evidence, surprises, raised questions, new unknowns and the cleanup list |\n| `spikes/<slug>/FINDINGS.md` | One spike's question, answer, how to reproduce it, measurements and cleanup. The code stays beside it as reference, never as a starting point |\n| `canvas/current-system.tldr.json` | Drawing of today's system (Railway API, Postgres, bucket, Cloudflare pipeline, Modal) |\n| `canvas/cloudflare-options.tldr.json` | Drawings of today's system, options A to D, the step-by-step item Workflow, option C in full, and the chosen design (shapes prefixed `ts-`) |\n| `briefs/` | The exact briefs sent to every scout, researcher and spike |\n\n## Spikes\n\n| Spike | Question | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| `image-processing` | Can SVG and WebP processing run in a Worker? | Works with caveats (~10 MP ceiling, fonts must be bundled). Superseded: rasters go through the Images binding and SVGs stay SVG |\n| `modal-http` | Can a Workflow call Modal over HTTP and get audio into R2? | Works; both a shared key and Modal proxy auth work |\n| `item-workflow` | Does the flattened item Workflow hold up (poll loop, retries, domain lease)? | Works with caveats: a deploy fails every step running at that moment |\n| `cross-script-binding` | Can the API Worker start the pipeline's Workflow across deployables? | Works; the API fails with `workflow.not_found` until the pipeline is deployed |\n| `shared-d1` | Can two Workers share one D1 with conditional claims and hand-written migrations? | Works with caveats: concurrent migration runs race |\n| `r2-presigned` | Can a Worker mint presigned R2 URLs that an audio element plays through a 307? | Works with caveats: an expired URL stalls silently |\n| `firecrawl-gemini` | Do Firecrawl and Gemini (through AI Gateway) work from a Worker, and do the cassettes match? | Works with caveats: the Firecrawl SDK fails in workerd; cassettes need a JSON-aware matcher |\n| `local-dev` | Can both Workers run together locally? | Works in three launch modes, state shared through one `--persist-to` |\n| `workflow-testing` | Introspection, MSW or a code seam for testing the Workflow? | All work; only HTTP interception replays cassettes |\n| `svg-display` | Do SVGs display correctly and safely from presigned R2 URLs? | Display works; scripts run only outside `<img>` |\n| `svg-describe` | How does Gemini describe an SVG: raster, markup as text, or inline SVG? | All return a description; inline SVG described formulas as black |\n| `local-dev-real` | Does the real pipeline run with the API under one `wrangler dev`? | Works with the built config only |\n| `local-dev-b` | Two separate `wrangler dev` processes? | Stopped early: the user chose `vite dev` for the pipeline |\n| `local-audio-upload` | Can Modal upload audio into local R2 through a tunnel? | Works through ngrok with the experimental local S3 endpoint or a local route |\n| `restart-resolve` | When does `restart()` resolve through a cross-script binding? | Once Cloudflare accepts it: 0.4–0.6 s, status `queued`; the restarted step starts 3.1–3.8 s later; `restart()` with no arguments reruns every step |\n| `parallel-steps` | Can one instance run many steps at once, survive failed ones, and nest them? | Yes: 25 steps ran concurrently (outbound fetches capped at 6 per instance), failed steps caught with `Promise.allSettled` leave the instance running, nested steps are recorded as top-level steps, and a parent retry reruns every child |\n| `local-audio-tunnel` | Can Cloudflare Tunnel replace ngrok? | Works with a local Node proxy that rewrites the path, because Cloudflare's edge blocks `/cdn-cgi/*` on quick tunnels |\n\n## Still to delete by hand\n\nThese credentials were created in dashboards for the spikes and can only be removed there:\n\n- The Modal proxy auth token (Modal, Settings, Proxy Auth Tokens), if not deleted yet.\n- The R2 API tokens created for `r2-presigned` and `svg-display` (Cloudflare, R2, Manage API tokens), if not revoked yet. `svg-display` printed one token's access key ID inside a URL; revoking the token covers it.\n\nThe stopped Modal apps `nagara-tts-spike` and `nagara-spike-upload` still appear in `modal app list`; the CLI cannot remove a stopped app.\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}},"displayPath":"README.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f68794ef-d1c2-4871-9d7f-ab8e30aa2cf0","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:42:16.122Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/README.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Cloudflare migration: working folder\n2\t\n3\tWork for the Linear milestone \"Cloudflare migration\" (project nagara). Git ignores this folder; nothing here is committed.\n4\t\n5\tRead `shape.md` first. It holds the settled design tree, and every later step (design, tasks, implementation) builds on it.\n6\t\n7\t## Contents\n8\t\n9\t| Path | What it holds |\n10\t|---|---|\n11\t| `shape.md` | The settled design tree from `mahou:shape`: every decision, grouped by area, plus what is out of scope |\n12\t| `spikes/REPORT.md` | The combined report of the first nine spikes, with answers, evidence, surprises, raised questions, new unknowns and the cleanup list |\n13\t| `spikes/<slug>/FINDINGS.md` | One spike's question, answer, how to reproduce it, measurements and cleanup. The code stays beside it as reference, never as a starting point |\n14\t| `canvas/current-system.tldr.json` | Drawing of today's system (Railway API, Postgres, bucket, Cloudflare pipeline, Modal) |\n15\t| `canvas/cloudflare-options.tldr.json` | Drawings of today's system, options A to D, the step-by-step item Workflow, option C in full, and the chosen design (shapes prefixed `ts-`) |\n16\t| `briefs/` | The exact briefs sent to every scout, researcher and spike |\n17\t\n18\t## Spikes\n19\t\n20\t| Spike | Question | Answer |\n21\t|---|---|---|\n22\t| `image-processing` | Can SVG and WebP processing run in a Worker? | Works with caveats (~10 MP ceiling, fonts must be bundled). Superseded: rasters go through the Images binding and SVGs stay SVG |\n23\t| `modal-http` | Can a Workflow call Modal over HTTP and get audio into R2? | Works; both a shared key and Modal proxy auth work |\n24\t| `item-workflow` | Does the flattened item Workflow hold up (poll loop, retries, domain lease)? | Works with caveats: a deploy fails every step running at that moment |\n25\t| `cross-script-binding` | Can the API Worker start the pipeline's Workflow across deployables? | Works; the API fails with `workflow.not_found` until the pipeline is deployed |\n26\t| `shared-d1` | Can two Workers share one D1 with conditional claims and hand-written migrations? | Works with caveats: concurrent migration runs race |\n27\t| `r2-presigned` | Can a Worker mint presigned R2 URLs that an audio element plays through a 307? | Works with caveats: an expired URL stalls silently |\n28\t| `firecrawl-gemini` | Do Firecrawl and Gemini (through AI Gateway) work from a Worker, and do the cassettes match? | Works with caveats: the Firecrawl SDK fails in workerd; cassettes need a JSON-aware matcher |\n29\t| `local-dev` | Can both Workers run together locally? | Works in three launch modes, state shared through one `--persist-to` |\n30\t| `workflow-testing` | Introspection, MSW or a code seam for testing the Workflow? | All work; only HTTP interception replays cassettes |\n31\t| `svg-display` | Do SVGs display correctly and safely from presigned R2 URLs? | Display works; scripts run only outside `<img>` |\n32\t| `svg-describe` | How does Gemini describe an SVG: raster, markup as text, or inline SVG? | All return a description; inline SVG described formulas as black |\n33\t| `local-dev-real` | Does the real pipeline run with the API under one `wrangler dev`? | Works with the built config only |\n34\t| `local-dev-b` | Two separate `wrangler dev` processes? | Stopped early: the user chose `vite dev` for the pipeline |\n35\t| `local-audio-upload` | Can Modal upload audio into local R2 through a tunnel? | Works through ngrok with the experimental local S3 endpoint or a local route |\n36\t| `restart-resolve` | When does `restart()` resolve through a cross-script binding? | Once Cloudflare accepts it: 0.4–0.6 s, status `queued`; the restarted step starts 3.1–3.8 s later; `restart()` with no arguments reruns every step |\n37\t| `parallel-steps` | Can one instance run many steps at once, survive failed ones, and nest them? | Yes: 25 steps ran concurrently (outbound fetches capped at 6 per instance), failed steps caught with `Promise.allSettled` leave the instance running, nested steps are recorded as top-level steps, and a parent retry reruns every child |\n38\t| `local-audio-tunnel` | Can Cloudflare Tunnel replace ngrok? | Works with a local Node proxy that rewrites the path, because Cloudflare's edge blocks `/cdn-cgi/*` on quick tunnels |\n39\t\n40\t## Still to delete by hand\n41\t\n42\tThese credentials were created in dashboards for the spikes and can only be removed there:\n43\t\n44\t- The Modal proxy auth token (Modal, Settings, Proxy Auth Tokens), if not deleted yet.\n45\t- The R2 API tokens created for `r2-presigned` and `svg-display` (Cloudflare, R2, Manage API tokens), if not revoked yet. `svg-display` printed one token's access key ID inside a URL; revoking the token covers it.\n46\t\n47\tThe stopped Modal apps `nagara-tts-spike` and `nagara-spike-upload` still appear in `modal app list`; the CLI cannot remove a stopped app.\n48\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"f68794ef-d1c2-4871-9d7f-ab8e30aa2cf0","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/shape.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/shape.md","content":"# Shape: Cloudflare migration\n\nThe settled design tree from `mahou:shape`, built on the decisions of the exploration (`mahou:explore`) and the spikes under `spikes/`. Every leaf is a decision the user made.\n\n```\nProblem: Move nagara's API, database and file storage from Railway to Cloudflare, stop using Railway, keep TTS on Modal\n├─ Platform and deployables                                    settled\n│  ✓ api/, the database and the file bucket move to Cloudflare; Railway stops being used\n│  ✓ tts/ stays in Python on Modal\n│  ✓ Everything nagara runs on Cloudflare is TypeScript: the API, the item Workflow, the pipeline\n│  ✓ The design is the \"Chosen: TypeScript API + item Workflow\" drawing (canvas/cloudflare-options.tldr.json, shapes prefixed ts-)\n│  ✓ The API is a TypeScript Worker and a deployable of its own, in api/\n│  ✓ Deploys use wrangler, not the cf CLI\n│  ✓ Vite stays as the pipeline's build tool, because Flue requires it\n│  ✓ CI deploys common/ migrations, then the pipeline, then the API; deploy conflicts are not handled for now\n├─ Data                                                        settled\n│  ✓ The database is D1 (SQLite) instead of Postgres\n│  ✓ The move starts empty: no rows or files are copied from Railway\n│  ✓ Migrations are hand-written SQL in common/, applied with wrangler by common/'s own CI job\n│  ✓ Two migration runs never overlap, because deploys aren't made twice in quick succession\n│  ✓ common/ holds the types shared by api/ and pipeline/: the Item row, the unit union, the Workflow's params and output\n│  ✓ Both Workers' type checks run whenever common/ changes\n│  ✓ The cost ledger keeps today's rules: Firecrawl credits from the response (creditsUsed), a flat price per successful describe, TTS by audio length; the Flue agents aren't recorded\n│  ✓ A describe answered from the gateway's cache writes a ledger row at the same flat price\n│  ✓ An item's units stay well under D1's 10 MB cell limit: a 70-minute article comes to about 150–250 KB (estimated)\n├─ Item Workflow                                               settled\n│  ✓ One flattened item Workflow in pipeline/ does all per-item work; the extraction steps become its steps\n│  ✓ Each module inside pipeline/ is as independent of the others as it can be\n│  ✓ DomainQueue serves the item Workflow; an item waits for its domain's lease through waitForEvent\n│  ✓ The lease is released right after the recipe step, so it blocks only extraction\n│  ✓ Firecrawl is called through our own fetch-based client, sending the same fields as today's Python SDK\n│  ✓ tts/ gains two web endpoints (start synthesis, fetch the result), protected with Modal's proxy auth\n│  ✓ Each synthesis request carries a destination URL, and Modal uploads the finished audio there (a presigned R2 PUT URL in production)\n│  ✓ The same Modal app serves production and local development\n│  ✓ A duplicate synthesis caused by a deploy re-running the start step is accepted\n│  ✓ A retry restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step that failed; the API caps retries at 3\n│  ✓ The poll route exposes today's statuses: queued, generating, ready, failed\n│  ✓ No time limit on a whole item; queued_at and its 300 s ceiling go\n│  ✓ Every step is retried 3 times and has a timeout per attempt: fetch 2 min, lease 1 h, recipe 15 min, release 1 min, images 5 min, describe 5 min, start synthesis 2 min, each poll 1 min, store 1 min\n│  ✓ Synthesis that fails every time, Modal results expiring after 7 days, the 1 MiB HTML cap and not-an-article pages keep today's handling\n├─ Images                                                      settled\n│  ✓ SVG images keep working\n│  ✓ Every raster goes through the Images binding; the in-Worker WebAssembly libraries are dropped\n│  ✓ SVGs stay SVG, with scripts stripped before they're stored\n│  ✓ Gemini, reached through AI Gateway, describes an SVG from its markup sent as text/plain, with no size cap\n│  ✓ Describe requests use AI Gateway's response cache\n│  ✓ The 200 px minimum for rasters and dropping a failed image as a degradation stay as today\n├─ Delivery to clients                                         settled\n│  ✓ Audio and images are served as a 307 to a presigned R2 URL with a 1 h lifetime\n│  ✓ SVGs are served like any other image and shown only in <img>\n│  ✓ Only a backend holds the API key; a web app's backend hands the browser the presigned URL; no ?key= on the audio route\n│  ✓ Clients build an image's URL from the item id and its hash, as today\n├─ API behaviour                                               settled\n│  ✓ Clients see no change: the same routes, the same item JSON, the same X-API-Key authentication and /health\n├─ Testing                                                     settled\n│  ✓ Each deployable runs integration tests on its own; the API's tests use a stand-in for the Workflow binding\n│  ✓ Tests intercept HTTP with MSW and run the real step code, with introspection only to skip sleeps\n│  ✓ A request that matches no recorded response fails the test\n│  ✓ Our own TypeScript copy of vcrpy, in common/, replays the existing cassettes with today's matching rules and filters\n│  ✓ The cassette library only replays for now\n├─ Local development                                           settled\n│  ✓ The pipeline runs under vite dev and the API under its own wrangler dev, sharing one state folder\n│  ✓ An item stuck because the pipeline wasn't running is restarted by hand through the retry route\n│  ✓ Local development calls the real Firecrawl, AI Gateway (Gemini, agents) and Modal\n│  ✓ Everything stays local: audio and images are stored in the local R2 on disk\n│  ✓ Modal uploads local audio through the experimental local S3 endpoint (local_dev.experimental_s3_credentials), with a presigned URL, as in production\n│  ✓ A Cloudflare quick tunnel exposes the local dev server to Modal, instead of ngrok\n│  ✓ A small local Node proxy maps the tunnel's /s3/<bucket>/<key> path to /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/..., because Cloudflare's edge answers /cdn-cgi/* on trycloudflare.com itself; URLs are signed for localhost and handed out with the tunnel's hostname (spikes/local-audio-tunnel/)\n│  ✓ The tunnel's hostname may change on every start\n├─ Switching over from Railway                                 settled\n│  ✓ nagara.asermax.com is pointed at the new API Worker; clients keep the same address\n│  ✓ Nothing from Railway's database or bucket is kept or migrated\n│  ✓ Every Railway resource (API service, Postgres, bucket) is deleted once everything works on Cloudflare\n│  ✓ The pipeline has no public HTTP routes in production; the Cloudflare Access application and its service token are removed\n└─ Out of scope                                                settled\n   ✓ Other runtimes for the API: Python Workers, Containers\n   ✓ Queues for deferred work\n   ✓ The cf CLI\n   ✓ Alembic and the Python migration tooling\n   ✓ Copying today's rows and files from Railway\n   ✓ A test that crosses from the API into the pipeline\n   ✓ Recording new cassettes\n   ✓ Deduplicating synthesis on the Modal side\n   ✓ Handling deploy conflicts and step edits while instances run\n   ✓ Removing Vite\n   ✓ What the item keeps of the source article: not part of this migration\n   ✓ D1's 10 GB database cap\n   ✓ D1 read latency when polling an item\n   ✓ The switchover itself: items in flight on Railway, going back to Railway, the old Railway address (no real users yet)\n```\n","numLines":91,"startLine":1,"totalLines":91}},"displayPath":"shape.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8807d4ad-bf20-43b3-8aa4-c8f4c3d65c2d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:42:16.122Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/shape.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Shape: Cloudflare migration\n2\t\n3\tThe settled design tree from `mahou:shape`, built on the decisions of the exploration (`mahou:explore`) and the spikes under `spikes/`. Every leaf is a decision the user made.\n4\t\n5\t```\n6\tProblem: Move nagara's API, database and file storage from Railway to Cloudflare, stop using Railway, keep TTS on Modal\n7\t├─ Platform and deployables                                    settled\n8\t│  ✓ api/, the database and the file bucket move to Cloudflare; Railway stops being used\n9\t│  ✓ tts/ stays in Python on Modal\n10\t│  ✓ Everything nagara runs on Cloudflare is TypeScript: the API, the item Workflow, the pipeline\n11\t│  ✓ The design is the \"Chosen: TypeScript API + item Workflow\" drawing (canvas/cloudflare-options.tldr.json, shapes prefixed ts-)\n12\t│  ✓ The API is a TypeScript Worker and a deployable of its own, in api/\n13\t│  ✓ Deploys use wrangler, not the cf CLI\n14\t│  ✓ Vite stays as the pipeline's build tool, because Flue requires it\n15\t│  ✓ CI deploys common/ migrations, then the pipeline, then the API; deploy conflicts are not handled for now\n16\t├─ Data                                                        settled\n17\t│  ✓ The database is D1 (SQLite) instead of Postgres\n18\t│  ✓ The move starts empty: no rows or files are copied from Railway\n19\t│  ✓ Migrations are hand-written SQL in common/, applied with wrangler by common/'s own CI job\n20\t│  ✓ Two migration runs never overlap, because deploys aren't made twice in quick succession\n21\t│  ✓ common/ holds the types shared by api/ and pipeline/: the Item row, the unit union, the Workflow's params and output\n22\t│  ✓ Both Workers' type checks run whenever common/ changes\n23\t│  ✓ The cost ledger keeps today's rules: Firecrawl credits from the response (creditsUsed), a flat price per successful describe, TTS by audio length; the Flue agents aren't recorded\n24\t│  ✓ A describe answered from the gateway's cache writes a ledger row at the same flat price\n25\t│  ✓ An item's units stay well under D1's 10 MB cell limit: a 70-minute article comes to about 150–250 KB (estimated)\n26\t├─ Item Workflow                                               settled\n27\t│  ✓ One flattened item Workflow in pipeline/ does all per-item work; the extraction steps become its steps\n28\t│  ✓ Each module inside pipeline/ is as independent of the others as it can be\n29\t│  ✓ DomainQueue serves the item Workflow; an item waits for its domain's lease through waitForEvent\n30\t│  ✓ The lease is released right after the recipe step, so it blocks only extraction\n31\t│  ✓ Firecrawl is called through our own fetch-based client, sending the same fields as today's Python SDK\n32\t│  ✓ tts/ gains two web endpoints (start synthesis, fetch the result), protected with Modal's proxy auth\n33\t│  ✓ Each synthesis request carries a destination URL, and Modal uploads the finished audio there (a presigned R2 PUT URL in production)\n34\t│  ✓ The same Modal app serves production and local development\n35\t│  ✓ A duplicate synthesis caused by a deploy re-running the start step is accepted\n36\t│  ✓ A retry restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step that failed; the API caps retries at 3\n37\t│  ✓ The poll route exposes today's statuses: queued, generating, ready, failed\n38\t│  ✓ No time limit on a whole item; queued_at and its 300 s ceiling go\n39\t│  ✓ Every step is retried 3 times and has a timeout per attempt: fetch 2 min, lease 1 h, recipe 15 min, release 1 min, images 5 min, describe 5 min, start synthesis 2 min, each poll 1 min, store 1 min\n40\t│  ✓ Synthesis that fails every time, Modal results expiring after 7 days, the 1 MiB HTML cap and not-an-article pages keep today's handling\n41\t├─ Images                                                      settled\n42\t│  ✓ SVG images keep working\n43\t│  ✓ Every raster goes through the Images binding; the in-Worker WebAssembly libraries are dropped\n44\t│  ✓ SVGs stay SVG, with scripts stripped before they're stored\n45\t│  ✓ Gemini, reached through AI Gateway, describes an SVG from its markup sent as text/plain, with no size cap\n46\t│  ✓ Describe requests use AI Gateway's response cache\n47\t│  ✓ The 200 px minimum for rasters and dropping a failed image as a degradation stay as today\n48\t├─ Delivery to clients                                         settled\n49\t│  ✓ Audio and images are served as a 307 to a presigned R2 URL with a 1 h lifetime\n50\t│  ✓ SVGs are served like any other image and shown only in <img>\n51\t│  ✓ Only a backend holds the API key; a web app's backend hands the browser the presigned URL; no ?key= on the audio route\n52\t│  ✓ Clients build an image's URL from the item id and its hash, as today\n53\t├─ API behaviour                                               settled\n54\t│  ✓ Clients see no change: the same routes, the same item JSON, the same X-API-Key authentication and /health\n55\t├─ Testing                                                     settled\n56\t│  ✓ Each deployable runs integration tests on its own; the API's tests use a stand-in for the Workflow binding\n57\t│  ✓ Tests intercept HTTP with MSW and run the real step code, with introspection only to skip sleeps\n58\t│  ✓ A request that matches no recorded response fails the test\n59\t│  ✓ Our own TypeScript copy of vcrpy, in common/, replays the existing cassettes with today's matching rules and filters\n60\t│  ✓ The cassette library only replays for now\n61\t├─ Local development                                           settled\n62\t│  ✓ The pipeline runs under vite dev and the API under its own wrangler dev, sharing one state folder\n63\t│  ✓ An item stuck because the pipeline wasn't running is restarted by hand through the retry route\n64\t│  ✓ Local development calls the real Firecrawl, AI Gateway (Gemini, agents) and Modal\n65\t│  ✓ Everything stays local: audio and images are stored in the local R2 on disk\n66\t│  ✓ Modal uploads local audio through the experimental local S3 endpoint (local_dev.experimental_s3_credentials), with a presigned URL, as in production\n67\t│  ✓ A Cloudflare quick tunnel exposes the local dev server to Modal, instead of ngrok\n68\t│  ✓ A small local Node proxy maps the tunnel's /s3/<bucket>/<key> path to /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/..., because Cloudflare's edge answers /cdn-cgi/* on trycloudflare.com itself; URLs are signed for localhost and handed out with the tunnel's hostname (spikes/local-audio-tunnel/)\n69\t│  ✓ The tunnel's hostname may change on every start\n70\t├─ Switching over from Railway                                 settled\n71\t│  ✓ nagara.asermax.com is pointed at the new API Worker; clients keep the same address\n72\t│  ✓ Nothing from Railway's database or bucket is kept or migrated\n73\t│  ✓ Every Railway resource (API service, Postgres, bucket) is deleted once everything works on Cloudflare\n74\t│  ✓ The pipeline has no public HTTP routes in production; the Cloudflare Access application and its service token are removed\n75\t└─ Out of scope                                                settled\n76\t   ✓ Other runtimes for the API: Python Workers, Containers\n77\t   ✓ Queues for deferred work\n78\t   ✓ The cf CLI\n79\t   ✓ Alembic and the Python migration tooling\n80\t   ✓ Copying today's rows and files from Railway\n81\t   ✓ A test that crosses from the API into the pipeline\n82\t   ✓ Recording new cassettes\n83\t   ✓ Deduplicating synthesis on the Modal side\n84\t   ✓ Handling deploy conflicts and step edits while instances run\n85\t   ✓ Removing Vite\n86\t   ✓ What the item keeps of the source article: not part of this migration\n87\t   ✓ D1's 10 GB database cap\n88\t   ✓ D1 read latency when polling an item\n89\t   ✓ The switchover itself: items in flight on Railway, going back to Railway, the old Railway address (no real users yet)\n90\t```\n91\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"8807d4ad-bf20-43b3-8aa4-c8f4c3d65c2d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes/svg-describe/FINDINGS.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes/svg-describe/FINDINGS.md","content":"# svg-describe\n\n## Question\n\nHow should an SVG be described by Gemini if nagara keeps it as SVG: as a raster (today), as the markup in a `text/plain` part, as markup pasted into the prompt, or as an `image/svg+xml` part?\n\n## Answer\n\nWorks with caveats. All four routes return 200 and a plausible one-sentence description for 8 real SVGs. Quality is not ranked here; the tables below are for the user to judge. Facts that bear on the choice:\n\n- (d) `image/svg+xml` is **not rejected**. The API returns 200 and treats the file as an image. It renders something, but on both formulas it saw an all-black image (\"Solid black rectangle\", \"Black pixels fill the entire frame\"), a wrong description with no error. A third probe with a plain prompt on `formula1.svg` returned \"This image is entirely black.\" The two formulas have black glyphs and no background, so the likely cause is transparency rendered as black. That cause was not tested.\n- (b) and (c) are the same in practice: same token counts (within 5 tokens), same latency, similar descriptions. Both cost 765 to 27,808 prompt tokens depending on markup size (the 63 KB chart cost 27.8k vs 1.3k for the PNG).\n- (a) costs a constant ~1,300 prompt tokens whatever the SVG size, and is the slowest on average, but needs the rasterizer.\n\n## What was run\n\n`src/run.mjs`, plain Node `fetch` against `https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account>/nagara/google-ai-studio/v1beta/models/gemini-3.5-flash-lite:generateContent` with `x-goog-api-key` (`NAGARA_GEMINI_API_KEY` from `api/.env`) and `cf-aig-authorization: Bearer` (`CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY` from `pipeline/.dev.vars`). Nothing secret is printed or stored.\n\n- Prompt: `_IMAGE_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` from `api/app/service/describe.py`, read from the file at run time, with title `(untitled)` and alt `(none)`. Same structured output config (`{\"spoken\": string}`). The prompt text says \"image\" in all four variants, unchanged for (b) and (c).\n- SVGs (8): badge, github-badge, codecov (badges), logo-python (logo), formula1, formula3 (formulas), osi (a diagram with text, from the Wikipedia OSI model article), normal (a chart, 63 KB).\n- (a) rendered with `@resvg/resvg-js` 2.6.2, width 768, system fonts (Liberation Sans). RGBA PNG, not WebP and not flattened onto white; the production describer receives WebP converted from a cairosvg RGBA PNG. The PNGs are in `results/<name>.png`.\n- (b) markup as base64 `inlineData` with mime `text/plain`; (c) `\"{prompt}\\n\\nSVG markup:\\n{svg}\"` in one text part; (d) base64 `inlineData` with `image/svg+xml`.\n- Reproduce: `pnpm install && node src/run.mjs` (env `FILES`, `VARIANTS`, `MODEL`, `OUT` override defaults), then `node src/report.mjs` for the tables. Raw output: `results/run1.json`, `results/run1.log`.\n- Calls: 33 (32 + 1 probe for (d) with a plain prompt, `src/probe.mjs`). All were gateway cache MISS. The fallback model `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` was not run.\n\n## Measurements\n\nSingle run per cell (one sample each), so latency differences of a few hundred ms are within noise. The first call (badge, (a)) took 4.2 s and includes connection setup. For (d) the response has no `promptTokenCount` in `usageMetadata`, only `totalTokenCount`; the prompt tokens in the tables are `total - out` (derived). No thinking tokens were reported on any call.\n\n## Descriptions side by side\n\n### badge.svg (1270 B markup, 51450 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | The badge displays the text npm on a dark gray background on the left and v1.2.0 on a blue background on the right. | 1305 | 38 | 4227 |\n| (b) text/plain part | An informational badge features the text npm on the left and the text v1.2.0 on the right. | 765 | 32 | 1181 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | A badge containing the text npm on a dark gray background alongside the text v1.2.0 on a blue background. | 770 | 30 | 1344 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | Npm version 1.2.0 badge displaying gray and blue segments with white text. | ~1349 (derived) | 27 | 1692 |\n\n### github-badge.svg (2263 B markup, 22159 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | CI passing badge displays a dark gray side with a cat silhouette and the letters CI, joined to a green side with the text passing. | 1299 | 36 | 2273 |\n| (b) text/plain part | A badge containing the text CI passing displays a gray section and a green section with a small icon. | 1631 | 29 | 1296 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | CI passing badge displaying white text on a dark gray and green background. | 1636 | 23 | 1209 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | CI passing badge displaying the text CI and passing alongside an animal icon. | ~1343 (derived) | 23 | 1496 |\n\n### codecov.svg (2274 B markup, 21485 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | Codecov badge displaying eighty-six percent in white text on a dark gray and olive green background. | 1355 | 29 | 2011 |\n| (b) text/plain part | A codecov badge displays the text codecov on a dark grey background alongside the percentage 86% on a gold background. | 1934 | 35 | 1337 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | A horizontal badge containing the text codecov on the left and the percentage 86% on the right. | 1939 | 27 | 1179 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | A badge displays the text codecov alongside its logo and the percentage 86%. | ~1399 (derived) | 27 | 1752 |\n\n### logo-python.svg (2247 B markup, 84477 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | Two interlocking snakes in blue and yellow form a stylized symbol with white circular eyes. | 1338 | 25 | 2030 |\n| (b) text/plain part | Two interlocking snakes in shades of blue and yellow form a continuous looped shape. | 1859 | 24 | 1157 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | Two intertwined curved shapes in blue and yellow form a complete square with circular negative space cutouts. | 1864 | 24 | 1174 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | Two interlocking snakes in blue and yellow form a stylized logo. | ~1382 (derived) | 21 | 1497 |\n\n### formula1.svg (3752 B markup, 24924 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | The mathematical expression S of R to the power of n is displayed in black serif font. | 1327 | 27 | 1927 |\n| (b) text/plain part | The mathematical expression S of R to the power of n is displayed. | 3368 | 23 | 1184 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | The curly letter S followed by parentheses containing the blackboard bold letter R with a superscript n. | 3373 | 23 | 1093 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | Solid black rectangle. | ~1371 (derived) | 13 | 1715 |\n\n### formula3.svg (10297 B markup, 15940 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | The equation minus partial squared over partial x squared times psi of x and t plus V of x times psi of x and t equals i times h over 2 pi times partial over partial t times psi of x and t is displayed. | 1329 | 56 | 2088 |\n| (b) text/plain part | The time-dependent Schrödinger equation is displayed as a mathematical formula. | 8308 | 22 | 1427 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | The equation for the time-dependent Schrodinger equation is displayed. | 8313 | 22 | 1236 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | Black pixels fill the entire frame. | ~1385 (derived) | 16 | 1780 |\n\n### osi.svg (22685 B markup, 103956 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | Seven network layers are grouped into Host Layers and Media Layers, with corresponding data units shown alongside each layer. | 1329 | 30 | 2189 |\n| (b) text/plain part | A seven-layer networking diagram displays data units alongside their corresponding application, presentation, session, transport, network, data link, and physical layers, divided into host and media categories. | 11841 | 41 | 1587 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | A seven-tiered diagram outlines network communication layers, divided into host layers and media layers with corresponding data units. | 11846 | 31 | 1397 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | Seven stacked network layers labeled Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, and Physical are grouped into Host Layers and Media Layers alongside corresponding data units. | ~1373 (derived) | 42 | 1958 |\n\n### normal.svg (62893 B markup, 51774 B PNG)\n\n| Variant | Description | Prompt tok | Out tok | ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | Four normal distribution curves with varying means and variances plotted on a grid ranging from negative five to five on the x-axis and zero to one on the y-axis. | 1315 | 43 | 2022 |\n| (b) text/plain part | Four bell-shaped probability density curves with different means and standard deviations plotted over an x-axis ranging from minus five to five. | 27803 | 35 | 1844 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | Line graph showing normal distributions with varying mean and standard deviation values plotted across an x-axis ranging from -5 to 5 and a y-axis from 0.0 to 1.0. | 27808 | 46 | 1848 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | Four bell-shaped probability curves of varying heights and widths plotted on a dark grid. | ~1359 (derived) | 26 | 2055 |\n\n### Averages over the 8 SVGs\n\n| Variant | ms | out tok |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) PNG 768 px | 2346 | 36 |\n| (b) text/plain part | 1377 | 30 |\n| (c) markup in prompt | 1310 | 28 |\n| (d) image/svg+xml part | 1743 | 24 |\n\n## Surprises\n\n- (d) is accepted although Google's docs do not list `image/svg+xml` as a supported image type, and the response omits `promptTokenCount`.\n- The (d) description for the badges, logo, OSI diagram and chart was good, and the OSI one named all seven layers. Only the two formulas failed (black image).\n- formula3 was read out symbol by symbol (\"minus partial squared over partial x squared ...\") while (b) and (c) named it as the Schrödinger equation, and the markup carries a `<title>` with the LaTeX source of the formula, which (b) and (c) could read and (a) and (d) could not. formula1 was described correctly by (a), (b), (c).\n- (a) and (d) saw the github-badge's cat icon (\"cat silhouette\", \"animal icon\"); (b) and (c) saw only \"a small icon\" or nothing about it.\n\n## Design questions raised\n\n- Is a wrong-but-successful description (the black formulas in (d)) acceptable, given that a green call is not evidence of a correct description? (b), (c) and (a) did not show it on this corpus.\n- If markup is sent as text, is a size cap needed? Prompt tokens scale with markup bytes (63 KB chart ≈ 27.8k tokens, ~21x the raster), the OSI diagram ≈ 11.8k.\n- Should the prompt wording change for markup input (it says \"image\" and \"what the image SHOWS\")? Not tested; the descriptions complied anyway.\n\n## New unknowns\n\n- Is the (d) black result caused by transparent backgrounds? Test: same formulas with a white `<rect>` added, or (a) with a transparent PNG vs flattened, was not run.\n- Does `image/svg+xml` acceptance hold across models (`gemini-3.1-flash-lite` fallback) and over time, given that it is undocumented?\n- Variance: one sample per cell; repeat runs could change wording and the formula result.\n\n## Cleanup\n\nNo Cloudflare resources created (no Worker, bucket, gateway or secret). No background processes started. Local only: this folder (`node_modules`, `results/`, `src/`), git-ignored. The calls appear in the `nagara` AI Gateway logs; no gateway setting was changed.\n","numLines":135,"startLine":1,"totalLines":135}},"displayPath":"spikes/svg-describe/FINDINGS.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"5cc8989b-0fc8-4ff2-a712-32d5d09f7caf","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:42:16.122Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes/svg-describe/FINDINGS.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# svg-describe\n2\t\n3\t## Question\n4\t\n5\tHow should an SVG be described by Gemini if nagara keeps it as SVG: as a raster (today), as the markup in a `text/plain` part, as markup pasted into the prompt, or as an `image/svg+xml` part?\n6\t\n7\t## Answer\n8\t\n9\tWorks with caveats. All four routes return 200 and a plausible one-sentence description for 8 real SVGs. Quality is not ranked here; the tables below are for the user to judge. Facts that bear on the choice:\n10\t\n11\t- (d) `image/svg+xml` is **not rejected**. The API returns 200 and treats the file as an image. It renders something, but on both formulas it saw an all-black image (\"Solid black rectangle\", \"Black pixels fill the entire frame\"), a wrong description with no error. A third probe with a plain prompt on `formula1.svg` returned \"This image is entirely black.\" The two formulas have black glyphs and no background, so the likely cause is transparency rendered as black. That cause was not tested.\n12\t- (b) and (c) are the same in practice: same token counts (within 5 tokens), same latency, similar descriptions. Both cost 765 to 27,808 prompt tokens depending on markup size (the 63 KB chart cost 27.8k vs 1.3k for the PNG).\n13\t- (a) costs a constant ~1,300 prompt tokens whatever the SVG size, and is the slowest on average, but needs the rasterizer.\n14\t\n15\t## What was run\n16\t\n17\t`src/run.mjs`, plain Node `fetch` against `https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account>/nagara/google-ai-studio/v1beta