max/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"4b676df0-e2d7-4ee4-856e-ff8b77b01eb4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bfa32ed9-babd-4bb0-92a6-0867b6b276d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BMA81pEkWvRjhWKtvd7ahW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttitle: \"Item lifecycle\"\n3\tsummary: \"The item's four-state machine: the API Worker writes a queued row and creates the item Workflow, nine steps inside one step wrapper carry it to ready or failed, DomainQueue serializes extraction per domain, and retry restarts the Workflow from the step that failed.\"\n4\tcreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t# Item lifecycle\n8\t\n9\t\n10\t## 🔭 Overview\n11\t\n12\tAn item is the record of one `enqueue(url, voice?)` call, from creation through to playable audio or a clear failure. The API Worker writes the item row as `queued` and creates the item's Workflow instance in the pipeline Worker; the item Workflow then does all the work for that item in nine steps and writes every later status into the row. The poll route reads only the row, never the Workflow instance. A retry restarts the same instance from the step that failed. [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) covers how the extracted markdown units become the item's units, [recipes](recipes.md) covers the scripts that extract them, and [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) covers what the TTS service hands back.\n13\t\n14\t## 🗺️ How an item moves through the system\n15\t\n16\t```mermaid\n17\tflowchart TB\n18\t  client@{ shape: stadium, label: \"Client\" }\n19\t  subgraph api [\"API Worker\"]\n20\t    direction TB\n21\t    enq@{ shape: rect, label: \"POST /items:<br/>row as queued,<br/>create instance\" }\n22\t    retry@{ shape: rect, label: \"POST retry:<br/>row as queued, restart<br/>from current_step\" }\n23\t    read@{ shape: rect, label: \"GET item, audio:<br/>read row, 307 to<br/>presigned URL\" }\n24\t  end\n25\t  subgraph wf [\"item Workflow, steps inside the wrapper\"]\n26\t    direction TB\n27\t    subgraph q [\"status queued\"]\n28\t      direction TB\n29\t      s1@{ shape: rect, label: \"1 fetch\" }\n30\t      s2@{ shape: rect, label: \"2 lease\" }\n31\t    end\n32\t    subgraph g [\"status generating\"]\n33\t      direction TB\n34\t      s3@{ shape: rect, label: \"3 recipe, then<br/>spoken form and drop\" }\n35\t      s4@{ shape: rect, label: \"4 release\" }\n36\t      s5@{ shape: rect, label: \"5 images\" }\n37\t      s6@{ shape: rect, label: \"6 describe<br/>(parent step)\" }\n38\t      c6@{ shape: procs, label: \"child describe steps,<br/>Promise.allSettled\" }\n39\t      s7@{ shape: rect, label: \"7 start synthesis\" }\n40\t      s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 poll\" }\n41\t      s9@{ shape: rect, label: \"9 store\" }\n42\t    end\n43\t  end\n44\t  ready@{ shape: stadium, label: \"ready\" }\n45\t  failed@{ shape: stadium, label: \"failed: error,<br/>current_step\" }\n46\t  fc@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Firecrawl\" }\n47\t  dq@{ shape: rect, label: \"DomainQueue\" }\n48\t  rt@{ shape: rect, label: \"Recipe runtime,<br/>Flue agents\" }\n49\t  img@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Images binding\" }\n50\t  aig@{ shape: cloud, label: \"AI Gateway\" }\n51\t  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n52\t  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n53\t  r2@{ shape: cyl, label: \"R2 bucket\" }\n54\t  client -->|\"enqueue\"| enq\n55\t  enq -->|\"queued row\"| d1\n56\t  enq -->|\"create\"| s1\n57\t  s1 --> s2 --> s3 --> s4 --> s5 --> s6 --> s7 --> s8 --> s9\n58\t  s6 -->|\"fan out\"| c6\n59\t  s1 -->|\"scrape\"| fc\n60\t  s2 -->|\"wait for lease\"| dq\n61\t  s4 -->|\"give lease back\"| dq\n62\t  s3 -->|\"run or author\"| rt\n63\t  rt -->|\"model calls\"| aig\n64\t  s5 -->|\"rasters\"| img\n65\t  s5 -->|\"images/\"| r2\n66\t  c6 -->|\"Gemini\"| aig\n67\t  s7 -->|\"spoken forms,<br/>PUT URL\"| modal\n68\t  s8 -->|\"result\"| modal\n69\t  modal -->|\"audio/\"| r2\n70\t  s9 --> ready\n71\t  wf -.->|\"last attempt fails\"| failed\n72\t  wf -.->|\"current_step, status,<br/>units, ledger\"| d1\n73\t  client -->|\"retry\"| retry\n74\t  failed -.-> retry\n75\t  retry -->|\"restart from the step,<br/>or lease for recipe<br/>or release\"| wf\n76\t  client -->|\"poll, audio\"| read\n77\t  ready -.-> read\n78\t  read -->|\"row\"| d1\n79\t  read -.->|\"re-queue, queued<br/>over 15 min\"| wf\n80\t  client -.->|\"presigned GET\"| r2\n81\t  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n82\t  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n83\t  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n84\t  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n85\t  classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n86\t  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n87\t  classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n88\t  class enq,retry,read worker\n89\t  class s1,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6,c6,s7,s8,s9,dq,rt flow\n90\t  class d1,r2 store\n91\t  class fc,img,aig,modal ext\n92\t  class client person\n93\t  class ready done\n94\t  class failed broken\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\tThe client enqueues through the API Worker, which writes the row as `queued` and creates the item Workflow. fetch and lease run with the item `queued`; every later step runs with it `generating`, and store ends it `ready`. A step whose last attempt fails ends it `failed`, with the error and `current_step` on the row. The retry route writes `queued` and restarts the instance from `current_step`, or from lease when the failed step is recipe or release. The client polls the row through the API Worker, which re-queues an item `queued` for more than 15 minutes, and follows a 307 to read the audio from R2.\n98\t\n99\t## 💽 Modeling\n100\t\n101\tThe item row in D1, one per `enqueue` call, with `status` one of `queued` / `generating` / `ready` / `failed`. Its TypeScript type lives in the [common/ package](common-package.md), which both Workers build from:\n102\t\n103\t| Field | Answers |\n104\t|---|---|\n105\t| `id` | the item's identity: `\"itm_\"` plus 8 hex characters |\n106\t| `url`, `title` | which article this item is for; `title` is written by the recipe step, together with the units |\n107\t| `voice` | which Kokoro voice this item's audio uses, fixed at creation |\n108\t| `created_at` | when it was enqueued; never moves |\n109\t| `updated_at` | when the row was last written |\n110\t| `queued_at` | when the item was last queued: set at enqueue and rewritten whenever the item is queued again, by a retry or by the re-queue of a stale `queued` item |\n111\t| `workflow_instance_id` | the item's Workflow instance, written by the Workflow's first step; the retry route restarts this instance |\n112\t| `current_step` | the name of the step the Workflow is running, written as each step starts; empty before the first step, and on a `failed` item the step that failed |\n113\t| `extraction_domain` | the final URL's domain after redirects, which picks the recipe |\n114\t| `recipe_version_id` | the recipe version the item was extracted with, or the one the agents authored or revised; see [recipes](recipes.md) |\n115\t| `retry_count` | how many times this item has been retried; bounds re-spend against the cap of 3 |\n116\t| `duration`, `audio_format` | populated once `ready` |\n117\t| `units` | the typed display/spoken/timing units: written by the recipe step, the described units' spoken forms rewritten by the describe step, and timed by the store step; the API shows them only once the item is `ready`; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) and [item-contract](item-contract.md) |\n118\t| `degradations` | per-unit enrichment failures that did not fail the item; null when the enrichment was clean |\n119\t| `error` | populated only when `status` is `failed` |\n120\t\n121\t`units` and `degradations` are JSON columns, and common/ encodes and decodes them for both Workers. Audio bytes are never a column on this row; they live in the R2 bucket under the `audio/` prefix, keyed by item id, as described in [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md). A step writes the units and the degradations to the row as soon as it produces them, and later steps read them from there. Only the values the Workflow needs and the row does not hold pass from one step to the next as step results: the fetched HTML, Modal's call id and the synthesis result.\n122\t\n123\t## 🟢 States\n124\t\n125\t```mermaid\n126\tstateDiagram-v2\n127\t    [*] --> queued : the API writes it at enqueue\n128\t    queued --> generating : the Workflow holds its domain's lease\n129\t    generating --> ready : store finishes\n130\t    queued --> failed : fetch or lease uses all its attempts\n131\t    generating --> failed : a step uses all its attempts, or the page is not an article\n132\t    failed --> queued : a client retries, at most 3 times\n133\t    ready --> [*]\n134\t    note right of queued : waiting for the Workflow<br/>to start, or for the<br/>domain's lease\n135\t    note right of generating : extracting, describing<br/>images, synthesizing\n136\t    note right of ready : audio, units, title<br/>and duration readable\n137\t    note right of failed : the error, and current_step<br/>names the failed step\n138\t    classDef waiting fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n139\t    classDef working fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n140\t    classDef done fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n141\t    classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n142\t    class queued waiting\n143\t    class generating working\n144\t    class ready done\n145\t    class failed broken\n146\t```\n147\t\n148\t**Enqueue** writes the item row as `queued`, with `queued_at`, then creates the item's Workflow instance through the API Worker's cross-script Workflow binding, with the item's id as the only param, and returns `202`. The Workflow reads the URL and the voice from the row.\n149\t\n150\t**`queued`** covers the time until the Workflow starts and the time it waits for its domain's lease behind another article from the same domain. **`generating`** starts once the Workflow holds the lease and covers extraction, images, describe and synthesis. **`ready`** and **`failed`** end the Workflow, which writes either one with the rest of the finished state: the timed units and the audio's duration and format for `ready`, the error for `failed`.\n151\t\n152\t**Poll** reads the row and never the Workflow instance. When the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again as it reads it: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. This re-queue also recovers an item stuck locally because the pipeline Worker was not running when the API created its instance. An item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the Workflow instance at the end of its retention window, because nothing the poll returns lives on the instance.\n153\t\n154\t**Retry** (`POST /items/{id}/retry`) moves a `failed` item back to `queued` and restarts its instance; the section below covers it.\n155\t\n156\t> [!NOTE] Why `queued` and `generating` are separate states\n157\t> `queued` is the time an item waits behind work it does not control: the Workflow starting, or another article from the same domain holding the lease. `generating` is the time the Workflow spends on this item's own work. A client that sees `queued` for a long time knows the item is waiting its turn rather than stuck in a step.\n158\t\n159\t> [!TIP] Rejected: one combined in-flight state\n160\t> Collapsing `queued` and `generating` into a single \"working\" status removes a column value but hides from the client whether the item is waiting for its domain's lease or doing its own work.\n161\t\n162\t## 📩 The item Workflow's steps\n163\t\n164\tThe item Workflow lives in the pipeline Worker and does all the work for one item, in nine steps run in order. Its first step writes the instance's id into the row.\n165\t\n166\t```mermaid\n167\tflowchart TB\n168\t  subgraph wf [\"item Workflow\"]\n169\t    s1@{ shape: rect, label: \"1 fetch<br/>(queued)\" }\n170\t    s2@{ shape: rect, label: \"2 lease<br/>(queued)\" }\n171\t    s3@{ shape: rect, label: \"3 recipe\" }\n172\t    s4@{ shape: rect, label: \"4 release\" }\n173\t    s5@{ shape: rect, label: \"5 images\" }\n174\t    s6@{ shape: rect, label: \"6 describe\" }\n175\t    s7@{ shape: rect, label: \"7 start synthesis\" }\n176\t    s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 poll\" }\n177\t    s9@{ shape: rect, label: \"9 store\" }\n178\t    s1 --> s2 --> s3 --> s4 --> s5 --> s6 --> s7 --> s8 --> s9\n179\t  end\n180\t  fc@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Firecrawl\" }\n181\t  dq@{ shape: rect, label: \"DomainQueue\" }\n182\t  rt@{ shape: rect, label: \"Recipe runtime,<br/>Flue agents\" }\n183\t  img@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Images binding\" }\n184\t  aig@{ shape: cloud, label: \"AI Gateway\" }\n185\t  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n186\t  r2@{ shape: cyl, label: \"R2 bucket\" }\n187\t  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n188\t  s1 -->|\"scrape\"| fc\n189\t  s2 -->|\"wait for the lease\"| dq\n190\t  s3 -->|\"run or author the recipe\"| rt\n191\t  s4 -->|\"give the lease back\"| dq\n192\t  s5 -->|\"rasters\"| img\n193\t  s5 -->|\"store images\"| r2\n194\t  s6 -->|\"describe\"| aig\n195\t  s7 -->|\"spoken forms, voice, PUT URL\"| modal\n196\t  s8 -->|\"result, until ready\"| modal\n197\t  modal -->|\"audio\"| r2\n198\t  s9 -->|\"finished state\"| d1\n199\t  wf -.->|\"current_step, status, ledger rows\"| d1\n200\t  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n201\t  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n202\t  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n203\t  class s1,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6,s7,s8,s9,dq,rt flow\n204\t  class r2,d1 store\n205\t  class fc,img,aig,modal ext\n206\t```\n207\t\n208\t| Step | What it does | Attempt timeout |\n209\t|---|---|---|\n210\t| fetch | gets the page through Firecrawl with the Workflow's own fetch client; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) | 2 min |\n211\t| lease | waits for the domain's lease from DomainQueue through `waitForEvent` | 1 h |\n212\t| recipe | runs the domain's recipe through the recipe runtime; the Flue agents author or revise it when the domain needs one, and the Workflow writes the new recipe version; at its end the step derives each unit's spoken form, drops a unit whose spoken form has no letter or digit, and writes the title and the units to the row; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) | 15 min |\n213\t| release | hands the lease back to DomainQueue | 1 min |\n214\t| images | fetches, processes and stores each image of the article; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) | 5 min |\n215\t| describe | runs one child step per code block and flagged image concurrently, each asking Gemini through AI Gateway for a spoken sentence, then writes the results to the row; see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md) | 5 min; 1 min per attempt for each child step |\n216\t| start synthesis | hands Modal the spoken forms, the voice and a presigned R2 PUT URL that the pipeline Worker signs; see [tts-service](tts-service.md) | 2 min |\n217\t| poll | asks Modal for the result until it is ready | 1 min per poll |\n218\t| store | writes the finished state into the row, with the synthesis's ledger row | 1 min |\n219\t\n220\tEach step is retried up to 3 times, and each attempt has its own timeout. A step that makes a metered call writes its ledger row. The Workflow returns no output; everything it produces is on the row.\n221\t\n222\t> [!NOTE] Why the units live on the row and the HTML in a step result\n223\t> A step result is capped near 1 MiB, and the Workflow keeps it so a later step, and a restart from a later step, can read it. The fetched HTML is needed only by the recipe step, so it rides as fetch's step result. The units are read and changed by several steps, so they sit on the row, and each step that changes them writes them there.\n224\t\n225\t> [!WARNING] A step can run twice\n226\t> A deploy can interrupt a step mid-attempt, and the Workflow runs it again. Modal accepts no idempotency key, so a start synthesis that runs again synthesizes a second time; the second upload overwrites the same audio key, because the key is the item id. The duplicate costs one synthesis and is accepted.\n227\t\n228\t### 🎁 The step wrapper\n229\t\n230\tEvery step runs inside one wrapper holding the logic common to all of them. As a step starts, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error. fetch and lease are given `queued`; every later step is given `generating`, so the item shows `generating` from the moment the lease is held. When a step's last attempt fails, the wrapper writes `failed` and the step's own error. A not-article verdict fails the recipe step at once, without its remaining attempts. An attempt that fails while attempts remain writes nothing, so a step's automatic retries never show `failed`.\n231\t\n232\tThe status write sets the status, clears the error and stamps `updated_at` in one statement. It belongs to the [common/ package](common-package.md), and the API uses the same write for `queued` at enqueue and on retry.\n233\t\n234\t### 🗣️ The describe fan-out\n235\t\n236\tdescribe is one step, inside the wrapper like every other. Inside it, one child step per description runs concurrently, and the parent awaits them all with `Promise.allSettled`. Each child calls Gemini and returns its result without writing to the row; like every step it has 3 attempts, each with a 1-minute timeout. The parent collects the results and writes every described unit's spoken form, the degradations and the ledger rows in one write at its end.\n237\t\n238\tA Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections, across all its steps, so at most 6 Gemini calls run at a time while the rest wait, and the wait counts toward the steps' timeouts. In the parallel-steps spike, 25 concurrent 5-second fetches finished in batches of 6, the last after 26 s.\n239\t\n240\tThe parent never fails because a child failed. A child that uses all its attempts leaves its unit on the fallback and records a degradation, even when every description fails, and the Workflow continues to start synthesis.\n241\t\n242\t> [!NOTE] Why one parent step writes once\n243\t> A single write at the parent's end leaves no concurrent writes to the `units` column to reconcile, and the wrapper handles describe as it handles every other step. The cost is in retries: a retry of the parent gives every child a new step identity and runs all of them again, so a repeated describe goes back to Gemini, where AI Gateway's response cache can answer it.\n244\t\n245\t## 🚦 One extraction per domain: DomainQueue\n246\t\n247\tDomainQueue is a Durable Object in the pipeline Worker, one instance per domain key, holding a lease and a FIFO queue in its storage. It lets one item per domain extract at a time. The lease step asks the domain's DomainQueue for the lease and waits for the grant through `waitForEvent`; DomainQueue grants the lease at once when it is free, or holds the item in its FIFO and grants it when the lease comes back. The release step returns the lease right after recipe, so the lease blocks only extraction, and DomainQueue grants it to the next item in the FIFO.\n248\t\n249\t```mermaid\n250\tsequenceDiagram\n251\t    autonumber\n252\t    participant W as Item Workflow\n253\t    participant G as DomainQueue - one per domain\n254\t    participant N as Next item's Workflow\n255\t    W->>G: lease step asks for the lease\n256\t    alt the lease is free\n257\t        G-->>W: lease granted\n258\t    else the lease is held\n259\t        G->>G: hold the item in the FIFO\n260\t        G-->>W: lease granted when it comes back\n261\t    end\n262\t    W->>W: recipe step\n263\t    W->>G: release step gives the lease back\n264\t    G-->>N: lease granted to the FIFO's next item\n265\t    Note over G: backstop - lease alarm\n266\t    alt holder ended or gone\n267\t        G->>G: sweep the lease, grant the next\n268\t    else holder still running\n269\t        G->>G: re-arm the alarm\n270\t    end\n271\t```\n272\t\n273\tThe release step is the fast path; the alarm is the backstop for an item whose Workflow never runs it. Acquiring the lease sets an alarm one minute out; when it fires on a busy domain, DomainQueue asks the platform for the holding instance's status. When the status is ended or the instance is unaddressable, DomainQueue sweeps the lease and grants it to the next item; when the instance is running, DomainQueue re-arms the alarm. A step that fails for good while the item holds the lease leaves the instance errored, so the alarm releases the lease. DomainQueue grants the lease to an instance id that asks for it again, so a retry that restarts from the lease step gets the lease even while DomainQueue still records the earlier attempt as holding it.\n274\t\n275\t## 🧩 The recipe runtime\n276\t\n277\tThe recipe step runs the domain's recipe through the recipe runtime, and the Flue agents' one tool runs the same runtime:\n278\t\n279\t| Member | Answers | Who asks |\n280\t|---|---|---|\n281\t| `run(recipe source, html)` | the title and the units, or the validation report | the recipe step, and the agents' one tool |\n282\t| `validate(extraction, html)` | the mechanical checks' report alone, over the recipe's units, container, inventory and ignores | `run`, on every execution |\n283\t\n284\t`run` executes the script inside a Dynamic Worker: the injection bundle carries cheerio, domino, turndown and its GFM plugin (`@truto/turndown-plugin-gfm`) with it, the article parses inside because the recipe's `$` must exist there, and what returns is JSON. The script's `toMarkdown` calls serialize each selected element with its own tag, so Turndown sees the tag that selects its markdown rule. `toMarkdown` also wraps a `<pre>` with no `<code>` child in `<code>` before conversion. The validator is not part of the bundle; the mechanical checks run in the main Worker, so one implementation serves the recipe step and the agent tool path. Once the script returns its declared units, each tied to its real element path, the host validator checks them. When they pass, `run` returns the title and the units (see [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)). A recipe that does not parse, throws against changed markup, or hangs until the CPU cap kills it, is a validation failure: the revision path's trigger, with its crash report as the validator report. A module that does not parse never starts the Dynamic Worker, so it cannot report itself as data; the load failure is recognised from the platform's own error, which names `recipe.js` in some runtimes and raises a bare `SyntaxError` in others. Only platform failures outside the recipe fail the step: a Dynamic Worker that is unreachable, a bundle that does not load.\n285\t\n286\t> [!WARNING] Recipe source executes in a Dynamic Worker\n287\t> workerd blocks every direct path to running source: `eval` and `new Function` throw, `data:` URL imports do not resolve. A Dynamic Worker, in open beta for runtime-given code, runs the script instead, and the beta is the standing risk. The local half of the proof holds: `pipeline/test/dynamic-workers-proof.test.ts` runs real recipe source through the Worker Loader under vitest on workerd and gets the same extraction from two consecutive runs. The edge half stays open. miniflare's Worker Loader does not enforce `limits.cpuMs`, so the kill that turns a spinning recipe into a validation failure is observable only against the real platform, and that case is skipped in the suite and run by hand. The deterministic stack around it is proven on workerd: cheerio parses a real article in 8 to 18 ms, and the fixed pass converts in 2 to 19 ms with turndown over domino-parsed nodes.\n288\t\n289\t## 🤖 The Flue author and revision agents\n290\t\n291\tThe recipe step dispatches the author agent when the domain has no recipe and the revision agent when the recipe fails validation. The conversation is keyed by the item's Workflow instance id alone, so a recipe step's own retries and a restart from the recipe step continue the same conversation. It carries no history from other items, and its prompts are self-contained. The bounds are one hundred turns, authoring one retry, revision two, set in configuration with the model; the model is flash-class, to be chosen by evaluation before the agent round runs, and its calls go through AI Gateway. The settled reply is a script validated in-loop by its own tool calls, a not-article verdict from the author, or the agent giving up. The reply never carries units. The recipe step runs every settled script through the runtime, so a fresh recipe and a years-old one extract through the same deterministic path (invariant 10).\n292\t\n293\t## 🔁 Retry restarts from the step that failed\n294\t\n295\t`POST /items/{id}/retry` re-drives a `failed` item. The route writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at` and clears the error, then restarts the item's instance from the step named in `current_step`, through the same Workflow binding enqueue uses, and returns `202`. The steps that succeeded do not run again: the restarted step reads their step results and the units already on the row. A failed recipe or release step restarts from the lease step instead, so the item asks for its domain's lease again and the recipe step re-reads the domain's current recipe. The Workflow then moves the item to `generating` as on its first run. [item-contract](item-contract.md) carries the route and wire detail.\n296\t\n297\t**Only a `failed` item under the cap is retryable.** `queued`, `generating` and `ready` all return `409`, and so does a `failed` item at or past 3 retries.\n298\t\n299\t> [!NOTE] The claim that stops two concurrent retries\n300\t> The `failed → queued` move is one conditional write gating on status still `failed` and `retry_count` under the cap, incrementing the count in the same statement. Two concurrent retries cannot both win: only the first finds the preconditions met, the second changes no row and the route refuses it with `409`. A read-then-write would let both pass and restart the instance twice, and would increment the count once, so the cap would read tighter than it is.\n301\t\n302\tThe API never calls Cloudflare's REST API to find where the Workflow stopped: `current_step` on the row names the step, and the wrapper writes it as each step starts. In the restart spike the restarted step started about 3.5 s after the restart was accepted.\n303\t\n304\tAn item stuck in `queued`, including one stuck in local development because the pipeline Worker was not running, needs no retry: the API re-queues it once its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, as described under States; see [local-development](local-development.md).\n305\t\n306\t> [!NOTE] Retry does not re-fetch once extraction resolved\n307\t> Re-fetching would not reliably reproduce the first extraction: firecrawl's output is non-deterministic, measured at a 5x spread on the same URL minutes apart. So a retry resumes from the step that failed and reuses the earlier steps' results rather than re-deriving them, which means it cannot repair an item whose extraction was wrong. A force-restart that re-fetches such an item is a separate, deliberate design problem, not something half-built here.\n308\t\n309\t## 🏷️ What a failure names\n310\t\n311\tEvery `failed` item carries the failed step's own error, with no phase prefix, and its `current_step` names the step that failed, so a reader can place a failure without a stack trace. An item fails when a step uses all 3 of its attempts, and in these cases:\n312\t\n313\t- **Not an article**: the author agent judges the page is not an article, and the recipe step fails the item without retrying.\n314\t- **HTML over the cap**: a fetched page over the 1 MiB a step result can carry fails the fetch step.\n315\t- **Synthesis that fails every time**: the poll step uses its attempts on a crashed synthesis and fails the item.\n316\t- **Result expired**: Modal holds a synthesis result for 7 days; a poll after that fails the item.\n317\t- **Storage failure on finalize**: synthesis completes, but writing the finished state fails; the store step fails the item rather than leaving it stuck `generating`.\n318\t\n319\tNo time limit bounds a whole item. An item that waits for its domain's lease longer than the lease step's 3 attempts of 1 h each fails like any step out of attempts. An item stuck in `queued` does not fail: the API re-queues it when it reads the item and finds `queued_at` more than 15 minutes old.\n320\t\n321\t> [!NOTE] A per-unit degradation is not a failure\n322\t> Enrichment distinguishes a systemic failure from a single unit that could not be acquired or described. A failed image fetch or a failed describe call for one unit is recorded in `degradations` and the item still reaches `ready`; a failed describe child never fails the describe step, and only an exception that stops a whole step fails the item. So `degradations` is populated on items that succeeded, and `error` only on items that did not.\n323\t\n324\t> [!WARNING] Extraction can succeed on the wrong thing\n325\t> A URL serving a 200-status error page (a GitHub Pages 404, say) extracts cleanly, generates audio, and reaches `ready`: the worst failure mode, because it looks like a working item and the status is never `failed`. Separating a real article from a plausible 200-status error page is unsolved.\n326\t\n327\t## ⚙️ Platform limits\n328\t\n329\tA Workflow instance has no wall-clock limit; the bound is CPU per invocation, tens of seconds by default and configurable upward. The agent loops never approach that bound, because model turns are input-output waits. Step results are each capped near 1 MiB, which bounds the fetched HTML; an item's units, a few hundred kilobytes for a long article, fit well inside. Instance state persists for the retention window, days on the free tier and a month on the paid tier; the poll reads only the row, so an item outlives its instance.\n330\t\n331\t## ⏩ What is not built yet\n332\t\n333\tQuota enforcement and a `GET /items` list endpoint are deferred and not part of this lifecycle; see [item-contract](item-contract.md)'s \"what is not built yet\".\n334\t\n335\t## ℹ️ Sources\n336\t\n337\t- Cloudflare Workflows, Workers API and limits: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/build/workers-api/ and https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/reference/limits/\n338\t- Triggering workflows, including from a Durable Object: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/build/trigger-workflows/\n339\t- Flue on Cloudflare, agents as Durable Objects: https://flueframework.com/docs/ecosystem/deploy/cloudflare/\n340\t- Flue workflows, dispatch and read from a step: https://flueframework.com/docs/guide/workflows/\n341\t- Dynamic Workers, the open-beta primitive for runtime-given code: https://developers.cloudflare.com/dynamic-workers/\n342\t\n343\t---\n344\t\n345\tRelated: [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [recipes](recipes.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) · [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md) · [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [authentication](authentication.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n346\t---\n347\ttitle: \"The common/ package\"\n348\tsummary: \"The package the API Worker and the pipeline Worker both build from: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the D1 migrations and the cassette replay library, checked when the code builds.\"\n349\tcreated: \"2026-10-11\"\n350\t---\n351\t\n352\t# The common/ package\n353\t\n354\t## 🔭 Overview\n355\t\n356\tcommon/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything more than one module uses: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the hand-written SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library the integration tests use. Of its contents only the migrations are deployed, by common/'s own CI workflow; the rest is compiled into each Worker. The shared types are checked when the code builds, never at runtime.\n357\t\n358\t## 🏗️ Architecture\n359\t\n360\t```mermaid\n361\tflowchart LR\n362\t  subgraph common [\"common/\"]\n363\t    direction TB\n364\t    types@{ shape: rect, label: \"Types: item row, units,<br/>degradations, statuses,<br/>recipe version, ledger row,<br/>Workflow params\" }\n365\t    keys@{ shape: rect, label: \"R2 key layout and<br/>key functions\" }\n366\t    sign@{ shape: rect, label: \"Presigning,<br/>GET or PUT\" }\n367\t    row@{ shape: rect, label: \"Item row encode,<br/>decode, status write\" }\n368\t    mig@{ shape: rect, label: \"SQL migrations\" }\n369\t    vcr@{ shape: rect, label: \"Cassette replay<br/>library\" }\n370\t  end\n371\t  api@{ shape: rect, label: \"API Worker\" }\n372\t  pipe@{ shape: rect, label: \"pipeline Worker\" }\n373\t  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n374\t  tests@{ shape: rect, label: \"Both Workers'<br/>integration tests\" }\n375\t  api -->|\"types, keys, GET signing,<br/>row, queued write\"| common\n376\t  pipe -->|\"types, keys, PUT signing,<br/>row, status writes\"| common\n377\t  mig -->|\"applied by common/'s CI job\"| d1\n378\t  tests -->|\"replay cassettes\"| vcr\n379\t  tests -->|\"build the test schema\"| mig\n380\t  classDef shared fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n381\t  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n382\t  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n383\t  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n384\t  classDef tool fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n385\t  class types,keys,sign,row,mig,vcr shared\n386\t  class api worker\n387\t  class pipe flow\n388\t  class d1 store\n389\t  class tests tool\n390\t```\n391\t\n392\tThe API Worker and the pipeline Worker import common/ at build time. common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations to D1, and both Workers' tests apply the same migrations to build their own D1 schema. The replay library runs only inside both Workers' integration tests.\n393\t\n394\t## 📦 What it holds\n395\t\n396\tEach piece is described in the note of the part that uses it.\n397\t\n398\t| Piece | Holds | Used by | Described in |\n399\t|---|---|---|---|\n400\t| Item row type | the item row with its statuses, units, degradations and error | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md), [item-contract](item-contract.md) |\n401\t| Unit union | the typed unit each item's display, spoken form and timing ride on | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [item-contract](item-contract.md) |\n402\t| Recipe version row type | one version of a domain's recipe | the item Workflow, the recipe runtime | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n403\t| Ledger row type | the ledger row with its event types and units | the item Workflow | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n404\t| Workflow params type | the item id the API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow with | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) |\n405\t| R2 key layout | the bucket's audio/ and images/ prefixes, and the functions that build an audio key from an item id and an image key from a content hash | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n406\t| Presigning | one function that signs a GET or a PUT URL for a key with a given R2 token and lifetime | the API Worker for GET, the pipeline Worker for PUT | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n407\t| Item row reads and writes | encoding and decoding the JSON columns, and the status write that sets a status, clears the error and stamps updated_at | the API Worker for queued, the step wrapper for every later status | [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) |\n408\t| SQL migrations | the D1 schema, as hand-written SQL files | common/'s CI workflow, both Workers' tests, local development | [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md), [local-development](local-development.md) |\n409\t| Cassette replay library | our TypeScript copy of vcrpy, replay only | both Workers' integration tests | this note |\n410\t\n411\t## 📼 Replaying cassettes in tests\n412\t\n413\tThe replay library reads the repository's vcrpy cassettes and answers each outgoing request from them. It matches a request on its method, scheme, host, port and query, and on its path and body with every item id normalized. Before matching, it removes the credential headers (authorization, x-api-key and x-goog-api-key), which the cassettes were recorded without. It records nothing. A request that matches no recorded response fails the test.\n414\t\n415\t## 🔒 Checking the shared types at build time\n416\t\n417\tThe shared types are checked when the code builds and deploys, never at runtime. A value read back from D1 or received from Modal is trusted to match its type, and nothing parses it against a schema.\n418\t\n419\tA change to common/ runs the type checks of api/ and pipeline/ against it in CI. When either check fails, the whole deploy stops: common/'s CI workflow applies no migration, and neither Worker deploys. When both pass, common/'s CI workflow applies the migrations to D1 with wrangler, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for it to succeed before they continue; the pipeline Worker deploys before the API Worker. [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) holds that order.\n420\t\n421\t> [!NOTE] Why the types are not validated at runtime\n422\t> Both Workers are type-checked against the same common/ before anything deploys, so a shape the two Workers disagree on stops the deploy before it reaches production. This departs from the code style's rule that a payload crossing a service boundary validates at runtime.\n423\t\n424\t---\n425\t\n426\tRelated: [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [local-development](local-development.md)\n427\t"}]},"uuid":"ad27c5e6-d0fb-4a2c-a987-17a340bc9cac","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:48:01.111Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agus/.claude/projects/-home-agus-workspace-asermax-nagara/f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e/tool-results/b9a723szt.txt","content":"---\ntitle: \"Item lifecycle\"\nsummary: \"The item's four-state machine: the API Worker writes a queued row and creates the item Workflow, nine steps inside one step wrapper carry it to ready or failed, DomainQueue serializes extraction per domain, and retry restarts the Workflow from the step that failed.\"\ncreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n---\n\n# Item lifecycle\n\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nAn item is the record of one `enqueue(url, voice?)` call, from creation through to playable audio or a clear failure. The API Worker writes the item row as `queued` and creates the item's Workflow instance in the pipeline Worker; the item Workflow then does all the work for that item in nine steps and writes every later status into the row. The poll route reads only the row, never the Workflow instance. A retry restarts the same instance from the step that failed. [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) covers how the extracted markdown units become the item's units, [recipes](recipes.md) covers the scripts that extract them, and [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) covers what the TTS service hands back.\n\n## 🗺️ How an item moves through the system\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n  client@{ shape: stadium, label: \"Client\" }\n  subgraph api [\"API Worker\"]\n    direction TB\n    enq@{ shape: rect, label: \"POST /items:<br/>row as queued,<br/>create instance\" }\n    retry@{ shape: rect, label: \"POST retry:<br/>row as queued, restart<br/>from current_step\" }\n    read@{ shape: rect, label: \"GET item, audio:<br/>read row, 307 to<br/>presigned URL\" }\n  end\n  subgraph wf [\"item Workflow, steps inside the wrapper\"]\n    direction TB\n    subgraph q [\"status queued\"]\n      direction TB\n      s1@{ shape: rect, label: \"1 fetch\" }\n      s2@{ shape: rect, label: \"2 lease\" }\n    end\n    subgraph g [\"status generating\"]\n      direction TB\n      s3@{ shape: rect, label: \"3 recipe, then<br/>spoken form and drop\" }\n      s4@{ shape: rect, label: \"4 release\" }\n      s5@{ shape: rect, label: \"5 images\" }\n      s6@{ shape: rect, label: \"6 describe<br/>(parent step)\" }\n      c6@{ shape: procs, label: \"child describe steps,<br/>Promise.allSettled\" }\n      s7@{ shape: rect, label: \"7 start synthesis\" }\n      s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 poll\" }\n      s9@{ shape: rect, label: \"9 store\" }\n    end\n  end\n  ready@{ shape: stadium, label: \"ready\" }\n  failed@{ shape: stadium, label: \"failed: error,<br/>current_step\" }\n  fc@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Firecrawl\" }\n  dq@{ shape: rect, label: \"DomainQueue\" }\n  rt@{ shape: rect, label: \"Recipe runtime,<br/>Flue agents\" }\n  img@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Images binding\" }\n  aig@{ shape: cloud, label: \"AI Gateway\" }\n  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n  r2@{ shape: cyl, label: \"R2 bucket\" }\n  client -->|\"enqueue\"| enq\n  enq -->|\"queued row\"| d1\n  enq -->|\"create\"| s1\n  s1 --> s2 --> s3 --> s4 --> s5 --> s6 --> s7 --> s8 --> s9\n  s6 -->|\"fan out\"| c6\n  s1 -->|\"scrape\"| fc\n  s2 -->|\"wait for lease\"| dq\n  s4 -->|\"give lease back\"| dq\n  s3 -->|\"run or author\"| rt\n  rt -->|\"model calls\"| aig\n  s5 -->|\"rasters\"| img\n  s5 -->|\"images/\"| r2\n  c6 -->|\"Gemini\"| aig\n  s7 -->|\"spoken forms,<br/>PUT URL\"| modal\n  s8 -->|\"result\"| modal\n  modal -->|\"audio/\"| r2\n  s9 --> ready\n  wf -.->|\"last attempt fails\"| failed\n  wf -.->|\"current_step, status,<br/>units, ledger\"| d1\n  client -->|\"retry\"| retry\n  failed -.-> retry\n  retry -->|\"restart from the step,<br/>or lease for recipe<br/>or release\"| wf\n  client -->|\"poll, audio\"| read\n  ready -.-> read\n  read -->|\"row\"| d1\n  read -.->|\"re-queue, queued<br/>over 15 min\"| wf\n  client -.->|\"presigned GET\"| r2\n  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n  classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n  class enq,retry,read worker\n  class s1,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6,c6,s7,s8,s9,dq,rt flow\n  class d1,r2 store\n  class fc,img,aig,modal ext\n  class client person\n  class ready done\n  class failed broken\n```\n\nThe client enqueues through the API Worker, which writes the row as `queued` and creates the item Workflow. fetch and lease run with the item `queued`; every later step runs with it `generating`, and store ends it `ready`. A step whose last attempt fails ends it `failed`, with the error and `current_step` on the row. The retry route writes `queued` and restarts the instance from `current_step`, or from lease when the failed step is recipe or release. The client polls the row through the API Worker, which re-queues an item `queued` for more than 15 minutes, and follows a 307 to read the audio from R2.\n\n## 💽 Modeling\n\nThe item row in D1, one per `enqueue` call, with `status` one of `queued` / `generating` / `ready` / `failed`. Its TypeScript type lives in the [common/ package](common-package.md), which both Workers build from:\n\n| Field | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| `id` | the item's identity: `\"itm_\"` plus 8 hex characters |\n| `url`, `title` | which article this item is for; `title` is written by the recipe step, together with the units |\n| `voice` | which Kokoro voice this item's audio uses, fixed at creation |\n| `created_at` | when it was enqueued; never moves |\n| `updated_at` | when the row was last written |\n| `queued_at` | when the item was last queued: set at enqueue and rewritten whenever the item is queued again, by a retry or by the re-queue of a stale `queued` item |\n| `workflow_instance_id` | the item's Workflow instance, written by the Workflow's first step; the retry route restarts this instance |\n| `current_step` | the name of the step the Workflow is running, written as each step starts; empty before the first step, and on a `failed` item the step that failed |\n| `extraction_domain` | the final URL's domain after redirects, which picks the recipe |\n| `recipe_version_id` | the recipe version the item was extracted with, or the one the agents authored or revised; see [recipes](recipes.md) |\n| `retry_count` | how many times this item has been retried; bounds re-spend against the cap of 3 |\n| `duration`, `audio_format` | populated once `ready` |\n| `units` | the typed display/spoken/timing units: written by the recipe step, the described units' spoken forms rewritten by the describe step, and timed by the store step; the API shows them only once the item is `ready`; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) and [item-contract](item-contract.md) |\n| `degradations` | per-unit enrichment failures that did not fail the item; null when the enrichment was clean |\n| `error` | populated only when `status` is `failed` |\n\n`units` and `degradations` are JSON columns, and common/ encodes and decodes them for both Workers. Audio bytes are never a column on this row; they live in the R2 bucket under the `audio/` prefix, keyed by item id, as described in [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md). A step writes the units and the degradations to the row as soon as it produces them, and later steps read them from there. Only the values the Workflow needs and the row does not hold pass from one step to the next as step results: the fetched HTML, Modal's call id and the synthesis result.\n\n## 🟢 States\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> queued : the API writes it at enqueue\n    queued --> generating : the Workflow holds its domain's lease\n    generating --> ready : store finishes\n    queued --> failed : fetch or lease uses all its attempts\n    generating --> failed : a step uses all its attempts, or the page is not an article\n    failed --> queued : a client retries, at most 3 times\n    ready --> [*]\n    note right of queued : waiting for the Workflow<br/>to start, or for the<br/>domain's lease\n    note right of generating : extracting, describing<br/>images, synthesizing\n    note right of ready : audio, units, title<br/>and duration readable\n    note right of failed : the error, and current_step<br/>names the failed step\n    classDef waiting fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n    classDef working fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n    classDef done fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n    class queued waiting\n    class generating working\n    class ready done\n    class failed broken\n```\n\n**Enqueue** writes the item row as `queued`, with `queued_at`, then creates the item's Workflow instance through the API Worker's cross-script Workflow binding, with the item's id as the only param, and returns `202`. The Workflow reads the URL and the voice from the row.\n\n**`queued`** covers the time until the Workflow starts and the time it waits for its domain's lease behind another article from the same domain. **`generating`** starts once the Workflow holds the lease and covers extraction, images, describe and synthesis. **`ready`** and **`failed`** end the Workflow, which writes either one with the rest of the finished state: the timed units and the audio's duration and format for `ready`, the error for `failed`.\n\n**Poll** reads the row and never the Workflow instance. When the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again as it reads it: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. This re-queue also recovers an item stuck locally because the pipeline Worker was not running when the API created its instance. An item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the Workflow instance at the end of its retention window, because nothing the poll returns lives on the instance.\n\n**Retry** (`POST /items/{id}/retry`) moves a `failed` item back to `queued` and restarts its instance; the section below covers it.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why `queued` and `generating` are separate states\n> `queued` is the time an item waits behind work it does not control: the Workflow starting, or another article from the same domain holding the lease. `generating` is the time the Workflow spends on this item's own work. A client that sees `queued` for a long time knows the item is waiting its turn rather than stuck in a step.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: one combined in-flight state\n> Collapsing `queued` and `generating` into a single \"working\" status removes a column value but hides from the client whether the item is waiting for its domain's lease or doing its own work.\n\n## 📩 The item Workflow's steps\n\nThe item Workflow lives in the pipeline Worker and does all the work for one item, in nine steps run in order. Its first step writes the instance's id into the row.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n  subgraph wf [\"item Workflow\"]\n    s1@{ shape: rect, label: \"1 fetch<br/>(queued)\" }\n    s2@{ shape: rect, label: \"2 lease<br/>(queued)\" }\n    s3@{ shape: rect, label: \"3 recipe\" }\n    s4@{ shape: rect, label: \"4 release\" }\n    s5@{ shape: rect, label: \"5 images\" }\n    s6@{ shape: rect, label: \"6 describe\" }\n    s7@{ shape: rect, label: \"7 start synthesis\" }\n    s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 poll\" }\n    s9@{ shape: rect, label: \"9 store\" }\n    s1 --> s2 --> s3 --> s4 --> s5 --> s6 --> s7 --> s8 --> s9\n  end\n  fc@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Firecrawl\" }\n  dq@{ shape: rect, label: \"DomainQueue\" }\n  rt@{ shape: rect, label: \"Recipe runtime,<br/>Flue agents\" }\n  img@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Images binding\" }\n  aig@{ shape: cloud, label: \"AI Gateway\" }\n  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n  r2@{ shape: cyl, label: \"R2 bucket\" }\n  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n  s1 -->|\"scrape\"| fc\n  s2 -->|\"wait for the lease\"| dq\n  s3 -->|\"run or author the recipe\"| rt\n  s4 -->|\"give the lease back\"| dq\n  s5 -->|\"rasters\"| img\n  s5 -->|\"store images\"| r2\n  s6 -->|\"describe\"| aig\n  s7 -->|\"spoken forms, voice, PUT URL\"| modal\n  s8 -->|\"result, until ready\"| modal\n  modal -->|\"audio\"| r2\n  s9 -->|\"finished state\"| d1\n  wf -.->|\"current_step, status, ledger rows\"| d1\n  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n  class s1,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6,s7,s8,s9,dq,rt flow\n  class r2,d1 store\n  class fc,img,aig,modal ext\n```\n\n| Step | What it does | Attempt timeout |\n|---|---|---|\n| fetch | gets the page through Firecrawl with the Workflow's own fetch client; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) | 2 min |\n| lease | waits for the domain's lease from DomainQueue through `waitForEvent` | 1 h |\n| recipe | runs the domain's recipe through the recipe runtime; the Flue agents author or revise it when the domain needs one, and the Workflow writes the new recipe version; at its end the step derives each unit's spoken form, drops a unit whose spoken form has no letter or digit, and writes the title and the units to the row; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) | 15 min |\n| release | hands the lease back to DomainQueue | 1 min |\n| images | fetches, processes and stores each image of the article; see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) | 5 min |\n| describe | runs one child step per code block and flagged image concurrently, each asking Gemini through AI Gateway for a spoken sentence, then writes the results to the row; see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md) | 5 min; 1 min per attempt for each child step |\n| start synthesis | hands Modal the spoken forms, the voice and a presigned R2 PUT URL that the pipeline Worker signs; see [tts-service](tts-service.md) | 2 min |\n| poll | asks Modal for the result until it is ready | 1 min per poll |\n| store | writes the finished state into the row, with the synthesis's ledger row | 1 min |\n\nEach step is retried up to 3 times, and each attempt has its own timeout. A step that makes a metered call writes its ledger row. The Workflow returns no output; everything it produces is on the row.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the units live on the row and the HTML in a step result\n> A step result is capped near 1 MiB, and the Workflow keeps it so a later step, and a restart from a later step, can read it. The fetched HTML is needed only by the recipe step, so it rides as fetch's step result. The units are read and changed by several steps, so they sit on the row, and each step that changes them writes them there.\n\n> [!WARNING] A step can run twice\n> A deploy can interrupt a step mid-attempt, and the Workflow runs it again. Modal accepts no idempotency key, so a start synthesis that runs again synthesizes a second time; the second upload overwrites the same audio key, because the key is the item id. The duplicate costs one synthesis and is accepted.\n\n### 🎁 The step wrapper\n\nEvery step runs inside one wrapper holding the logic common to all of them. As a step starts, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error. fetch and lease are given `queued`; every later step is given `generating`, so the item shows `generating` from the moment the lease is held. When a step's last attempt fails, the wrapper writes `failed` and the step's own error. A not-article verdict fails the recipe step at once, without its remaining attempts. An attempt that fails while attempts remain writes nothing, so a step's automatic retries never show `failed`.\n\nThe status write sets the status, clears the error and stamps `updated_at` in one statement. It belongs to the [common/ package](common-package.md), and the API uses the same write for `queued` at enqueue and on retry.\n\n### 🗣️ The describe fan-out\n\ndescribe is one step, inside the wrapper like every other. Inside it, one child step per description runs concurrently, and the parent awaits them all with `Promise.allSettled`. Each child calls Gemini and returns its result without writing to the row; like every step it has 3 attempts, each with a 1-minute timeout. The parent collects the results and writes every described unit's spoken form, the degradations and the ledger rows in one write at its end.\n\nA Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections, across all its steps, so at most 6 Gemini calls run at a time while the rest wait, and the wait counts toward the steps' timeouts. In the parallel-steps spike, 25 concurrent 5-second fetches finished in batches of 6, the last after 26 s.\n\nThe parent never fails because a child failed. A child that uses all its attempts leaves its unit on the fallback and records a degradation, even when every description fails, and the Workflow continues to start synthesis.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one parent step writes once\n> A single write at the parent's end leaves no concurrent writes to the `units` column to reconcile, and the wrapper handles describe as it handles every other step. The cost is in retries: a retry of the parent gives every child a new step identity and runs all of them again, so a repeated describe goes back to Gemini, where AI Gateway's response cache can answer it.\n\n## 🚦 One extraction per domain: DomainQueue\n\nDomainQueue is a Durable Object in the pipeline Worker, one instance per domain key, holding a lease and a FIFO queue in its storage. It lets one item per domain extract at a time. The lease step asks the domain's DomainQueue for the lease and waits for the grant through `waitForEvent`; DomainQueue grants the lease at once when it is free, or holds the item in its FIFO and grants it when the lease comes back. The release step returns the lease right after recipe, so the lease blocks only extraction, and DomainQueue grants it to the next item in the FIFO.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant W as Item Workflow\n    participant G as DomainQueue - one per domain\n    participant N as Next item's Workflow\n    W->>G: lease step asks for the lease\n    alt the lease is free\n        G-->>W: lease granted\n    else the lease is held\n        G->>G: hold the item in the FIFO\n        G-->>W: lease granted when it comes back\n    end\n    W->>W: recipe step\n    W->>G: release step gives the lease back\n    G-->>N: lease granted to the FIFO's next item\n    Note over G: backstop - lease alarm\n    alt holder ended or gone\n        G->>G: sweep the lease, grant the next\n    else holder still running\n        G->>G: re-arm the alarm\n    end\n```\n\nThe release step is the fast path; the alarm is the backstop for an item whose Workflow never runs it. Acquiring the lease sets an alarm one minute out; when it fires on a busy domain, DomainQueue asks the platform for the holding instance's status. When the status is ended or the instance is unaddressable, DomainQueue sweeps the lease and grants it to the next item; when the instance is running, DomainQueue re-arms the alarm. A step that fails for good while the item holds the lease leaves the instance errored, so the alarm releases the lease. DomainQueue grants the lease to an instance id that asks for it again, so a retry that restarts from the lease step gets the lease even while DomainQueue still records the earlier attempt as holding it.\n\n## 🧩 The recipe runtime\n\nThe recipe step runs the domain's recipe through the recipe runtime, and the Flue agents' one tool runs the same runtime:\n\n| Member | Answers | Who asks |\n|---|---|---|\n| `run(recipe source, html)` | the title and the units, or the validation report | the recipe step, and the agents' one tool |\n| `validate(extraction, html)` | the mechanical checks' report alone, over the recipe's units, container, inventory and ignores | `run`, on every execution |\n\n`run` executes the script inside a Dynamic Worker: the injection bundle carries cheerio, domino, turndown and its GFM plugin (`@truto/turndown-plugin-gfm`) with it, the article parses inside because the recipe's `$` must exist there, and what returns is JSON. The script's `toMarkdown` calls serialize each selected element with its own tag, so Turndown sees the tag that selects its markdown rule. `toMarkdown` also wraps a `<pre>` with no `<code>` child in `<code>` before conversion. The validator is not part of the bundle; the mechanical checks run in the main Worker, so one implementation serves the recipe step and the agent tool path. Once the script returns its declared units, each tied to its real element path, the host validator checks them. When they pass, `run` returns the title and the units (see [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)). A recipe that does not parse, throws against changed markup, or hangs until the CPU cap kills it, is a validation failure: the revision path's trigger, with its crash report as the validator report. A module that does not parse never starts the Dynamic Worker, so it cannot report itself as data; the load failure is recognised from the platform's own error, which names `recipe.js` in some runtimes and raises a bare `SyntaxError` in others. Only platform failures outside the recipe fail the step: a Dynamic Worker that is unreachable, a bundle that does not load.\n\n> [!WARNING] Recipe source executes in a Dynamic Worker\n> workerd blocks every direct path to running source: `eval` and `new Function` throw, `data:` URL imports do not resolve. A Dynamic Worker, in open beta for runtime-given code, runs the script instead, and the beta is the standing risk. The local half of the proof holds: `pipeline/test/dynamic-workers-proof.test.ts` runs real recipe source through the Worker Loader under vitest on workerd and gets the same extraction from two consecutive runs. The edge half stays open. miniflare's Worker Loader does not enforce `limits.cpuMs`, so the kill that turns a spinning recipe into a validation failure is observable only against the real platform, and that case is skipped in the suite and run by hand. The deterministic stack around it is proven on workerd: cheerio parses a real article in 8 to 18 ms, and the fixed pass converts in 2 to 19 ms with turndown over domino-parsed nodes.\n\n## 🤖 The Flue author and revision agents\n\nThe recipe step dispatches the author agent when the domain has no recipe and the revision agent when the recipe fails validation. The conversation is keyed by the item's Workflow instance id alone, so a recipe step's own retries and a restart from the recipe step continue the same conversation. It carries no history from other items, and its prompts are self-contained. The bounds are one hundred turns, authoring one retry, revision two, set in configuration with the model; the model is flash-class, to be chosen by evaluation before the agent round runs, and its calls go through AI Gateway. The settled reply is a script validated in-loop by its own tool calls, a not-article verdict from the author, or the agent giving up. The reply never carries units. The recipe step runs every settled script through the runtime, so a fresh recipe and a years-old one extract through the same deterministic path (invariant 10).\n\n## 🔁 Retry restarts from the step that failed\n\n`POST /items/{id}/retry` re-drives a `failed` item. The route writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at` and clears the error, then restarts the item's instance from the step named in `current_step`, through the same Workflow binding enqueue uses, and returns `202`. The steps that succeeded do not run again: the restarted step reads their step results and the units already on the row. A failed recipe or release step restarts from the lease step instead, so the item asks for its domain's lease again and the recipe step re-reads the domain's current recipe. The Workflow then moves the item to `generating` as on its first run. [item-contract](item-contract.md) carries the route and wire detail.\n\n**Only a `failed` item under the cap is retryable.** `queued`, `generating` and `ready` all return `409`, and so does a `failed` item at or past 3 retries.\n\n> [!NOTE] The claim that stops two concurrent retries\n> The `failed → queued` move is one conditional write gating on status still `failed` and `retry_count` under the cap, incrementing the count in the same statement. Two concurrent retries cannot both win: only the first finds the preconditions met, the second changes no row and the route refuses it with `409`. A read-then-write would let both pass and restart the instance twice, and would increment the count once, so the cap would read tighter than it is.\n\nThe API never calls Cloudflare's REST API to find where the Workflow stopped: `current_step` on the row names the step, and the wrapper writes it as each step starts. In the restart spike the restarted step started about 3.5 s after the restart was accepted.\n\nAn item stuck in `queued`, including one stuck in local development because the pipeline Worker was not running, needs no retry: the API re-queues it once its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, as described under States; see [local-development](local-development.md).\n\n> [!NOTE] Retry does not re-fetch once extraction resolved\n> Re-fetching would not reliably reproduce the first extraction: firecrawl's output is non-deterministic, measured at a 5x spread on the same URL minutes apart. So a retry resumes from the step that failed and reuses the earlier steps' results rather than re-deriving them, which means it cannot repair an item whose extraction was wrong. A force-restart that re-fetches such an item is a separate, deliberate design problem, not something half-built here.\n\n## 🏷️ What a failure names\n\nEvery `failed` item carries the failed step's own error, with no phase prefix, and its `current_step` names the step that failed, so a reader can place a failure without a stack trace. An item fails when a step uses all 3 of its attempts, and in these cases:\n\n- **Not an article**: the author agent judges the page is not an article, and the recipe step fails the item without retrying.\n- **HTML over the cap**: a fetched page over the 1 MiB a step result can carry fails the fetch step.\n- **Synthesis that fails every time**: the poll step uses its attempts on a crashed synthesis and fails the item.\n- **Result expired**: Modal holds a synthesis result for 7 days; a poll after that fails the item.\n- **Storage failure on finalize**: synthesis completes, but writing the finished state fails; the store step fails the item rather than leaving it stuck `generating`.\n\nNo time limit bounds a whole item. An item that waits for its domain's lease longer than the lease step's 3 attempts of 1 h each fails like any step out of attempts. An item stuck in `queued` does not fail: the API re-queues it when it reads the item and finds `queued_at` more than 15 minutes old.\n\n> [!NOTE] A per-unit degradation is not a failure\n> Enrichment distinguishes a systemic failure from a single unit that could not be acquired or described. A failed image fetch or a failed describe call for one unit is recorded in `degradations` and the item still reaches `ready`; a failed describe child never fails the describe step, and only an exception that stops a whole step fails the item. So `degradations` is populated on items that succeeded, and `error` only on items that did not.\n\n> [!WARNING] Extraction can succeed on the wrong thing\n> A URL serving a 200-status error page (a GitHub Pages 404, say) extracts cleanly, generates audio, and reaches `ready`: the worst failure mode, because it looks like a working item and the status is never `failed`. Separating a real article from a plausible 200-status error page is unsolved.\n\n## ⚙️ Platform limits\n\nA Workflow instance has no wall-clock limit; the bound is CPU per invocation, tens of seconds by default and configurable upward. The agent loops never approach that bound, because model turns are input-output waits. Step results are each capped near 1 MiB, which bounds the fetched HTML; an item's units, a few hundred kilobytes for a long article, fit well inside. Instance state persists for the retention window, days on the free tier and a month on the paid tier; the poll reads only the row, so an item outlives its instance.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\nQuota enforcement and a `GET /items` list endpoint are deferred and not part of this lifecycle; see [item-contract](item-contract.md)'s \"what is not built yet\".\n\n## ℹ️ Sources\n\n- Cloudflare Workflows, Workers API and limits: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/build/workers-api/ and https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/reference/limits/\n- Triggering workflows, including from a Durable Object: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/build/trigger-workflows/\n- Flue on Cloudflare, agents as Durable Objects: https://flueframework.com/docs/ecosystem/deploy/cloudflare/\n- Flue workflows, dispatch and read from a step: https://flueframework.com/docs/guide/workflows/\n- Dynamic Workers, the open-beta primitive for runtime-given code: https://developers.cloudflare.com/dynamic-workers/\n\n---\n\nRelated: [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [recipes](recipes.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) · [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md) · [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [authentication](authentication.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"The common/ package\"\nsummary: \"The package the API Worker and the pipeline Worker both build from: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the D1 migrations and the cassette replay library, checked when the code builds.\"\ncreated: \"2026-10-11\"\n---\n\n# The common/ package\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\ncommon/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything more than one module uses: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the hand-written SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library the integration tests use. Of its contents only the migrations are deployed, by common/'s own CI workflow; the rest is compiled into each Worker. The shared types are checked when the code builds, never at runtime.\n\n## 🏗️ Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph common [\"common/\"]\n    direction TB\n    types@{ shape: rect, label: \"Types: item row, units,<br/>degradations, statuses,<br/>recipe version, ledger row,<br/>Workflow params\" }\n    keys@{ shape: rect, label: \"R2 key layout and<br/>key functions\" }\n    sign@{ shape: rect, label: \"Presigning,<br/>GET or PUT\" }\n    row@{ shape: rect, label: \"Item row encode,<br/>decode, status write\" }\n    mig@{ shape: rect, label: \"SQL migrations\" }\n    vcr@{ shape: rect, label: \"Cassette replay<br/>library\" }\n  end\n  api@{ shape: rect, label: \"API Worker\" }\n  pipe@{ shape: rect, label: \"pipeline Worker\" }\n  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n  tests@{ shape: rect, label: \"Both Workers'<br/>integration tests\" }\n  api -->|\"types, keys, GET signing,<br/>row, queued write\"| common\n  pipe -->|\"types, keys, PUT signing,<br/>row, status writes\"| common\n  mig -->|\"applied by common/'s CI job\"| d1\n  tests -->|\"replay cassettes\"| vcr\n  tests -->|\"build the test schema\"| mig\n  classDef shared fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706\n  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n  classDef tool fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n  class types,keys,sign,row,mig,vcr shared\n  class api worker\n  class pipe flow\n  class d1 store\n  class tests tool\n```\n\nThe API Worker and the pipeline Worker import common/ at build time. common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations to D1, and both Workers' tests apply the same migrations to build their own D1 schema. The replay library runs only inside both Workers' integration tests.\n\n## 📦 What it holds\n\nEach piece is described in the note of the part that uses it.\n\n| Piece | Holds | Used by | Described in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Item row type | the item row with its statuses, units, degradations and error | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md), [item-contract](item-contract.md) |\n| Unit union | the typed unit each item's display, spoken form and timing ride on | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [item-contract](item-contract.md) |\n| Recipe version row type | one version of a domain's recipe | the item Workflow, the recipe runtime | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n| Ledger row type | the ledger row with its event types and units | the item Workflow | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n| Workflow params type | the item id the API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow with | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) |\n| R2 key layout | the bucket's audio/ and images/ prefixes, and the functions that build an audio key from an item id and an image key from a content hash | the API Worker, the item Workflow | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n| Presigning | one function that signs a GET or a PUT URL for a key with a given R2 token and lifetime | the API Worker for GET, the pipeline Worker for PUT | [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) |\n| Item row reads and writes | encoding and decoding the JSON columns, and the status write that sets a status, clears the error and stamps updated_at | the API Worker for queued, the step wrapper for every later status | [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) |\n| SQL migrations | the D1 schema, as hand-written SQL files | common/'s CI workflow, both Workers' tests, local development | [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md), [local-development](local-development.md) |\n| Cassette replay library | our TypeScript copy of vcrpy, replay only | both Workers' integration tests | this note |\n\n## 📼 Replaying cassettes in tests\n\nThe replay library reads the repository's vcrpy cassettes and answers each outgoing request from them. It matches a request on its method, scheme, host, port and query, and on its path and body with every item id normalized. Before matching, it removes the credential headers (authorization, x-api-key and x-goog-api-key), which the cassettes were recorded without. It records nothing. A request that matches no recorded response fails the test.\n\n## 🔒 Checking the shared types at build time\n\nThe shared types are checked when the code builds and deploys, never at runtime. A value read back from D1 or received from Modal is trusted to match its type, and nothing parses it against a schema.\n\nA change to common/ runs the type checks of api/ and pipeline/ against it in CI. When either check fails, the whole deploy stops: common/'s CI workflow applies no migration, and neither Worker deploys. When both pass, common/'s CI workflow applies the migrations to D1 with wrangler, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for it to succeed before they continue; the pipeline Worker deploys before the API Worker. [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) holds that order.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the types are not validated at runtime\n> Both Workers are type-checked against the same common/ before anything deploys, so a shape the two Workers disagree on stops the deploy before it reaches production. This departs from the code style's rule that a payload crossing a service boundary validates at runtime.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [local-development](local-development.md)\n","numLines":427,"startLine":1,"totalLines":427}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4b676df0-e2d7-4ee4-856e-ff8b77b01eb4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
