{"parentUuid":"485c48d4-a22c-458a-8ec1-644fc5584bd2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuqwAiZbSXTjv9TaeCnw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0131J4THhoVXDUjvthncdT5z","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design; sed -n '/^# /,$p' article-extraction.md recipes.md | head -270"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14137,"cache_read_input_tokens":97815,"output_tokens":323,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14137,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":323,"cache_read_input_tokens":97815,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14137,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14137},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0131J4THhoVXDUjvthncdT5z":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design; sed -n '/^# /,$p' article-extraction.md recipes.md | head -270"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"7b579651-b780-4d9e-9dce-e0b458360976","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CfuqwANE1Sdm7r86RDGpM","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:program-design","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"da3f9037-27eb-47f1-bd46-dcb806754864","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:48:05.678Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"45402743-426f-4a70-ba80-380fef6d231e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bfa32ed9-babd-4bb0-92a6-0867b6b276d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Zg4NhqUSyxAbXtNbhBdxk","type":"tool_result","content":"# Persistence and storage\n\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nWhat the item row persists, where its audio and images live, and the ledger that records what each item cost. One D1 database holds every row and one R2 bucket holds every object; the API Worker and the pipeline Worker bind both. Both Workers build the row's shape, the R2 key layout and the URL presigning from [the common/ package](common-package.md), so the two Workers agree on every column and every key.\n\n\n## 💽 Modeling\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    ITEMS }o--o| RECIPE_VERSIONS : \"extracted by\"\n    ITEMS ||--o{ COST_ENTRIES : \"costs\"\n    ITEMS {\n        string id PK\n        string status\n        string current_step \"nullable\"\n        string workflow_instance_id \"nullable\"\n        json units \"nullable\"\n        json degradations\n        datetime queued_at\n        datetime updated_at\n    }\n    COST_ENTRIES {\n        string item_id FK\n        string type\n        number quantity\n        string unit\n        number dollars\n        boolean cached\n        json detail \"nullable\"\n    }\n```\n\nThe database has three tables: `items`, one row per enqueued article; `recipe_versions`, one row per version of a domain's recipe; and `cost_entries`, one row per metered call. The item row's full field list is in [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md); the diagram shows the fields this note is about. Audio bytes are never a column; they live in the bucket below, keyed by item id, so a multi-megabyte file never bloats the row.\n\nThe row always holds the item's status. The API Worker writes `queued` at enqueue; the item Workflow's step wrapper writes every later status together with `current_step` as each step starts. The Workflow writes the finished state when it ends, and a failed item's `error` is the failing step's own error, with `current_step` naming that step. `workflow_instance_id` names the item's Workflow instance, which the retry route restarts; `updated_at` records the last write. `queued_at` is set at enqueue and rewritten whenever the item is queued again: when the API reads a `queued` item whose `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. The poll reads only the row and never the instance, so an item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the instance at the end of its retention window. Both writers go through the status write in [common/](common-package.md), which sets a status, clears the error and stamps `updated_at`.\n\nThe Workflow writes `units` and `degradations` into the row as soon as a step produces them, and later steps read them back from the row. The recipe step writes the title together with the units, which carry their display and spoken forms; the images step rewrites them, and the describe step writes every described unit's spoken form, with its degradations and ledger rows, in one write at its end. Its child steps, one per description and run concurrently, only return their results to it. The API returns the units to a client only once the item is `ready`.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the describe children write nothing to the row\n> Up to 25 children run at once. A child that read the list, changed its unit and wrote the whole list back would overwrite the results its siblings wrote in between, so the parent collects every result and makes the one write.\n\n`units` is the one list the whole pipeline rides on. Each element is a typed unit carrying its display markdown, its spoken form, and its timing window, and that single list serves display, synthesis, and read-along alike (see [item-contract](item-contract.md) for the unit shape and the wire projection that drops `spoken`). Because there is one list rather than two parallel lists, a dropped unit takes its timing window with it instead of leaving one list longer than the other; [invariants](invariants.md) carries why that matters.\n\n`units` and `degradations` are JSON text columns. The row's TypeScript type and the functions that encode and decode its JSON columns live in [common/](common-package.md); the types are checked when each Worker builds, and a value read back from D1 is trusted to match its type.\n\nA long article's units come to a few hundred kilobytes (about 150 to 250 KB for a 70-minute article), far below D1's 10 MB limit for one value.\n\n`degradations` is a JSON list of typed objects the client never sees, written by the images and describe steps as each one records a loss. Each object carries a `type` and a short `reason`, and an acquisition failure also carries the origin `url`, the locator worth re-fetching once the unit is gone. `type` is the unit discriminator, so in practice only `image` and `code` appear: a paragraph cannot enrich, so it cannot degrade. The `reason` says both what happened and what nagara did about it:\n\n| `reason` | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| `404`, `timeout`, `undecodable` | acquisition failed, so the unit was dropped from the list |\n| `describe failed`, `describe cap reached` | the unit stays, but its spoken form fell back to alt text or the honest floor |\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a separate column and not `error`\n> `error` is failed-only by design, and that rule is worth keeping. So it cannot describe an item that reached `ready` after dropping six of twelve images: technically `ready`, quietly worse. `degradations` gives the operator a full record of what was lost while the client, reading only the wire fields, sees an item that looks whole.\n\nThe schema also has a `recipe_versions` table: one row per version of a domain's recipe, and an item points at the version that extracted it through `recipe_version_id`. The row's shape and why a revision is a pure insert are in [recipes](recipes.md).\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: audio as a database blob, or proxied through the API\n> Storing audio bytes in the database bloats the row store with multi-megabyte blobs; proxying them through the API routes large downloads through the Worker, and a headerless `<audio>` element cannot follow a credential-guarded byte stream anyway. A separate store reached through a signed link avoids both.\n\n## 🗃️ How the schema changes\n\nThe schema is a set of hand-written SQL migrations in [common/](common-package.md). common/'s own CI job applies them to D1 with wrangler before the pipeline Worker and the API Worker deploy, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for that job to succeed, so neither Worker ever runs against a schema older than its code (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)). Deploys are not made twice in quick succession, so two migration runs never overlap. Locally, the developer applies the same migrations with wrangler to the D1 database in the shared state folder (see [local-development](local-development.md)), and the Workers' tests build their D1 schema by applying them too.\n\n## 🏗️ Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    api@{ shape: rect, label: \"API Worker\" }\n    pipe@{ shape: rect, label: \"pipeline Worker\" }\n    modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n    d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n    subgraph bucket [\"R2 bucket\"]\n        direction TB\n        audio@{ shape: cyl, label: \"audio/<br/>keyed by item id\" }\n        images@{ shape: cyl, label: \"images/<br/>keyed by content hash\" }\n    end\n    api -->|\"item row at enqueue,<br/>retry, reads\"| d1\n    pipe -->|\"status, finished state,<br/>ledger, recipes\"| d1\n    pipe -->|\"stores images\"| images\n    modal -->|\"PUT to a URL the<br/>pipeline signs\"| audio\n    api -.->|\"signs GET URLs\"| bucket\n```\n\nBoth Workers bind the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket. The pipeline Worker writes images into the bucket directly through its binding. Modal writes the audio from outside Cloudflare, so it uploads to a presigned PUT URL the pipeline Worker signs with an R2 API token of its own; the API Worker signs GET URLs for clients with a separate R2 API token. The key layout and the presigning function both come from [common/](common-package.md):\n\n| Piece | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| audio prefix and key function | the object key for an item's audio, built from the item id alone |\n| images prefix and key function | the object key for an image, built from its content hash alone |\n| presigning | a GET or PUT URL for one key, signed with the R2 token and lifetime the caller passes |\n\n> [!NOTE] Why audio is keyed by the item id\n> Two items never share an audio file, even for the same article and voice. Modal accepts no idempotency key, so a start step that runs twice after a deploy synthesizes twice; both uploads land on the same key and the second overwrites the first.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a content hash keys an image, not the item id or the origin URL\n> Hashing the stored bytes dedupes for free: across items, across re-enqueues of the same article, and across a repeat of the same image within one article, which happens whenever `og:image` is echoed in the body. The format is verified by decoding through the Images binding rather than trusting `Content-Type` (wrong or missing on most image CDNs), and the WebP re-encode collapses source-format variants of one raster to a single stored object. An SVG is stored as SVG with its scripts stripped, and its content type is stored on the object, so the key carries no extension.\n\nLocally, `wrangler dev` and `vite dev` back the same bindings with a D1 database and an R2 bucket on disk in one shared state folder, and Modal reaches the local bucket through a tunnel; [local-development](local-development.md) carries how.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why configuration and not an environment name\n> \"Which backend\" is expressed as configuration a deployment supplies (the bindings, the R2 endpoint a URL is signed for, a set of credentials), never as an `if production` or `if testing` branch in runtime code. The same code path runs everywhere; only the supplied configuration differs, so there is no production-only path the test suite never exercises because a flag hid it.\n\n## 🔗 Serving audio and an image\n\nBoth media routes mint the response at request time and persist nothing. Each returns a 307 to a GET URL the API Worker signs for one hour, in every environment. `GET /items/{id}/audio` serves the one audio file; `GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` serves one image by its content hash. The bucket serves that image by its hash alone, since the hash is deduped across items; the route confirms the item exists and leaves the hash-to-item association to [item-contract](item-contract.md). Both routes require the key, so the auth invariant holds on every route that touches an item.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    R[\"GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}\"] --> K{\"key valid?\"}\n    K -->|no| E[\"401\"]\n    K -->|yes| I{\"item exists?\"}\n    I -->|no| N[\"404\"]\n    I -->|yes| P[\"307 to a URL<br/>signed for one hour\"]\n```\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a presigned URL is never written into the row\n> The unit list is persisted indefinitely, and a presigned URL is dead an hour after it is signed. So a unit stores the content hash and its display markdown embeds the route path; the signed URL is minted only when the route is called. Writing it into the row would persist a link that expires an hour later.\n\n## 🧾 The cost ledger\n\nEvery metered call writes one row to `cost_entries`, scoped to its `item_id`. The ledger records spend and enforces nothing: a budget or a rate cap is a separate concern.\n\n| Column | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `type` | the event kind: `firecrawl` / `describer` / `tts` |\n| `quantity` + `unit` | the raw measure: `credits`, `calls`, or `seconds` |\n| `dollars` | the cost, snapshotted from configured prices at write time |\n| `cached` | true when AI Gateway's response cache answered the call, false otherwise |\n| `detail` | nullable JSON: firecrawl destination and proxy, describer kind (`code`/`image`), tts duration |\n\n`type` and `unit` are constant maps in [common/](common-package.md) with their types derived from them, per the project's no-enum rule. Prices are configuration per invariant 6, each a plan- or vendor-dependent estimate. The Flue agents' model calls write no ledger row.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why both the raw measure and the dollars\n> The raw measure never goes stale and re-prices against future rates; the dollar snapshot gives an instant total without joining a price table. Keeping only one loses one of those, so each row carries both.\n\nEach item Workflow step that makes a metered call writes its own ledger row:\n\n| Event | Measured from | Written by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `firecrawl` | `creditsUsed` off the scrape response | the fetch step |\n| `describer` | one row per successful call, tagged by kind | the describe step, one row per child's call |\n| `tts` | the synthesized audio duration | the store step |\n\nA describe answered from AI Gateway's cache writes a row at the same flat price as one that reached Gemini, with `cached` set; the flag tells the two apart.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why each metered call writes its own row\n> A firecrawl scrape and a describer call are billed the moment they return, whether or not the item goes on to reach `ready`. So each step records its metered fact as it happens rather than waiting for the finished state, and a scrape is recorded even when the page turns out not to be an article.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\nAudio caching by `(url, voice)`: identical audio is regenerated today rather than reused.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [recipes](recipes.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Item contract\"\nsummary: \"The item routes plus /health, the item JSON, and the typed display unit whose spoken form is projected out at the response boundary.\"\ncreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n---\n\n# Item contract\n\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nThe HTTP routes and JSON a client (Tachikoma today; the read-along player, not yet built in `web/`, later) reads and writes. The item routes all sit behind the auth guard ([authentication](authentication.md)), plus one public liveness route.\n\n\n## ♠️ What it exposes\n\nAn `ItemResponse`, its `units` list carrying one `UnitResponse` per read-along window once timed:\n\n| Field | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| `id`, `url`, `title` | which item this is, and the article it points at |\n| `status` | `queued`, `generating`, `ready`, or `failed`: the state machine in [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) |\n| `voice` | which Kokoro voice this item's audio uses, fixed at creation |\n| `created_at` | when it was enqueued |\n| `duration` | total audio length once `ready`; absent until then |\n| `units[]` | the read-along list, one element per display unit; `null` until every window is timed, see below |\n| `error` | populated only when `status` is `failed` |\n| `audio_url` (computed, not stored) | present only once `ready`, so a client never has to check status before deciding whether to trust the link |\n\nA display unit is a discriminated union on `type`, one of `paragraph`, `code`, or `image`, declared as a TypeScript type in [common/](common-package.md) so the API Worker and the pipeline Worker build against the same shape; the type is checked when each Worker builds, not at runtime. Each variant carries its rendered markdown in `display`, its `type`, and an internal `spoken` form; an image unit adds `image`, a content hash. The persisted unit holds all of that; the wire element drops `spoken` and adds the timing window:\n\n| Shape | Carries |\n|---|---|\n| persisted `Unit` | `type`, `display`, `spoken`, plus `image` on an image unit |\n| wire `UnitResponse` | `index`, `type`, `display`, `start`, `end`, plus `image` on an image unit |\n\n`units` is `null` until every window is timed, and carries the complete timed list from `ready` onward.\n\n> [!NOTE] The spoken form never reaches a client\n> `display` and `spoken` come from one markdown segmentation on one unit; the spoken form is a synthesis detail, joined onto the timing at finalize and then filtered out by the response model rather than exposed (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)). Projecting it out at the response boundary is the mechanism behind invariant 1's oldest clause: no client ever reads the spoken text, and the display form is never synthesized. A caption-export route that needs the spoken form gets its own field then; no consumer needs it today.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the list is held back until it is timed\n> A wire element carries `start` and `end`, and timing only exists once synthesis finishes. The item Workflow's recipe step writes the units into the row with `type`/`display`/`spoken` but no window, and the images and describe steps rewrite them, so a partial, untimed list sits on the row while the item is `queued` or `generating`. Returning `null` until the item is `ready` keeps that partial state off the wire, so a client never renders half an article. Nothing is lost: a client polling a not-yet-`ready` item has no timeline to render against anyway.\n\n## 🛣️ What each route does\n\n| Route | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `POST /items` | Create an item from `{url, voice?}`. Writes the item row at `queued`, then creates the item's Workflow instance with the item id, and returns `202` with the item at `queued`; the item Workflow does the rest (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). |\n| `GET /items/{id}` | Poll. Reads the item row and returns the current item; it never reads the Workflow instance. Queues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. `404` for an unknown id. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/audio` | Serve audio for a `ready` item: a `307` to a GET URL for the item's audio key in the R2 bucket, signed for one hour (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). `404` if the item is not `ready`, has no audio format yet, or is unknown: no link is ever minted for a non-ready item. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` | Serve an image for the item, keyed by the content hash carried on an image unit. A `307` to a GET URL for the hash's key in the R2 bucket, signed fresh at read time for one hour. `404` for an unknown item. An unknown *hash* gets the `307` too, and R2 answers the signed URL with its own `404`; see below. Behind the same key as everything else. |\n| `POST /items/{id}/retry` | Re-drive a `failed` item in place, restarting its Workflow instance from the step that failed. `202` with the item back at `queued`; `409` unless the item is `failed` and under the retry cap, `404` for an unknown id. See the section below and [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md). |\n| `GET /health` | The one unauthenticated route; carries no item data. |\n\nVoice selection: an enqueue request that names a voice uses it; one that omits it gets a voice chosen at random from a curated pool at creation time, recorded on the item, and stable across every later poll.\n\nThe `url` string reaches the pipeline as given: nothing at the route or the schema validates it, and the first thing that can refuse it is the item Workflow's fetch step, which fails the item when Firecrawl returns nothing at all; the item's `error` is the fetch step's own error and `current_step` names `fetch`.\n\n## 🖼️ Image serving\n\n`GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` serves an image that belongs to the article. An image unit carries the content hash in its `image` field; a client reconstructs the path from the item id and the hash. The route requires the key, so invariant 4 holds uniformly.\n\n> [!NOTE] The URL is minted at read time, never persisted\n> A presigned URL written into the row would be dead an hour after it is signed, and the unit list is persisted indefinitely. The API Worker signs a fresh URL on every request with its own R2 API token, building the object key from the hash through the key function in [common/](common-package.md). The image object is keyed by its content hash, not the item id or the origin URL, so one image is stored once and dedupes across items and re-enqueues.\n\nAn unknown hash is the one miss the API does not answer itself. The API Worker signs the URL without asking whether the object is there, so the client gets a `307` to a real signed URL that then answers `404` in R2's own format.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the route does not check the object first\n> Answering the API's own `404` means a `HEAD` per request, and this route is hot in a way the audio one is not: audio is one file per item fetched once, images are many per item fetched on every render. A round-trip per image to convert an R2 `404` into an API `404` is a poor trade when both already say not-found.\n>\n> The audio route has the same shape and does not have the same exposure, because it gates on `ready` and a recorded audio format first, so a missing object there is a genuine anomaly rather than a routine miss. Image serving has no equivalent gate on purpose, per the association warning below.\n>\n> A caller that needs one uniform shape should treat any non-`200` from this route as absent rather than reading the body.\n\n> [!WARNING] The item lookup checks existence, not association\n> The route 404s an unknown item id but never checks that the hash belongs to that item, because the object is keyed by content hash alone so one image stores once and dedupes across items. Under today's single shared key that is not an escalation: there is nothing to reach that the key does not already grant. Per-key quota and API-key management are not built yet; once they are, the item id in the path is decoration and one key can read another key's images by hash, so whoever builds per-key auth must either check the hash against the item's own units or accept that images are shared across keys.\n\n## 🔁 Retry\n\n`POST /items/{id}/retry` re-drives a failed item in place rather than re-enqueuing the URL. It refuses anything but a `failed` item, and a `failed` item past the retry cap, with `409`; `404` for an unknown id. An accepted retry writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at`, increments `retry_count` and clears the old error in one conditional write, then restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`. The steps before it do not run again. A retry whose failed step is `recipe` or `release` restarts from `lease` instead, so the item asks for its domain's lease again and the recipe step reads the domain's current recipe. The Workflow moves the item to `generating` once it holds the lease, as on its first run; [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) carries the steps and what each one costs.\n\n> [!NOTE] Double-submitting is refused by the write, not by the read\n> The transition is one conditional `UPDATE` gating on status still `failed` and `retry_count` under the cap, incrementing the count in SQL. A rowcount of zero is the `409`. Reading the row first and then writing would let two concurrent retries both pass the guard, which restarts one instance twice and increments the count once, so the cap would read tighter than it is. The pre-read only picks which refusal message to send.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the cap is local and quota is not\n> The per-item retry cap (`retry_max`, default 3) is a cheap local bound on the worst case: a flapping item spends at most a few synthesises before it stays failed. Broader per-key and quota enforcement is deliberately not built here. The cap does not need the full hardening apparatus, and building half of one here would be worse than either.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n- **Quota and API-key management.** No enforcement and no create/revoke route exist yet.\n- **A list endpoint.** There is no `GET /items` today; a client tracks ids itself.\n- **Word-level timing.** One window per unit, not per word, by design; see [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md).\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) · [authentication](authentication.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [queue](../product-design/queue.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Local development\"\nsummary: \"How the API Worker and the pipeline Worker run locally on one shared state folder, call the real Firecrawl, AI Gateway and Modal, and receive Modal's audio upload through a quick tunnel and a local proxy.\"\ncreated: \"2026-10-11\"\n---\n\n# Local development\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nLocally, the pipeline Worker runs under vite dev and the API Worker under its own wrangler dev, and both keep their state in one shared folder, so they see the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket on disk. The Workers call the real Firecrawl, the real AI Gateway for Gemini and the Flue agents, and the same Modal app production uses. Modal uploads the audio into the local bucket through a Cloudflare quick tunnel and a small local Node proxy.\n\n## 🏗️ Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph laptop [\"Developer machine\"]\n    direction TB\n    api@{ shape: rect, label: \"API Worker<br/>(wrangler dev)\" }\n    pipe@{ shape: rect, label: \"pipeline Worker<br/>(vite dev)\" }\n    state@{ shape: cyl, label: \"Shared state folder:<br/>D1 and R2 on disk\" }\n    s3@{ shape: rect, label: \"Local S3 endpoint<br/>/cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/\" }\n    proxy@{ shape: rect, label: \"Node proxy\" }\n  end\n  tunnel@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Cloudflare<br/>quick tunnel\" }\n  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n  ext@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Firecrawl,<br/>AI Gateway\" }\n  api -->|\"create, restart\"| pipe\n  api --- state\n  pipe --- state\n  pipe -->|\"scrape, describe,<br/>agents\"| ext\n  pipe -->|\"start: PUT URL with<br/>the tunnel's hostname\"| modal\n  modal -->|\"PUT /s3/bucket/key\"| tunnel\n  tunnel --> proxy\n  proxy -->|\"rewrites to<br/>/cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/\"| s3\n  s3 --- state\n  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n  classDef tool fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n  class api worker\n  class pipe flow\n  class state store\n  class tunnel,modal,ext ext\n  class s3,proxy tool\n```\n\nThe API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow through the same cross-script Workflow binding it uses in production; the two dev servers find each other locally. Both Workers point at the same state folder, so D1, R2 and the Durable Objects are one set of files on disk. Locally, D1's schema comes from common/'s migrations, applied with wrangler against that folder (see [the common/ package](common-package.md)).\n\n> [!WARNING] A Worker that does not share the state folder sees an empty database\n> Each dev server keeps its state under its own config directory unless it is pointed at the shared folder. A Worker started without it finds no tables and an empty bucket.\n\n## 📤 How Modal's audio reaches the local bucket\n\nModal runs in the cloud and uploads the audio to a presigned PUT URL, as in production. Locally that URL points at wrangler's experimental local S3 endpoint, which the pipeline Worker's dev server serves at /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/ and which checks the URL's signature.\n\n1. The pipeline Worker signs the PUT URL for localhost with the same presigning function it uses against R2 in production (see [the common/ package](common-package.md)).\n2. It hands that URL to Modal with the quick tunnel's hostname, which it reads from its configuration, in place of localhost and the path /s3/<bucket>/<key>.\n3. Modal PUTs the audio to the tunnel, which forwards it to the Node proxy.\n4. The proxy maps /s3/<bucket>/<key> to /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/<bucket>/<key> with localhost as the host, so the signature matches, and the local endpoint writes the object into the shared state folder.\n\nThe API Worker then reads the audio through its own R2 binding, from the same folder.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a proxy sits behind the tunnel\n> Cloudflare's edge answers every /cdn-cgi/ path on trycloudflare.com itself, so a request for /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/ never reaches the dev server. The proxy gives the tunnel a path outside /cdn-cgi/ and restores the endpoint's path and host locally.\n\n> [!WARNING] The tunnel's hostname can change on every start\n> A quick tunnel gets a new random hostname each time it starts. The pipeline Worker reads the hostname from its configuration, so the configuration has to name the tunnel that is running; an upload to an old hostname never reaches the local bucket.\n\n## 🔁 Re-queuing a stuck item\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running gets stuck in queued: its Workflow instance never starts, or ends in error, and it stays that way once the pipeline Worker comes up. The API re-queues it the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes: when it reads the item, it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n> [!WARNING] A dev-server reload strands the running Workflow instance\n> An edit reloads the dev server, and a Workflow instance that is running during the reload never resumes: its item stays in the status it had. An item stranded in queued is re-queued once its queued_at is more than 15 minutes old.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [the common/ package](common-package.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"62dde8ef-0759-4af9-991c-fa88440a3611","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:48:06.010Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Persistence and storage\n\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nWhat the item row persists, where its audio and images live, and the ledger that records what each item cost. One D1 database holds every row and one R2 bucket holds every object; the API Worker and the pipeline Worker bind both. Both Workers build the row's shape, the R2 key layout and the URL presigning from [the common/ package](common-package.md), so the two Workers agree on every column and every key.\n\n\n## 💽 Modeling\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    ITEMS }o--o| RECIPE_VERSIONS : \"extracted by\"\n    ITEMS ||--o{ COST_ENTRIES : \"costs\"\n    ITEMS {\n        string id PK\n        string status\n        string current_step \"nullable\"\n        string workflow_instance_id \"nullable\"\n        json units \"nullable\"\n        json degradations\n        datetime queued_at\n        datetime updated_at\n    }\n    COST_ENTRIES {\n        string item_id FK\n        string type\n        number quantity\n        string unit\n        number dollars\n        boolean cached\n        json detail \"nullable\"\n    }\n```\n\nThe database has three tables: `items`, one row per enqueued article; `recipe_versions`, one row per version of a domain's recipe; and `cost_entries`, one row per metered call. The item row's full field list is in [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md); the diagram shows the fields this note is about. Audio bytes are never a column; they live in the bucket below, keyed by item id, so a multi-megabyte file never bloats the row.\n\nThe row always holds the item's status. The API Worker writes `queued` at enqueue; the item Workflow's step wrapper writes every later status together with `current_step` as each step starts. The Workflow writes the finished state when it ends, and a failed item's `error` is the failing step's own error, with `current_step` naming that step. `workflow_instance_id` names the item's Workflow instance, which the retry route restarts; `updated_at` records the last write. `queued_at` is set at enqueue and rewritten whenever the item is queued again: when the API reads a `queued` item whose `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. The poll reads only the row and never the instance, so an item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the instance at the end of its retention window. Both writers go through the status write in [common/](common-package.md), which sets a status, clears the error and stamps `updated_at`.\n\nThe Workflow writes `units` and `degradations` into the row as soon as a step produces them, and later steps read them back from the row. The recipe step writes the title together with the units, which carry their display and spoken forms; the images step rewrites them, and the describe step writes every described unit's spoken form, with its degradations and ledger rows, in one write at its end. Its child steps, one per description and run concurrently, only return their results to it. The API returns the units to a client only once the item is `ready`.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the describe children write nothing to the row\n> Up to 25 children run at once. A child that read the list, changed its unit and wrote the whole list back would overwrite the results its siblings wrote in between, so the parent collects every result and makes the one write.\n\n`units` is the one list the whole pipeline rides on. Each element is a typed unit carrying its display markdown, its spoken form, and its timing window, and that single list serves display, synthesis, and read-along alike (see [item-contract](item-contract.md) for the unit shape and the wire projection that drops `spoken`). Because there is one list rather than two parallel lists, a dropped unit takes its timing window with it instead of leaving one list longer than the other; [invariants](invariants.md) carries why that matters.\n\n`units` and `degradations` are JSON text columns. The row's TypeScript type and the functions that encode and decode its JSON columns live in [common/](common-package.md); the types are checked when each Worker builds, and a value read back from D1 is trusted to match its type.\n\nA long article's units come to a few hundred kilobytes (about 150 to 250 KB for a 70-minute article), far below D1's 10 MB limit for one value.\n\n`degradations` is a JSON list of typed objects the client never sees, written by the images and describe steps as each one records a loss. Each object carries a `type` and a short `reason`, and an acquisition failure also carries the origin `url`, the locator worth re-fetching once the unit is gone. `type` is the unit discriminator, so in practice only `image` and `code` appear: a paragraph cannot enrich, so it cannot degrade. The `reason` says both what happened and what nagara did about it:\n\n| `reason` | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| `404`, `timeout`, `undecodable` | acquisition failed, so the unit was dropped from the list |\n| `describe failed`, `describe cap reached` | the unit stays, but its spoken form fell back to alt text or the honest floor |\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a separate column and not `error`\n> `error` is failed-only by design, and that rule is worth keeping. So it cannot describe an item that reached `ready` after dropping six of twelve images: technically `ready`, quietly worse. `degradations` gives the operator a full record of what was lost while the client, reading only the wire fields, sees an item that looks whole.\n\nThe schema also has a `recipe_versions` table: one row per version of a domain's recipe, and an item points at the version that extracted it through `recipe_version_id`. The row's shape and why a revision is a pure insert are in [recipes](recipes.md).\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: audio as a database blob, or proxied through the API\n> Storing audio bytes in the database bloats the row store with multi-megabyte blobs; proxying them through the API routes large downloads through the Worker, and a headerless `<audio>` element cannot follow a credential-guarded byte stream anyway. A separate store reached through a signed link avoids both.\n\n## 🗃️ How the schema changes\n\nThe schema is a set of hand-written SQL migrations in [common/](common-package.md). common/'s own CI job applies them to D1 with wrangler before the pipeline Worker and the API Worker deploy, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for that job to succeed, so neither Worker ever runs against a schema older than its code (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)). Deploys are not made twice in quick succession, so two migration runs never overlap. Locally, the developer applies the same migrations with wrangler to the D1 database in the shared state folder (see [local-development](local-development.md)), and the Workers' tests build their D1 schema by applying them too.\n\n## 🏗️ Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    api@{ shape: rect, label: \"API Worker\" }\n    pipe@{ shape: rect, label: \"pipeline Worker\" }\n    modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n    d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n    subgraph bucket [\"R2 bucket\"]\n        direction TB\n        audio@{ shape: cyl, label: \"audio/<br/>keyed by item id\" }\n        images@{ shape: cyl, label: \"images/<br/>keyed by content hash\" }\n    end\n    api -->|\"item row at enqueue,<br/>retry, reads\"| d1\n    pipe -->|\"status, finished state,<br/>ledger, recipes\"| d1\n    pipe -->|\"stores images\"| images\n    modal -->|\"PUT to a URL the<br/>pipeline signs\"| audio\n    api -.->|\"signs GET URLs\"| bucket\n```\n\nBoth Workers bind the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket. The pipeline Worker writes images into the bucket directly through its binding. Modal writes the audio from outside Cloudflare, so it uploads to a presigned PUT URL the pipeline Worker signs with an R2 API token of its own; the API Worker signs GET URLs for clients with a separate R2 API token. The key layout and the presigning function both come from [common/](common-package.md):\n\n| Piece | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| audio prefix and key function | the object key for an item's audio, built from the item id alone |\n| images prefix and key function | the object key for an image, built from its content hash alone |\n| presigning | a GET or PUT URL for one key, signed with the R2 token and lifetime the caller passes |\n\n> [!NOTE] Why audio is keyed by the item id\n> Two items never share an audio file, even for the same article and voice. Modal accepts no idempotency key, so a start step that runs twice after a deploy synthesizes twice; both uploads land on the same key and the second overwrites the first.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a content hash keys an image, not the item id or the origin URL\n> Hashing the stored bytes dedupes for free: across items, across re-enqueues of the same article, and across a repeat of the same image within one article, which happens whenever `og:image` is echoed in the body. The format is verified by decoding through the Images binding rather than trusting `Content-Type` (wrong or missing on most image CDNs), and the WebP re-encode collapses source-format variants of one raster to a single stored object. An SVG is stored as SVG with its scripts stripped, and its content type is stored on the object, so the key carries no extension.\n\nLocally, `wrangler dev` and `vite dev` back the same bindings with a D1 database and an R2 bucket on disk in one shared state folder, and Modal reaches the local bucket through a tunnel; [local-development](local-development.md) carries how.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why configuration and not an environment name\n> \"Which backend\" is expressed as configuration a deployment supplies (the bindings, the R2 endpoint a URL is signed for, a set of credentials), never as an `if production` or `if testing` branch in runtime code. The same code path runs everywhere; only the supplied configuration differs, so there is no production-only path the test suite never exercises because a flag hid it.\n\n## 🔗 Serving audio and an image\n\nBoth media routes mint the response at request time and persist nothing. Each returns a 307 to a GET URL the API Worker signs for one hour, in every environment. `GET /items/{id}/audio` serves the one audio file; `GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` serves one image by its content hash. The bucket serves that image by its hash alone, since the hash is deduped across items; the route confirms the item exists and leaves the hash-to-item association to [item-contract](item-contract.md). Both routes require the key, so the auth invariant holds on every route that touches an item.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    R[\"GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}\"] --> K{\"key valid?\"}\n    K -->|no| E[\"401\"]\n    K -->|yes| I{\"item exists?\"}\n    I -->|no| N[\"404\"]\n    I -->|yes| P[\"307 to a URL<br/>signed for one hour\"]\n```\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a presigned URL is never written into the row\n> The unit list is persisted indefinitely, and a presigned URL is dead an hour after it is signed. So a unit stores the content hash and its display markdown embeds the route path; the signed URL is minted only when the route is called. Writing it into the row would persist a link that expires an hour later.\n\n## 🧾 The cost ledger\n\nEvery metered call writes one row to `cost_entries`, scoped to its `item_id`. The ledger records spend and enforces nothing: a budget or a rate cap is a separate concern.\n\n| Column | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `type` | the event kind: `firecrawl` / `describer` / `tts` |\n| `quantity` + `unit` | the raw measure: `credits`, `calls`, or `seconds` |\n| `dollars` | the cost, snapshotted from configured prices at write time |\n| `cached` | true when AI Gateway's response cache answered the call, false otherwise |\n| `detail` | nullable JSON: firecrawl destination and proxy, describer kind (`code`/`image`), tts duration |\n\n`type` and `unit` are constant maps in [common/](common-package.md) with their types derived from them, per the project's no-enum rule. Prices are configuration per invariant 6, each a plan- or vendor-dependent estimate. The Flue agents' model calls write no ledger row.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why both the raw measure and the dollars\n> The raw measure never goes stale and re-prices against future rates; the dollar snapshot gives an instant total without joining a price table. Keeping only one loses one of those, so each row carries both.\n\nEach item Workflow step that makes a metered call writes its own ledger row:\n\n| Event | Measured from | Written by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `firecrawl` | `creditsUsed` off the scrape response | the fetch step |\n| `describer` | one row per successful call, tagged by kind | the describe step, one row per child's call |\n| `tts` | the synthesized audio duration | the store step |\n\nA describe answered from AI Gateway's cache writes a row at the same flat price as one that reached Gemini, with `cached` set; the flag tells the two apart.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why each metered call writes its own row\n> A firecrawl scrape and a describer call are billed the moment they return, whether or not the item goes on to reach `ready`. So each step records its metered fact as it happens rather than waiting for the finished state, and a scrape is recorded even when the page turns out not to be an article.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\nAudio caching by `(url, voice)`: identical audio is regenerated today rather than reused.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [recipes](recipes.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Item contract\"\nsummary: \"The item routes plus /health, the item JSON, and the typed display unit whose spoken form is projected out at the response boundary.\"\ncreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n---\n\n# Item contract\n\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nThe HTTP routes and JSON a client (Tachikoma today; the read-along player, not yet built in `web/`, later) reads and writes. The item routes all sit behind the auth guard ([authentication](authentication.md)), plus one public liveness route.\n\n\n## ♠️ What it exposes\n\nAn `ItemResponse`, its `units` list carrying one `UnitResponse` per read-along window once timed:\n\n| Field | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| `id`, `url`, `title` | which item this is, and the article it points at |\n| `status` | `queued`, `generating`, `ready`, or `failed`: the state machine in [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) |\n| `voice` | which Kokoro voice this item's audio uses, fixed at creation |\n| `created_at` | when it was enqueued |\n| `duration` | total audio length once `ready`; absent until then |\n| `units[]` | the read-along list, one element per display unit; `null` until every window is timed, see below |\n| `error` | populated only when `status` is `failed` |\n| `audio_url` (computed, not stored) | present only once `ready`, so a client never has to check status before deciding whether to trust the link |\n\nA display unit is a discriminated union on `type`, one of `paragraph`, `code`, or `image`, declared as a TypeScript type in [common/](common-package.md) so the API Worker and the pipeline Worker build against the same shape; the type is checked when each Worker builds, not at runtime. Each variant carries its rendered markdown in `display`, its `type`, and an internal `spoken` form; an image unit adds `image`, a content hash. The persisted unit holds all of that; the wire element drops `spoken` and adds the timing window:\n\n| Shape | Carries |\n|---|---|\n| persisted `Unit` | `type`, `display`, `spoken`, plus `image` on an image unit |\n| wire `UnitResponse` | `index`, `type`, `display`, `start`, `end`, plus `image` on an image unit |\n\n`units` is `null` until every window is timed, and carries the complete timed list from `ready` onward.\n\n> [!NOTE] The spoken form never reaches a client\n> `display` and `spoken` come from one markdown segmentation on one unit; the spoken form is a synthesis detail, joined onto the timing at finalize and then filtered out by the response model rather than exposed (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)). Projecting it out at the response boundary is the mechanism behind invariant 1's oldest clause: no client ever reads the spoken text, and the display form is never synthesized. A caption-export route that needs the spoken form gets its own field then; no consumer needs it today.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the list is held back until it is timed\n> A wire element carries `start` and `end`, and timing only exists once synthesis finishes. The item Workflow's recipe step writes the units into the row with `type`/`display`/`spoken` but no window, and the images and describe steps rewrite them, so a partial, untimed list sits on the row while the item is `queued` or `generating`. Returning `null` until the item is `ready` keeps that partial state off the wire, so a client never renders half an article. Nothing is lost: a client polling a not-yet-`ready` item has no timeline to render against anyway.\n\n## 🛣️ What each route does\n\n| Route | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `POST /items` | Create an item from `{url, voice?}`. Writes the item row at `queued`, then creates the item's Workflow instance with the item id, and returns `202` with the item at `queued`; the item Workflow does the rest (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). |\n| `GET /items/{id}` | Poll. Reads the item row and returns the current item; it never reads the Workflow instance. Queues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. `404` for an unknown id. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/audio` | Serve audio for a `ready` item: a `307` to a GET URL for the item's audio key in the R2 bucket, signed for one hour (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). `404` if the item is not `ready`, has no audio format yet, or is unknown: no link is ever minted for a non-ready item. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` | Serve an image for the item, keyed by the content hash carried on an image unit. A `307` to a GET URL for the hash's key in the R2 bucket, signed fresh at read time for one hour. `404` for an unknown item. An unknown *hash* gets the `307` too, and R2 answers the signed URL with its own `404`; see below. Behind the same key as everything else. |\n| `POST /items/{id}/retry` | Re-drive a `failed` item in place, restarting its Workflow instance from the step that failed. `202` with the item back at `queued`; `409` unless the item is `failed` and under the retry cap, `404` for an unknown id. See the section below and [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md). |\n| `GET /health` | The one unauthenticated route; carries no item data. |\n\nVoice selection: an enqueue request that names a voice uses it; one that omits it gets a voice chosen at random from a curated pool at creation time, recorded on the item, and stable across every later poll.\n\nThe `url` string reaches the pipeline as given: nothing at the route or the schema validates it, and the first thing that can refuse it is the item Workflow's fetch step, which fails the item when Firecrawl returns nothing at all; the item's `error` is the fetch step's own error and `current_step` names `fetch`.\n\n## 🖼️ Image serving\n\n`GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` serves an image that belongs to the article. An image unit carries the content hash in its `image` field; a client reconstructs the path from the item id and the hash. The route requires the key, so invariant 4 holds uniformly.\n\n> [!NOTE] The URL is minted at read time, never persisted\n> A presigned URL written into the row would be dead an hour after it is signed, and the unit list is persisted indefinitely. The API Worker signs a fresh URL on every request with its own R2 API token, building the object key from the hash through the key function in [common/](common-package.md). The image object is keyed by its content hash, not the item id or the origin URL, so one image is stored once and dedupes across items and re-enqueues.\n\nAn unknown hash is the one miss the API does not answer itself. The API Worker signs the URL without asking whether the object is there, so the client gets a `307` to a real signed URL that then answers `404` in R2's own format.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the route does not check the object first\n> Answering the API's own `404` means a `HEAD` per request, and this route is hot in a way the audio one is not: audio is one file per item fetched once, images are many per item fetched on every render. A round-trip per image to convert an R2 `404` into an API `404` is a poor trade when both already say not-found.\n>\n> The audio route has the same shape and does not have the same exposure, because it gates on `ready` and a recorded audio format first, so a missing object there is a genuine anomaly rather than a routine miss. Image serving has no equivalent gate on purpose, per the association warning below.\n>\n> A caller that needs one uniform shape should treat any non-`200` from this route as absent rather than reading the body.\n\n> [!WARNING] The item lookup checks existence, not association\n> The route 404s an unknown item id but never checks that the hash belongs to that item, because the object is keyed by content hash alone so one image stores once and dedupes across items. Under today's single shared key that is not an escalation: there is nothing to reach that the key does not already grant. Per-key quota and API-key management are not built yet; once they are, the item id in the path is decoration and one key can read another key's images by hash, so whoever builds per-key auth must either check the hash against the item's own units or accept that images are shared across keys.\n\n## 🔁 Retry\n\n`POST /items/{id}/retry` re-drives a failed item in place rather than re-enqueuing the URL. It refuses anything but a `failed` item, and a `failed` item past the retry cap, with `409`; `404` for an unknown id. An accepted retry writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at`, increments `retry_count` and clears the old error in one conditional write, then restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`. The steps before it do not run again. A retry whose failed step is `recipe` or `release` restarts from `lease` instead, so the item asks for its domain's lease again and the recipe step reads the domain's current recipe. The Workflow moves the item to `generating` once it holds the lease, as on its first run; [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) carries the steps and what each one costs.\n\n> [!NOTE] Double-submitting is refused by the write, not by the read\n> The transition is one conditional `UPDATE` gating on status still `failed` and `retry_count` under the cap, incrementing the count in SQL. A rowcount of zero is the `409`. Reading the row first and then writing would let two concurrent retries both pass the guard, which restarts one instance twice and increments the count once, so the cap would read tighter than it is. The pre-read only picks which refusal message to send.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the cap is local and quota is not\n> The per-item retry cap (`retry_max`, default 3) is a cheap local bound on the worst case: a flapping item spends at most a few synthesises before it stays failed. Broader per-key and quota enforcement is deliberately not built here. The cap does not need the full hardening apparatus, and building half of one here would be worse than either.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n- **Quota and API-key management.** No enforcement and no create/revoke route exist yet.\n- **A list endpoint.** There is no `GET /items` today; a client tracks ids itself.\n- **Word-level timing.** One window per unit, not per word, by design; see [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md).\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) · [authentication](authentication.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [queue](../product-design/queue.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Local development\"\nsummary: \"How the API Worker and the pipeline Worker run locally on one shared state folder, call the real Firecrawl, AI Gateway and Modal, and receive Modal's audio upload through a quick tunnel and a local proxy.\"\ncreated: \"2026-10-11\"\n---\n\n# Local development\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nLocally, the pipeline Worker runs under vite dev and the API Worker under its own wrangler dev, and both keep their state in one shared folder, so they see the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket on disk. The Workers call the real Firecrawl, the real AI Gateway for Gemini and the Flue agents, and the same Modal app production uses. Modal uploads the audio into the local bucket through a Cloudflare quick tunnel and a small local Node proxy.\n\n## 🏗️ Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph laptop [\"Developer machine\"]\n    direction TB\n    api@{ shape: rect, label: \"API Worker<br/>(wrangler dev)\" }\n    pipe@{ shape: rect, label: \"pipeline Worker<br/>(vite dev)\" }\n    state@{ shape: cyl, label: \"Shared state folder:<br/>D1 and R2 on disk\" }\n    s3@{ shape: rect, label: \"Local S3 endpoint<br/>/cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/\" }\n    proxy@{ shape: rect, label: \"Node proxy\" }\n  end\n  tunnel@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Cloudflare<br/>quick tunnel\" }\n  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal TTS\" }\n  ext@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Firecrawl,<br/>AI Gateway\" }\n  api -->|\"create, restart\"| pipe\n  api --- state\n  pipe --- state\n  pipe -->|\"scrape, describe,<br/>agents\"| ext\n  pipe -->|\"start: PUT URL with<br/>the tunnel's hostname\"| modal\n  modal -->|\"PUT /s3/bucket/key\"| tunnel\n  tunnel --> proxy\n  proxy -->|\"rewrites to<br/>/cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/\"| s3\n  s3 --- state\n  classDef worker fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb\n  classDef flow fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n  classDef tool fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n  class api worker\n  class pipe flow\n  class state store\n  class tunnel,modal,ext ext\n  class s3,proxy tool\n```\n\nThe API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow through the same cross-script Workflow binding it uses in production; the two dev servers find each other locally. Both Workers point at the same state folder, so D1, R2 and the Durable Objects are one set of files on disk. Locally, D1's schema comes from common/'s migrations, applied with wrangler against that folder (see [the common/ package](common-package.md)).\n\n> [!WARNING] A Worker that does not share the state folder sees an empty database\n> Each dev server keeps its state under its own config directory unless it is pointed at the shared folder. A Worker started without it finds no tables and an empty bucket.\n\n## 📤 How Modal's audio reaches the local bucket\n\nModal runs in the cloud and uploads the audio to a presigned PUT URL, as in production. Locally that URL points at wrangler's experimental local S3 endpoint, which the pipeline Worker's dev server serves at /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/ and which checks the URL's signature.\n\n1. The pipeline Worker signs the PUT URL for localhost with the same presigning function it uses against R2 in production (see [the common/ package](common-package.md)).\n2. It hands that URL to Modal with the quick tunnel's hostname, which it reads from its configuration, in place of localhost and the path /s3/<bucket>/<key>.\n3. Modal PUTs the audio to the tunnel, which forwards it to the Node proxy.\n4. The proxy maps /s3/<bucket>/<key> to /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/<bucket>/<key> with localhost as the host, so the signature matches, and the local endpoint writes the object into the shared state folder.\n\nThe API Worker then reads the audio through its own R2 binding, from the same folder.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a proxy sits behind the tunnel\n> Cloudflare's edge answers every /cdn-cgi/ path on trycloudflare.com itself, so a request for /cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/ never reaches the dev server. The proxy gives the tunnel a path outside /cdn-cgi/ and restores the endpoint's path and host locally.\n\n> [!WARNING] The tunnel's hostname can change on every start\n> A quick tunnel gets a new random hostname each time it starts. The pipeline Worker reads the hostname from its configuration, so the configuration has to name the tunnel that is running; an upload to an old hostname never reaches the local bucket.\n\n## 🔁 Re-queuing a stuck item\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running gets stuck in queued: its Workflow instance never starts, or ends in error, and it stays that way once the pipeline Worker comes up. The API re-queues it the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes: when it reads the item, it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n> [!WARNING] A dev-server reload strands the running Workflow instance\n> An edit reloads the dev server, and a Workflow instance that is running during the reload never resumes: its item stays in the status it had. An item stranded in queued is re-queued once its queued_at is more than 15 minutes old.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [the common/ package](common-package.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7b579651-b780-4d9e-9dce-e0b458360976","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45402743-426f-4a70-ba80-380fef6d231e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"485c48d4-a22c-458a-8ec1-644fc5584bd2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bfa32ed9-babd-4bb0-92a6-0867b6b276d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LSt3g7EauAbYCfEjJC7EmL","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (34.1KB). Full output saved to: /home/agus/.claude/projects/-home-agus-workspace-asermax-nagara/f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e/tool-results/bhv7e6i1p.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# Deployment and CI\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nNagara ships as three deployables: the API Worker and the pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, and the TTS service on Modal. Each tree runs its own CI checks, path-filtered to its directory. The two Workers deploy with wrangler in a fixed order, after `common/`'s migrations are applied to D1; `tts/` deploys to Modal on its own.\n\n## ☁️ How the Workers deploy\n\nBoth Workers deploy with wrangler. Their configurations bind the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket, and the API Worker's configuration binds the item Workflow by the pipeline Worker's script name. `nagara.asermax.com` points at the API Worker, the only Worker clients reach.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the pipeline Worker has no public routes\n> The API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow through the cross-script Workflow binding, and nothing else calls the pipeline. With no route to reach, the pipeline Worker carries no authentication of its own and no edge policy guards it.\n\n`api/` deploys with wrangler from its own configuration, from within `api/` or via CI below; see [item-contract](item-contract.md) for the routes it serves.\n\n`pipeline/` deploys with `pnpm deploy`, from within `pipeline/` or via CI below; see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) for what runs on it. That script builds before it ships: `pnpm build` runs Vite with the Flue and Cloudflare plugins, writing the bundled Worker and a generated `wrangler.json` into `dist/nagara/`, and `wrangler deploy -c dist/nagara/wrangler.json` ships that generated config rather than the hand-written `pipeline/wrangler.jsonc`. The Flue plugin adds the author and revision agents' Durable Object bindings at build time, so the source config alone would ship a Worker without them.\n\n`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which its own CI workflow applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why migrations, then the pipeline, then the API\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"53248f3f-fada-45ce-b3da-b4db203e3605","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:48:06.091Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Deployment and CI\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nNagara ships as three deployables: the API Worker and the pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, and the TTS service on Modal. Each tree runs its own CI checks, path-filtered to its directory. The two Workers deploy with wrangler in a fixed order, after `common/`'s migrations are applied to D1; `tts/` deploys to Modal on its own.\n\n## ☁️ How the Workers deploy\n\nBoth Workers deploy with wrangler. Their configurations bind the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket, and the API Worker's configuration binds the item Workflow by the pipeline Worker's script name. `nagara.asermax.com` points at the API Worker, the only Worker clients reach.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the pipeline Worker has no public routes\n> The API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow through the cross-script Workflow binding, and nothing else calls the pipeline. With no route to reach, the pipeline Worker carries no authentication of its own and no edge policy guards it.\n\n`api/` deploys with wrangler from its own configuration, from within `api/` or via CI below; see [item-contract](item-contract.md) for the routes it serves.\n\n`pipeline/` deploys with `pnpm deploy`, from within `pipeline/` or via CI below; see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) for what runs on it. That script builds before it ships: `pnpm build` runs Vite with the Flue and Cloudflare plugins, writing the bundled Worker and a generated `wrangler.json` into `dist/nagara/`, and `wrangler deploy -c dist/nagara/wrangler.json` ships that generated config rather than the hand-written `pipeline/wrangler.jsonc`. The Flue plugin adds the author and revision agents' Durable Object bindings at build time, so the source config alone would ship a Worker without them.\n\n`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which its own CI workflow applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why migrations, then the pipeline, then the API\n> Both Workers read and write D1, so the schema they build against is applied before either deploys. The API Worker's Workflow binding fails with `workflow.not_found` until the pipeline Worker that holds the item Workflow is deployed, so the pipeline always goes before the API.\n\n## 🔐 Which secrets each Worker holds\n\nEach Worker holds only the secrets its own calls need.\n\n| Worker | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| API Worker | the API key, and an R2 API token it signs GET URLs with |\n| pipeline Worker | the Firecrawl and Gemini keys, the AI Gateway token, Modal's proxy auth token, and an R2 API token it signs PUT URLs with |\n\n## 🎙️ How the TTS service deploys\n\n`tts/` is pushed independently with `modal deploy`, from within `tts/` or via CI below; see [tts-service](tts-service.md) for what actually runs on Modal.\n\n## 🔁 CI: one path-filtered workflow per tree, deploys in a fixed order\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  push@{ shape: stadium, label: \"Push to main\" }\n  subgraph cw [\"common workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    cchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types,<br/>both Workers' types\" }\n    mig@{ shape: rect, label: \"1 apply common/<br/>migrations to D1\" }\n    cchk --> mig\n  end\n  subgraph pw [\"pipeline workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    pchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n    pdep@{ shape: rect, label: \"2 deploy the<br/>pipeline Worker\" }\n    pchk --> pdep\n  end\n  subgraph aw [\"api workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    achk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n    adep@{ shape: rect, label: \"3 deploy the<br/>API Worker\" }\n    achk --> adep\n  end\n  subgraph tw [\"tts workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    tchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n    tdep@{ shape: rect, label: \"deploy tts/<br/>to Modal\" }\n    tchk --> tdep\n  end\n  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n  cfw@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Cloudflare<br/>Workers\" }\n  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal\" }\n  push -->|\"common/\"| cchk\n  push -->|\"pipeline/\"| pchk\n  push -->|\"api/\"| achk\n  push -->|\"tts/\"| tchk\n  mig -.->|\"waits for\"| pdep\n  mig -.->|\"waits for\"| adep\n  pdep -.->|\"waits for\"| adep\n  mig -->|\"wrangler\"| d1\n  pdep -->|\"wrangler\"| cfw\n  adep -->|\"wrangler\"| cfw\n  tdep -->|\"modal deploy\"| modal\n  classDef step fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n  classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n  class cchk,mig,pchk,pdep,achk,adep,tchk,tdep step\n  class d1 store\n  class cfw,modal ext\n  class push person\n```\n\nEach tree runs **test, lint and types as three parallel jobs** on pushes to `main`, path-filtered to its own directory (`api/**`, `pipeline/**`, `common/**`, `tts/**`), so a docs-only push, or a change to one tree, runs neither another tree's checks nor its deploy. A change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/` against the changed package, inside `common/`'s workflow. The `api`, `pipeline` and `common` jobs install with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` on Node 24 and run `pnpm test`, `pnpm lint` and `pnpm types`; the `tts` jobs provision the pinned toolchain (uv/ruff/ty/pytest, see the repository's `CLAUDE.md`) with `uv sync --frozen` against `tts/`'s lockfile.\n\nThree workflows ship the Cloudflare side with `cloudflare/wrangler-action`, each after its own checks pass: `common/`'s workflow applies its SQL migrations to D1, the pipeline's workflow runs `pnpm build` and deploys `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, and the API's workflow deploys the API Worker. The pipeline and API workflows wait for `common/`'s workflow to succeed before they deploy, and the API deploys after the pipeline. When a check in `common/`'s workflow fails, nothing deploys: no migration, no pipeline deploy and no API deploy. The three authenticate with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`. `tts` adds a **`deploy` job that depends on all three of its checks**, runs `modal deploy` and authenticates with `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` / `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. All four are GitHub Actions secrets.\n\n> [!WARNING] Two migration runs at once are not guarded\n> Nothing serializes two runs of `common/`'s workflow, so two pushes to `main` in quick succession can apply `common/`'s migrations twice at the same time. The chain relies on deploys never being made twice in quick succession.\n\nThe `tts` check jobs install the full dev dependency group (torch-cpu, kokoro, numpy) because `ty` needs them to resolve `app.py`'s image-runtime imports locally; the deploy job installs only the Modal client (`--no-dev`). uv's lockfile-keyed cache absorbs most of the repeat install cost.\n\n## 📦 What binaries the repository accepts\n\nAudio and images are handled differently.\n\n**Audio is ignored outright.** `.gitignore` covers `*.ogg`, `*.wav`, `*.mp3`, `*.m4a`, `*.opus` and `*.flac`, so no audio file can be staged by accident. Every audio file here is output of the TTS pipeline: regenerable for roughly $0.008 an article (see [tts-service](tts-service.md)) and megabytes each, so there is no audio artifact worth the space, and LFS would only make an unwanted commit cheaper rather than preventing it. A test that needs audio generates it into a working directory the ignore already covers.\n\n**Images go through Git LFS.** `.gitattributes` routes `*.png`, `*.jpg`, `*.jpeg` and `*.webp` as LFS pointers, because `web/` will carry real assets that have to be checked in, and a pointer keeps the bytes out of the packfile every clone downloads.\n\n> [!WARNING] A binary committed as a plain blob is permanent until someone rewrites history\n> Deleting the file in a later commit removes it from the working tree and leaves the blob in every clone at full size. Two Opus fixtures, 9.35 MB together, reached pushed history this way, and getting them back out took a `filter-repo` rewrite and a force push over a shared branch: `.git` dropped from 12 MB to 672 KB, but the remote still serves each unreachable blob by hash afterwards, because rewriting history does not reach objects already distributed to other clones and caches.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n`web/` and `api/` are served from separate hosts today; serving them from one origin once `web/` exists is not built.\n\nA Worker deploy that lands while item Workflow instances are running is not handled; a deploy can run a step again, and the item Workflow accepts that for synthesis (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)).\n\n---\n\nRelated: [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [authentication](authentication.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"TTS service\"\nsummary: \"Kokoro-82M on Modal, a separate deployable the item Workflow reaches over two web endpoints behind Modal's proxy auth: start a synthesis that uploads its audio to a presigned R2 URL, then poll for its timing.\"\ncreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n---\n\n# TTS service\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nRenders an item's spoken paragraphs to audio on a GPU host, uploads the audio to the R2 bucket and produces their read-along timing. Lives at `tts/app.py`, deployed as its own Modal app (`nagara-tts`), and is invoked by the item Workflow over HTTP rather than imported. Production and local development call the same Modal app.\n\n> [!NOTE] The TTS code is an image definition, not a callable library\n> The synthesis code *is* the specification of the container image that runs on the GPU host: it declares its own base image, system packages, and model bake step, and is uploaded as a unit. It cannot execute inside a Worker; the item Workflow can only invoke it as a separately deployed service.\n\n## ♠️ What it exposes\n\nTwo web endpoints, both behind Modal's proxy auth, so every request carries the proxy auth headers:\n\n| Endpoint | Takes | Answers |\n|---|---|---|\n| start synthesis | the spoken forms, one string per read-along unit in unit order; the Kokoro voice, defaulting to `af_heart`; the destination URL, a presigned R2 PUT URL for the item's audio key | Modal's call id for this synthesis, at once |\n| fetch result | the call id | not ready while the synthesis runs; once it is done, the result below |\n\n| Result field | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| format | which codec the audio is: `audio/ogg`, or `audio/wav` on the ffmpeg fallback |\n| duration | the total audio length in seconds, what a progress bar divides by |\n| timings | one `{start, end}` window per unit, in seconds, the last `end` equal to the duration; see [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) |\n\nThe result carries no audio bytes, because the audio is already in the bucket under the item's audio key. The pipeline Worker declares the result's shape as a TypeScript type and trusts Modal's reply to match it; nothing checks it at runtime (see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rule the shared types follow).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why Modal uploads the audio instead of returning it\n> A Workflow step can return about 1 MiB, and a full article's audio is far larger. Handing Modal a presigned PUT URL keeps the audio out of the result, so the result endpoint answers with timing and metadata alone. The pipeline Worker signs the URL with an R2 API token of its own, using common/'s presigning function and its audio key function (see [common-package](common-package.md)).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why proxy auth\n> Modal's proxy auth rejects a request at Modal's edge, before any container starts. A shared key the app checks itself works too, but the web container has to start before it can reject the request. The pipeline Worker holds the proxy auth token as a secret.\n\n## 📩 Flow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    P[\"paragraphs[]\"] --> S[\"synthesize each<br/>paragraph with Kokoro\"]\n    S --> A[\"concatenate audio<br/>+ 0.1s gaps\"]\n    S --> T[\"build_timeline:<br/>pause-fold windows\"]\n    A --> E[\"encode: Opus,<br/>WAV fallback\"]\n    E --> U[\"upload to the<br/>destination URL\"]\n    U --> R[\"result: timeline<br/>+ duration + format\"]\n    T --> R\n```\n\nEach paragraph is synthesized independently through Kokoro-82M and its duration recorded, then the audios are concatenated with a fixed 0.1-second silence gap between them: none after the last paragraph, since there is nothing left to separate it from. [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md)'s `build_timeline` turns those per-paragraph durations into contiguous windows over the same concatenated audio. The encoded audio is uploaded to the destination URL, and the result Modal keeps for the call carries the timeline and the duration; the item Workflow joins the timeline onto the display markdown, described in [article-extraction](article-extraction.md), and never reads text back from the result.\n\n> [!NOTE] A newline inside one paragraph string is Kokoro's own split, not this service's\n> Kokoro splits a paragraph string at each newline before synthesizing it and joins those pieces back with no gap between them; the 0.1-second silence only sits between two separate entries of the `paragraphs` list. So a unit whose spoken form carries several lines, such as a list's items or a table's rows (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)), plays as one continuous paragraph audio with no silence added between its lines. The contract does not change. `paragraphs` stays `list[str]`, one string per unit, whether that string carries one line or several.\n\nAudio is encoded to Opus through a `soundfile`-then-`ffmpeg` pipeline; if that codec path fails, it falls back to plain WAV. \"Playable audio\" is the only property either path is judged on, and WAV runs roughly 18 times larger than the equivalent Opus for a full article.\n\nGPU and memory snapshotting are both enabled on the deployed class, bringing cold start down from roughly 27 seconds to roughly 6; a container then stays warm for 300 seconds after its last call, to absorb a session's burst of pushes.\n\n## 📡 How the item Workflow invokes it with no broker\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant W as Item Workflow\n    participant M as Modal TTS\n    participant R as R2 bucket\n    W->>M: start synthesis with spoken forms, voice and PUT URL\n    M-->>W: call id\n    M->>R: PUT the encoded audio\n    loop until the result is ready\n        W->>M: fetch result for the call id\n        M-->>W: not ready, or timings, duration and format\n    end\n```\n\nThe item Workflow's start synthesis step signs the PUT URL and calls the start endpoint, which spawns the synthesis and answers with Modal's call id at once (1, 2). Modal uploads the audio to the bucket when the synthesis finishes (3). The poll step asks the result endpoint for the call id until the result is ready (4, 5), and the store step writes the finished state into the item row together with the tts ledger row, priced by the audio's length (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The call id and the result pass from step to step as step results; the item row holds neither. Each step gets 3 attempts: start synthesis has 2 minutes per attempt and each poll 1 minute.\n\nA synthesis that fails every time fails the item once the poll step has used its attempts, and so does a result Modal has discarded, which happens 7 days after the call.\n\n> [!WARNING] A start repeated after a deploy synthesizes twice\n> Modal accepts no idempotency key and no caller-chosen call id, so a start synthesis step that runs again after a deploy spawns a second synthesis. Both upload to the same audio key, keyed by the item id, so the second upload overwrites the first and the item still has one audio file. The cost is one extra synthesis, which is accepted.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the compute platform is the async layer, and there is no broker\n> The platform's own invocation primitives (spawn plus a non-blocking result read, exposed as the two endpoints) are sufficient async infrastructure at this scale: the running-versus-crashed distinction comes for free from the result read, not from anything the item Workflow tracks separately. The two deployables also ship independently: the TTS service ships on its own deploy cadence (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)), decoupled from the Workers.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: a broker and a worker process\n> A conventional task-queue stack, a message broker plus a separate worker, would add infrastructure to run, scale, and monitor for no benefit the platform's own spawn/poll primitives do not already provide, and it would still need the same crash/running bookkeeping the result read gives for free.\n\n> [!NOTE] Clients poll; nothing pushes completion\n> A client observes `generating` until the item Workflow writes `ready` or `failed` into the item row, and its next poll reads it. There is no notification path; see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) for the state machine this participates in.\n\nThe execution layer is coupled to Modal as a result: moving off it means replacing both the compute host and the async layer, and the GPU with them.\n\nIn local development Modal still runs in the cloud and uploads into the local bucket through a Cloudflare quick tunnel; [local-development](local-development.md) explains how the PUT URL reaches it.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\nStreaming paragraph audio from one warm container, so time-to-first-audio is roughly one to two seconds instead of waiting for the whole article. Word-level timestamps exist inside Kokoro but are not returned or used; a caption export would be the consumer that asks for them.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Authentication\"\nsummary: \"A single API key acts as the user's identity and guards every item route; audio and images reach a browser through a one-hour presigned R2 link the API Worker signs only for an authenticated caller.\"\ncreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n---\n\n# Authentication\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nItems are private: an article someone enqueues is theirs, not public. A single API key is the user's identity, and it guards every route that touches an item.\n\n## ⚙️ Implementation details\n\nThe API Worker checks the key once, in front of every route under `/items`, so no handler checks it individually; a missing or wrong key answers `401`. The key is a secret only the API Worker holds.\n\nPossessing the key **is** being the user: there is no separate identity, session, or login. The key is presented on an `X-API-Key` header and checked against the single configured value on every item route: creation, polling, retry, audio and images alike. `/health` is the one public route, and it carries no item data, a deliberate, narrow exception for platform health checks (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one key rather than sessions or OAuth, for now\n> The first consumer is an agent (Tachikoma), not a browser: it wants to present one credential on each call, not run an interactive login. There is no audience yet to onboard with real accounts; multi-user identity comes later, once anyone besides the owner wants this, and the private queue works for its first user without it.\n\n## 📩 How audio reaches a browser without the key\n\nThe audio and image routes require the key to **obtain** a link, and the audio route mints one only for a `ready` item. Each answers with a `307` to a GET URL for the object in the R2 bucket, signed by the API Worker with its own R2 API token for one hour, in every environment (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The URL itself carries the signature, so the request that follows it needs no header at all. The bucket stays private: every way to read it is a link minted to an authenticated caller, and those links expire.\n\nOnly a backend holds the API key. A web app's own backend calls the route, takes the presigned URL from the `307`, and hands that URL to the browser. The audio route accepts no key in the query string, so the key never reaches a page.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Backend as Client backend\n    participant API as API Worker\n    participant R2 as R2 bucket\n    participant Browser\n    Backend->>API: GET /items/id/audio with X-API-Key\n    API->>API: key valid and item ready?\n    API->>API: sign a GET URL for one hour\n    API-->>Backend: 307 to the presigned URL\n    Backend-->>Browser: the presigned URL\n    Browser->>R2: GET the presigned URL, no header\n    R2-->>Browser: audio bytes\n```\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the route mints a link instead of gating the bytes\n> A browser `<audio>` element cannot attach a custom header to the request it makes for the audio file, so the key alone cannot gate the byte stream. Gating the link that leads to the bytes is the check a browser can pass.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n**Multi-user is a rewrite of the guard.** Going multi-user means introducing genuine identity and per-user key management, with a Google OAuth login taking the place of the single-key shim rather than sitting beside it. See [the queue](../product-design/queue.md) for what a user sees once multiple keys and key management exist.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Image and code describer\"\nsummary: \"The Gemini describer, reached through AI Gateway with its response cache: one generated sentence for a block a listener can't see, reused by the code path and the image path, floored and capped against one shared per-item budget.\"\ncreated: \"2026-08-16\"\n---\n\n# Image and code describer\n\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nGives a listener one spoken sentence for a block they cannot see: a code block, or an image with no author-written text. It is a reusable core with two callers: the code path and the image path both reach the same Gemini call, layered under a fan-out that floors, caps, and fails per unit. It never speaks what a block *means*, only what it *is* or *shows*: meaning comes from the author, and the describer only makes the block visible. It runs as the item Workflow's describe step, which starts one child step per description (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)), and every call goes through AI Gateway with the gateway's response cache on.\n\n\n> [!NOTE] The describer is unchanged by the recipes architecture\n> Recipes stop at units ([recipes](recipes.md)); describing code and images stays this generic path, whatever extracted them.\n\n## ♠️ What it exposes\n\n| Member | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| the core call | one structured-output call plus the sanitize tail, returning the spoken sentence. The caller owns the contents: a prompt string, or a prompt plus the image as an inline part |\n| the code prompt | kind and what-for, no opener, the introducing unit as authority |\n| the image prompt | one specific sentence of what is shown, alt passed as unreliable context |\n| an image describe request | one image the precedence flagged for a describe (case 2): its index in the unit list, its raw alt, and the stored image, a WebP raster or the SVG's markup |\n| the fan-out | describe every code unit and every flagged image against one shared budget, in document order, returning the updated units and a degradation list |\n\n## 📞 The core call: structured output, then sanitize\n\nThe core call makes one call to `gemini-3.5-flash-lite` through AI Gateway, parses the JSON, and runs the value through the shared sanitize tail. The response schema is a JSON object with one required string field, `spoken`.\n\nThe response is requested as `application/json` against that schema, so the model returns `{\"spoken\": \"...\"}` and there is nothing to parse out of prose. The parsed `spoken` then passes through the sanitize tail article prose runs through, including the rule that replaces a marker only where it touches text and the rule that speaks a comparison operator as a word (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)).\n\nA raster goes to the model as the stored WebP image. An SVG goes as its markup, an inline part with the `text/plain` type, whatever its size.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why an SVG is described from its markup\n> The model reads text the markup carries, such as a formula's LaTeX in a `<title>` element, so it names an equation that a rasterised copy only lets it read out symbol by symbol. Every SVG tried this way came back with a usable description. The markup's token count grows with its size, from under a thousand tokens for a small badge to about 28 thousand for a 63 KB chart, and no cap bounds it.\n\n> [!NOTE] Both guards, because a leaked marker is stochastic and only ever caught by ear\n> Structured output makes one whole failure class impossible: the model cannot open with a heading or a preamble when the value is a bare string field. But a JSON schema cannot forbid a backtick or a `**` *inside* that string, and a marker reached narration exactly once in the bake-off and did not reproduce across 48 re-runs. \"It did not happen this time\" is the judgement a pipeline cannot rely on, so sanitize catches the marker the schema cannot.\n\n> [!NOTE] The schema is not repeated in the prompt\n> Gemini's own docs warn that duplicating the schema, or JSON examples, in the prompt lowers output quality. The config carries the shape; the prompt describes only the task.\n\n### 🗄️ The gateway's response cache\n\nAI Gateway answers an identical request from its response cache without calling Gemini, and reports the hit in the `cf-aig-cache-status` response header. A describe repeated with the same prompt and the same image, such as a code snippet two articles share, costs no model call. Each successful describe writes one ledger row at the flat describe price, whether Gemini or the cache answered, and sets the row's cached flag when the header reports a hit (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)).\n\n## ✍️ The prompts: no opener, an invention guard, minimal asymmetric context\n\nBoth prompts share three rules and differ only in subject.\n\n**No self-opener.** The sentence must begin with the thing itself, the kind-noun for code or the visible subject for an image, and the word \"This\" is forbidden anywhere in it. `Code: ` and `Image: ` are nagara's announcement, so the model owns only the content. An image noun-opens naturally; code needs the ban spelled out, because forbidding \"This block\" and \"This example\" alone leaves a residual \"This is a…\".\n\n**The invention guard** is a sharpened positive instruction with no few-shot. Code: name the kind and the purpose, never the mechanics, return values, or framework, unless the introducing text states them. Image: describe only what is visibly there, and **name no person, organization, brand, place, or region unless that exact name appears as written text inside the image**. The one confirmed invention in the bake-off was geography read onto an artwork, and no run under this guard reproduced it.\n\n**Context is minimal and asymmetric.** The code prompt gets the article title, the introducing paragraph (the unit just before, which the listener heard seconds ago as authority), and the block. The paragraph *after* is dropped. The image prompt gets the title and the alt, labelled as often wrong or SEO filler, to use only where it matches the pixels.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the paragraph after the block is dropped\n> It is the elaboration the listener is about to hear anyway, and including it nudges the model toward describing behaviour rather than naming what the block is.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one sentence per code block, even when the prose already named it\n> Every code block gets its own spoken sentence because it needs its own audio window in the read-along, and dropping it would remove the code from a reader's view. The redundancy with the introducing prose is the price of that window. What the sentence adds above restatement is the *kind*: \"the syntax for creating a variable\" names the concept; the specific field names and query values that would add most are exactly what the guard forbids as invention.\n\n## 🗣️ What each unit says\n\nA **code** unit's spoken form becomes `Code: <sentence>`, overwriting the interim `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder from the spoken-form derivation.\n\nAn **image** follows a four-step precedence, top to bottom. Cases 1, 3, and 4 are the fallback already on the unit from acquisition (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)); the describer owns case 2 and the filter that routes between 1 and 2.\n\n| # | Condition | Spoken form |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | good alt | the alt, verbatim |\n| 2 | no good alt | **one generated sentence of what the image shows** |\n| 3 | generation failed, alt non-empty | any alt, verbatim |\n| 4 | generation failed, alt empty | `Image with no description.` |\n\nThe **good-alt filter** decides case 1 against case 2. Alt is trusted verbatim only when it reads as a grammatical sentence, is not the article title (the cruft test in [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)), and clears a small CMS denylist (`subscribe`, `appears in`, `courtesy`, `photograph by`, `click`) and filename patterns. Everything else goes to the describer: empty, keyword soup, title-as-alt, a subscribe prompt.\n\n> [!NOTE] The denylist separates good alt from boilerplate where no clean heuristic exists\n> \"Image of tank rolling over a world map\" and \"This article appears in the October 2023 issue. Subscribe to WIRED.\" are *both* grammatical sentences, so grammaticality alone cannot separate good alt from boilerplate. The denylist keeps the first and sends the second to the describer, at the cost of a maintained list a novel boilerplate phrase can slip past.\n\n> [!NOTE] A describe failure never goes silent\n> On failure o","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/agus/.claude/projects/-home-agus-workspace-asermax-nagara/f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e/tool-results/bhv7e6i1p.txt","persistedOutputSize":34897},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7b579651-b780-4d9e-9dce-e0b458360976","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"485c48d4-a22c-458a-8ec1-644fc5584bd2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"da3f9037-27eb-47f1-bd46-dcb806754864","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bfa32ed9-babd-4bb0-92a6-0867b6b276d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0131J4THhoVXDUjvthncdT5z","type":"tool_result","content":"# Article extraction\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nThe item Workflow fetches an article's HTML, runs the domain's [recipe](recipes.md) on it, and turns what comes back into the list of typed units that every later step reads. The recipe returns a title and markdown units: paragraphs, code blocks and declared images. The item Workflow derives each text unit's spoken form from its display markdown, drops a unit whose spoken form has nothing to say, and acquires each declared image. The result is one list in which every unit carries its `display`, its `type` and its `spoken` form (see [item-contract](item-contract.md)); what a described unit says comes from [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md).\n\n## 🌐 Fetching the page\n\nThe item Workflow's fetch step scrapes the page through Firecrawl with its own fetch-based client. Its raw HTML goes to the recipe step unsegmented. The domain of the final URL, after redirects, picks the recipe. A scrape that cannot reach Firecrawl, or returns no HTML, fails the step; once the step has used its 3 attempts the item fails, with `current_step` naming fetch (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The fetch step writes what each scrape bills, from Firecrawl's `creditsUsed`, as a ledger row (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)), so a failed item still records the credit it spent.\n\n> [!NOTE] What firecrawl is called with, and why rawHtml\n> The scrape asks for `rawHtml` and `markdown` at `proxy: \"auto\"`. The fetch step asks for `rawHtml` rather than firecrawl's cleaned HTML because the cleaning throws away images (9 against 5 on one corpus article), and the recipe declares the article's images from the page's own markup. `markdown` costs no extra credit, so the fetch step requests it as evidence; nothing reads it. `proxy: \"auto\"` bills 1 credit when a basic proxy suffices and 5 on a stealth escalation.\n\n> [!WARNING] firecrawl's output is non-deterministic\n> The same URL scraped minutes apart returned a 5x spread in size, and `rawHtml` is post-JavaScript, so it can carry hydrated page chrome a plain fetch never had. The recipe's container and ignores are what keep that chrome out of the units. The spread affects re-recording a test cassette, never a replay, which returns recorded bytes verbatim.\n\n## 📩 How the recipe's units become typed units\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    start([\"The recipe returns<br/>a title and<br/>display units\"]) --> loop\n    subgraph loop [\"For each recipe unit,<br/>in document order\"]\n        direction TB\n        kind{\"Image unit?\"}\n        kind -->|No| spoken[\"1 Derive the spoken<br/>form from the display\"]\n        spoken --> test{\"2 Drop rule<br/>matches?\"}\n        test -->|Yes| drop[\"3 Drop: no typed unit\"]\n        test -->|No| keep[\"4 Keep as a typed<br/>paragraph or code unit\"]\n        kind -->|Yes| stash[\"5 Hold it after the<br/>last kept text unit\"]\n    end\n    loop --> acquire[\"6 Acquire held images,<br/>interleave with kept units\"]\n    acquire --> any{\"7 Any unit survives?\"}\n    any -. no .-> fail([\"Fail the item:<br/>no surviving units\"])\n    any -->|Yes| write[\"8 Write the units<br/>into the item row\"]\n    write --> next([\"Describe, synthesis,<br/>store: survivors only\"])\n```\n\nSteps 1 to 5 run at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step, on the units the recipe returns. Steps 1 and 2 are in a required order, because the drop rule reads the spoken form. The loop must also run in document order, because step 5 anchors an image to the last text unit kept so far. When that unit is dropped, the image anchors to the kept unit before it. An image that comes before every text unit goes first in the list. Step 6, the images step, acquires the held images and drops any that will not acquire (see below). Step 7 fails the item when nothing survives, before the list is written. Step 8 writes the survivors and the degradations into the item row as soon as the step has them, and the later steps read them from there. The recipe step writes the title into the row together with its units. A recipe the agents authored or revised is written as its next version by the item Workflow itself (see [recipes](recipes.md)).\n\nFrom step 8 on, describe, synthesis and store work on the survivors only and never drop a unit, so the length guard at store compares one list against its own timeline (invariant 2).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the display list is persisted rather than re-extracted\n> The display list is written onto the item row as the steps produce it and joined onto the timing at store (see [item-contract](item-contract.md)) rather than re-fetched and re-extracted when synthesis completes. Re-extracting risks a different result across the async gap: the same URL a minute later is not guaranteed to produce the same page. The API shows the units only once the item is `ready`.\n\nThe unit list's shape, including `ImageUnit`, is a type in common/ that both Workers build against (see [common-package](common-package.md)).\n\n## 🗣️ Deriving the spoken form\n\nThe spoken form is derived from the unit's display markdown by walking its CommonMark token tree, so the display and spoken forms come from one extraction (invariant 1). A fenced code block becomes the fixed placeholder `\"Code sample.\"`. A table is spoken as its body rows only, each row as its own line of header-aware prose (`\"Feature: Extraction, Status: done.\"`) and never as pipe characters. The header row gets no line of its own. A row gets a full stop only when it does not already end in `.`, `!` or `?`. A cell goes through the same inline walk as a paragraph, so a code span keeps its text, and then through the sanitize tail, described below. A heading and a quote are read as their words, without their markers. Every block inside a unit becomes its own line, so a unit carrying several paragraphs or list items never runs the last word of one into the first word of the next. A list item gets no full stop. An ordered item keeps its `1.` at the start of its line, and the spoken form does not announce nesting depth. A code block or an image nested inside a list unit is silent in the spoken form. It stays inside the list's own display and is never described or acquired on its own (see [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)). A list item that holds only a code block, an image or a nested list gets no line. The derivation strips a task-list checkbox and reads struck-through text as its words, with no sign of the strike. Emphasis markers and link destinations are dropped throughout.\n\nA shared sanitize tail then turns any leftover emphasis or code marker that touches text into a space, so the newlines between a unit's lines survive the pass. It drops the space a marker left before punctuation and collapses whitespace. It reduces an opening or closing tag around a word to the word inside (`<software>` to \"software\", `</div>` to \"div\"). A lone `<` or `>` is not a tag, so this step leaves it for the operator rule that follows. A last pass turns a comparison-operator sequence into the word it stands for, longest match first: `<=` \"less than or equal to\", `>=` \"greater than or equal to\", `->` \"to\", `=>` \"to\", `<-` \"from\", `<<` \"much less than\", `>>` \"much greater than\", `<3` \"heart\", then the bare `<` \"less than\" and `>` \"greater than\". `<3` becomes \"heart\" only when no digit follows, so `<30` reads \"less than 30\". The pass puts a space on each side of every word it inserts, so `a<b` reads \"a less than b\". The describer's model output runs through the same tail (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)), since a JSON schema cannot forbid a marker inside its string value.\n\nThe `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder is the **interim** spoken form of a code block. During the describe step the describer overwrites a code unit's spoken form with `Code: <one sentence>` and a describable image's with `Image: <one sentence>`; a code block keeps the floor `\"Code with no description.\"` only when the describer fails or the per-item budget is spent.\n\n> [!NOTE] The words inside a surviving tag are the article's own\n> An author writing about software writes `<software>`, `<your-api-key>` or `<T>` as text, and a tag still present in a unit's markdown is prose the author escaped. Dropping it silently loses a word the article meant to say, and a whitelist of real HTML element names would be actively wrong here, since an author writing about `<div>` intends the listener to hear \"div\".\n\n## 🗑️ Dropping a unit with nothing to say\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Text unit from the recipe,<br/>with its spoken form\"] --> C{\"Spoken form has a<br/>letter or digit?\"}\n    C -->|No| D((Drop))\n    C -->|Yes| K((Keep))\n```\n\nThe rule runs on every paragraph and code unit the recipe returns and reads only the spoken form: a letter inside a link URL or a code block's language tag never keeps a unit. \"Letter or digit\" follows Unicode, so non-Latin text is kept. An empty spoken form fails the same test. A dropped unit never becomes a typed unit, so its display, its spoken form and its timing window leave together (invariant 2), and it leaves no trace: no degradation, no log.\n\nWhat the rule catches is a section-break ornament (`❦`, `* * *`, `§`) or bare punctuation that a recipe emitted as its own paragraph. A code unit always passes, because its spoken form is the `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a drop leaves no trace\n> The rule drops only units whose spoken form carries no letter or digit, so what disappears is never a word the author wrote. A degradation records content the item lost, and an ornament carries no content.\n\n> [!WARNING] A unit with nothing to pronounce crashes synthesis\n> The synthesizer produces no audio for a paragraph made only of a symbol, and the TTS service fails the whole call on it. A sockpuppet.org recipe that kept its `❦` section breaks as paragraphs failed its items at synthesis while every extraction step reported success. The drop is what keeps such a unit from reaching synthesis.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: echoed-title and navigation-label matching on recipe units\n> The recipe already names the title element and carries the ignore list the author agent wrote, so an echoed title or a \"Contents\" label is the recipe's to exclude. Matching them again after the recipe step would add a heuristic that repeats a decision the recipe already makes.\n\n## 🖼️ Article images: acquisition and spoken form\n\nThe recipe declares which images belong to the article: each image unit carries its `src` and `alt`, and the images step acquires exactly the images the recipe declares, with no selection of its own. Each acquired image gets a spoken form and goes after the text unit it followed in the page. Each image is an `ImageUnit` with its own spoken form and therefore its own timing window.\n\n### ⬇️ Acquisition: download, validate, re-encode, store\n\nA declared image is downloaded under a per-host semaphore (default 2) beneath a global one (default 10), with a 10-second timeout and a 10 MB streamed size cap. The per-host bound is the one that matters: one article's many same-host images must never open that many connections to a server nagara has no relationship with. A Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections at a time, so at most 6 downloads run at once whatever the global bound.\n\nValidation runs on the **decoded bytes**, never on HTTP or HTML metadata. The bytes are sniffed for an `<svg` root. A raster image goes through Cloudflare's Images binding, which decodes it outside the Worker, and is **kept when `min(width, height) >= 200`**, measured on the decoded file. What survives is re-encoded to WebP through the same binding. Each kept image is stored once per content hash, taken over the stored bytes, under the images prefix of the R2 bucket, with its content type on the object, and the hash becomes the unit's image reference, so no origin URL leaks into persisted markdown. common/'s image key function builds the object's key from the hash (see [common-package](common-package.md), and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) for the bucket layout).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why validate by decoding, not by trusting the attributes or the Content-Type\n> Most of the corpus omits `width`/`height` entirely (all four New Yorker contact sheets carry neither), and a Content-Type header can lie, so the only trustworthy size and format come from decoding the file itself. The 200 px floor sits in a gap in the corpus: avatars and tracking pixels cluster around 40 px and every legitimate figure is 305 px or larger. Two square brand logos survive it, accepted rather than chased, because a square-ratio rule has no legitimate square image in the corpus to test against and a false positive costs only one cheap describe call.\n\nAn SVG stays an SVG. Its scripts are stripped before it is stored, and it is stored under the content hash of the stripped markup, with the SVG content type on the object. It has no pixel size to measure, so the 200 px floor does not apply to it. It is describable from its markup (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)) and is served like any other image; a client shows it only through an `<img>` element, where its scripts would not run (see [item-contract](item-contract.md)).\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: an image library inside the Worker\n> The Images binding rejects every SVG, so rasterising one would take a WebAssembly rasteriser in the Worker: about 1.5 MiB gzipped added to the bundle, and every text element in the SVG disappears unless a font is bundled with it. Decoding a raster above about 10 MP inside the Worker exceeds its 128 MB of memory and ends the invocation where no code can catch it. The binding decodes rasters outside the Worker's memory, and a browser renders an SVG natively, so keeping the SVG needs no rasteriser at all.\n\n### 💬 An image's spoken form\n\nAn acquired image speaks its alt verbatim as `Image: <alt>`, or the floor `\"Image with no description.\"` when the alt is empty. The good-alt filter decides whether the describer replaces that form with a generated sentence (see the precedence in [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)). One of its checks is the cruft test: an alt that repeats the article title, or carries no letter or digit, is not a good alt.\n\n### ❌ An image that will not acquire is dropped from both lists\n\nAn image that 404s, times out, exceeds the size cap, will not decode, or fails to process through the Images binding is dropped from `display` and `spoken` alike (invariant 2), and the drop is recorded as a `degradation`: a typed object `{\"type\": \"image\", \"url\": <origin>, \"reason\": <short>}` that never rides on the wire. This is acquisition failure only, kept distinct from a **describe** failure, which never goes silent (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)). The two divide by layer: an image that never arrived has no unit to speak for, so it drops; an image that arrived but could not be described keeps a spoken fallback.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a degradation column, when the item is still `ready`\n> `error` stays failed-only, and that rule is worth keeping. A `ready` item that silently dropped six of twelve images exposes nothing to the client and a full record to the operator: technically `ready` and quietly worse. The degradation list is what makes that visible without failing the item. Its scope is runtime degradations only.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n- **Prose-boilerplate stripping.** Footer donation asides and sponsor mentions arrive as full sentences and reach the audio unless the recipe ignores them. The item Workflow does not filter them, because a generic filter risks trimming real content.\n- **Quote voice switching.** A listener hears a quote in the one narrator voice, and nobody has yet validated the blockquote audio round-trip end to end.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [recipes](recipes.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) · [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [invariants](invariants.md) · [what-gets-read-aloud](../product-design/what-gets-read-aloud.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Recipes\"\nsummary: \"How one agent-authored script per domain extracts the domain's articles into markdown units, its versioned record, how a URL matches to it, and how agents author and revise it.\"\ncreated: \"2026-09-13\"\n---\n\n# Recipes\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nA recipe is one domain's extraction knowledge: a script that receives an article's HTML as a Cheerio document and returns the article as a title and a list of markdown units, deterministically, so the same page always yields the same units. Agents write recipes. The first article from a domain authors the domain's recipe, and every later article extracts with it. When a recipe fails an article, that article triggers a revision. The model runs at authoring time only.\n\n## 🧾 The script and its declarations\n\nA recipe is a script plus the declarations around it. The container selector names the element that contains the article, and everything outside the container is site chrome. The inventory names every element kind the domain's pages carry inside the container and the role each plays: a unit, inline content, a wrapper, or the title. The ignores are the selectors deliberately excluded, each with the judgement that justified it; typical ignores are ads, date stamps and share rows. Every recipe excludes footnote markers and footnote bodies, because the authoring prompts instruct it to. The author prompt and the revision prompt embed one shared contract of plain instructions. The prompts also require GFM markdown. They have the recipe replace every `<math>` element with its alttext as plain text, with no delimiter, before calling `toMarkdown`. The rule covers a block formula and an inline one in a paragraph, list item, table cell or quote alike. The recipe passes the whole element to `toMarkdown`, never a piece of it, and declares a whole list as one unit even when the list holds a code block or an image. They exclude a blockquote's attribution, a `<dl>`, a heading self-link and a heading that repeats the title, and they keep a task list's checkboxes. See [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) for what each of those becomes in a unit's display.\n\nThe inventory is the union across the domain's pages, and an element kind absent from one page is normal variation across a domain's posts.\n\nThe script receives the Cheerio document and `toMarkdown`, the fixed HTML-to-markdown converter, from the runtime, and imports nothing. The script reads the document without changing it. Domain knowledge enters as structural preparation of a cloned element. The script builds a `<thead>` for a table that lacks one, swaps MathML for the LaTeX in its `alttext`, removes footnote subtrees, and wraps a span-built listing in a `<pre>`. The fixed pass converts the prepared clone.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: the agent writes its own conversion\n> A trial where the agent hand-converted html to markdown matched the fixed pass on quality and gained nothing by it: conversion stopped being identical across domains, and a recipe could get conversion wrong. The per-domain tricks survived as clone preparation, which is reviewable DOM structure, never a rewritten converter.\n\n## 📦 Units and the fixed pass\n\nUnits come back in document order, one element per unit as the recipe declares them, never overlapping: once an element is a unit, everything inside it is that unit's content. The kinds are `paragraph`, `code` and `image`. A paragraph is any flow that renders as markdown: prose, headings, quotes, lists, tables; a list is a single declared unit, even one holding a code block or an image; the nested code block or image stays inside the list's own markdown and never becomes a unit of its own (see [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)). The fixed conversion pass produces a paragraph or code unit's display markdown. An image unit carries `src` and `alt` exactly as the page presents them: the recipe never invents `alt`, and the src is how the recipe resolves the domain's lazy-load conventions. An image the article carries is always emitted, even when its src cannot be resolved. Acquiring, validating, describing or dropping images is downstream's business.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why three kinds and not one per markdown shape\n> Downstream distinguishes exactly three things: text rendered as markdown and read as prose, code that gets described instead of read, and images that get acquired and follow the alt precedence. Headings, quotes, lists and tables all derive their display from their markdown, so one paragraph kind covers them; even the read-along table of contents derives from a `#`-prefixed unit's display text, not from a kind.\n\nThe same validator runs at authoring, revision and extraction. It checks that every unit's element is inside the container, in document order, none nested inside another; that every element kind in the container is declared or reported as unexpected; that no readable text is left after the units and ignores are removed, with the leftover report naming the element kinds that carry it; and that no declared unit is an `<li>` on its own. The report line for an orphaned `<li>` names the unit, the list it belongs to and the rule that a recipe hands a list over whole. It also checks that every unit's markdown is valid: non-empty, no residual HTML outside code, balanced fences. The fence check accepts an opening fence right after a list, quote or task marker, because a list item holding only a code block puts the fence on the marker's own line. Invalid markdown fails a recipe like any other problem.\n\n## 💽 The recipe record\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    RECIPE_VERSION ||--o{ ITEM : extracts\n    RECIPE_VERSION {\n        string id PK\n        string domain\n        int version\n        text script\n        datetime created_at\n    }\n    ITEM {\n        string recipe_version_id FK \"nullable\"\n        string extraction_domain\n    }\n```\n\nEach version of a domain's recipe is one row in the `recipe_versions` table of the D1 database the items share, and the item Workflow writes it. Its shape is a type in common/, and common/'s SQL migrations create the table (see [common-package](common-package.md)). A revision is a pure insert that updates no row, and the current recipe for a domain is the highest version, so the per-article read is one indexed descent. The `script` column carries the entire recipe with its declarations embedded, stored exactly as the authoring loop validated it and kept whole so a failing recipe reaches a prompt as one piece. `id` is a generated string following the house pattern. Extraction sets `recipe_version_id` on the item to the version whose script extracted it. That link traces a revision that made things worse to the items it touched. The column is null for items that never reached extraction. Recipes are global: one recipe per domain, shared by every listener.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: a current flag or a separate history table\n> A flag mutates the previous row and is denormalized state that can disagree with the version number; a current-plus-history pair overwrites the payload in place and duplicates the schema.\n\n## ⚖️ Matching a URL to a recipe\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Article URL arrives\"] --> B[\"Fetch it, redirects<br/>resolve\"]\n    B --> C[\"Host of the final URL,<br/>leading www dropped\"]\n    C --> D{\"Any recipe version<br/>for that domain?\"}\n    D -->|\"No\"| E[\"Author one: the base prompt<br/>against this article\"]\n    E --> F{\"Authoring succeeded?\"}\n    F -->|\"No, after its one retry\"| N((\"Nothing: the<br/>item fails\"))\n    F -->|\"Yes\"| G[\"Save it as the domain's<br/>version 1\"]\n    D -->|\"Yes\"| H[\"Current version:<br/>the highest\"]\n    G --> R((\"The recipe to<br/>extract with\"))\n    H --> R\n```\n\nThe key is the host of the fetch's final URL with only a leading `www` dropped: subdomains are distinct domains, so `blog.exe.dev` and `exe.dev` hold separate recipes, and a canonical link declared inside the page does not change it. Consolidation comes from redirects: a hundred domains redirecting to one place all land on that domain's recipe. No recipe serves a set of domains, and platform-hosted hostnames with identical markup each author their own.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why no recipe serves several domains\n> Platform-hosted blogs put many hostnames on identical markup, and one recipe describing that markup would save every later hostname its authoring run. Sharing was rejected to keep one recipe per domain strict: a recipe's identity is its domain's key, and a set-valued key complicates matching, storage and revision for a cost, repeated authoring, that only platforms pay.\n\n## 📩 The lifecycle: authoring and revision\n\nFirst authoring happens when a domain's first article arrives: the base prompt runs against that article's HTML, best-effort within one hundred turns, with one retry of the whole run, and if that fails the item fails with nothing saved for the domain. A domain's homepage enqueued by mistake fails and saves nothing, and the next real article authors fresh.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor L as Listener\n    participant P as Item Workflow\n    participant A as Agent\n    participant V as Recipe runtime\n    participant S as D1\n\n    L->>P: Enqueues the article URL\n    P->>P: Extract with the current version\n    P->>P: Validation fails\n    P->>A: Iteration prompt, current script and report\n    Note over P,A: The domain is locked, its other articles hold\n    alt the page is not an article\n        A-->>P: The verdict\n        P-->>L: Item fails, the recipe stands\n    else a fix\n        A->>V: Run the fix against this article\n        loop within 100 turns, then two retries of the run\n            V-->>A: Report\n            A->>A: Fix additively, re-validate\n        end\n        V-->>A: Problems none\n        A-->>P: The passing script\n        P->>S: Insert as the new highest version\n        P->>P: The held item re-extracts with the new version\n        P-->>L: Item proceeds\n    else the fix gives up\n        A-->>P: No passing version\n        P-->>L: Item fails, the old version stays current\n    end\n```\n\nRevision triggers when an article fails validation under the current version. The iteration prompt receives the current script, stored whole, and the validator's report. The standing instruction is additive: keep the recipe's previous assumptions and add what this article needs. The exception is a wholesale markup change, where the agent judges the domain was redesigned and may rewrite the recipe outright. Validation is against the triggering article only. The item that triggered the revision holds while the revision runs, then retries with the new version; the listener sees a slow item and nothing else. Authoring and revision both run inside the item Workflow's recipe step, while the item holds its domain's lease (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). Each attempt of the recipe step has 15 minutes; one measured authoring run took 97 s, and the hundred-turn bound is stated in the prompt alone. A revision that gives up writes no version, so the older one stays current and the next article from the domain triggers revision again. Each attempt is independently bounded.\n\n> [!WARNING] A failed revision leaves the domain exposed\n> Every new article from a permanently broken domain triggers one bounded revision attempt before failing, and nothing counts these consecutive attempts: the failures are visible, but the agent spend is unbounded.\n\n> [!NOTE] The not-an-article verdict is what catches the 200-status error page\n> A URL serving an HTTP 200 error page extracts cleanly under a generic extractor and reaches `ready` as audio of the error page. An agent looking at the page to author or revise reports the page for what it is. The verdict fails the item, from either prompt, without retrying the recipe step. From revision, the verdict also means no version is written and the recipe stands.\n\n## ♠️ What extraction hands the pipeline\n\n| Input | What it is | Where it comes from |\n|---|---|---|\n| The article's HTML | Firecrawl's rawHtml for the URL | The item Workflow's fetch step, which resolves redirects and so decides the domain |\n| The recipe | The domain's current version, the script stored whole | The `recipe_versions` table in D1, by the matched domain |\n\n| Output | What it carries |\n|---|---|\n| The title | The article's own title; the agent prefers the element that contains it, its judgement when there is none |\n| The units | `{ kind, markdown or src+alt }`, document order |\n| The verdict | Empty when extraction succeeded; not-an-article or not-accessible fails the item |\n\nThe recipe's markdown is the single source of truth: display shows it as-is; the spoken form derives from it generically and never reaches a client, and the item Workflow drops a unit whose spoken form has no letter or digit (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)). The image handler receives the recipe-declared images, acquires and validates them, and prefers a good alt, then a generated description. When acquisition fails, it drops the image with a degradation. The `og:image` lede has no generic rule; when a page's `og:image` belongs in the article, declaring it is the recipe's judgement.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b7cac967-e5f2-4809-a5c4-b9605ad8a2f8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:48:06.151Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Article extraction\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nThe item Workflow fetches an article's HTML, runs the domain's [recipe](recipes.md) on it, and turns what comes back into the list of typed units that every later step reads. The recipe returns a title and markdown units: paragraphs, code blocks and declared images. The item Workflow derives each text unit's spoken form from its display markdown, drops a unit whose spoken form has nothing to say, and acquires each declared image. The result is one list in which every unit carries its `display`, its `type` and its `spoken` form (see [item-contract](item-contract.md)); what a described unit says comes from [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md).\n\n## 🌐 Fetching the page\n\nThe item Workflow's fetch step scrapes the page through Firecrawl with its own fetch-based client. Its raw HTML goes to the recipe step unsegmented. The domain of the final URL, after redirects, picks the recipe. A scrape that cannot reach Firecrawl, or returns no HTML, fails the step; once the step has used its 3 attempts the item fails, with `current_step` naming fetch (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The fetch step writes what each scrape bills, from Firecrawl's `creditsUsed`, as a ledger row (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)), so a failed item still records the credit it spent.\n\n> [!NOTE] What firecrawl is called with, and why rawHtml\n> The scrape asks for `rawHtml` and `markdown` at `proxy: \"auto\"`. The fetch step asks for `rawHtml` rather than firecrawl's cleaned HTML because the cleaning throws away images (9 against 5 on one corpus article), and the recipe declares the article's images from the page's own markup. `markdown` costs no extra credit, so the fetch step requests it as evidence; nothing reads it. `proxy: \"auto\"` bills 1 credit when a basic proxy suffices and 5 on a stealth escalation.\n\n> [!WARNING] firecrawl's output is non-deterministic\n> The same URL scraped minutes apart returned a 5x spread in size, and `rawHtml` is post-JavaScript, so it can carry hydrated page chrome a plain fetch never had. The recipe's container and ignores are what keep that chrome out of the units. The spread affects re-recording a test cassette, never a replay, which returns recorded bytes verbatim.\n\n## 📩 How the recipe's units become typed units\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    start([\"The recipe returns<br/>a title and<br/>display units\"]) --> loop\n    subgraph loop [\"For each recipe unit,<br/>in document order\"]\n        direction TB\n        kind{\"Image unit?\"}\n        kind -->|No| spoken[\"1 Derive the spoken<br/>form from the display\"]\n        spoken --> test{\"2 Drop rule<br/>matches?\"}\n        test -->|Yes| drop[\"3 Drop: no typed unit\"]\n        test -->|No| keep[\"4 Keep as a typed<br/>paragraph or code unit\"]\n        kind -->|Yes| stash[\"5 Hold it after the<br/>last kept text unit\"]\n    end\n    loop --> acquire[\"6 Acquire held images,<br/>interleave with kept units\"]\n    acquire --> any{\"7 Any unit survives?\"}\n    any -. no .-> fail([\"Fail the item:<br/>no surviving units\"])\n    any -->|Yes| write[\"8 Write the units<br/>into the item row\"]\n    write --> next([\"Describe, synthesis,<br/>store: survivors only\"])\n```\n\nSteps 1 to 5 run at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step, on the units the recipe returns. Steps 1 and 2 are in a required order, because the drop rule reads the spoken form. The loop must also run in document order, because step 5 anchors an image to the last text unit kept so far. When that unit is dropped, the image anchors to the kept unit before it. An image that comes before every text unit goes first in the list. Step 6, the images step, acquires the held images and drops any that will not acquire (see below). Step 7 fails the item when nothing survives, before the list is written. Step 8 writes the survivors and the degradations into the item row as soon as the step has them, and the later steps read them from there. The recipe step writes the title into the row together with its units. A recipe the agents authored or revised is written as its next version by the item Workflow itself (see [recipes](recipes.md)).\n\nFrom step 8 on, describe, synthesis and store work on the survivors only and never drop a unit, so the length guard at store compares one list against its own timeline (invariant 2).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the display list is persisted rather than re-extracted\n> The display list is written onto the item row as the steps produce it and joined onto the timing at store (see [item-contract](item-contract.md)) rather than re-fetched and re-extracted when synthesis completes. Re-extracting risks a different result across the async gap: the same URL a minute later is not guaranteed to produce the same page. The API shows the units only once the item is `ready`.\n\nThe unit list's shape, including `ImageUnit`, is a type in common/ that both Workers build against (see [common-package](common-package.md)).\n\n## 🗣️ Deriving the spoken form\n\nThe spoken form is derived from the unit's display markdown by walking its CommonMark token tree, so the display and spoken forms come from one extraction (invariant 1). A fenced code block becomes the fixed placeholder `\"Code sample.\"`. A table is spoken as its body rows only, each row as its own line of header-aware prose (`\"Feature: Extraction, Status: done.\"`) and never as pipe characters. The header row gets no line of its own. A row gets a full stop only when it does not already end in `.`, `!` or `?`. A cell goes through the same inline walk as a paragraph, so a code span keeps its text, and then through the sanitize tail, described below. A heading and a quote are read as their words, without their markers. Every block inside a unit becomes its own line, so a unit carrying several paragraphs or list items never runs the last word of one into the first word of the next. A list item gets no full stop. An ordered item keeps its `1.` at the start of its line, and the spoken form does not announce nesting depth. A code block or an image nested inside a list unit is silent in the spoken form. It stays inside the list's own display and is never described or acquired on its own (see [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)). A list item that holds only a code block, an image or a nested list gets no line. The derivation strips a task-list checkbox and reads struck-through text as its words, with no sign of the strike. Emphasis markers and link destinations are dropped throughout.\n\nA shared sanitize tail then turns any leftover emphasis or code marker that touches text into a space, so the newlines between a unit's lines survive the pass. It drops the space a marker left before punctuation and collapses whitespace. It reduces an opening or closing tag around a word to the word inside (`<software>` to \"software\", `</div>` to \"div\"). A lone `<` or `>` is not a tag, so this step leaves it for the operator rule that follows. A last pass turns a comparison-operator sequence into the word it stands for, longest match first: `<=` \"less than or equal to\", `>=` \"greater than or equal to\", `->` \"to\", `=>` \"to\", `<-` \"from\", `<<` \"much less than\", `>>` \"much greater than\", `<3` \"heart\", then the bare `<` \"less than\" and `>` \"greater than\". `<3` becomes \"heart\" only when no digit follows, so `<30` reads \"less than 30\". The pass puts a space on each side of every word it inserts, so `a<b` reads \"a less than b\". The describer's model output runs through the same tail (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)), since a JSON schema cannot forbid a marker inside its string value.\n\nThe `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder is the **interim** spoken form of a code block. During the describe step the describer overwrites a code unit's spoken form with `Code: <one sentence>` and a describable image's with `Image: <one sentence>`; a code block keeps the floor `\"Code with no description.\"` only when the describer fails or the per-item budget is spent.\n\n> [!NOTE] The words inside a surviving tag are the article's own\n> An author writing about software writes `<software>`, `<your-api-key>` or `<T>` as text, and a tag still present in a unit's markdown is prose the author escaped. Dropping it silently loses a word the article meant to say, and a whitelist of real HTML element names would be actively wrong here, since an author writing about `<div>` intends the listener to hear \"div\".\n\n## 🗑️ Dropping a unit with nothing to say\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Text unit from the recipe,<br/>with its spoken form\"] --> C{\"Spoken form has a<br/>letter or digit?\"}\n    C -->|No| D((Drop))\n    C -->|Yes| K((Keep))\n```\n\nThe rule runs on every paragraph and code unit the recipe returns and reads only the spoken form: a letter inside a link URL or a code block's language tag never keeps a unit. \"Letter or digit\" follows Unicode, so non-Latin text is kept. An empty spoken form fails the same test. A dropped unit never becomes a typed unit, so its display, its spoken form and its timing window leave together (invariant 2), and it leaves no trace: no degradation, no log.\n\nWhat the rule catches is a section-break ornament (`❦`, `* * *`, `§`) or bare punctuation that a recipe emitted as its own paragraph. A code unit always passes, because its spoken form is the `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a drop leaves no trace\n> The rule drops only units whose spoken form carries no letter or digit, so what disappears is never a word the author wrote. A degradation records content the item lost, and an ornament carries no content.\n\n> [!WARNING] A unit with nothing to pronounce crashes synthesis\n> The synthesizer produces no audio for a paragraph made only of a symbol, and the TTS service fails the whole call on it. A sockpuppet.org recipe that kept its `❦` section breaks as paragraphs failed its items at synthesis while every extraction step reported success. The drop is what keeps such a unit from reaching synthesis.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: echoed-title and navigation-label matching on recipe units\n> The recipe already names the title element and carries the ignore list the author agent wrote, so an echoed title or a \"Contents\" label is the recipe's to exclude. Matching them again after the recipe step would add a heuristic that repeats a decision the recipe already makes.\n\n## 🖼️ Article images: acquisition and spoken form\n\nThe recipe declares which images belong to the article: each image unit carries its `src` and `alt`, and the images step acquires exactly the images the recipe declares, with no selection of its own. Each acquired image gets a spoken form and goes after the text unit it followed in the page. Each image is an `ImageUnit` with its own spoken form and therefore its own timing window.\n\n### ⬇️ Acquisition: download, validate, re-encode, store\n\nA declared image is downloaded under a per-host semaphore (default 2) beneath a global one (default 10), with a 10-second timeout and a 10 MB streamed size cap. The per-host bound is the one that matters: one article's many same-host images must never open that many connections to a server nagara has no relationship with. A Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections at a time, so at most 6 downloads run at once whatever the global bound.\n\nValidation runs on the **decoded bytes**, never on HTTP or HTML metadata. The bytes are sniffed for an `<svg` root. A raster image goes through Cloudflare's Images binding, which decodes it outside the Worker, and is **kept when `min(width, height) >= 200`**, measured on the decoded file. What survives is re-encoded to WebP through the same binding. Each kept image is stored once per content hash, taken over the stored bytes, under the images prefix of the R2 bucket, with its content type on the object, and the hash becomes the unit's image reference, so no origin URL leaks into persisted markdown. common/'s image key function builds the object's key from the hash (see [common-package](common-package.md), and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) for the bucket layout).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why validate by decoding, not by trusting the attributes or the Content-Type\n> Most of the corpus omits `width`/`height` entirely (all four New Yorker contact sheets carry neither), and a Content-Type header can lie, so the only trustworthy size and format come from decoding the file itself. The 200 px floor sits in a gap in the corpus: avatars and tracking pixels cluster around 40 px and every legitimate figure is 305 px or larger. Two square brand logos survive it, accepted rather than chased, because a square-ratio rule has no legitimate square image in the corpus to test against and a false positive costs only one cheap describe call.\n\nAn SVG stays an SVG. Its scripts are stripped before it is stored, and it is stored under the content hash of the stripped markup, with the SVG content type on the object. It has no pixel size to measure, so the 200 px floor does not apply to it. It is describable from its markup (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)) and is served like any other image; a client shows it only through an `<img>` element, where its scripts would not run (see [item-contract](item-contract.md)).\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: an image library inside the Worker\n> The Images binding rejects every SVG, so rasterising one would take a WebAssembly rasteriser in the Worker: about 1.5 MiB gzipped added to the bundle, and every text element in the SVG disappears unless a font is bundled with it. Decoding a raster above about 10 MP inside the Worker exceeds its 128 MB of memory and ends the invocation where no code can catch it. The binding decodes rasters outside the Worker's memory, and a browser renders an SVG natively, so keeping the SVG needs no rasteriser at all.\n\n### 💬 An image's spoken form\n\nAn acquired image speaks its alt verbatim as `Image: <alt>`, or the floor `\"Image with no description.\"` when the alt is empty. The good-alt filter decides whether the describer replaces that form with a generated sentence (see the precedence in [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)). One of its checks is the cruft test: an alt that repeats the article title, or carries no letter or digit, is not a good alt.\n\n### ❌ An image that will not acquire is dropped from both lists\n\nAn image that 404s, times out, exceeds the size cap, will not decode, or fails to process through the Images binding is dropped from `display` and `spoken` alike (invariant 2), and the drop is recorded as a `degradation`: a typed object `{\"type\": \"image\", \"url\": <origin>, \"reason\": <short>}` that never rides on the wire. This is acquisition failure only, kept distinct from a **describe** failure, which never goes silent (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)). The two divide by layer: an image that never arrived has no unit to speak for, so it drops; an image that arrived but could not be described keeps a spoken fallback.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a degradation column, when the item is still `ready`\n> `error` stays failed-only, and that rule is worth keeping. A `ready` item that silently dropped six of twelve images exposes nothing to the client and a full record to the operator: technically `ready` and quietly worse. The degradation list is what makes that visible without failing the item. Its scope is runtime degradations only.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n- **Prose-boilerplate stripping.** Footer donation asides and sponsor mentions arrive as full sentences and reach the audio unless the recipe ignores them. The item Workflow does not filter them, because a generic filter risks trimming real content.\n- **Quote voice switching.** A listener hears a quote in the one narrator voice, and nobody has yet validated the blockquote audio round-trip end to end.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [recipes](recipes.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) · [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [invariants](invariants.md) · [what-gets-read-aloud](../product-design/what-gets-read-aloud.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Recipes\"\nsummary: \"How one agent-authored script per domain extracts the domain's articles into markdown units, its versioned record, how a URL matches to it, and how agents author and revise it.\"\ncreated: \"2026-09-13\"\n---\n\n# Recipes\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nA recipe is one domain's extraction knowledge: a script that receives an article's HTML as a Cheerio document and returns the article as a title and a list of markdown units, deterministically, so the same page always yields the same units. Agents write recipes. The first article from a domain authors the domain's recipe, and every later article extracts with it. When a recipe fails an article, that article triggers a revision. The model runs at authoring time only.\n\n## 🧾 The script and its declarations\n\nA recipe is a script plus the declarations around it. The container selector names the element that contains the article, and everything outside the container is site chrome. The inventory names every element kind the domain's pages carry inside the container and the role each plays: a unit, inline content, a wrapper, or the title. The ignores are the selectors deliberately excluded, each with the judgement that justified it; typical ignores are ads, date stamps and share rows. Every recipe excludes footnote markers and footnote bodies, because the authoring prompts instruct it to. The author prompt and the revision prompt embed one shared contract of plain instructions. The prompts also require GFM markdown. They have the recipe replace every `<math>` element with its alttext as plain text, with no delimiter, before calling `toMarkdown`. The rule covers a block formula and an inline one in a paragraph, list item, table cell or quote alike. The recipe passes the whole element to `toMarkdown`, never a piece of it, and declares a whole list as one unit even when the list holds a code block or an image. They exclude a blockquote's attribution, a `<dl>`, a heading self-link and a heading that repeats the title, and they keep a task list's checkboxes. See [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) for what each of those becomes in a unit's display.\n\nThe inventory is the union across the domain's pages, and an element kind absent from one page is normal variation across a domain's posts.\n\nThe script receives the Cheerio document and `toMarkdown`, the fixed HTML-to-markdown converter, from the runtime, and imports nothing. The script reads the document without changing it. Domain knowledge enters as structural preparation of a cloned element. The script builds a `<thead>` for a table that lacks one, swaps MathML for the LaTeX in its `alttext`, removes footnote subtrees, and wraps a span-built listing in a `<pre>`. The fixed pass converts the prepared clone.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: the agent writes its own conversion\n> A trial where the agent hand-converted html to markdown matched the fixed pass on quality and gained nothing by it: conversion stopped being identical across domains, and a recipe could get conversion wrong. The per-domain tricks survived as clone preparation, which is reviewable DOM structure, never a rewritten converter.\n\n## 📦 Units and the fixed pass\n\nUnits come back in document order, one element per unit as the recipe declares them, never overlapping: once an element is a unit, everything inside it is that unit's content. The kinds are `paragraph`, `code` and `image`. A paragraph is any flow that renders as markdown: prose, headings, quotes, lists, tables; a list is a single declared unit, even one holding a code block or an image; the nested code block or image stays inside the list's own markdown and never becomes a unit of its own (see [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)). The fixed conversion pass produces a paragraph or code unit's display markdown. An image unit carries `src` and `alt` exactly as the page presents them: the recipe never invents `alt`, and the src is how the recipe resolves the domain's lazy-load conventions. An image the article carries is always emitted, even when its src cannot be resolved. Acquiring, validating, describing or dropping images is downstream's business.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why three kinds and not one per markdown shape\n> Downstream distinguishes exactly three things: text rendered as markdown and read as prose, code that gets described instead of read, and images that get acquired and follow the alt precedence. Headings, quotes, lists and tables all derive their display from their markdown, so one paragraph kind covers them; even the read-along table of contents derives from a `#`-prefixed unit's display text, not from a kind.\n\nThe same validator runs at authoring, revision and extraction. It checks that every unit's element is inside the container, in document order, none nested inside another; that every element kind in the container is declared or reported as unexpected; that no readable text is left after the units and ignores are removed, with the leftover report naming the element kinds that carry it; and that no declared unit is an `<li>` on its own. The report line for an orphaned `<li>` names the unit, the list it belongs to and the rule that a recipe hands a list over whole. It also checks that every unit's markdown is valid: non-empty, no residual HTML outside code, balanced fences. The fence check accepts an opening fence right after a list, quote or task marker, because a list item holding only a code block puts the fence on the marker's own line. Invalid markdown fails a recipe like any other problem.\n\n## 💽 The recipe record\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    RECIPE_VERSION ||--o{ ITEM : extracts\n    RECIPE_VERSION {\n        string id PK\n        string domain\n        int version\n        text script\n        datetime created_at\n    }\n    ITEM {\n        string recipe_version_id FK \"nullable\"\n        string extraction_domain\n    }\n```\n\nEach version of a domain's recipe is one row in the `recipe_versions` table of the D1 database the items share, and the item Workflow writes it. Its shape is a type in common/, and common/'s SQL migrations create the table (see [common-package](common-package.md)). A revision is a pure insert that updates no row, and the current recipe for a domain is the highest version, so the per-article read is one indexed descent. The `script` column carries the entire recipe with its declarations embedded, stored exactly as the authoring loop validated it and kept whole so a failing recipe reaches a prompt as one piece. `id` is a generated string following the house pattern. Extraction sets `recipe_version_id` on the item to the version whose script extracted it. That link traces a revision that made things worse to the items it touched. The column is null for items that never reached extraction. Recipes are global: one recipe per domain, shared by every listener.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: a current flag or a separate history table\n> A flag mutates the previous row and is denormalized state that can disagree with the version number; a current-plus-history pair overwrites the payload in place and duplicates the schema.\n\n## ⚖️ Matching a URL to a recipe\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Article URL arrives\"] --> B[\"Fetch it, redirects<br/>resolve\"]\n    B --> C[\"Host of the final URL,<br/>leading www dropped\"]\n    C --> D{\"Any recipe version<br/>for that domain?\"}\n    D -->|\"No\"| E[\"Author one: the base prompt<br/>against this article\"]\n    E --> F{\"Authoring succeeded?\"}\n    F -->|\"No, after its one retry\"| N((\"Nothing: the<br/>item fails\"))\n    F -->|\"Yes\"| G[\"Save it as the domain's<br/>version 1\"]\n    D -->|\"Yes\"| H[\"Current version:<br/>the highest\"]\n    G --> R((\"The recipe to<br/>extract with\"))\n    H --> R\n```\n\nThe key is the host of the fetch's final URL with only a leading `www` dropped: subdomains are distinct domains, so `blog.exe.dev` and `exe.dev` hold separate recipes, and a canonical link declared inside the page does not change it. Consolidation comes from redirects: a hundred domains redirecting to one place all land on that domain's recipe. No recipe serves a set of domains, and platform-hosted hostnames with identical markup each author their own.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why no recipe serves several domains\n> Platform-hosted blogs put many hostnames on identical markup, and one recipe describing that markup would save every later hostname its authoring run. Sharing was rejected to keep one recipe per domain strict: a recipe's identity is its domain's key, and a set-valued key complicates matching, storage and revision for a cost, repeated authoring, that only platforms pay.\n\n## 📩 The lifecycle: authoring and revision\n\nFirst authoring happens when a domain's first article arrives: the base prompt runs against that article's HTML, best-effort within one hundred turns, with one retry of the whole run, and if that fails the item fails with nothing saved for the domain. A domain's homepage enqueued by mistake fails and saves nothing, and the next real article authors fresh.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor L as Listener\n    participant P as Item Workflow\n    participant A as Agent\n    participant V as Recipe runtime\n    participant S as D1\n\n    L->>P: Enqueues the article URL\n    P->>P: Extract with the current version\n    P->>P: Validation fails\n    P->>A: Iteration prompt, current script and report\n    Note over P,A: The domain is locked, its other articles hold\n    alt the page is not an article\n        A-->>P: The verdict\n        P-->>L: Item fails, the recipe stands\n    else a fix\n        A->>V: Run the fix against this article\n        loop within 100 turns, then two retries of the run\n            V-->>A: Report\n            A->>A: Fix additively, re-validate\n        end\n        V-->>A: Problems none\n        A-->>P: The passing script\n        P->>S: Insert as the new highest version\n        P->>P: The held item re-extracts with the new version\n        P-->>L: Item proceeds\n    else the fix gives up\n        A-->>P: No passing version\n        P-->>L: Item fails, the old version stays current\n    end\n```\n\nRevision triggers when an article fails validation under the current version. The iteration prompt receives the current script, stored whole, and the validator's report. The standing instruction is additive: keep the recipe's previous assumptions and add what this article needs. The exception is a wholesale markup change, where the agent judges the domain was redesigned and may rewrite the recipe outright. Validation is against the triggering article only. The item that triggered the revision holds while the revision runs, then retries with the new version; the listener sees a slow item and nothing else. Authoring and revision both run inside the item Workflow's recipe step, while the item holds its domain's lease (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). Each attempt of the recipe step has 15 minutes; one measured authoring run took 97 s, and the hundred-turn bound is stated in the prompt alone. A revision that gives up writes no version, so the older one stays current and the next article from the domain triggers revision again. Each attempt is independently bounded.\n\n> [!WARNING] A failed revision leaves the domain exposed\n> Every new article from a permanently broken domain triggers one bounded revision attempt before failing, and nothing counts these consecutive attempts: the failures are visible, but the agent spend is unbounded.\n\n> [!NOTE] The not-an-article verdict is what catches the 200-status error page\n> A URL serving an HTTP 200 error page extracts cleanly under a generic extractor and reaches `ready` as audio of the error page. An agent looking at the page to author or revise reports the page for what it is. The verdict fails the item, from either prompt, without retrying the recipe step. From revision, the verdict also means no version is written and the recipe stands.\n\n## ♠️ What extraction hands the pipeline\n\n| Input | What it is | Where it comes from |\n|---|---|---|\n| The article's HTML | Firecrawl's rawHtml for the URL | The item Workflow's fetch step, which resolves redirects and so decides the domain |\n| The recipe | The domain's current version, the script stored whole | The `recipe_versions` table in D1, by the matched domain |\n\n| Output | What it carries |\n|---|---|\n| The title | The article's own title; the agent prefers the element that contains it, its judgement when there is none |\n| The units | `{ kind, markdown or src+alt }`, document order |\n| The verdict | Empty when extraction succeeded; not-an-article or not-accessible fails the item |\n\nThe recipe's markdown is the single source of truth: display shows it as-is; the spoken form derives from it generically and never reaches a client, and the item Workflow drops a unit whose spoken form has no letter or digit (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)). The image handler receives the recipe-declared images, acquires and validates them, and prefers a good alt, then a generated description. When acquisition fails, it drops the image with a degradation. The `og:image` lede has no generic rule; when a page's `og:image` belongs in the article, declaring it is the recipe's judgement.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [markdown-units](markdown-units.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7b579651-b780-4d9e-9dce-e0b458360976","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"da3f9037-27eb-47f1-bd46-dcb806754864","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
