e/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"b1b0f783-b54f-4a63-b978-65abcff69e0e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bfa32ed9-babd-4bb0-92a6-0867b6b276d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016fntCyCmk38u9f3xTRwHfg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Deployment and CI\n2\t\n3\t## 🔭 Overview\n4\t\n5\tNagara ships as three deployables: the API Worker and the pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, and the TTS service on Modal. Each tree runs its own CI checks, path-filtered to its directory. The two Workers deploy with wrangler in a fixed order, after `common/`'s migrations are applied to D1; `tts/` deploys to Modal on its own.\n6\t\n7\t## ☁️ How the Workers deploy\n8\t\n9\tBoth Workers deploy with wrangler. Their configurations bind the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket, and the API Worker's configuration binds the item Workflow by the pipeline Worker's script name. `nagara.asermax.com` points at the API Worker, the only Worker clients reach.\n10\t\n11\t> [!NOTE] Why the pipeline Worker has no public routes\n12\t> The API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow through the cross-script Workflow binding, and nothing else calls the pipeline. With no route to reach, the pipeline Worker carries no authentication of its own and no edge policy guards it.\n13\t\n14\t`api/` deploys with wrangler from its own configuration, from within `api/` or via CI below; see [item-contract](item-contract.md) for the routes it serves.\n15\t\n16\t`pipeline/` deploys with `pnpm deploy`, from within `pipeline/` or via CI below; see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) for what runs on it. That script builds before it ships: `pnpm build` runs Vite with the Flue and Cloudflare plugins, writing the bundled Worker and a generated `wrangler.json` into `dist/nagara/`, and `wrangler deploy -c dist/nagara/wrangler.json` ships that generated config rather than the hand-written `pipeline/wrangler.jsonc`. The Flue plugin adds the author and revision agents' Durable Object bindings at build time, so the source config alone would ship a Worker without them.\n17\t\n18\t`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which its own CI workflow applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n19\t\n20\t> [!NOTE] Why migrations, then the pipeline, then the API\n21\t> Both Workers read and write D1, so the schema they build against is applied before either deploys. The API Worker's Workflow binding fails with `workflow.not_found` until the pipeline Worker that holds the item Workflow is deployed, so the pipeline always goes before the API.\n22\t\n23\t## 🔐 Which secrets each Worker holds\n24\t\n25\tEach Worker holds only the secrets its own calls need.\n26\t\n27\t| Worker | Holds |\n28\t|---|---|\n29\t| API Worker | the API key, and an R2 API token it signs GET URLs with |\n30\t| pipeline Worker | the Firecrawl and Gemini keys, the AI Gateway token, Modal's proxy auth token, and an R2 API token it signs PUT URLs with |\n31\t\n32\t## 🎙️ How the TTS service deploys\n33\t\n34\t`tts/` is pushed independently with `modal deploy`, from within `tts/` or via CI below; see [tts-service](tts-service.md) for what actually runs on Modal.\n35\t\n36\t## 🔁 CI: one path-filtered workflow per tree, deploys in a fixed order\n37\t\n38\t```mermaid\n39\tflowchart LR\n40\t  push@{ shape: stadium, label: \"Push to main\" }\n41\t  subgraph cw [\"common workflow\"]\n42\t    direction TB\n43\t    cchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types,<br/>both Workers' types\" }\n44\t    mig@{ shape: rect, label: \"1 apply common/<br/>migrations to D1\" }\n45\t    cchk --> mig\n46\t  end\n47\t  subgraph pw [\"pipeline workflow\"]\n48\t    direction TB\n49\t    pchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n50\t    pdep@{ shape: rect, label: \"2 deploy the<br/>pipeline Worker\" }\n51\t    pchk --> pdep\n52\t  end\n53\t  subgraph aw [\"api workflow\"]\n54\t    direction TB\n55\t    achk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n56\t    adep@{ shape: rect, label: \"3 deploy the<br/>API Worker\" }\n57\t    achk --> adep\n58\t  end\n59\t  subgraph tw [\"tts workflow\"]\n60\t    direction TB\n61\t    tchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n62\t    tdep@{ shape: rect, label: \"deploy tts/<br/>to Modal\" }\n63\t    tchk --> tdep\n64\t  end\n65\t  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n66\t  cfw@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Cloudflare<br/>Workers\" }\n67\t  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal\" }\n68\t  push -->|\"common/\"| cchk\n69\t  push -->|\"pipeline/\"| pchk\n70\t  push -->|\"api/\"| achk\n71\t  push -->|\"tts/\"| tchk\n72\t  mig -.->|\"waits for\"| pdep\n73\t  mig -.->|\"waits for\"| adep\n74\t  pdep -.->|\"waits for\"| adep\n75\t  mig -->|\"wrangler\"| d1\n76\t  pdep -->|\"wrangler\"| cfw\n77\t  adep -->|\"wrangler\"| cfw\n78\t  tdep -->|\"modal deploy\"| modal\n79\t  classDef step fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n80\t  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n81\t  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n82\t  classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n83\t  class cchk,mig,pchk,pdep,achk,adep,tchk,tdep step\n84\t  class d1 store\n85\t  class cfw,modal ext\n86\t  class push person\n87\t```\n88\t\n89\tEach tree runs **test, lint and types as three parallel jobs** on pushes to `main`, path-filtered to its own directory (`api/**`, `pipeline/**`, `common/**`, `tts/**`), so a docs-only push, or a change to one tree, runs neither another tree's checks nor its deploy. A change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/` against the changed package, inside `common/`'s workflow. The `api`, `pipeline` and `common` jobs install with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` on Node 24 and run `pnpm test`, `pnpm lint` and `pnpm types`; the `tts` jobs provision the pinned toolchain (uv/ruff/ty/pytest, see the repository's `CLAUDE.md`) with `uv sync --frozen` against `tts/`'s lockfile.\n90\t\n91\tThree workflows ship the Cloudflare side with `cloudflare/wrangler-action`, each after its own checks pass: `common/`'s workflow applies its SQL migrations to D1, the pipeline's workflow runs `pnpm build` and deploys `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, and the API's workflow deploys the API Worker. The pipeline and API workflows wait for `common/`'s workflow to succeed before they deploy, and the API deploys after the pipeline. When a check in `common/`'s workflow fails, nothing deploys: no migration, no pipeline deploy and no API deploy. The three authenticate with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`. `tts` adds a **`deploy` job that depends on all three of its checks**, runs `modal deploy` and authenticates with `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` / `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. All four are GitHub Actions secrets.\n92\t\n93\t> [!WARNING] Two migration runs at once are not guarded\n94\t> Nothing serializes two runs of `common/`'s workflow, so two pushes to `main` in quick succession can apply `common/`'s migrations twice at the same time. The chain relies on deploys never being made twice in quick succession.\n95\t\n96\tThe `tts` check jobs install the full dev dependency group (torch-cpu, kokoro, numpy) because `ty` needs them to resolve `app.py`'s image-runtime imports locally; the deploy job installs only the Modal client (`--no-dev`). uv's lockfile-keyed cache absorbs most of the repeat install cost.\n97\t\n98\t## 📦 What binaries the repository accepts\n99\t\n100\tAudio and images are handled differently.\n101\t\n102\t**Audio is ignored outright.** `.gitignore` covers `*.ogg`, `*.wav`, `*.mp3`, `*.m4a`, `*.opus` and `*.flac`, so no audio file can be staged by accident. Every audio file here is output of the TTS pipeline: regenerable for roughly $0.008 an article (see [tts-service](tts-service.md)) and megabytes each, so there is no audio artifact worth the space, and LFS would only make an unwanted commit cheaper rather than preventing it. A test that needs audio generates it into a working directory the ignore already covers.\n103\t\n104\t**Images go through Git LFS.** `.gitattributes` routes `*.png`, `*.jpg`, `*.jpeg` and `*.webp` as LFS pointers, because `web/` will carry real assets that have to be checked in, and a pointer keeps the bytes out of the packfile every clone downloads.\n105\t\n106\t> [!WARNING] A binary committed as a plain blob is permanent until someone rewrites history\n107\t> Deleting the file in a later commit removes it from the working tree and leaves the blob in every clone at full size. Two Opus fixtures, 9.35 MB together, reached pushed history this way, and getting them back out took a `filter-repo` rewrite and a force push over a shared branch: `.git` dropped from 12 MB to 672 KB, but the remote still serves each unreachable blob by hash afterwards, because rewriting history does not reach objects already distributed to other clones and caches.\n108\t\n109\t## ⏩ What is not built yet\n110\t\n111\t`web/` and `api/` are served from separate hosts today; serving them from one origin once `web/` exists is not built.\n112\t\n113\tA Worker deploy that lands while item Workflow instances are running is not handled; a deploy can run a step again, and the item Workflow accepts that for synthesis (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)).\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\tRelated: [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [authentication](authentication.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n118\t---\n119\ttitle: \"TTS service\"\n120\tsummary: \"Kokoro-82M on Modal, a separate deployable the item Workflow reaches over two web endpoints behind Modal's proxy auth: start a synthesis that uploads its audio to a presigned R2 URL, then poll for its timing.\"\n121\tcreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n122\t---\n123\t\n124\t# TTS service\n125\t\n126\t## 🔭 Overview\n127\t\n128\tRenders an item's spoken paragraphs to audio on a GPU host, uploads the audio to the R2 bucket and produces their read-along timing. Lives at `tts/app.py`, deployed as its own Modal app (`nagara-tts`), and is invoked by the item Workflow over HTTP rather than imported. Production and local development call the same Modal app.\n129\t\n130\t> [!NOTE] The TTS code is an image definition, not a callable library\n131\t> The synthesis code *is* the specification of the container image that runs on the GPU host: it declares its own base image, system packages, and model bake step, and is uploaded as a unit. It cannot execute inside a Worker; the item Workflow can only invoke it as a separately deployed service.\n132\t\n133\t## ♠️ What it exposes\n134\t\n135\tTwo web endpoints, both behind Modal's proxy auth, so every request carries the proxy auth headers:\n136\t\n137\t| Endpoint | Takes | Answers |\n138\t|---|---|---|\n139\t| start synthesis | the spoken forms, one string per read-along unit in unit order; the Kokoro voice, defaulting to `af_heart`; the destination URL, a presigned R2 PUT URL for the item's audio key | Modal's call id for this synthesis, at once |\n140\t| fetch result | the call id | not ready while the synthesis runs; once it is done, the result below |\n141\t\n142\t| Result field | Answers |\n143\t|---|---|\n144\t| format | which codec the audio is: `audio/ogg`, or `audio/wav` on the ffmpeg fallback |\n145\t| duration | the total audio length in seconds, what a progress bar divides by |\n146\t| timings | one `{start, end}` window per unit, in seconds, the last `end` equal to the duration; see [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) |\n147\t\n148\tThe result carries no audio bytes, because the audio is already in the bucket under the item's audio key. The pipeline Worker declares the result's shape as a TypeScript type and trusts Modal's reply to match it; nothing checks it at runtime (see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rule the shared types follow).\n149\t\n150\t> [!NOTE] Why Modal uploads the audio instead of returning it\n151\t> A Workflow step can return about 1 MiB, and a full article's audio is far larger. Handing Modal a presigned PUT URL keeps the audio out of the result, so the result endpoint answers with timing and metadata alone. The pipeline Worker signs the URL with an R2 API token of its own, using common/'s presigning function and its audio key function (see [common-package](common-package.md)).\n152\t\n153\t> [!NOTE] Why proxy auth\n154\t> Modal's proxy auth rejects a request at Modal's edge, before any container starts. A shared key the app checks itself works too, but the web container has to start before it can reject the request. The pipeline Worker holds the proxy auth token as a secret.\n155\t\n156\t## 📩 Flow\n157\t\n158\t```mermaid\n159\tflowchart LR\n160\t    P[\"paragraphs[]\"] --> S[\"synthesize each<br/>paragraph with Kokoro\"]\n161\t    S --> A[\"concatenate audio<br/>+ 0.1s gaps\"]\n162\t    S --> T[\"build_timeline:<br/>pause-fold windows\"]\n163\t    A --> E[\"encode: Opus,<br/>WAV fallback\"]\n164\t    E --> U[\"upload to the<br/>destination URL\"]\n165\t    U --> R[\"result: timeline<br/>+ duration + format\"]\n166\t    T --> R\n167\t```\n168\t\n169\tEach paragraph is synthesized independently through Kokoro-82M and its duration recorded, then the audios are concatenated with a fixed 0.1-second silence gap between them: none after the last paragraph, since there is nothing left to separate it from. [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md)'s `build_timeline` turns those per-paragraph durations into contiguous windows over the same concatenated audio. The encoded audio is uploaded to the destination URL, and the result Modal keeps for the call carries the timeline and the duration; the item Workflow joins the timeline onto the display markdown, described in [article-extraction](article-extraction.md), and never reads text back from the result.\n170\t\n171\t> [!NOTE] A newline inside one paragraph string is Kokoro's own split, not this service's\n172\t> Kokoro splits a paragraph string at each newline before synthesizing it and joins those pieces back with no gap between them; the 0.1-second silence only sits between two separate entries of the `paragraphs` list. So a unit whose spoken form carries several lines, such as a list's items or a table's rows (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)), plays as one continuous paragraph audio with no silence added between its lines. The contract does not change. `paragraphs` stays `list[str]`, one string per unit, whether that string carries one line or several.\n173\t\n174\tAudio is encoded to Opus through a `soundfile`-then-`ffmpeg` pipeline; if that codec path fails, it falls back to plain WAV. \"Playable audio\" is the only property either path is judged on, and WAV runs roughly 18 times larger than the equivalent Opus for a full article.\n175\t\n176\tGPU and memory snapshotting are both enabled on the deployed class, bringing cold start down from roughly 27 seconds to roughly 6; a container then stays warm for 300 seconds after its last call, to absorb a session's burst of pushes.\n177\t\n178\t## 📡 How the item Workflow invokes it with no broker\n179\t\n180\t```mermaid\n181\tsequenceDiagram\n182\t    autonumber\n183\t    participant W as Item Workflow\n184\t    participant M as Modal TTS\n185\t    participant R as R2 bucket\n186\t    W->>M: start synthesis with spoken forms, voice and PUT URL\n187\t    M-->>W: call id\n188\t    M->>R: PUT the encoded audio\n189\t    loop until the result is ready\n190\t        W->>M: fetch result for the call id\n191\t        M-->>W: not ready, or timings, duration and format\n192\t    end\n193\t```\n194\t\n195\tThe item Workflow's start synthesis step signs the PUT URL and calls the start endpoint, which spawns the synthesis and answers with Modal's call id at once (1, 2). Modal uploads the audio to the bucket when the synthesis finishes (3). The poll step asks the result endpoint for the call id until the result is ready (4, 5), and the store step writes the finished state into the item row together with the tts ledger row, priced by the audio's length (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The call id and the result pass from step to step as step results; the item row holds neither. Each step gets 3 attempts: start synthesis has 2 minutes per attempt and each poll 1 minute.\n196\t\n197\tA synthesis that fails every time fails the item once the poll step has used its attempts, and so does a result Modal has discarded, which happens 7 days after the call.\n198\t\n199\t> [!WARNING] A start repeated after a deploy synthesizes twice\n200\t> Modal accepts no idempotency key and no caller-chosen call id, so a start synthesis step that runs again after a deploy spawns a second synthesis. Both upload to the same audio key, keyed by the item id, so the second upload overwrites the first and the item still has one audio file. The cost is one extra synthesis, which is accepted.\n201\t\n202\t> [!NOTE] Why the compute platform is the async layer, and there is no broker\n203\t> The platform's own invocation primitives (spawn plus a non-blocking result read, exposed as the two endpoints) are sufficient async infrastructure at this scale: the running-versus-crashed distinction comes for free from the result read, not from anything the item Workflow tracks separately. The two deployables also ship independently: the TTS service ships on its own deploy cadence (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)), decoupled from the Workers.\n204\t\n205\t> [!TIP] Rejected: a broker and a worker process\n206\t> A conventional task-queue stack, a message broker plus a separate worker, would add infrastructure to run, scale, and monitor for no benefit the platform's own spawn/poll primitives do not already provide, and it would still need the same crash/running bookkeeping the result read gives for free.\n207\t\n208\t> [!NOTE] Clients poll; nothing pushes completion\n209\t> A client observes `generating` until the item Workflow writes `ready` or `failed` into the item row, and its next poll reads it. There is no notification path; see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) for the state machine this participates in.\n210\t\n211\tThe execution layer is coupled to Modal as a result: moving off it means replacing both the compute host and the async layer, and the GPU with them.\n212\t\n213\tIn local development Modal still runs in the cloud and uploads into the local bucket through a Cloudflare quick tunnel; [local-development](local-development.md) explains how the PUT URL reaches it.\n214\t\n215\t## ⏩ What is not built yet\n216\t\n217\tStreaming paragraph audio from one warm container, so time-to-first-audio is roughly one to two seconds instead of waiting for the whole article. Word-level timestamps exist inside Kokoro but are not returned or used; a caption export would be the consumer that asks for them.\n218\t\n219\t---\n220\t\n221\tRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n222\t---\n223\ttitle: \"Authentication\"\n224\tsummary: \"A single API key acts as the user's identity and guards every item route; audio and images reach a browser through a one-hour presigned R2 link the API Worker signs only for an authenticated caller.\"\n225\tcreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n226\t---\n227\t\n228\t# Authentication\n229\t\n230\t## 🔭 Overview\n231\t\n232\tItems are private: an article someone enqueues is theirs, not public. A single API key is the user's identity, and it guards every route that touches an item.\n233\t\n234\t## ⚙️ Implementation details\n235\t\n236\tThe API Worker checks the key once, in front of every route under `/items`, so no handler checks it individually; a missing or wrong key answers `401`. The key is a secret only the API Worker holds.\n237\t\n238\tPossessing the key **is** being the user: there is no separate identity, session, or login. The key is presented on an `X-API-Key` header and checked against the single configured value on every item route: creation, polling, retry, audio and images alike. `/health` is the one public route, and it carries no item data, a deliberate, narrow exception for platform health checks (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)).\n239\t\n240\t> [!NOTE] Why one key rather than sessions or OAuth, for now\n241\t> The first consumer is an agent (Tachikoma), not a browser: it wants to present one credential on each call, not run an interactive login. There is no audience yet to onboard with real accounts; multi-user identity comes later, once anyone besides the owner wants this, and the private queue works for its first user without it.\n242\t\n243\t## 📩 How audio reaches a browser without the key\n244\t\n245\tThe audio and image routes require the key to **obtain** a link, and the audio route mints one only for a `ready` item. Each answers with a `307` to a GET URL for the object in the R2 bucket, signed by the API Worker with its own R2 API token for one hour, in every environment (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The URL itself carries the signature, so the request that follows it needs no header at all. The bucket stays private: every way to read it is a link minted to an authenticated caller, and those links expire.\n246\t\n247\tOnly a backend holds the API key. A web app's own backend calls the route, takes the presigned URL from the `307`, and hands that URL to the browser. The audio route accepts no key in the query string, so the key never reaches a page.\n248\t\n249\t```mermaid\n250\tsequenceDiagram\n251\t    participant Backend as Client backend\n252\t    participant API as API Worker\n253\t    participant R2 as R2 bucket\n254\t    participant Browser\n255\t    Backend->>API: GET /items/id/audio with X-API-Key\n256\t    API->>API: key valid and item ready?\n257\t    API->>API: sign a GET URL for one hour\n258\t    API-->>Backend: 307 to the presigned URL\n259\t    Backend-->>Browser: the presigned URL\n260\t    Browser->>R2: GET the presigned URL, no header\n261\t    R2-->>Browser: audio bytes\n262\t```\n263\t\n264\t> [!NOTE] Why the route mints a link instead of gating the bytes\n265\t> A browser `<audio>` element cannot attach a custom header to the request it makes for the audio file, so the key alone cannot gate the byte stream. Gating the link that leads to the bytes is the check a browser can pass.\n266\t\n267\t## ⏩ What is not built yet\n268\t\n269\t**Multi-user is a rewrite of the guard.** Going multi-user means introducing genuine identity and per-user key management, with a Google OAuth login taking the place of the single-key shim rather than sitting beside it. See [the queue](../product-design/queue.md) for what a user sees once multiple keys and key management exist.\n270\t\n271\t---\n272\t\n273\tRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n274\t---\n275\ttitle: \"Image and code describer\"\n276\tsummary: \"The Gemini describer, reached through AI Gateway with its response cache: one generated sentence for a block a listener can't see, reused by the code path and the image path, floored and capped against one shared per-item budget.\"\n277\tcreated: \"2026-08-16\"\n278\t---\n279\t\n280\t# Image and code describer\n281\t\n282\t\n283\t## 🔭 Overview\n284\t\n285\tGives a listener one spoken sentence for a block they cannot see: a code block, or an image with no author-written text. It is a reusable core with two callers: the code path and the image path both reach the same Gemini call, layered under a fan-out that floors, caps, and fails per unit. It never speaks what a block *means*, only what it *is* or *shows*: meaning comes from the author, and the describer only makes the block visible. It runs as the item Workflow's describe step, which starts one child step per description (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)), and every call goes through AI Gateway with the gateway's response cache on.\n286\t\n287\t\n288\t> [!NOTE] The describer is unchanged by the recipes architecture\n289\t> Recipes stop at units ([recipes](recipes.md)); describing code and images stays this generic path, whatever extracted them.\n290\t\n291\t## ♠️ What it exposes\n292\t\n293\t| Member | Answers |\n294\t|---|---|\n295\t| the core call | one structured-output call plus the sanitize tail, returning the spoken sentence. The caller owns the contents: a prompt string, or a prompt plus the image as an inline part |\n296\t| the code prompt | kind and what-for, no opener, the introducing unit as authority |\n297\t| the image prompt | one specific sentence of what is shown, alt passed as unreliable context |\n298\t| an image describe request | one image the precedence flagged for a describe (case 2): its index in the unit list, its raw alt, and the stored image, a WebP raster or the SVG's markup |\n299\t| the fan-out | describe every code unit and every flagged image against one shared budget, in document order, returning the updated units and a degradation list |\n300\t\n301\t## 📞 The core call: structured output, then sanitize\n302\t\n303\tThe core call makes one call to `gemini-3.5-flash-lite` through AI Gateway, parses the JSON, and runs the value through the shared sanitize tail. The response schema is a JSON object with one required string field, `spoken`.\n304\t\n305\tThe response is requested as `application/json` against that schema, so the model returns `{\"spoken\": \"...\"}` and there is nothing to parse out of prose. The parsed `spoken` then passes through the sanitize tail article prose runs through, including the rule that replaces a marker only where it touches text and the rule that speaks a comparison operator as a word (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)).\n306\t\n307\tA raster goes to the model as the stored WebP image. An SVG goes as its markup, an inline part with the `text/plain` type, whatever its size.\n308\t\n309\t> [!NOTE] Why an SVG is described from its markup\n310\t> The model reads text the markup carries, such as a formula's LaTeX in a `<title>` element, so it names an equation that a rasterised copy only lets it read out symbol by symbol. Every SVG tried this way came back with a usable description. The markup's token count grows with its size, from under a thousand tokens for a small badge to about 28 thousand for a 63 KB chart, and no cap bounds it.\n311\t\n312\t> [!NOTE] Both guards, because a leaked marker is stochastic and only ever caught by ear\n313\t> Structured output makes one whole failure class impossible: the model cannot open with a heading or a preamble when the value is a bare string field. But a JSON schema cannot forbid a backtick or a `**` *inside* that string, and a marker reached narration exactly once in the bake-off and did not reproduce across 48 re-runs. \"It did not happen this time\" is the judgement a pipeline cannot rely on, so sanitize catches the marker the schema cannot.\n314\t\n315\t> [!NOTE] The schema is not repeated in the prompt\n316\t> Gemini's own docs warn that duplicating the schema, or JSON examples, in the prompt lowers output quality. The config carries the shape; the prompt describes only the task.\n317\t\n318\t### 🗄️ The gateway's response cache\n319\t\n320\tAI Gateway answers an identical request from its response cache without calling Gemini, and reports the hit in the `cf-aig-cache-status` response header. A describe repeated with the same prompt and the same image, such as a code snippet two articles share, costs no model call. Each successful describe writes one ledger row at the flat describe price, whether Gemini or the cache answered, and sets the row's cached flag when the header reports a hit (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)).\n321\t\n322\t## ✍️ The prompts: no opener, an invention guard, minimal asymmetric context\n323\t\n324\tBoth prompts share three rules and differ only in subject.\n325\t\n326\t**No self-opener.** The sentence must begin with the thing itself, the kind-noun for code or the visible subject for an image, and the word \"This\" is forbidden anywhere in it. `Code: ` and `Image: ` are nagara's announcement, so the model owns only the content. An image noun-opens naturally; code needs the ban spelled out, because forbidding \"This block\" and \"This example\" alone leaves a residual \"This is a…\".\n327\t\n328\t**The invention guard** is a sharpened positive instruction with no few-shot. Code: name the kind and the purpose, never the mechanics, return values, or framework, unless the introducing text states them. Image: describe only what is visibly there, and **name no person, organization, brand, place, or region unless that exact name appears as written text inside the image**. The one confirmed invention in the bake-off was geography read onto an artwork, and no run under this guard reproduced it.\n329\t\n330\t**Context is minimal and asymmetric.** The code prompt gets the article title, the introducing paragraph (the unit just before, which the listener heard seconds ago as authority), and the block. The paragraph *after* is dropped. The image prompt gets the title and the alt, labelled as often wrong or SEO filler, to use only where it matches the pixels.\n331\t\n332\t> [!NOTE] Why the paragraph after the block is dropped\n333\t> It is the elaboration the listener is about to hear anyway, and including it nudges the model toward describing behaviour rather than naming what the block is.\n334\t\n335\t> [!NOTE] Why one sentence per code block, even when the prose already named it\n336\t> Every code block gets its own spoken sentence because it needs its own audio window in the read-along, and dropping it would remove the code from a reader's view. The redundancy with the introducing prose is the price of that window. What the sentence adds above restatement is the *kind*: \"the syntax for creating a variable\" names the concept; the specific field names and query values that would add most are exactly what the guard forbids as invention.\n337\t\n338\t## 🗣️ What each unit says\n339\t\n340\tA **code** unit's spoken form becomes `Code: <sentence>`, overwriting the interim `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder from the spoken-form derivation.\n341\t\n342\tAn **image** follows a four-step precedence, top to bottom. Cases 1, 3, and 4 are the fallback already on the unit from acquisition (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)); the describer owns case 2 and the filter that routes between 1 and 2.\n343\t\n344\t| # | Condition | Spoken form |\n345\t|---|---|---|\n346\t| 1 | good alt | the alt, verbatim |\n347\t| 2 | no good alt | **one generated sentence of what the image shows** |\n348\t| 3 | generation failed, alt non-empty | any alt, verbatim |\n349\t| 4 | generation failed, alt empty | `Image with no description.` |\n350\t\n351\tThe **good-alt filter** decides case 1 against case 2. Alt is trusted verbatim only when it reads as a grammatical sentence, is not the article title (the cruft test in [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)), and clears a small CMS denylist (`subscribe`, `appears in`, `courtesy`, `photograph by`, `click`) and filename patterns. Everything else goes to the describer: empty, keyword soup, title-as-alt, a subscribe prompt.\n352\t\n353\t> [!NOTE] The denylist separates good alt from boilerplate where no clean heuristic exists\n354\t> \"Image of tank rolling over a world map\" and \"This article appears in the October 2023 issue. Subscribe to WIRED.\" are *both* grammatical sentences, so grammaticality alone cannot separate good alt from boilerplate. The denylist keeps the first and sends the second to the describer, at the cost of a maintained list a novel boilerplate phrase can slip past.\n355\t\n356\t> [!NOTE] A describe failure never goes silent\n357\t> On failure or over-budget, an image keeps its precedence fallback (any non-empty alt, else the floor), so invariant 2's drop-from-both never fires for a *description* failure. It fires one layer earlier, for an *acquisition* failure, which belongs to [article-extraction](article-extraction.md). The floor is honest: it tells the listener an image is there that could not be described.\n358\t\n359\t## 🔀 The fan-out, and its bounds\n360\t\n361\tThe fan-out walks the interleaved unit list once into one document-ordered job list of every code unit and every flagged image. That single ordered list is what makes the cap a shared budget across both kinds.\n362\t\n363\t```mermaid\n364\tflowchart TD\n365\t    J[\"jobs in document order:<br/>code units + flagged images\"] --> C{\"within the<br/>per-item cap?\"}\n366\t    C -->|beyond cap| Floor[\"code floors; image keeps<br/>its precedence fallback;<br/>degrade: cap reached\"]\n367\t    C -->|within cap| G[\"one child step per job,<br/>all started at once,<br/>at most 6 calls in flight\"]\n368\t    G --> R{\"per child\"}\n369\t    R -->|success| W[\"Code:/Image: sentence;<br/>one ledger row\"]\n370\t    R -->|failure| D[\"code floors; image<br/>keeps fallback; degrade:<br/>describe failed\"]\n371\t    W --> Done[\"one write at the end:<br/>spoken forms, degradations,<br/>ledger rows\"]\n372\t    D --> Done\n373\t    Floor --> Done\n374\t```\n375\t\n376\t**One shared budget, counted in document order.** The per-item cap (default 25) caps the combined code-and-image total, so the two kinds can never each spend a full budget. Past the cap a code unit floors and an image keeps its precedence fallback; each records a `describe cap reached` degradation. The cap never fails an item and never goes silent.\n377\t\n378\t**One child step per description.** The describe step runs inside the wrapper like every other step, so it writes `current_step` and the status as it starts. Inside it, one child step per job within the cap starts at once, and the describe step awaits them together with `Promise.allSettled`. Each child calls Gemini and returns its result without writing to the item row. The describe step collects the results and writes every unit's spoken form, the degradations and the ledger rows in one write at its end. A fifty-unit article where one call fails keeps the other forty-nine, and the describe step never fails because a child failed.\n379\t\n380\tA Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections at a time, across all its steps, so at most 6 Gemini calls are in flight and the rest wait for a free connection.\n381\t\n382\t> [!NOTE] Why the children return results and one write lands at the end\n383\t> The children run concurrently, and a child that wrote its own unit into the row would race its siblings on the same units column. Returning results to the describe step leaves it as the only writer, so the row changes once, with every unit's spoken form, degradation and ledger row together.\n384\t\n385\t> [!WARNING] Waiting for a connection counts toward a step's timeout\n386\t> All the child steps start together, but their calls run 6 at a time, and the time a child waits for a free connection counts toward its 1-minute timeout. In the spike that measured it, the last of 25 five-second calls finished 26 s after the fan-out started. A slow gateway or a full budget of large SVGs can push the last children in the queue past their timeout, and each such child fails that attempt.\n387\t\n388\t**Retry policy.** The describe step has 3 attempts of 5 minutes each, and each child step has 3 attempts of 1 minute each. A retry of the describe step runs every child again as new steps, so a repeated describe goes back to AI Gateway, whose response cache answers a request identical to one that already succeeded.\n389\t\n390\t**A failed description degrades the unit and never fails the item.** A unit whose child step failed gets a `describe failed` degradation and floors (code) or keeps the fallback (image). The describe phase never fails the item, even when every description failed: a code-bearing item whose Gemini calls all failed reaches `ready` with every code block on the floor `\"Code with no description.\"` and a degradation for each.\n391\t\n392\tThe pipeline Worker holds the Gemini key and the AI Gateway token as secrets. A failed or capped unit made no billable call, so only a successful describe writes a ledger row.\n393\t\n394\t## ⏩ What is not built yet\n395\t\n396\t- **The fallback model is not wired.** `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` is named in the code as the fallback for when 3.5 is unavailable, but nothing ever selects it: every call goes to `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`. It stays unwired because under structured output it was seen to write \"A TypeScript interface…\" for a one-line JSON block, a kind-level error rather than a behaviour-level one.\n397\t\n398\t---\n399\t\n400\tRelated: [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [what-gets-read-aloud](../product-design/what-gets-read-aloud.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n401\t"}]},"uuid":"8af7499e-869c-401e-8f21-eab2da98fe95","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:48:10.173Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agus/.claude/projects/-home-agus-workspace-asermax-nagara/f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e/tool-results/bhv7e6i1p.txt","content":"# Deployment and CI\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nNagara ships as three deployables: the API Worker and the pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, and the TTS service on Modal. Each tree runs its own CI checks, path-filtered to its directory. The two Workers deploy with wrangler in a fixed order, after `common/`'s migrations are applied to D1; `tts/` deploys to Modal on its own.\n\n## ☁️ How the Workers deploy\n\nBoth Workers deploy with wrangler. Their configurations bind the same D1 database and the same R2 bucket, and the API Worker's configuration binds the item Workflow by the pipeline Worker's script name. `nagara.asermax.com` points at the API Worker, the only Worker clients reach.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the pipeline Worker has no public routes\n> The API Worker creates and restarts the item Workflow through the cross-script Workflow binding, and nothing else calls the pipeline. With no route to reach, the pipeline Worker carries no authentication of its own and no edge policy guards it.\n\n`api/` deploys with wrangler from its own configuration, from within `api/` or via CI below; see [item-contract](item-contract.md) for the routes it serves.\n\n`pipeline/` deploys with `pnpm deploy`, from within `pipeline/` or via CI below; see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) for what runs on it. That script builds before it ships: `pnpm build` runs Vite with the Flue and Cloudflare plugins, writing the bundled Worker and a generated `wrangler.json` into `dist/nagara/`, and `wrangler deploy -c dist/nagara/wrangler.json` ships that generated config rather than the hand-written `pipeline/wrangler.jsonc`. The Flue plugin adds the author and revision agents' Durable Object bindings at build time, so the source config alone would ship a Worker without them.\n\n`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which its own CI workflow applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why migrations, then the pipeline, then the API\n> Both Workers read and write D1, so the schema they build against is applied before either deploys. The API Worker's Workflow binding fails with `workflow.not_found` until the pipeline Worker that holds the item Workflow is deployed, so the pipeline always goes before the API.\n\n## 🔐 Which secrets each Worker holds\n\nEach Worker holds only the secrets its own calls need.\n\n| Worker | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| API Worker | the API key, and an R2 API token it signs GET URLs with |\n| pipeline Worker | the Firecrawl and Gemini keys, the AI Gateway token, Modal's proxy auth token, and an R2 API token it signs PUT URLs with |\n\n## 🎙️ How the TTS service deploys\n\n`tts/` is pushed independently with `modal deploy`, from within `tts/` or via CI below; see [tts-service](tts-service.md) for what actually runs on Modal.\n\n## 🔁 CI: one path-filtered workflow per tree, deploys in a fixed order\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  push@{ shape: stadium, label: \"Push to main\" }\n  subgraph cw [\"common workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    cchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types,<br/>both Workers' types\" }\n    mig@{ shape: rect, label: \"1 apply common/<br/>migrations to D1\" }\n    cchk --> mig\n  end\n  subgraph pw [\"pipeline workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    pchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n    pdep@{ shape: rect, label: \"2 deploy the<br/>pipeline Worker\" }\n    pchk --> pdep\n  end\n  subgraph aw [\"api workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    achk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n    adep@{ shape: rect, label: \"3 deploy the<br/>API Worker\" }\n    achk --> adep\n  end\n  subgraph tw [\"tts workflow\"]\n    direction TB\n    tchk@{ shape: rect, label: \"test, lint, types<br/>(parallel)\" }\n    tdep@{ shape: rect, label: \"deploy tts/<br/>to Modal\" }\n    tchk --> tdep\n  end\n  d1@{ shape: cyl, label: \"D1\" }\n  cfw@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Cloudflare<br/>Workers\" }\n  modal@{ shape: cloud, label: \"Modal\" }\n  push -->|\"common/\"| cchk\n  push -->|\"pipeline/\"| pchk\n  push -->|\"api/\"| achk\n  push -->|\"tts/\"| tchk\n  mig -.->|\"waits for\"| pdep\n  mig -.->|\"waits for\"| adep\n  pdep -.->|\"waits for\"| adep\n  mig -->|\"wrangler\"| d1\n  pdep -->|\"wrangler\"| cfw\n  adep -->|\"wrangler\"| cfw\n  tdep -->|\"modal deploy\"| modal\n  classDef step fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n  classDef store fill:#ffedd5,stroke:#ea580c\n  classDef ext fill:#ede9fe,stroke:#7c3aed\n  classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n  class cchk,mig,pchk,pdep,achk,adep,tchk,tdep step\n  class d1 store\n  class cfw,modal ext\n  class push person\n```\n\nEach tree runs **test, lint and types as three parallel jobs** on pushes to `main`, path-filtered to its own directory (`api/**`, `pipeline/**`, `common/**`, `tts/**`), so a docs-only push, or a change to one tree, runs neither another tree's checks nor its deploy. A change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/` against the changed package, inside `common/`'s workflow. The `api`, `pipeline` and `common` jobs install with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` on Node 24 and run `pnpm test`, `pnpm lint` and `pnpm types`; the `tts` jobs provision the pinned toolchain (uv/ruff/ty/pytest, see the repository's `CLAUDE.md`) with `uv sync --frozen` against `tts/`'s lockfile.\n\nThree workflows ship the Cloudflare side with `cloudflare/wrangler-action`, each after its own checks pass: `common/`'s workflow applies its SQL migrations to D1, the pipeline's workflow runs `pnpm build` and deploys `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, and the API's workflow deploys the API Worker. The pipeline and API workflows wait for `common/`'s workflow to succeed before they deploy, and the API deploys after the pipeline. When a check in `common/`'s workflow fails, nothing deploys: no migration, no pipeline deploy and no API deploy. The three authenticate with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`. `tts` adds a **`deploy` job that depends on all three of its checks**, runs `modal deploy` and authenticates with `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` / `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. All four are GitHub Actions secrets.\n\n> [!WARNING] Two migration runs at once are not guarded\n> Nothing serializes two runs of `common/`'s workflow, so two pushes to `main` in quick succession can apply `common/`'s migrations twice at the same time. The chain relies on deploys never being made twice in quick succession.\n\nThe `tts` check jobs install the full dev dependency group (torch-cpu, kokoro, numpy) because `ty` needs them to resolve `app.py`'s image-runtime imports locally; the deploy job installs only the Modal client (`--no-dev`). uv's lockfile-keyed cache absorbs most of the repeat install cost.\n\n## 📦 What binaries the repository accepts\n\nAudio and images are handled differently.\n\n**Audio is ignored outright.** `.gitignore` covers `*.ogg`, `*.wav`, `*.mp3`, `*.m4a`, `*.opus` and `*.flac`, so no audio file can be staged by accident. Every audio file here is output of the TTS pipeline: regenerable for roughly $0.008 an article (see [tts-service](tts-service.md)) and megabytes each, so there is no audio artifact worth the space, and LFS would only make an unwanted commit cheaper rather than preventing it. A test that needs audio generates it into a working directory the ignore already covers.\n\n**Images go through Git LFS.** `.gitattributes` routes `*.png`, `*.jpg`, `*.jpeg` and `*.webp` as LFS pointers, because `web/` will carry real assets that have to be checked in, and a pointer keeps the bytes out of the packfile every clone downloads.\n\n> [!WARNING] A binary committed as a plain blob is permanent until someone rewrites history\n> Deleting the file in a later commit removes it from the working tree and leaves the blob in every clone at full size. Two Opus fixtures, 9.35 MB together, reached pushed history this way, and getting them back out took a `filter-repo` rewrite and a force push over a shared branch: `.git` dropped from 12 MB to 672 KB, but the remote still serves each unreachable blob by hash afterwards, because rewriting history does not reach objects already distributed to other clones and caches.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n`web/` and `api/` are served from separate hosts today; serving them from one origin once `web/` exists is not built.\n\nA Worker deploy that lands while item Workflow instances are running is not handled; a deploy can run a step again, and the item Workflow accepts that for synthesis (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)).\n\n---\n\nRelated: [tts-service](tts-service.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [authentication](authentication.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"TTS service\"\nsummary: \"Kokoro-82M on Modal, a separate deployable the item Workflow reaches over two web endpoints behind Modal's proxy auth: start a synthesis that uploads its audio to a presigned R2 URL, then poll for its timing.\"\ncreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n---\n\n# TTS service\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nRenders an item's spoken paragraphs to audio on a GPU host, uploads the audio to the R2 bucket and produces their read-along timing. Lives at `tts/app.py`, deployed as its own Modal app (`nagara-tts`), and is invoked by the item Workflow over HTTP rather than imported. Production and local development call the same Modal app.\n\n> [!NOTE] The TTS code is an image definition, not a callable library\n> The synthesis code *is* the specification of the container image that runs on the GPU host: it declares its own base image, system packages, and model bake step, and is uploaded as a unit. It cannot execute inside a Worker; the item Workflow can only invoke it as a separately deployed service.\n\n## ♠️ What it exposes\n\nTwo web endpoints, both behind Modal's proxy auth, so every request carries the proxy auth headers:\n\n| Endpoint | Takes | Answers |\n|---|---|---|\n| start synthesis | the spoken forms, one string per read-along unit in unit order; the Kokoro voice, defaulting to `af_heart`; the destination URL, a presigned R2 PUT URL for the item's audio key | Modal's call id for this synthesis, at once |\n| fetch result | the call id | not ready while the synthesis runs; once it is done, the result below |\n\n| Result field | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| format | which codec the audio is: `audio/ogg`, or `audio/wav` on the ffmpeg fallback |\n| duration | the total audio length in seconds, what a progress bar divides by |\n| timings | one `{start, end}` window per unit, in seconds, the last `end` equal to the duration; see [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) |\n\nThe result carries no audio bytes, because the audio is already in the bucket under the item's audio key. The pipeline Worker declares the result's shape as a TypeScript type and trusts Modal's reply to match it; nothing checks it at runtime (see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rule the shared types follow).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why Modal uploads the audio instead of returning it\n> A Workflow step can return about 1 MiB, and a full article's audio is far larger. Handing Modal a presigned PUT URL keeps the audio out of the result, so the result endpoint answers with timing and metadata alone. The pipeline Worker signs the URL with an R2 API token of its own, using common/'s presigning function and its audio key function (see [common-package](common-package.md)).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why proxy auth\n> Modal's proxy auth rejects a request at Modal's edge, before any container starts. A shared key the app checks itself works too, but the web container has to start before it can reject the request. The pipeline Worker holds the proxy auth token as a secret.\n\n## 📩 Flow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    P[\"paragraphs[]\"] --> S[\"synthesize each<br/>paragraph with Kokoro\"]\n    S --> A[\"concatenate audio<br/>+ 0.1s gaps\"]\n    S --> T[\"build_timeline:<br/>pause-fold windows\"]\n    A --> E[\"encode: Opus,<br/>WAV fallback\"]\n    E --> U[\"upload to the<br/>destination URL\"]\n    U --> R[\"result: timeline<br/>+ duration + format\"]\n    T --> R\n```\n\nEach paragraph is synthesized independently through Kokoro-82M and its duration recorded, then the audios are concatenated with a fixed 0.1-second silence gap between them: none after the last paragraph, since there is nothing left to separate it from. [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md)'s `build_timeline` turns those per-paragraph durations into contiguous windows over the same concatenated audio. The encoded audio is uploaded to the destination URL, and the result Modal keeps for the call carries the timeline and the duration; the item Workflow joins the timeline onto the display markdown, described in [article-extraction](article-extraction.md), and never reads text back from the result.\n\n> [!NOTE] A newline inside one paragraph string is Kokoro's own split, not this service's\n> Kokoro splits a paragraph string at each newline before synthesizing it and joins those pieces back with no gap between them; the 0.1-second silence only sits between two separate entries of the `paragraphs` list. So a unit whose spoken form carries several lines, such as a list's items or a table's rows (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)), plays as one continuous paragraph audio with no silence added between its lines. The contract does not change. `paragraphs` stays `list[str]`, one string per unit, whether that string carries one line or several.\n\nAudio is encoded to Opus through a `soundfile`-then-`ffmpeg` pipeline; if that codec path fails, it falls back to plain WAV. \"Playable audio\" is the only property either path is judged on, and WAV runs roughly 18 times larger than the equivalent Opus for a full article.\n\nGPU and memory snapshotting are both enabled on the deployed class, bringing cold start down from roughly 27 seconds to roughly 6; a container then stays warm for 300 seconds after its last call, to absorb a session's burst of pushes.\n\n## 📡 How the item Workflow invokes it with no broker\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant W as Item Workflow\n    participant M as Modal TTS\n    participant R as R2 bucket\n    W->>M: start synthesis with spoken forms, voice and PUT URL\n    M-->>W: call id\n    M->>R: PUT the encoded audio\n    loop until the result is ready\n        W->>M: fetch result for the call id\n        M-->>W: not ready, or timings, duration and format\n    end\n```\n\nThe item Workflow's start synthesis step signs the PUT URL and calls the start endpoint, which spawns the synthesis and answers with Modal's call id at once (1, 2). Modal uploads the audio to the bucket when the synthesis finishes (3). The poll step asks the result endpoint for the call id until the result is ready (4, 5), and the store step writes the finished state into the item row together with the tts ledger row, priced by the audio's length (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The call id and the result pass from step to step as step results; the item row holds neither. Each step gets 3 attempts: start synthesis has 2 minutes per attempt and each poll 1 minute.\n\nA synthesis that fails every time fails the item once the poll step has used its attempts, and so does a result Modal has discarded, which happens 7 days after the call.\n\n> [!WARNING] A start repeated after a deploy synthesizes twice\n> Modal accepts no idempotency key and no caller-chosen call id, so a start synthesis step that runs again after a deploy spawns a second synthesis. Both upload to the same audio key, keyed by the item id, so the second upload overwrites the first and the item still has one audio file. The cost is one extra synthesis, which is accepted.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the compute platform is the async layer, and there is no broker\n> The platform's own invocation primitives (spawn plus a non-blocking result read, exposed as the two endpoints) are sufficient async infrastructure at this scale: the running-versus-crashed distinction comes for free from the result read, not from anything the item Workflow tracks separately. The two deployables also ship independently: the TTS service ships on its own deploy cadence (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)), decoupled from the Workers.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: a broker and a worker process\n> A conventional task-queue stack, a message broker plus a separate worker, would add infrastructure to run, scale, and monitor for no benefit the platform's own spawn/poll primitives do not already provide, and it would still need the same crash/running bookkeeping the result read gives for free.\n\n> [!NOTE] Clients poll; nothing pushes completion\n> A client observes `generating` until the item Workflow writes `ready` or `failed` into the item row, and its next poll reads it. There is no notification path; see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) for the state machine this participates in.\n\nThe execution layer is coupled to Modal as a result: moving off it means replacing both the compute host and the async layer, and the GPU with them.\n\nIn local development Modal still runs in the cloud and uploads into the local bucket through a Cloudflare quick tunnel; [local-development](local-development.md) explains how the PUT URL reaches it.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\nStreaming paragraph audio from one warm container, so time-to-first-audio is roughly one to two seconds instead of waiting for the whole article. Word-level timestamps exist inside Kokoro but are not returned or used; a caption export would be the consumer that asks for them.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [read-along-timing](read-along-timing.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [common-package](common-package.md) · [local-development](local-development.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Authentication\"\nsummary: \"A single API key acts as the user's identity and guards every item route; audio and images reach a browser through a one-hour presigned R2 link the API Worker signs only for an authenticated caller.\"\ncreated: \"2026-07-29\"\n---\n\n# Authentication\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nItems are private: an article someone enqueues is theirs, not public. A single API key is the user's identity, and it guards every route that touches an item.\n\n## ⚙️ Implementation details\n\nThe API Worker checks the key once, in front of every route under `/items`, so no handler checks it individually; a missing or wrong key answers `401`. The key is a secret only the API Worker holds.\n\nPossessing the key **is** being the user: there is no separate identity, session, or login. The key is presented on an `X-API-Key` header and checked against the single configured value on every item route: creation, polling, retry, audio and images alike. `/health` is the one public route, and it carries no item data, a deliberate, narrow exception for platform health checks (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)).\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one key rather than sessions or OAuth, for now\n> The first consumer is an agent (Tachikoma), not a browser: it wants to present one credential on each call, not run an interactive login. There is no audience yet to onboard with real accounts; multi-user identity comes later, once anyone besides the owner wants this, and the private queue works for its first user without it.\n\n## 📩 How audio reaches a browser without the key\n\nThe audio and image routes require the key to **obtain** a link, and the audio route mints one only for a `ready` item. Each answers with a `307` to a GET URL for the object in the R2 bucket, signed by the API Worker with its own R2 API token for one hour, in every environment (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The URL itself carries the signature, so the request that follows it needs no header at all. The bucket stays private: every way to read it is a link minted to an authenticated caller, and those links expire.\n\nOnly a backend holds the API key. A web app's own backend calls the route, takes the presigned URL from the `307`, and hands that URL to the browser. The audio route accepts no key in the query string, so the key never reaches a page.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Backend as Client backend\n    participant API as API Worker\n    participant R2 as R2 bucket\n    participant Browser\n    Backend->>API: GET /items/id/audio with X-API-Key\n    API->>API: key valid and item ready?\n    API->>API: sign a GET URL for one hour\n    API-->>Backend: 307 to the presigned URL\n    Backend-->>Browser: the presigned URL\n    Browser->>R2: GET the presigned URL, no header\n    R2-->>Browser: audio bytes\n```\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the route mints a link instead of gating the bytes\n> A browser `<audio>` element cannot attach a custom header to the request it makes for the audio file, so the key alone cannot gate the byte stream. Gating the link that leads to the bytes is the check a browser can pass.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n**Multi-user is a rewrite of the guard.** Going multi-user means introducing genuine identity and per-user key management, with a Google OAuth login taking the place of the single-key shim rather than sitting beside it. See [the queue](../product-design/queue.md) for what a user sees once multiple keys and key management exist.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n---\ntitle: \"Image and code describer\"\nsummary: \"The Gemini describer, reached through AI Gateway with its response cache: one generated sentence for a block a listener can't see, reused by the code path and the image path, floored and capped against one shared per-item budget.\"\ncreated: \"2026-08-16\"\n---\n\n# Image and code describer\n\n\n## 🔭 Overview\n\nGives a listener one spoken sentence for a block they cannot see: a code block, or an image with no author-written text. It is a reusable core with two callers: the code path and the image path both reach the same Gemini call, layered under a fan-out that floors, caps, and fails per unit. It never speaks what a block *means*, only what it *is* or *shows*: meaning comes from the author, and the describer only makes the block visible. It runs as the item Workflow's describe step, which starts one child step per description (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)), and every call goes through AI Gateway with the gateway's response cache on.\n\n\n> [!NOTE] The describer is unchanged by the recipes architecture\n> Recipes stop at units ([recipes](recipes.md)); describing code and images stays this generic path, whatever extracted them.\n\n## ♠️ What it exposes\n\n| Member | Answers |\n|---|---|\n| the core call | one structured-output call plus the sanitize tail, returning the spoken sentence. The caller owns the contents: a prompt string, or a prompt plus the image as an inline part |\n| the code prompt | kind and what-for, no opener, the introducing unit as authority |\n| the image prompt | one specific sentence of what is shown, alt passed as unreliable context |\n| an image describe request | one image the precedence flagged for a describe (case 2): its index in the unit list, its raw alt, and the stored image, a WebP raster or the SVG's markup |\n| the fan-out | describe every code unit and every flagged image against one shared budget, in document order, returning the updated units and a degradation list |\n\n## 📞 The core call: structured output, then sanitize\n\nThe core call makes one call to `gemini-3.5-flash-lite` through AI Gateway, parses the JSON, and runs the value through the shared sanitize tail. The response schema is a JSON object with one required string field, `spoken`.\n\nThe response is requested as `application/json` against that schema, so the model returns `{\"spoken\": \"...\"}` and there is nothing to parse out of prose. The parsed `spoken` then passes through the sanitize tail article prose runs through, including the rule that replaces a marker only where it touches text and the rule that speaks a comparison operator as a word (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)).\n\nA raster goes to the model as the stored WebP image. An SVG goes as its markup, an inline part with the `text/plain` type, whatever its size.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why an SVG is described from its markup\n> The model reads text the markup carries, such as a formula's LaTeX in a `<title>` element, so it names an equation that a rasterised copy only lets it read out symbol by symbol. Every SVG tried this way came back with a usable description. The markup's token count grows with its size, from under a thousand tokens for a small badge to about 28 thousand for a 63 KB chart, and no cap bounds it.\n\n> [!NOTE] Both guards, because a leaked marker is stochastic and only ever caught by ear\n> Structured output makes one whole failure class impossible: the model cannot open with a heading or a preamble when the value is a bare string field. But a JSON schema cannot forbid a backtick or a `**` *inside* that string, and a marker reached narration exactly once in the bake-off and did not reproduce across 48 re-runs. \"It did not happen this time\" is the judgement a pipeline cannot rely on, so sanitize catches the marker the schema cannot.\n\n> [!NOTE] The schema is not repeated in the prompt\n> Gemini's own docs warn that duplicating the schema, or JSON examples, in the prompt lowers output quality. The config carries the shape; the prompt describes only the task.\n\n### 🗄️ The gateway's response cache\n\nAI Gateway answers an identical request from its response cache without calling Gemini, and reports the hit in the `cf-aig-cache-status` response header. A describe repeated with the same prompt and the same image, such as a code snippet two articles share, costs no model call. Each successful describe writes one ledger row at the flat describe price, whether Gemini or the cache answered, and sets the row's cached flag when the header reports a hit (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)).\n\n## ✍️ The prompts: no opener, an invention guard, minimal asymmetric context\n\nBoth prompts share three rules and differ only in subject.\n\n**No self-opener.** The sentence must begin with the thing itself, the kind-noun for code or the visible subject for an image, and the word \"This\" is forbidden anywhere in it. `Code: ` and `Image: ` are nagara's announcement, so the model owns only the content. An image noun-opens naturally; code needs the ban spelled out, because forbidding \"This block\" and \"This example\" alone leaves a residual \"This is a…\".\n\n**The invention guard** is a sharpened positive instruction with no few-shot. Code: name the kind and the purpose, never the mechanics, return values, or framework, unless the introducing text states them. Image: describe only what is visibly there, and **name no person, organization, brand, place, or region unless that exact name appears as written text inside the image**. The one confirmed invention in the bake-off was geography read onto an artwork, and no run under this guard reproduced it.\n\n**Context is minimal and asymmetric.** The code prompt gets the article title, the introducing paragraph (the unit just before, which the listener heard seconds ago as authority), and the block. The paragraph *after* is dropped. The image prompt gets the title and the alt, labelled as often wrong or SEO filler, to use only where it matches the pixels.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the paragraph after the block is dropped\n> It is the elaboration the listener is about to hear anyway, and including it nudges the model toward describing behaviour rather than naming what the block is.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one sentence per code block, even when the prose already named it\n> Every code block gets its own spoken sentence because it needs its own audio window in the read-along, and dropping it would remove the code from a reader's view. The redundancy with the introducing prose is the price of that window. What the sentence adds above restatement is the *kind*: \"the syntax for creating a variable\" names the concept; the specific field names and query values that would add most are exactly what the guard forbids as invention.\n\n## 🗣️ What each unit says\n\nA **code** unit's spoken form becomes `Code: <sentence>`, overwriting the interim `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder from the spoken-form derivation.\n\nAn **image** follows a four-step precedence, top to bottom. Cases 1, 3, and 4 are the fallback already on the unit from acquisition (see [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)); the describer owns case 2 and the filter that routes between 1 and 2.\n\n| # | Condition | Spoken form |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | good alt | the alt, verbatim |\n| 2 | no good alt | **one generated sentence of what the image shows** |\n| 3 | generation failed, alt non-empty | any alt, verbatim |\n| 4 | generation failed, alt empty | `Image with no description.` |\n\nThe **good-alt filter** decides case 1 against case 2. Alt is trusted verbatim only when it reads as a grammatical sentence, is not the article title (the cruft test in [article-extraction](article-extraction.md)), and clears a small CMS denylist (`subscribe`, `appears in`, `courtesy`, `photograph by`, `click`) and filename patterns. Everything else goes to the describer: empty, keyword soup, title-as-alt, a subscribe prompt.\n\n> [!NOTE] The denylist separates good alt from boilerplate where no clean heuristic exists\n> \"Image of tank rolling over a world map\" and \"This article appears in the October 2023 issue. Subscribe to WIRED.\" are *both* grammatical sentences, so grammaticality alone cannot separate good alt from boilerplate. The denylist keeps the first and sends the second to the describer, at the cost of a maintained list a novel boilerplate phrase can slip past.\n\n> [!NOTE] A describe failure never goes silent\n> On failure or over-budget, an image keeps its precedence fallback (any non-empty alt, else the floor), so invariant 2's drop-from-both never fires for a *description* failure. It fires one layer earlier, for an *acquisition* failure, which belongs to [article-extraction](article-extraction.md). The floor is honest: it tells the listener an image is there that could not be described.\n\n## 🔀 The fan-out, and its bounds\n\nThe fan-out walks the interleaved unit list once into one document-ordered job list of every code unit and every flagged image. That single ordered list is what makes the cap a shared budget across both kinds.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    J[\"jobs in document order:<br/>code units + flagged images\"] --> C{\"within the<br/>per-item cap?\"}\n    C -->|beyond cap| Floor[\"code floors; image keeps<br/>its precedence fallback;<br/>degrade: cap reached\"]\n    C -->|within cap| G[\"one child step per job,<br/>all started at once,<br/>at most 6 calls in flight\"]\n    G --> R{\"per child\"}\n    R -->|success| W[\"Code:/Image: sentence;<br/>one ledger row\"]\n    R -->|failure| D[\"code floors; image<br/>keeps fallback; degrade:<br/>describe failed\"]\n    W --> Done[\"one write at the end:<br/>spoken forms, degradations,<br/>ledger rows\"]\n    D --> Done\n    Floor --> Done\n```\n\n**One shared budget, counted in document order.** The per-item cap (default 25) caps the combined code-and-image total, so the two kinds can never each spend a full budget. Past the cap a code unit floors and an image keeps its precedence fallback; each records a `describe cap reached` degradation. The cap never fails an item and never goes silent.\n\n**One child step per description.** The describe step runs inside the wrapper like every other step, so it writes `current_step` and the status as it starts. Inside it, one child step per job within the cap starts at once, and the describe step awaits them together with `Promise.allSettled`. Each child calls Gemini and returns its result without writing to the item row. The describe step collects the results and writes every unit's spoken form, the degradations and the ledger rows in one write at its end. A fifty-unit article where one call fails keeps the other forty-nine, and the describe step never fails because a child failed.\n\nA Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections at a time, across all its steps, so at most 6 Gemini calls are in flight and the rest wait for a free connection.\n\n> [!NOTE] Why the children return results and one write lands at the end\n> The children run concurrently, and a child that wrote its own unit into the row would race its siblings on the same units column. Returning results to the describe step leaves it as the only writer, so the row changes once, with every unit's spoken form, degradation and ledger row together.\n\n> [!WARNING] Waiting for a connection counts toward a step's timeout\n> All the child steps start together, but their calls run 6 at a time, and the time a child waits for a free connection counts toward its 1-minute timeout. In the spike that measured it, the last of 25 five-second calls finished 26 s after the fan-out started. A slow gateway or a full budget of large SVGs can push the last children in the queue past their timeout, and each such child fails that attempt.\n\n**Retry policy.** The describe step has 3 attempts of 5 minutes each, and each child step has 3 attempts of 1 minute each. A retry of the describe step runs every child again as new steps, so a repeated describe goes back to AI Gateway, whose response cache answers a request identical to one that already succeeded.\n\n**A failed description degrades the unit and never fails the item.** A unit whose child step failed gets a `describe failed` degradation and floors (code) or keeps the fallback (image). The describe phase never fails the item, even when every description failed: a code-bearing item whose Gemini calls all failed reaches `ready` with every code block on the floor `\"Code with no description.\"` and a degradation for each.\n\nThe pipeline Worker holds the Gemini key and the AI Gateway token as secrets. A failed or capped unit made no billable call, so only a successful describe writes a ledger row.\n\n## ⏩ What is not built yet\n\n- **The fallback model is not wired.** `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` is named in the code as the fallback for when 3.5 is unavailable, but nothing ever selects it: every call goes to `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`. It stays unwired because under structured output it was seen to write \"A TypeScript interface…\" for a one-line JSON block, a kind-level error rather than a behaviour-level one.\n\n---\n\nRelated: [article-extraction](article-extraction.md) · [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) · [item-contract](item-contract.md) · [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) · [what-gets-read-aloud](../product-design/what-gets-read-aloud.md) · [invariants](invariants.md)\n","numLines":401,"startLine":1,"totalLines":401}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b1b0f783-b54f-4a63-b978-65abcff69e0e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
