-4adc-8ec0-912562cc5cbe","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:49:14.522Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"d8133f5b-529b-4adc-8ec0-912562cc5cbe","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bfa32ed9-babd-4bb0-92a6-0867b6b276d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011Zf8mi6KPLvtcKkGJb3hgZ","type":"tool_result","content":"item-workflow:\nFINDINGS.md\nmigrations\npackage.json\npost.sh\nresults\nshow.sh\nsrc\ntsconfig.json\nwait.sh\nwrangler.jsonc\n\nitem-workflow/src:\nindex.ts\n# item-workflow\n\n## Question\n\nWhat shape does the flattened item Workflow take on Cloudflare: the sleep-and-poll loop against the step limit, retries per step, and how a running item Workflow waits for and releases its domain's lease from a DomainQueue DO (today DomainQueue starts one Workflow per job)?\n\n## Answer\n\nWorks, with caveats.\n\n- **(a) TTS sleep-and-poll** is cheap against the step limit. A 12,128-word essay (70 min of audio, 176 s of synthesis) took 31 poll steps at a 5 s interval and 39 `step.do` steps for the whole item. At 5 s per poll, 10,000 steps would cover about 13.8 hours of synthesis.\n- **(b) Retries per step** work as configured. A failure in a late step retries only that step, and earlier steps are not re-run. `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"tts: submit\" } })` on an errored item re-ran only from that step forward, which gives the retry route a shape. Caveat: a deploy made **every** step in flight fail with `WorkflowInternalError` (8 of 8 in round 8) and run its body again, even when the body had already finished. Sometimes the platform recorded that failure only when the step's timeout fired, up to 15 minutes later, and the lease stayed held the whole time.\n- **(c) The lease** works both ways tried: the DO signals the waiter with `sendEvent` into `step.waitForEvent`, or the waiter polls the DO with `step.sleep` + a poll step. Handoffs were in FIFO order across concurrent items on one domain, and an item on another domain ran in parallel. Release worked on success (explicit step), on failure (try/catch in `run()`, or a rollback handler on the acquire step), and on termination (`terminate({rollback: true})` runs the rollback). A plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance; the DO's alarm sweep frees the lease. An instance that dies mid-step (a deploy) keeps its lease, because its id is not terminal, and resumes holding it.\n\n## Shape built\n\nOne Worker `nagara-spike-item-wf` with the item Workflow, a per-domain DomainQueue DO (`idFromName(domain)`, as in today's pipeline), D1 for item status and a per-attempt log, and R2 for audio. Code: `src/index.ts`.\n\nWorkflow steps, with the retry config used:\n\n| Step name | Retries (limit, delay, backoff) | Timeout | What it does here |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fetch` | 3, 5 s, exponential | 2 min | Firecrawl `v2/scrape` markdown → paragraphs, or the paragraphs passed in params |\n| `lease: request` | 5, 2 s, exponential | 30 s | `DomainQueue.acquire(itemId, mode)` → `{granted}` or `{granted:false, position}` |\n| `lease: wait N` (waitForEvent, type `lease-granted`) / `lease: recheck N` | (recheck as above) | wait timeout 10 min | event variant: park until the DO signals; on timeout re-ask the DO |\n| `lease: sleep N` / `lease: poll N` | 5, 2 s, exponential | 30 s | poll variant: sleep, then `acquire` again |\n| `recipe` | 1, 5 s, constant | 15 min (configurable) | pass-through holding the lease for `holdSeconds` (stands in for the Flue agents) |\n| `lease: release` | as lease | | `DomainQueue.release(itemId)` |\n| `images`, `describe` | 2, 3 s, constant | 5 min | pass-through |\n| `tts: submit` | 3, 10 s, exponential | 1 min | `POST /submit` on `nagara-tts-spike` (shared-key variant) |\n| `tts: sleep N` / `tts: poll N` | 5, 5 s, linear | 30 s | `GET /result/{call_id}?part=meta`; 202 → keep looping |\n| `store` | 3, 5 s, exponential | 2 min | `GET ?part=audio` streamed into `R2.put`; writes `ready`, duration and timing to D1 |\n| `lease: release (failed)` | as lease | | `catch` in `run()` when release mode is `catch` |\n| `mark failed` | default | | writes `failed` + error to D1, then rethrows |\n\nStep names in loops carry a counter (`tts: poll 7`) because a repeated name replays the cached result. `wrangler workflows instances describe` shows them as `tts: poll 7-1`.\n\nDomainQueue state: `lease {holder, mode, since}` and `waiting [{holder, mode}]` in the SQLite-backed sync KV (`ctx.storage.kv`), plus a log. The methods:\n\n- `acquire(holder, mode)` is synchronous: it grants when the holder already holds the lease, or when the lease is free and the holder is first in line (or the line is empty). Otherwise it appends the holder and returns its position. With no `await` in the method, nothing can interleave.\n- `release(holder, reason)` drops the holder from `waiting`, and if it holds the lease, deletes it and calls `advance()`. Release is keyed on the holder id, so a second release, or a release that arrives after the lease has moved on, is a no-op.\n- `advance()` shifts the next waiter, checks `status()`, writes the lease to it and, for event waiters, calls `sendEvent({type: \"lease-granted\"})`. If `status()` is terminal or `sendEvent` throws, it skips to the next waiter. Poll waiters find the lease on their next poll.\n- `alarm()` runs every `LEASE_ALARM_MS` (15 s here, 60 s in today's pipeline) while a lease is held. When the holder's status is `complete`/`errored`/`terminated`, or unaddressable, it sweeps the lease and calls `advance()`.\n\nRelease modes, chosen per item by a param:\n\n- `catch`: `run()` wraps everything in try/catch; the catch runs `lease: release (failed)` and `mark failed`, then rethrows.\n- `rollback`: `lease: request` registers a rollback handler that calls `release`. The handler runs when the instance fails, and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`.\n\n## What was run\n\n```\ncd .scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes/item-workflow\nW=../../../../pipeline/node_modules/.bin/wrangler          # 4.135.0\n$W d1 create nagara-spike-item-wf\n$W r2 bucket create nagara-spike-item-wf\n$W d1 migrations apply nagara-spike-item-wf --remote         # migrations/0001, 0002\n$W deploy\n$W secret put TTS_API_KEY        # from ../modal-http/.secrets.env\n$W secret put FIRECRAWL_API_KEY  # from api/.env\n```\n\nRoutes on `https://nagara-spike-item-wf.asermax.workers.dev`: `POST /items` (params as JSON, `id` optional), `GET /items/{id}` (D1 row, instance status, attempt log), `POST /items/{id}/terminate[?rollback=1]`, `POST /items/{id}/restart?from=<step>`, `POST /items/{id}/clear-fault`, `GET /queue/{domain}`. Helpers: `post.sh` (enqueue a one-paragraph item), `show.sh` (print an item's timeline), `wait.sh` (block until instances are terminal). Raw output of every round is in `results/`.\n\n| Round | What | Items |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Real item, Firecrawl → TTS | `r1-long` (paulgraham.com/greatwork.html) |\n| 2 | 3 event waiters on `a.test`, 1 on `b.test`, 3 poll waiters on `c.test`, all at once, 20 s hold | `r2-*` |\n| 3 | Long synthesis: the same essay, paragraphs extracted locally (231 paragraphs, 12,128 words); a deploy landed mid-poll | `r3-long` |\n| 4 | Transient `describe` failure; `tts: submit` failing until cleared; `recipe` always failing with catch vs rollback, each with a waiter behind; holder terminated plainly + a waiter terminated; holder terminated with rollback; event sent 30 s before the waiter reaches `waitForEvent` | `r4-*` |\n| 5 | Clear r4-late's fault + `restart({from: \"tts: submit\"})`; repeat of round 4's transient, catch and early-event cases with no deploy nearby | `r4-late`, `r5-*` |\n| 6 | 3 event waiters, deploy 5 s in | `r6-*` |\n| 7 | 3 event + 3 poll waiters, deploy 5 s in | `r7-*` |\n| 8 | 8 items in `recipe` (40 s hold) during a deploy: 4 with a 2 min step timeout, 4 with 15 min | `r8-*` |\n\n## Measurements\n\n**(a) TTS loop and step count**\n\n| Item | Audio | Synthesis (submit → store) | Poll steps | `step.do` steps total | Sleeps |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| r3-long, 231 paragraphs, 12,128 words | 4,217 s (70 min), 12.2 MB Opus in R2 | 176 s | 31 | 39 | 31 |\n| one or two short paragraphs | 6-14 s | 6-15 s | 1-2 | 9-10 | 1-2 |\n\n- Each poll took 0.2-0.4 s; 202 responses were fast. The poll that returned the 200 with metadata for the 70-min item took 1.9 s.\n- The `store` step streamed the 12.2 MB file into R2 in 3.0 s. `ffprobe` on the R2 object reads 4,217.2 s. I did not listen to it.\n- Polling at 5 s, the 10,000-step default allows about 13.8 h of synthesis per item; with exponential backoff it would allow far more.\n\n**(b) Retries and resume**\n\n- `describe` failing on attempts 1-2 (r5-transient, r6-t): attempts 2 and 3 ran 3.0 s apart, matching the `3 seconds` constant delay. `fetch`, `lease: request`, `recipe` and `images` each ran once.\n- `tts: submit` failing on every attempt (r4-late): attempts landed at +10.9, +21.0, +41.2 and +81.4 s, matching 10 s exponential backoff (10, 20, 40 s). The step then threw, `run()` released the lease (already released after `recipe`, so a no-op) and wrote `failed`, and the instance ended `errored`.\n- After clearing the fault, `restart({from: {name: \"tts: submit\"}})` resumed r4-late: `tts: submit` ran again as attempt 1, then poll and store, and the item reached `ready`. `fetch`, `lease`, `recipe`, `images` and `describe` did not run again. The D1 `error` column still held the old error, because the spike never clears it.\n\n**(c) Lease handoff**\n\n| Case | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| 3 event waiters on one domain (r2, r5) | FIFO order. Release → `sendEvent` 0.3-0.5 s → the next item's `recipe` starts within about a second |\n| 3 poll waiters on one domain (r2, r7) | FIFO order. Release → pickup on the next poll, 0-5 s. A waiter spends one step per 5 s of waiting: 9 poll steps for a 47 s wait (r7-po-3) |\n| Another domain at the same time | Ran in parallel, never queued |\n| Holder fails in `recipe`, catch mode (r4/r5) | `lease: release (failed)` ran 0.2 s after the last attempt and the waiter was granted 0.5 s later |\n| Holder fails in `recipe`, rollback mode (r4-rb-1) | The rollback handler on `lease: request` ran 0.2 s after the last attempt and the waiter was granted 0.5 s later |\n| Holder `terminate()` without rollback (r4-term-1) | No code ran in the instance. The alarm swept the lease when it next fired; here that was 1.4 s after the terminate, because the alarm happened to be due. The worst case is `LEASE_ALARM_MS` |\n| A waiter terminated while queued (r4-term-2) | `status()` still reported it as not terminal about 1 s after the terminate; `sendEvent` threw `(instance.in_finite_state) Instance reached a finite state, cannot send events to it`; the DO skipped it and granted the next waiter |\n| Holder `terminate({rollback: true})` (r4-termrb-1) | The rollback handler released the lease 13 s after the terminate call |\n| Event sent before the waiter reached `waitForEvent` (r4-early-2, r5-early-2) | The event was kept: `waitForEvent` returned at once when the waiter got there (20-30 s later) |\n\n## Surprises\n\n- **A deploy fails every step that is executing** (rounds 4, 6, 7, 8). Each such attempt is recorded as `WorkflowInternalError: Attempt failed due to internal workflows error`, and the step body runs again. That attempt counts against the step's retry limit: `recipe` has `limit: 1`, so a real failure on the re-run would have exhausted it. The body is re-executed even when it had already finished. In r4-late, `recipe` logged `ok` at +4.0 s and ran again at +6.5 s. Step bodies must therefore be idempotent: the recipe step's D1 write, `tts: submit` (a second Modal call), and R2 puts.\n- **When the platform records that failure varies.** Of the 8 steps in round 8, 5 were marked failed as soon as their body finished (about 42 s). One was marked failed at its 2-minute timeout, one at its 15-minute timeout, and one at 5:00, which matches neither. In round 7, r7-ev-2 held the lease for 15 minutes this way while a waiter queued behind it, and the waiter's 10-minute `lease: recheck 1` found the lease still held. The step timeout bounds the stall in the cases seen, except the 5:00 one.\n- **`waitForEvent` across a deploy**: twice (r4-catch-2, r6-2) the event reached the instance at send time (the step's output carries the send timestamp), but the step ended exactly 5:00 after the wait began. Without a deploy nearby (rounds 2, 4's other cases, 5), waits resolved in under a second.\n- **Poll waiters sleeping across a deploy were not delayed** (r7-po-2/3, 6 and 9 polls, all 5 s apart). The deploy did not disturb `r3-long` either, which was mid TTS loop.\n- **`status()` does not tell a parked waiter from a running one**: the DO saw `running` for instances inside `waitForEvent` in most handoffs, and `waiting` in some (r6-3, r7-ev-3).\n- **A deterministic TTS failure is retried as if transient.** In r1-long, one Firecrawl paragraph made Kokoro raise `ValueError: need at least one array to concatenate` (`_synth_paragraph`, line 61 of the spike app). `/result` then answered 500 on every poll, and the `tts: poll 14` step retried 5 times over 76 s before the item failed. The modal-http endpoint returns no status that marks a failed call as distinct from a broken poll.\n- Rollback handlers and `restart({from})` exist in the runtime and types (rollbacks added June 2026). Neither appears in the global wiki entry on Workflows.\n\n## Design decisions raised\n\n13:class _UnitBase(BaseModel):\n16:    start: float | None = None\n17:    end: float | None = None\n20:class ParagraphUnit(_UnitBase):\n26:class CodeUnit(_UnitBase):\n32:class ImageUnit(_UnitBase):\n42:class _UnitResponseBase(BaseModel):\n45:    start: float\n46:    end: float\n49:class ParagraphResponse(_UnitResponseBase):\n53:class CodeResponse(_UnitResponseBase):\n57:class ImageResponse(_UnitResponseBase):\n97:        if isinstance(first, dict) and first.get(\"start\") is None:","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3d9d6dc3-8cf6-4584-a132-8b080edae40d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:49:14.848Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"item-workflow:\nFINDINGS.md\nmigrations\npackage.json\npost.sh\nresults\nshow.sh\nsrc\ntsconfig.json\nwait.sh\nwrangler.jsonc\n\nitem-workflow/src:\nindex.ts\n# item-workflow\n\n## Question\n\nWhat shape does the flattened item Workflow take on Cloudflare: the sleep-and-poll loop against the step limit, retries per step, and how a running item Workflow waits for and releases its domain's lease from a DomainQueue DO (today DomainQueue starts one Workflow per job)?\n\n## Answer\n\nWorks, with caveats.\n\n- **(a) TTS sleep-and-poll** is cheap against the step limit. A 12,128-word essay (70 min of audio, 176 s of synthesis) took 31 poll steps at a 5 s interval and 39 `step.do` steps for the whole item. At 5 s per poll, 10,000 steps would cover about 13.8 hours of synthesis.\n- **(b) Retries per step** work as configured. A failure in a late step retries only that step, and earlier steps are not re-run. `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"tts: submit\" } })` on an errored item re-ran only from that step forward, which gives the retry route a shape. Caveat: a deploy made **every** step in flight fail with `WorkflowInternalError` (8 of 8 in round 8) and run its body again, even when the body had already finished. Sometimes the platform recorded that failure only when the step's timeout fired, up to 15 minutes later, and the lease stayed held the whole time.\n- **(c) The lease** works both ways tried: the DO signals the waiter with `sendEvent` into `step.waitForEvent`, or the waiter polls the DO with `step.sleep` + a poll step. Handoffs were in FIFO order across concurrent items on one domain, and an item on another domain ran in parallel. Release worked on success (explicit step), on failure (try/catch in `run()`, or a rollback handler on the acquire step), and on termination (`terminate({rollback: true})` runs the rollback). A plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance; the DO's alarm sweep frees the lease. An instance that dies mid-step (a deploy) keeps its lease, because its id is not terminal, and resumes holding it.\n\n## Shape built\n\nOne Worker `nagara-spike-item-wf` with the item Workflow, a per-domain DomainQueue DO (`idFromName(domain)`, as in today's pipeline), D1 for item status and a per-attempt log, and R2 for audio. Code: `src/index.ts`.\n\nWorkflow steps, with the retry config used:\n\n| Step name | Retries (limit, delay, backoff) | Timeout | What it does here |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fetch` | 3, 5 s, exponential | 2 min | Firecrawl `v2/scrape` markdown → paragraphs, or the paragraphs passed in params |\n| `lease: request` | 5, 2 s, exponential | 30 s | `DomainQueue.acquire(itemId, mode)` → `{granted}` or `{granted:false, position}` |\n| `lease: wait N` (waitForEvent, type `lease-granted`) / `lease: recheck N` | (recheck as above) | wait timeout 10 min | event variant: park until the DO signals; on timeout re-ask the DO |\n| `lease: sleep N` / `lease: poll N` | 5, 2 s, exponential | 30 s | poll variant: sleep, then `acquire` again |\n| `recipe` | 1, 5 s, constant | 15 min (configurable) | pass-through holding the lease for `holdSeconds` (stands in for the Flue agents) |\n| `lease: release` | as lease | | `DomainQueue.release(itemId)` |\n| `images`, `describe` | 2, 3 s, constant | 5 min | pass-through |\n| `tts: submit` | 3, 10 s, exponential | 1 min | `POST /submit` on `nagara-tts-spike` (shared-key variant) |\n| `tts: sleep N` / `tts: poll N` | 5, 5 s, linear | 30 s | `GET /result/{call_id}?part=meta`; 202 → keep looping |\n| `store` | 3, 5 s, exponential | 2 min | `GET ?part=audio` streamed into `R2.put`; writes `ready`, duration and timing to D1 |\n| `lease: release (failed)` | as lease | | `catch` in `run()` when release mode is `catch` |\n| `mark failed` | default | | writes `failed` + error to D1, then rethrows |\n\nStep names in loops carry a counter (`tts: poll 7`) because a repeated name replays the cached result. `wrangler workflows instances describe` shows them as `tts: poll 7-1`.\n\nDomainQueue state: `lease {holder, mode, since}` and `waiting [{holder, mode}]` in the SQLite-backed sync KV (`ctx.storage.kv`), plus a log. The methods:\n\n- `acquire(holder, mode)` is synchronous: it grants when the holder already holds the lease, or when the lease is free and the holder is first in line (or the line is empty). Otherwise it appends the holder and returns its position. With no `await` in the method, nothing can interleave.\n- `release(holder, reason)` drops the holder from `waiting`, and if it holds the lease, deletes it and calls `advance()`. Release is keyed on the holder id, so a second release, or a release that arrives after the lease has moved on, is a no-op.\n- `advance()` shifts the next waiter, checks `status()`, writes the lease to it and, for event waiters, calls `sendEvent({type: \"lease-granted\"})`. If `status()` is terminal or `sendEvent` throws, it skips to the next waiter. Poll waiters find the lease on their next poll.\n- `alarm()` runs every `LEASE_ALARM_MS` (15 s here, 60 s in today's pipeline) while a lease is held. When the holder's status is `complete`/`errored`/`terminated`, or unaddressable, it sweeps the lease and calls `advance()`.\n\nRelease modes, chosen per item by a param:\n\n- `catch`: `run()` wraps everything in try/catch; the catch runs `lease: release (failed)` and `mark failed`, then rethrows.\n- `rollback`: `lease: request` registers a rollback handler that calls `release`. The handler runs when the instance fails, and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`.\n\n## What was run\n\n```\ncd .scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes/item-workflow\nW=../../../../pipeline/node_modules/.bin/wrangler          # 4.135.0\n$W d1 create nagara-spike-item-wf\n$W r2 bucket create nagara-spike-item-wf\n$W d1 migrations apply nagara-spike-item-wf --remote         # migrations/0001, 0002\n$W deploy\n$W secret put TTS_API_KEY        # from ../modal-http/.secrets.env\n$W secret put FIRECRAWL_API_KEY  # from api/.env\n```\n\nRoutes on `https://nagara-spike-item-wf.asermax.workers.dev`: `POST /items` (params as JSON, `id` optional), `GET /items/{id}` (D1 row, instance status, attempt log), `POST /items/{id}/terminate[?rollback=1]`, `POST /items/{id}/restart?from=<step>`, `POST /items/{id}/clear-fault`, `GET /queue/{domain}`. Helpers: `post.sh` (enqueue a one-paragraph item), `show.sh` (print an item's timeline), `wait.sh` (block until instances are terminal). Raw output of every round is in `results/`.\n\n| Round | What | Items |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Real item, Firecrawl → TTS | `r1-long` (paulgraham.com/greatwork.html) |\n| 2 | 3 event waiters on `a.test`, 1 on `b.test`, 3 poll waiters on `c.test`, all at once, 20 s hold | `r2-*` |\n| 3 | Long synthesis: the same essay, paragraphs extracted locally (231 paragraphs, 12,128 words); a deploy landed mid-poll | `r3-long` |\n| 4 | Transient `describe` failure; `tts: submit` failing until cleared; `recipe` always failing with catch vs rollback, each with a waiter behind; holder terminated plainly + a waiter terminated; holder terminated with rollback; event sent 30 s before the waiter reaches `waitForEvent` | `r4-*` |\n| 5 | Clear r4-late's fault + `restart({from: \"tts: submit\"})`; repeat of round 4's transient, catch and early-event cases with no deploy nearby | `r4-late`, `r5-*` |\n| 6 | 3 event waiters, deploy 5 s in | `r6-*` |\n| 7 | 3 event + 3 poll waiters, deploy 5 s in | `r7-*` |\n| 8 | 8 items in `recipe` (40 s hold) during a deploy: 4 with a 2 min step timeout, 4 with 15 min | `r8-*` |\n\n## Measurements\n\n**(a) TTS loop and step count**\n\n| Item | Audio | Synthesis (submit → store) | Poll steps | `step.do` steps total | Sleeps |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| r3-long, 231 paragraphs, 12,128 words | 4,217 s (70 min), 12.2 MB Opus in R2 | 176 s | 31 | 39 | 31 |\n| one or two short paragraphs | 6-14 s | 6-15 s | 1-2 | 9-10 | 1-2 |\n\n- Each poll took 0.2-0.4 s; 202 responses were fast. The poll that returned the 200 with metadata for the 70-min item took 1.9 s.\n- The `store` step streamed the 12.2 MB file into R2 in 3.0 s. `ffprobe` on the R2 object reads 4,217.2 s. I did not listen to it.\n- Polling at 5 s, the 10,000-step default allows about 13.8 h of synthesis per item; with exponential backoff it would allow far more.\n\n**(b) Retries and resume**\n\n- `describe` failing on attempts 1-2 (r5-transient, r6-t): attempts 2 and 3 ran 3.0 s apart, matching the `3 seconds` constant delay. `fetch`, `lease: request`, `recipe` and `images` each ran once.\n- `tts: submit` failing on every attempt (r4-late): attempts landed at +10.9, +21.0, +41.2 and +81.4 s, matching 10 s exponential backoff (10, 20, 40 s). The step then threw, `run()` released the lease (already released after `recipe`, so a no-op) and wrote `failed`, and the instance ended `errored`.\n- After clearing the fault, `restart({from: {name: \"tts: submit\"}})` resumed r4-late: `tts: submit` ran again as attempt 1, then poll and store, and the item reached `ready`. `fetch`, `lease`, `recipe`, `images` and `describe` did not run again. The D1 `error` column still held the old error, because the spike never clears it.\n\n**(c) Lease handoff**\n\n| Case | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| 3 event waiters on one domain (r2, r5) | FIFO order. Release → `sendEvent` 0.3-0.5 s → the next item's `recipe` starts within about a second |\n| 3 poll waiters on one domain (r2, r7) | FIFO order. Release → pickup on the next poll, 0-5 s. A waiter spends one step per 5 s of waiting: 9 poll steps for a 47 s wait (r7-po-3) |\n| Another domain at the same time | Ran in parallel, never queued |\n| Holder fails in `recipe`, catch mode (r4/r5) | `lease: release (failed)` ran 0.2 s after the last attempt and the waiter was granted 0.5 s later |\n| Holder fails in `recipe`, rollback mode (r4-rb-1) | The rollback handler on `lease: request` ran 0.2 s after the last attempt and the waiter was granted 0.5 s later |\n| Holder `terminate()` without rollback (r4-term-1) | No code ran in the instance. The alarm swept the lease when it next fired; here that was 1.4 s after the terminate, because the alarm happened to be due. The worst case is `LEASE_ALARM_MS` |\n| A waiter terminated while queued (r4-term-2) | `status()` still reported it as not terminal about 1 s after the terminate; `sendEvent` threw `(instance.in_finite_state) Instance reached a finite state, cannot send events to it`; the DO skipped it and granted the next waiter |\n| Holder `terminate({rollback: true})` (r4-termrb-1) | The rollback handler released the lease 13 s after the terminate call |\n| Event sent before the waiter reached `waitForEvent` (r4-early-2, r5-early-2) | The event was kept: `waitForEvent` returned at once when the waiter got there (20-30 s later) |\n\n## Surprises\n\n- **A deploy fails every step that is executing** (rounds 4, 6, 7, 8). Each such attempt is recorded as `WorkflowInternalError: Attempt failed due to internal workflows error`, and the step body runs again. That attempt counts against the step's retry limit: `recipe` has `limit: 1`, so a real failure on the re-run would have exhausted it. The body is re-executed even when it had already finished. In r4-late, `recipe` logged `ok` at +4.0 s and ran again at +6.5 s. Step bodies must therefore be idempotent: the recipe step's D1 write, `tts: submit` (a second Modal call), and R2 puts.\n- **When the platform records that failure varies.** Of the 8 steps in round 8, 5 were marked failed as soon as their body finished (about 42 s). One was marked failed at its 2-minute timeout, one at its 15-minute timeout, and one at 5:00, which matches neither. In round 7, r7-ev-2 held the lease for 15 minutes this way while a waiter queued behind it, and the waiter's 10-minute `lease: recheck 1` found the lease still held. The step timeout bounds the stall in the cases seen, except the 5:00 one.\n- **`waitForEvent` across a deploy**: twice (r4-catch-2, r6-2) the event reached the instance at send time (the step's output carries the send timestamp), but the step ended exactly 5:00 after the wait began. Without a deploy nearby (rounds 2, 4's other cases, 5), waits resolved in under a second.\n- **Poll waiters sleeping across a deploy were not delayed** (r7-po-2/3, 6 and 9 polls, all 5 s apart). The deploy did not disturb `r3-long` either, which was mid TTS loop.\n- **`status()` does not tell a parked waiter from a running one**: the DO saw `running` for instances inside `waitForEvent` in most handoffs, and `waiting` in some (r6-3, r7-ev-3).\n- **A deterministic TTS failure is retried as if transient.** In r1-long, one Firecrawl paragraph made Kokoro raise `ValueError: need at least one array to concatenate` (`_synth_paragraph`, line 61 of the spike app). `/result` then answered 500 on every poll, and the `tts: poll 14` step retried 5 times over 76 s before the item failed. The modal-http endpoint returns no status that marks a failed call as distinct from a broken poll.\n- Rollback handlers and `restart({from})` exist in the runtime and types (rollbacks added June 2026). Neither appears in the global wiki entry on Workflows.\n\n## Design decisions raised\n\n13:class _UnitBase(BaseModel):\n16:    start: float | None = None\n17:    end: float | None = None\n20:class ParagraphUnit(_UnitBase):\n26:class CodeUnit(_UnitBase):\n32:class ImageUnit(_UnitBase):\n42:class _UnitResponseBase(BaseModel):\n45:    start: float\n46:    end: float\n49:class ParagraphResponse(_UnitResponseBase):\n53:class CodeResponse(_UnitResponseBase):\n57:class ImageResponse(_UnitResponseBase):\n97:        if isinstance(first, dict) and first.get(\"start\") is None:","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"4df238de-396a-4036-8d77-08f10bba3c1b","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d8133f5b-529b-4adc-8ec0-912562cc5cbe","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
