{"parentUuid":"4151891a-19a4-4f95-a2f0-9673f18f7de0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfur2byF1W3zhFeQSiZS4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DBc5CwGznYRX4DnWT1wVAS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration; grep -n -i \"wrapper\\|outside the step\\|current_step\" recording-decisions.md design.md | head -40"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9441,"cache_read_input_tokens":168001,"output_tokens":1762,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1643},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9441,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1762,"cache_read_input_tokens":168001,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9441,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9441},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DBc5CwGznYRX4DnWT1wVAS":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration; grep -n -i \"wrapper\\|outside the step\\|current_step\" recording-decisions.md design.md | head -40"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"094fe883-afc6-4184-9490-553c1209bd8d","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cfur2bZRsS48BzJ2iUNRR","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:program-design","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"6d6aed33-2e5f-466d-92a7-d4187515f1b8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:49:34.786Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"6d6aed33-2e5f-466d-92a7-d4187515f1b8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bfa32ed9-babd-4bb0-92a6-0867b6b276d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DBc5CwGznYRX4DnWT1wVAS","type":"tool_result","content":"recording-decisions.md:21:- D1 A failed item's error is the step's own error; current_step names the step. No phase prefixes.\nrecording-decisions.md:24:- D4 describe is one parent step, run inside the wrapper like every other step (it writes current_step and the status as it starts). Inside it, one child step per description runs concurrently, awaited with Promise.allSettled; each child calls Gemini and returns its result without writing to the row. The parent collects the results and writes every unit's spoken form, the degradations and the ledger rows in one write at its end. The parent never fails because a child failed. A retry of the parent runs every child again as new steps (parallel-steps spike), so a repeated describe goes back to Gemini, where AI Gateway's cache can answer it. Gemini calls run at most 6 at a time, the outbound connections one instance holds. The rule that an item fails when every code block failed goes.\ndesign.md:18:│  ✓ The item row keeps current_step: the name of the step the Workflow is running, written as each step starts; empty before the first\ndesign.md:76:│  ✓ Every step runs inside one wrapper holding the logic common to all steps: as a step starts it writes current_step and the status the step is given and clears the error; when a step's last attempt fails it writes failed and the error\ndesign.md:82:│  ✓ Every step runs inside the wrapper settled in branch 4\ndesign.md:92:│  ✓ The wrapper takes, per step, the status it writes as the step starts: queued for fetch and lease, generating for every step after\ndesign.md:150:   ✓ Reading and writing the item row: decoding and encoding its JSON columns, and the status write that sets a status, clears the error and stamps updated_at, used by the API (queued) and the Workflow's wrapper\ndesign.md:178:        string current_step \"nullable\"\ndesign.md:189:An item row keeps its unit list and its degradations as JSON columns; a long article's units come to a few hundred kilobytes, far below D1's 10 MB limit for one value. workflow_instance_id names the item's Workflow instance, and updated_at records the last write to the row. current_step names the step the Workflow is running; the Workflow writes it as each step starts, it is empty before the first step, and on a failed item it names the step that failed, which a retry restarts from. status always holds the item's status. The API writes queued at enqueue; the Workflow writes generating once it holds its domain's lease, and ready or failed when it finishes, together with the title, the audio duration and format, and the error of a failed item. The poll reads only the row and never the instance, so an item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the instance at the end of its retention window; the instance id is kept to restart the instance on retry. The row has no fields for an attempt in progress.\ndesign.md:379:    note right of failed : the error, and current_step<br/>names the failed step\ndesign.md:394:A client can retry a failed item up to 3 times. The retry route writes queued and clears the error, then restarts the item's instance from the step named in current_step, so the steps that succeeded are not run again; the Workflow then moves the item to generating as on its first run. In the restart-resolve spike the restarted step started about 3.5 s after the restart was accepted. The same route restarts an item that got stuck because the pipeline was not running locally.\ndesign.md:400:  subgraph wf [\"item Workflow, every step inside the wrapper\"]\ndesign.md:431:  wf -.->|\"current_step, status, ledger rows\"| d1\ndesign.md:440:The item Workflow does all the work for one item, in nine steps run in order. It is created with the item's id, reads the URL and the voice from the item row, and its first step writes the instance's id into the row. Every step runs inside one wrapper, which writes current_step and the status the step gives it as the step starts, clears the error, and writes failed with the error when the step's last attempt fails. fetch and lease give queued; every later step gives generating.\ndesign.md:630:common/ is the package api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything more than one module uses. Its types describe the item row with its statuses, units, degradations and error, the recipe version row, the ledger row with its event types and units, and the item Workflow's params. It owns the R2 key layout, the audio and images prefixes and the functions that build an audio key from an item id and an image key from a content hash, and one presigning function that signs a GET or a PUT URL for a key with a given R2 token and lifetime. It reads and writes the item row: it encodes and decodes the JSON columns, and its status write sets a status, clears the error and stamps updated_at, for the API's queued and the Workflow wrapper's later statuses.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d082a6fc-d9b6-41d8-992d-ecb693d748d8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:49:35.156Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"recording-decisions.md:21:- D1 A failed item's error is the step's own error; current_step names the step. No phase prefixes.\nrecording-decisions.md:24:- D4 describe is one parent step, run inside the wrapper like every other step (it writes current_step and the status as it starts). Inside it, one child step per description runs concurrently, awaited with Promise.allSettled; each child calls Gemini and returns its result without writing to the row. The parent collects the results and writes every unit's spoken form, the degradations and the ledger rows in one write at its end. The parent never fails because a child failed. A retry of the parent runs every child again as new steps (parallel-steps spike), so a repeated describe goes back to Gemini, where AI Gateway's cache can answer it. Gemini calls run at most 6 at a time, the outbound connections one instance holds. The rule that an item fails when every code block failed goes.\ndesign.md:18:│  ✓ The item row keeps current_step: the name of the step the Workflow is running, written as each step starts; empty before the first\ndesign.md:76:│  ✓ Every step runs inside one wrapper holding the logic common to all steps: as a step starts it writes current_step and the status the step is given and clears the error; when a step's last attempt fails it writes failed and the error\ndesign.md:82:│  ✓ Every step runs inside the wrapper settled in branch 4\ndesign.md:92:│  ✓ The wrapper takes, per step, the status it writes as the step starts: queued for fetch and lease, generating for every step after\ndesign.md:150:   ✓ Reading and writing the item row: decoding and encoding its JSON columns, and the status write that sets a status, clears the error and stamps updated_at, used by the API (queued) and the Workflow's wrapper\ndesign.md:178:        string current_step \"nullable\"\ndesign.md:189:An item row keeps its unit list and its degradations as JSON columns; a long article's units come to a few hundred kilobytes, far below D1's 10 MB limit for one value. workflow_instance_id names the item's Workflow instance, and updated_at records the last write to the row. current_step names the step the Workflow is running; the Workflow writes it as each step starts, it is empty before the first step, and on a failed item it names the step that failed, which a retry restarts from. status always holds the item's status. The API writes queued at enqueue; the Workflow writes generating once it holds its domain's lease, and ready or failed when it finishes, together with the title, the audio duration and format, and the error of a failed item. The poll reads only the row and never the instance, so an item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the instance at the end of its retention window; the instance id is kept to restart the instance on retry. The row has no fields for an attempt in progress.\ndesign.md:379:    note right of failed : the error, and current_step<br/>names the failed step\ndesign.md:394:A client can retry a failed item up to 3 times. The retry route writes queued and clears the error, then restarts the item's instance from the step named in current_step, so the steps that succeeded are not run again; the Workflow then moves the item to generating as on its first run. In the restart-resolve spike the restarted step started about 3.5 s after the restart was accepted. The same route restarts an item that got stuck because the pipeline was not running locally.\ndesign.md:400:  subgraph wf [\"item Workflow, every step inside the wrapper\"]\ndesign.md:431:  wf -.->|\"current_step, status, ledger rows\"| d1\ndesign.md:440:The item Workflow does all the work for one item, in nine steps run in order. It is created with the item's id, reads the URL and the voice from the item row, and its first step writes the instance's id into the row. Every step runs inside one wrapper, which writes current_step and the status the step gives it as the step starts, clears the error, and writes failed with the error when the step's last attempt fails. fetch and lease give queued; every later step gives generating.\ndesign.md:630:common/ is the package api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything more than one module uses. Its types describe the item row with its statuses, units, degradations and error, the recipe version row, the ledger row with its event types and units, and the item Workflow's params. It owns the R2 key layout, the audio and images prefixes and the functions that build an audio key from an item id and an image key from a content hash, and one presigning function that signs a GET or a PUT URL for a key with a given R2 token and lifetime. It reads and writes the item row: it encodes and decodes the JSON columns, and its status write sets a status, clears the error and stamps updated_at, for the API's queued and the Workflow wrapper's later statuses.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"094fe883-afc6-4184-9490-553c1209bd8d","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6d6aed33-2e5f-466d-92a7-d4187515f1b8","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
