{"parentUuid":"24161c57-3bba-43cf-93a6-0c26a9be510a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"866e279e-27ce-438f-82de-cca5de874443","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xi6brTrMma2kMiCMGkqWa2","type":"tool_result","content":"CI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory,\non pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. The\nCloudflare side deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: `common/`'s workflow applies its migrations to\nD1, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for it before deploying, the pipeline Worker from the built\n`dist/nagara/wrangler.json` and then the API Worker; a failed check in `common/`'s workflow stops all\nthree. `tts` adds a deploy job to Modal gated on its own three checks. Read\n[`docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md`](docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md) before\ntouching deploy configuration.\n**Enqueue** writes the item row as `queued`, with `queued_at`, then creates the item's Workflow instance through the API Worker's cross-script Workflow binding, with the item's id as the only param, and returns `202`. The Workflow reads the URL and the voice from the row.\n\n**`queued`** covers the time until the Workflow starts and the time it waits for its domain's lease behind another article from the same domain. **`generating`** starts once the Workflow holds the lease and covers extraction, images, describe and synthesis. **`ready`** and **`failed`** end the Workflow, which writes either one with the rest of the finished state: the timed units and the audio's duration and format for `ready`, the error for `failed`.\n\n**Poll** reads the row and never the Workflow instance. When the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again as it reads it: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. This re-queue also recovers an item stuck locally because the pipeline Worker was not running when the API created its instance. An item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the Workflow instance at the end of its retention window, because nothing the poll returns lives on the instance.\nEvery step runs inside one wrapper holding the logic common to all of them. As a step starts, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error. fetch and lease are given `queued`; every later step is given `generating`, so the item shows `generating` from the moment the lease is held. When a step's last attempt fails, the wrapper writes `failed` and the step's own error. A not-article verdict fails the recipe step at once, without its remaining attempts. An attempt that fails while attempts remain writes nothing, so a step's automatic retries never show `failed`.\n\nThe status write sets the status, clears the error and stamps `updated_at` in one statement. It belongs to the [common/ package](common-package.md), and the API uses the same write for `queued` at enqueue and on retry.\n| `GET /items/{id}` | Poll. Reads the item row and returns the current item; it never reads the Workflow instance. Queues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. `404` for an unknown id. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/audio` | Serve audio for a `ready` item: a `307` to a GET URL for the item's audio key in the R2 bucket, signed for one hour (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). `404` if the item is not `ready`, has no audio format yet, or is unknown: no link is ever minted for a non-ready item. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` | Serve an image for the item, keyed by the content hash carried on an image unit. A `307` to a GET URL for the hash's key in the R2 bucket, signed fresh at read time for one hour. `404` for an unknown item. An unknown *hash* gets the `307` too, and R2 answers the signed URL with its own `404`; see below. Behind the same key as everything else. |\n44:Steps 1 to 5 run at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step, on the units the recipe returns. Steps 1 and 2 are in a required order, because the drop rule reads the spoken form. The loop must also run in document order, because step 5 anchors an image to the last text unit kept so far. When that unit is dropped, the image anchors to the kept unit before it. An image that comes before every text unit goes first in the list. Step 6, the images step, acquires the held images and drops any that will not acquire (see below). Step 7 fails the item when nothing survives, before the list is written. Step 8 writes the survivors and the degradations into the item row as soon as the step has them, and the later steps read them from there. The recipe step writes the title into the row together with its units. A recipe the agents authored or revised is written as its next version by the item Workflow itself (see [recipes](recipes.md)).\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running gets stuck in queued: its Workflow instance never starts, or ends in error, and it stays that way once the pipeline Worker comes up. The API re-queues it the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes: when it reads the item, it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n> [!WARNING] A dev-server reload strands the running Workflow instance\n> An edit reloads the dev server, and a Workflow instance that is running during the reload never resumes: its item stays in the status it had. An item stranded in queued is re-queued once its queued_at is more than 15 minutes old.\n\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:11:common/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything more than one modu\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:32:  mig -->|\"applied by common/'s CI job\"| d1\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:47:The API Worker and the pipeline Worker import common/ at build time. common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:63:| SQL migrations | the D1 schema, as hand-written SQL files | common/'s CI workflow, both Workers' tests, local devel\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:74:A change to common/ runs the type checks of api/ and pipeline/ against it in CI. When either check fails, the whole d\ndocs/technical-design/tts-service.md:23:| fetch result | the call id | not ready while the synthesis runs; once it is done, the result below |\ndocs/technical-design/tts-service.md:73:        W->>M: fetch result for the call id\ndocs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md:74:The schema is a set of hand-written SQL migrations in [common/](common-package.md). common/'s own CI job app","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"653c2fbc-2854-41ee-a130-928a7dc3b3c6","timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:22:16.535Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"CI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory,\non pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. The\nCloudflare side deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: `common/`'s workflow applies its migrations to\nD1, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for it before deploying, the pipeline Worker from the built\n`dist/nagara/wrangler.json` and then the API Worker; a failed check in `common/`'s workflow stops all\nthree. `tts` adds a deploy job to Modal gated on its own three checks. Read\n[`docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md`](docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md) before\ntouching deploy configuration.\n**Enqueue** writes the item row as `queued`, with `queued_at`, then creates the item's Workflow instance through the API Worker's cross-script Workflow binding, with the item's id as the only param, and returns `202`. The Workflow reads the URL and the voice from the row.\n\n**`queued`** covers the time until the Workflow starts and the time it waits for its domain's lease behind another article from the same domain. **`generating`** starts once the Workflow holds the lease and covers extraction, images, describe and synthesis. **`ready`** and **`failed`** end the Workflow, which writes either one with the rest of the finished state: the timed units and the audio's duration and format for `ready`, the error for `failed`.\n\n**Poll** reads the row and never the Workflow instance. When the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again as it reads it: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. This re-queue also recovers an item stuck locally because the pipeline Worker was not running when the API created its instance. An item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the Workflow instance at the end of its retention window, because nothing the poll returns lives on the instance.\nEvery step runs inside one wrapper holding the logic common to all of them. As a step starts, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error. fetch and lease are given `queued`; every later step is given `generating`, so the item shows `generating` from the moment the lease is held. When a step's last attempt fails, the wrapper writes `failed` and the step's own error. A not-article verdict fails the recipe step at once, without its remaining attempts. An attempt that fails while attempts remain writes nothing, so a step's automatic retries never show `failed`.\n\nThe status write sets the status, clears the error and stamps `updated_at` in one statement. It belongs to the [common/ package](common-package.md), and the API uses the same write for `queued` at enqueue and on retry.\n| `GET /items/{id}` | Poll. Reads the item row and returns the current item; it never reads the Workflow instance. Queues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes: it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites `queued_at`. `404` for an unknown id. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/audio` | Serve audio for a `ready` item: a `307` to a GET URL for the item's audio key in the R2 bucket, signed for one hour (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). `404` if the item is not `ready`, has no audio format yet, or is unknown: no link is ever minted for a non-ready item. |\n| `GET /items/{id}/images/{hash}` | Serve an image for the item, keyed by the content hash carried on an image unit. A `307` to a GET URL for the hash's key in the R2 bucket, signed fresh at read time for one hour. `404` for an unknown item. An unknown *hash* gets the `307` too, and R2 answers the signed URL with its own `404`; see below. Behind the same key as everything else. |\n44:Steps 1 to 5 run at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step, on the units the recipe returns. Steps 1 and 2 are in a required order, because the drop rule reads the spoken form. The loop must also run in document order, because step 5 anchors an image to the last text unit kept so far. When that unit is dropped, the image anchors to the kept unit before it. An image that comes before every text unit goes first in the list. Step 6, the images step, acquires the held images and drops any that will not acquire (see below). Step 7 fails the item when nothing survives, before the list is written. Step 8 writes the survivors and the degradations into the item row as soon as the step has them, and the later steps read them from there. The recipe step writes the title into the row together with its units. A recipe the agents authored or revised is written as its next version by the item Workflow itself (see [recipes](recipes.md)).\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running gets stuck in queued: its Workflow instance never starts, or ends in error, and it stays that way once the pipeline Worker comes up. The API re-queues it the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes: when it reads the item, it restarts the item's instance, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n> [!WARNING] A dev-server reload strands the running Workflow instance\n> An edit reloads the dev server, and a Workflow instance that is running during the reload never resumes: its item stays in the status it had. An item stranded in queued is re-queued once its queued_at is more than 15 minutes old.\n\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:11:common/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything more than one modu\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:32:  mig -->|\"applied by common/'s CI job\"| d1\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:47:The API Worker and the pipeline Worker import common/ at build time. common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:63:| SQL migrations | the D1 schema, as hand-written SQL files | common/'s CI workflow, both Workers' tests, local devel\ndocs/technical-design/common-package.md:74:A change to common/ runs the type checks of api/ and pipeline/ against it in CI. When either check fails, the whole d\ndocs/technical-design/tts-service.md:23:| fetch result | the call id | not ready while the synthesis runs; once it is done, the result below |\ndocs/technical-design/tts-service.md:73:        W->>M: fetch result for the call id\ndocs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md:74:The schema is a set of hand-written SQL migrations in [common/](common-package.md). common/'s own CI job app","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"609e5b9c-451e-4a56-97bf-4cc96763a762","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":1,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"24161c57-3bba-43cf-93a6-0c26a9be510a","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
