{"parentUuid":"278b34ef-1e65-41ac-b6d3-e436259cefbd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfutYgZsuogNGouUhAKdM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Writing the amendment drafts to the scratch folder."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6510,"cache_read_input_tokens":293847,"output_tokens":5377,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":45},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6510,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5377,"cache_read_input_tokens":293847,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6510},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"44db8c32-c973-42c9-91b2-37be815f6fc0","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfutYg7bmYWJqyicsgSVN","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:write-documentation","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"9bd374f9-07fd-4891-8507-62b62fc9ec84","timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:22:19.176Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"9bd374f9-07fd-4891-8507-62b62fc9ec84","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfutYgZsuogNGouUhAKdM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AHRRcPEDCtsH96VTnhxTMs","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/program-design-amendments.md","content":"**docs/technical-design/item-lifecycle.md**\n\n\"How an item moves through the system\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n      s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for<br/>synthesis\" }\n\nReplace the last sentence of the first paragraph under \"Modeling\" (after the field table):\n\nOnly the values the Workflow needs and the row does not hold pass from one step to the next as step results: the fetched HTML, the images the recipe declared, Modal's call id and the synthesis result.\n\nAppend to the \"Enqueue\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with the error the binding returned, `current_step` stays empty, and a retry creates the instance.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the \"Poll\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again as it reads it. It first claims the row with one conditional write that rewrites `queued_at` only while the row is still `queued` and its `queued_at` still that old, so two polls that arrive together queue it once. Then it restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, the same restart point a retry uses, or creates an instance when the row holds no instance id.\n\nNew callout after the \"Poll\" paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a re-queue does not count toward the retry cap\n> The cap bounds how often a client spends again on an item that failed. A re-queue recovers an item that never failed, waiting on a Workflow that has not moved, so counting it would let a slow start use up a client's retries.\n\n\"The item Workflow's steps\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n    s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for synthesis\" }\n\nSteps table, row lease, both columns after the step:\n\n| lease | waits for the domain's lease from DomainQueue through `waitForEvent`, asking again after each wait that times out | 5 min per wait, up to 3 waits |\n\nSteps table, row poll, replaced:\n\n| wait for synthesis | asks Modal for the result every 5 seconds until it is ready | 15 min |\n\nNew callout after the \"Why the units live on the row and the HTML in a step result\" callout:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why waiting for synthesis is one step\n> The Workflow replays a step's stored result by the step's name, so a loop of separate polling steps would need a new name for every round. One step that asks Modal every 5 seconds inside its own body keeps one name in `current_step`, and a retry restarts it without starting a second synthesis.\n\nReplace the second and third sentences of the first paragraph under \"The step wrapper\":\n\nAt the start of each attempt, inside the step, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error, so a restart from that step writes them again.\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph after the DomainQueue sequence diagram:\n\nDomainQueue grants the lease to an instance id that asks for it again, so a retry that restarts from the lease step gets the lease even while DomainQueue still records the earlier attempt as holding it. An instance id that asks again while it waits in the FIFO keeps its place, so a re-queue of an item waiting for the lease does not send it to the back.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the first paragraph under \"Retry restarts from the step that failed\":\n\nThe route writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at` and clears the error, then restarts the item's instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, through the same Workflow binding enqueue uses, and returns `202`.\n\n\"What a failure names\", replace the \"Synthesis that fails every time\" and \"Result expired\" bullets and add one after them:\n\n- **Synthesis that fails every time**: the wait for synthesis step uses its attempts on a crashed synthesis and fails the item.\n- **Result expired**: Modal holds a synthesis result for 7 days; the wait for synthesis step then fails the item without retrying.\n- **Instance not created**: creating the Workflow instance at enqueue fails; the API writes the item as `failed` with that error.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the paragraph after the failure bullets:\n\nAn item that waits for its domain's lease through 3 waits of 5 minutes each fails like any step out of attempts.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/item-contract.md**\n\nRoute table, `POST /items`, \"Does\" column, append:\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with that error and returns it; a retry creates the instance.\n\nRoute table, `GET /items/{id}`, replace the third sentence of the \"Does\" column:\n\nQueues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes: one conditional write claims the row and rewrites `queued_at`, so concurrent polls queue it once, then the API restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, or creates one when the row holds no instance id. The re-queue does not count toward the retry cap.\n\nReplace \"then restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`\" in the first paragraph under \"Retry\" with:\n\nthen restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates one when the row holds no instance id\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md**\n\nReplace the sentence \"Step 6, the images step, acquires the held images and drops any that will not acquire (see below).\" in the paragraph after the units flowchart:\n\nThe recipe step hands the held images to the images step as its step result, each with its src, its alt and the kept unit it follows, so the row holds only finished units. Step 6, the images step, acquires them, places each after its unit and drops any that will not acquire (see below).\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/tts-service.md**\n\n\"What it exposes\", endpoint table, row fetch result, \"Answers\" column:\n\n`202` while the synthesis runs; `200` with the result below once it is done; `500` when the synthesis raised; `410` once Modal no longer holds the result\n\nReplace the third and fourth sentences of the paragraph after the sequence diagram:\n\nThe wait for synthesis step asks the result endpoint for the call id every 5 seconds until the result is ready (4, 5), and the store step writes the finished state into the item row together with the tts ledger row, priced by the audio's length (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The call id and the result pass from step to step as step results; the item row holds neither. Each step gets 3 attempts: start synthesis has 2 minutes per attempt and wait for synthesis 15 minutes.\n\nReplace the paragraph after it:\n\nA synthesis that fails every time fails the item once the wait for synthesis step has used its attempts. A result Modal has discarded, 7 days after the call, answers `410` and fails the item at once.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md**\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph on `common/` under \"How the Workers deploy\":\n\n`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which the Cloudflare workflow's migrate job applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n\nReplace the heading \"🔁 CI: one path-filtered workflow per tree, deploys in a fixed order\" and the diagram under it:\n\n## 🔁 CI: path-filtered checks per tree, deploys in a fixed order\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    push([\"push to main\"]) --> ch[\"changes:<br/>paths-filter for<br/>common, pipeline, api\"]\n    ch -->|\"common/ changed\"| cc[\"common checks:<br/>test, lint, types,<br/>api and pipeline types\"]\n    ch -->|\"pipeline/ changed\"| pc[\"pipeline checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    ch -->|\"api/ changed\"| ac[\"api checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    subgraph chain [\"deploy chain: a job runs only when no job before it failed\"]\n        direction TB\n        mig[\"1 migrate: wrangler d1<br/>migrations apply --remote\"]\n        pd[\"2 deploy pipeline:<br/>pnpm build,<br/>deploy dist config\"]\n        ad[\"3 deploy api\"]\n        mig --> pd --> ad\n    end\n    cc --> mig\n    pc --> pd\n    ac --> ad\n    cc -. \"fails\" .-> s1([\"1, 2 and 3 do not run:<br/>no migration, no<br/>Worker deploys\"])\n    pc -. \"fails\" .-> s2([\"2 and 3 do not run\"])\n    ac -. \"fails\" .-> s3([\"3 does not run\"])\n    tw[\"tts workflow: test, lint,<br/>types, then modal deploy\"]\n    push -->|\"tts/ changed\"| tw\n    classDef step fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n    classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n    class ch,cc,pc,ac,mig,pd,ad,tw step\n    class s1,s2,s3 broken\n    class push person\n```\n\nReplace \"inside `common/`'s workflow\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nin `common/`'s checks\n\nReplace the paragraph beginning \"Three workflows ship the Cloudflare side\":\n\nOne workflow ships the Cloudflare side with `cloudflare/wrangler-action`. A paths filter decides which of `common/`, `pipeline/` and `api/` changed, and each changed tree's checks run. The deploy chain runs in a fixed order: the migrate job applies `common/`'s SQL migrations to D1, the pipeline job runs `pnpm build` and deploys `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, and the API job deploys the API Worker. A job in the chain runs only when no job before it failed; a job skipped because its tree did not change does not stop the ones after it, so a push that changes only `pipeline/` deploys the pipeline without a migration. A failed `common/` check stops all three: no migration, no pipeline deploy and no API deploy. A failed `pipeline/` check stops both Worker deploys, and a failed `api/` check stops the API deploy. The jobs authenticate with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`. `tts` keeps its own workflow, with a **`deploy` job that depends on all three of its checks**, runs `modal deploy` and authenticates with `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` / `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. All four are GitHub Actions secrets.\n\nNew callout after that paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one workflow for the Cloudflare side\n> The deploys depend on each other: the migrations go first and the API Worker last. Jobs in one workflow can wait on each other within a push, and separate workflows cannot without polling one another.\n\nReplace the first sentence of the \"Two migration runs at once are not guarded\" warning:\n\nNothing serializes two runs of the Cloudflare workflow, so two pushes to `main` in quick succession can apply `common/`'s migrations twice at the same time.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/common-package.md**\n\nReplace the first sentence of \"Overview\":\n\ncommon/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything both Workers use: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the hand-written SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library the integration tests use. Code that only the pipeline's own modules share stays in pipeline/.\n\nReplace \"by common/'s own CI workflow\" in the second sentence of \"Overview\" with:\n\nby the Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job\n\nArchitecture diagram, edge from `mig`:\n\n  mig -->|\"applied by the migrate job\"| d1\n\nReplace \"common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations to D1\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies the SQL migrations to D1\n\n\"What it holds\" table, row SQL migrations, \"Used by\" column:\n\nthe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job, both Workers' tests, local development\n\nReplace the second and third sentences of the first paragraph under \"Checking the shared types at build time\":\n\nWhen either check fails, the whole deploy stops: the migrate job applies no migration, and neither Worker deploys. When both pass, the migrate job applies the migrations to D1 with wrangler, then the pipeline Worker deploys and then the API Worker, all in one CI workflow.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md**\n\nReplace the second sentence of \"How the schema changes\":\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies them to D1 with wrangler before the pipeline Worker and the API Worker deploy, in the same workflow, so neither Worker ever runs against a schema older than its code (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)).\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/local-development.md**\n\nNew section after \"Overview\":\n\n## 🚀 Starting it\n\n1. Apply common/'s migrations to the shared state folder: `pnpm migrate:local` in `common/`.\n2. Start the pipeline Worker: `pnpm dev` in `pipeline/`.\n3. Start the Node proxy: `pnpm dev:proxy` in `pipeline/`.\n4. Start a quick tunnel to the proxy's port with `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:<port>`, and write its hostname into `pipeline/.dev.vars` as `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT`, `https://<hostname>/s3`.\n5. Start the API Worker: `pnpm dev` in `api/`.\n\nReplace step 2 under \"How Modal's audio reaches the local bucket\":\n\n2. It hands that URL to Modal with the endpoint in `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT`, the quick tunnel's hostname and the path /s3/<bucket>/<key>, in place of localhost's. In production both endpoints are R2's, so the same code runs in both places.\n\nReplace the paragraph under \"Re-queuing a stuck item\":\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running cannot start. When the API fails to create its Workflow instance, the item fails at enqueue, and a retry once the pipeline Worker is running creates the instance. When the instance is created but never starts, or ends in error, the item stays queued, and the API re-queues it the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes: when it reads the item, it restarts the item's instance and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n---\n\n**CLAUDE.md**\n\nReplace the CI paragraph under \"Layout and commands\", from \"CI runs test/lint/types\" to \"stops all three.\":\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory, on pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. One Cloudflare workflow deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: it applies `common/`'s migrations to D1, then deploys the pipeline Worker from the built `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, then the API Worker; a job runs only when no job before it failed, so a failed check in `common/` stops all three.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6510,"cache_read_input_tokens":293847,"output_tokens":5377,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":45},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6510,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5377,"cache_read_input_tokens":293847,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6510},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AHRRcPEDCtsH96VTnhxTMs":{"file_path":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/program-design-amendments.md","content":"**docs/technical-design/item-lifecycle.md**\n\n\"How an item moves through the system\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n      s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for<br/>synthesis\" }\n\nReplace the last sentence of the first paragraph under \"Modeling\" (after the field table):\n\nOnly the values the Workflow needs and the row does not hold pass from one step to the next as step results: the fetched HTML, the images the recipe declared, Modal's call id and the synthesis result.\n\nAppend to the \"Enqueue\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with the error the binding returned, `current_step` stays empty, and a retry creates the instance.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the \"Poll\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again as it reads it. It first claims the row with one conditional write that rewrites `queued_at` only while the row is still `queued` and its `queued_at` still that old, so two polls that arrive together queue it once. Then it restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, the same restart point a retry uses, or creates an instance when the row holds no instance id.\n\nNew callout after the \"Poll\" paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a re-queue does not count toward the retry cap\n> The cap bounds how often a client spends again on an item that failed. A re-queue recovers an item that never failed, waiting on a Workflow that has not moved, so counting it would let a slow start use up a client's retries.\n\n\"The item Workflow's steps\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n    s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for synthesis\" }\n\nSteps table, row lease, both columns after the step:\n\n| lease | waits for the domain's lease from DomainQueue through `waitForEvent`, asking again after each wait that times out | 5 min per wait, up to 3 waits |\n\nSteps table, row poll, replaced:\n\n| wait for synthesis | asks Modal for the result every 5 seconds until it is ready | 15 min |\n\nNew callout after the \"Why the units live on the row and the HTML in a step result\" callout:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why waiting for synthesis is one step\n> The Workflow replays a step's stored result by the step's name, so a loop of separate polling steps would need a new name for every round. One step that asks Modal every 5 seconds inside its own body keeps one name in `current_step`, and a retry restarts it without starting a second synthesis.\n\nReplace the second and third sentences of the first paragraph under \"The step wrapper\":\n\nAt the start of each attempt, inside the step, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error, so a restart from that step writes them again.\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph after the DomainQueue sequence diagram:\n\nDomainQueue grants the lease to an instance id that asks for it again, so a retry that restarts from the lease step gets the lease even while DomainQueue still records the earlier attempt as holding it. An instance id that asks again while it waits in the FIFO keeps its place, so a re-queue of an item waiting for the lease does not send it to the back.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the first paragraph under \"Retry restarts from the step that failed\":\n\nThe route writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at` and clears the error, then restarts the item's instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, through the same Workflow binding enqueue uses, and returns `202`.\n\n\"What a failure names\", replace the \"Synthesis that fails every time\" and \"Result expired\" bullets and add one after them:\n\n- **Synthesis that fails every time**: the wait for synthesis step uses its attempts on a crashed synthesis and fails the item.\n- **Result expired**: Modal holds a synthesis result for 7 days; the wait for synthesis step then fails the item without retrying.\n- **Instance not created**: creating the Workflow instance at enqueue fails; the API writes the item as `failed` with that error.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the paragraph after the failure bullets:\n\nAn item that waits for its domain's lease through 3 waits of 5 minutes each fails like any step out of attempts.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/item-contract.md**\n\nRoute table, `POST /items`, \"Does\" column, append:\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with that error and returns it; a retry creates the instance.\n\nRoute table, `GET /items/{id}`, replace the third sentence of the \"Does\" column:\n\nQueues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes: one conditional write claims the row and rewrites `queued_at`, so concurrent polls queue it once, then the API restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, or creates one when the row holds no instance id. The re-queue does not count toward the retry cap.\n\nReplace \"then restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`\" in the first paragraph under \"Retry\" with:\n\nthen restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates one when the row holds no instance id\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md**\n\nReplace the sentence \"Step 6, the images step, acquires the held images and drops any that will not acquire (see below).\" in the paragraph after the units flowchart:\n\nThe recipe step hands the held images to the images step as its step result, each with its src, its alt and the kept unit it follows, so the row holds only finished units. Step 6, the images step, acquires them, places each after its unit and drops any that will not acquire (see below).\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/tts-service.md**\n\n\"What it exposes\", endpoint table, row fetch result, \"Answers\" column:\n\n`202` while the synthesis runs; `200` with the result below once it is done; `500` when the synthesis raised; `410` once Modal no longer holds the result\n\nReplace the third and fourth sentences of the paragraph after the sequence diagram:\n\nThe wait for synthesis step asks the result endpoint for the call id every 5 seconds until the result is ready (4, 5), and the store step writes the finished state into the item row together with the tts ledger row, priced by the audio's length (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The call id and the result pass from step to step as step results; the item row holds neither. Each step gets 3 attempts: start synthesis has 2 minutes per attempt and wait for synthesis 15 minutes.\n\nReplace the paragraph after it:\n\nA synthesis that fails every time fails the item once the wait for synthesis step has used its attempts. A result Modal has discarded, 7 days after the call, answers `410` and fails the item at once.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md**\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph on `common/` under \"How the Workers deploy\":\n\n`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which the Cloudflare workflow's migrate job applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n\nReplace the heading \"🔁 CI: one path-filtered workflow per tree, deploys in a fixed order\" and the diagram under it:\n\n## 🔁 CI: path-filtered checks per tree, deploys in a fixed order\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    push([\"push to main\"]) --> ch[\"changes:<br/>paths-filter for<br/>common, pipeline, api\"]\n    ch -->|\"common/ changed\"| cc[\"common checks:<br/>test, lint, types,<br/>api and pipeline types\"]\n    ch -->|\"pipeline/ changed\"| pc[\"pipeline checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    ch -->|\"api/ changed\"| ac[\"api checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    subgraph chain [\"deploy chain: a job runs only when no job before it failed\"]\n        direction TB\n        mig[\"1 migrate: wrangler d1<br/>migrations apply --remote\"]\n        pd[\"2 deploy pipeline:<br/>pnpm build,<br/>deploy dist config\"]\n        ad[\"3 deploy api\"]\n        mig --> pd --> ad\n    end\n    cc --> mig\n    pc --> pd\n    ac --> ad\n    cc -. \"fails\" .-> s1([\"1, 2 and 3 do not run:<br/>no migration, no<br/>Worker deploys\"])\n    pc -. \"fails\" .-> s2([\"2 and 3 do not run\"])\n    ac -. \"fails\" .-> s3([\"3 does not run\"])\n    tw[\"tts workflow: test, lint,<br/>types, then modal deploy\"]\n    push -->|\"tts/ changed\"| tw\n    classDef step fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n    classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n    class ch,cc,pc,ac,mig,pd,ad,tw step\n    class s1,s2,s3 broken\n    class push person\n```\n\nReplace \"inside `common/`'s workflow\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nin `common/`'s checks\n\nReplace the paragraph beginning \"Three workflows ship the Cloudflare side\":\n\nOne workflow ships the Cloudflare side with `cloudflare/wrangler-action`. A paths filter decides which of `common/`, `pipeline/` and `api/` changed, and each changed tree's checks run. The deploy chain runs in a fixed order: the migrate job applies `common/`'s SQL migrations to D1, the pipeline job runs `pnpm build` and deploys `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, and the API job deploys the API Worker. A job in the chain runs only when no job before it failed; a job skipped because its tree did not change does not stop the ones after it, so a push that changes only `pipeline/` deploys the pipeline without a migration. A failed `common/` check stops all three: no migration, no pipeline deploy and no API deploy. A failed `pipeline/` check stops both Worker deploys, and a failed `api/` check stops the API deploy. The jobs authenticate with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`. `tts` keeps its own workflow, with a **`deploy` job that depends on all three of its checks**, runs `modal deploy` and authenticates with `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` / `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. All four are GitHub Actions secrets.\n\nNew callout after that paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one workflow for the Cloudflare side\n> The deploys depend on each other: the migrations go first and the API Worker last. Jobs in one workflow can wait on each other within a push, and separate workflows cannot without polling one another.\n\nReplace the first sentence of the \"Two migration runs at once are not guarded\" warning:\n\nNothing serializes two runs of the Cloudflare workflow, so two pushes to `main` in quick succession can apply `common/`'s migrations twice at the same time.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/common-package.md**\n\nReplace the first sentence of \"Overview\":\n\ncommon/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything both Workers use: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the hand-written SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library the integration tests use. Code that only the pipeline's own modules share stays in pipeline/.\n\nReplace \"by common/'s own CI workflow\" in the second sentence of \"Overview\" with:\n\nby the Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job\n\nArchitecture diagram, edge from `mig`:\n\n  mig -->|\"applied by the migrate job\"| d1\n\nReplace \"common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations to D1\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies the SQL migrations to D1\n\n\"What it holds\" table, row SQL migrations, \"Used by\" column:\n\nthe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job, both Workers' tests, local development\n\nReplace the second and third sentences of the first paragraph under \"Checking the shared types at build time\":\n\nWhen either check fails, the whole deploy stops: the migrate job applies no migration, and neither Worker deploys. When both pass, the migrate job applies the migrations to D1 with wrangler, then the pipeline Worker deploys and then the API Worker, all in one CI workflow.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md**\n\nReplace the second sentence of \"How the schema changes\":\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies them to D1 with wrangler before the pipeline Worker and the API Worker deploy, in the same workflow, so neither Worker ever runs against a schema older than its code (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)).\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/local-development.md**\n\nNew section after \"Overview\":\n\n## 🚀 Starting it\n\n1. Apply common/'s migrations to the shared state folder: `pnpm migrate:local` in `common/`.\n2. Start the pipeline Worker: `pnpm dev` in `pipeline/`.\n3. Start the Node proxy: `pnpm dev:proxy` in `pipeline/`.\n4. Start a quick tunnel to the proxy's port with `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:<port>`, and write its hostname into `pipeline/.dev.vars` as `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT`, `https://<hostname>/s3`.\n5. Start the API Worker: `pnpm dev` in `api/`.\n\nReplace step 2 under \"How Modal's audio reaches the local bucket\":\n\n2. It hands that URL to Modal with the endpoint in `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT`, the quick tunnel's hostname and the path /s3/<bucket>/<key>, in place of localhost's. In production both endpoints are R2's, so the same code runs in both places.\n\nReplace the paragraph under \"Re-queuing a stuck item\":\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running cannot start. When the API fails to create its Workflow instance, the item fails at enqueue, and a retry once the pipeline Worker is running creates the instance. When the instance is created but never starts, or ends in error, the item stays queued, and the API re-queues it the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes: when it reads the item, it restarts the item's instance and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n---\n\n**CLAUDE.md**\n\nReplace the CI paragraph under \"Layout and commands\", from \"CI runs test/lint/types\" to \"stops all three.\":\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory, on pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. One Cloudflare workflow deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: it applies `common/`'s migrations to D1, then deploys the pipeline Worker from the built `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, then the API Worker; a job runs only when no job before it failed, so a failed check in `common/` stops all three.\n"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"44db8c32-c973-42c9-91b2-37be815f6fc0","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CfutYg7bmYWJqyicsgSVN","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:write-documentation","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"66de954b-959a-42b5-a84b-c3717c241990","timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:22:56.866Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"66de954b-959a-42b5-a84b-c3717c241990","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"866e279e-27ce-438f-82de-cca5de874443","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AHRRcPEDCtsH96VTnhxTMs","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/program-design-amendments.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"8cb1003d-18aa-40f9-9918-1ec41f35dc7c","timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:22:56.936Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/program-design-amendments.md","content":"**docs/technical-design/item-lifecycle.md**\n\n\"How an item moves through the system\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n      s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for<br/>synthesis\" }\n\nReplace the last sentence of the first paragraph under \"Modeling\" (after the field table):\n\nOnly the values the Workflow needs and the row does not hold pass from one step to the next as step results: the fetched HTML, the images the recipe declared, Modal's call id and the synthesis result.\n\nAppend to the \"Enqueue\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with the error the binding returned, `current_step` stays empty, and a retry creates the instance.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the \"Poll\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again as it reads it. It first claims the row with one conditional write that rewrites `queued_at` only while the row is still `queued` and its `queued_at` still that old, so two polls that arrive together queue it once. Then it restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, the same restart point a retry uses, or creates an instance when the row holds no instance id.\n\nNew callout after the \"Poll\" paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a re-queue does not count toward the retry cap\n> The cap bounds how often a client spends again on an item that failed. A re-queue recovers an item that never failed, waiting on a Workflow that has not moved, so counting it would let a slow start use up a client's retries.\n\n\"The item Workflow's steps\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n    s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for synthesis\" }\n\nSteps table, row lease, both columns after the step:\n\n| lease | waits for the domain's lease from DomainQueue through `waitForEvent`, asking again after each wait that times out | 5 min per wait, up to 3 waits |\n\nSteps table, row poll, replaced:\n\n| wait for synthesis | asks Modal for the result every 5 seconds until it is ready | 15 min |\n\nNew callout after the \"Why the units live on the row and the HTML in a step result\" callout:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why waiting for synthesis is one step\n> The Workflow replays a step's stored result by the step's name, so a loop of separate polling steps would need a new name for every round. One step that asks Modal every 5 seconds inside its own body keeps one name in `current_step`, and a retry restarts it without starting a second synthesis.\n\nReplace the second and third sentences of the first paragraph under \"The step wrapper\":\n\nAt the start of each attempt, inside the step, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error, so a restart from that step writes them again.\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph after the DomainQueue sequence diagram:\n\nDomainQueue grants the lease to an instance id that asks for it again, so a retry that restarts from the lease step gets the lease even while DomainQueue still records the earlier attempt as holding it. An instance id that asks again while it waits in the FIFO keeps its place, so a re-queue of an item waiting for the lease does not send it to the back.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the first paragraph under \"Retry restarts from the step that failed\":\n\nThe route writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at` and clears the error, then restarts the item's instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates one when the row holds no instance id, through the same Workflow binding enqueue uses, and returns `202`.\n\n\"What a failure names\", replace the \"Synthesis that fails every time\" and \"Result expired\" bullets and add one after them:\n\n- **Synthesis that fails every time**: the wait for synthesis step uses its attempts on a crashed synthesis and fails the item.\n- **Result expired**: Modal holds a synthesis result for 7 days; the wait for synthesis step then fails the item without retrying.\n- **Instance not created**: creating the Workflow instance at enqueue fails; the API writes the item as `failed` with that error.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the paragraph after the failure bullets:\n\nAn item that waits for its domain's lease through 3 waits of 5 minutes each fails like any step out of attempts.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/item-contract.md**\n\nRoute table, `POST /items`, \"Does\" column, append:\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with that error and returns it; a retry creates the instance.\n\nRoute table, `GET /items/{id}`, replace the third sentence of the \"Does\" column:\n\nQueues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes: one conditional write claims the row and rewrites `queued_at`, so concurrent polls queue it once, then the API restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, or creates one when the row holds no instance id. The re-queue does not count toward the retry cap.\n\nReplace \"then restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`\" in the first paragraph under \"Retry\" with:\n\nthen restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates one when the row holds no instance id\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md**\n\nReplace the sentence \"Step 6, the images step, acquires the held images and drops any that will not acquire (see below).\" in the paragraph after the units flowchart:\n\nThe recipe step hands the held images to the images step as its step result, each with its src, its alt and the kept unit it follows, so the row holds only finished units. Step 6, the images step, acquires them, places each after its unit and drops any that will not acquire (see below).\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/tts-service.md**\n\n\"What it exposes\", endpoint table, row fetch result, \"Answers\" column:\n\n`202` while the synthesis runs; `200` with the result below once it is done; `500` when the synthesis raised; `410` once Modal no longer holds the result\n\nReplace the third and fourth sentences of the paragraph after the sequence diagram:\n\nThe wait for synthesis step asks the result endpoint for the call id every 5 seconds until the result is ready (4, 5), and the store step writes the finished state into the item row together with the tts ledger row, priced by the audio's length (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The call id and the result pass from step to step as step results; the item row holds neither. Each step gets 3 attempts: start synthesis has 2 minutes per attempt and wait for synthesis 15 minutes.\n\nReplace the paragraph after it:\n\nA synthesis that fails every time fails the item once the wait for synthesis step has used its attempts. A result Modal has discarded, 7 days after the call, answers `410` and fails the item at once.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md**\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph on `common/` under \"How the Workers deploy\":\n\n`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which the Cloudflare workflow's migrate job applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n\nReplace the heading \"🔁 CI: one path-filtered workflow per tree, deploys in a fixed order\" and the diagram under it:\n\n## 🔁 CI: path-filtered checks per tree, deploys in a fixed order\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    push([\"push to main\"]) --> ch[\"changes:<br/>paths-filter for<br/>common, pipeline, api\"]\n    ch -->|\"common/ changed\"| cc[\"common checks:<br/>test, lint, types,<br/>api and pipeline types\"]\n    ch -->|\"pipeline/ changed\"| pc[\"pipeline checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    ch -->|\"api/ changed\"| ac[\"api checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    subgraph chain [\"deploy chain: a job runs only when no job before it failed\"]\n        direction TB\n        mig[\"1 migrate: wrangler d1<br/>migrations apply --remote\"]\n        pd[\"2 deploy pipeline:<br/>pnpm build,<br/>deploy dist config\"]\n        ad[\"3 deploy api\"]\n        mig --> pd --> ad\n    end\n    cc --> mig\n    pc --> pd\n    ac --> ad\n    cc -. \"fails\" .-> s1([\"1, 2 and 3 do not run:<br/>no migration, no<br/>Worker deploys\"])\n    pc -. \"fails\" .-> s2([\"2 and 3 do not run\"])\n    ac -. \"fails\" .-> s3([\"3 does not run\"])\n    tw[\"tts workflow: test, lint,<br/>types, then modal deploy\"]\n    push -->|\"tts/ changed\"| tw\n    classDef step fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n    classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n    class ch,cc,pc,ac,mig,pd,ad,tw step\n    class s1,s2,s3 broken\n    class push person\n```\n\nReplace \"inside `common/`'s workflow\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nin `common/`'s checks\n\nReplace the paragraph beginning \"Three workflows ship the Cloudflare side\":\n\nOne workflow ships the Cloudflare side with `cloudflare/wrangler-action`. A paths filter decides which of `common/`, `pipeline/` and `api/` changed, and each changed tree's checks run. The deploy chain runs in a fixed order: the migrate job applies `common/`'s SQL migrations to D1, the pipeline job runs `pnpm build` and deploys `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, and the API job deploys the API Worker. A job in the chain runs only when no job before it failed; a job skipped because its tree did not change does not stop the ones after it, so a push that changes only `pipeline/` deploys the pipeline without a migration. A failed `common/` check stops all three: no migration, no pipeline deploy and no API deploy. A failed `pipeline/` check stops both Worker deploys, and a failed `api/` check stops the API deploy. The jobs authenticate with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`. `tts` keeps its own workflow, with a **`deploy` job that depends on all three of its checks**, runs `modal deploy` and authenticates with `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` / `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. All four are GitHub Actions secrets.\n\nNew callout after that paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one workflow for the Cloudflare side\n> The deploys depend on each other: the migrations go first and the API Worker last. Jobs in one workflow can wait on each other within a push, and separate workflows cannot without polling one another.\n\nReplace the first sentence of the \"Two migration runs at once are not guarded\" warning:\n\nNothing serializes two runs of the Cloudflare workflow, so two pushes to `main` in quick succession can apply `common/`'s migrations twice at the same time.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/common-package.md**\n\nReplace the first sentence of \"Overview\":\n\ncommon/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything both Workers use: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the hand-written SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library the integration tests use. Code that only the pipeline's own modules share stays in pipeline/.\n\nReplace \"by common/'s own CI workflow\" in the second sentence of \"Overview\" with:\n\nby the Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job\n\nArchitecture diagram, edge from `mig`:\n\n  mig -->|\"applied by the migrate job\"| d1\n\nReplace \"common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations to D1\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies the SQL migrations to D1\n\n\"What it holds\" table, row SQL migrations, \"Used by\" column:\n\nthe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job, both Workers' tests, local development\n\nReplace the second and third sentences of the first paragraph under \"Checking the shared types at build time\":\n\nWhen either check fails, the whole deploy stops: the migrate job applies no migration, and neither Worker deploys. When both pass, the migrate job applies the migrations to D1 with wrangler, then the pipeline Worker deploys and then the API Worker, all in one CI workflow.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md**\n\nReplace the second sentence of \"How the schema changes\":\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies them to D1 with wrangler before the pipeline Worker and the API Worker deploy, in the same workflow, so neither Worker ever runs against a schema older than its code (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)).\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/local-development.md**\n\nNew section after \"Overview\":\n\n## 🚀 Starting it\n\n1. Apply common/'s migrations to the shared state folder: `pnpm migrate:local` in `common/`.\n2. Start the pipeline Worker: `pnpm dev` in `pipeline/`.\n3. Start the Node proxy: `pnpm dev:proxy` in `pipeline/`.\n4. Start a quick tunnel to the proxy's port with `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:<port>`, and write its hostname into `pipeline/.dev.vars` as `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT`, `https://<hostname>/s3`.\n5. Start the API Worker: `pnpm dev` in `api/`.\n\nReplace step 2 under \"How Modal's audio reaches the local bucket\":\n\n2. It hands that URL to Modal with the endpoint in `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT`, the quick tunnel's hostname and the path /s3/<bucket>/<key>, in place of localhost's. In production both endpoints are R2's, so the same code runs in both places.\n\nReplace the paragraph under \"Re-queuing a stuck item\":\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running cannot start. When the API fails to create its Workflow instance, the item fails at enqueue, and a retry once the pipeline Worker is running creates the instance. When the instance is created but never starts, or ends in error, the item stays queued, and the API re-queues it the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes: when it reads the item, it restarts the item's instance and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n---\n\n**CLAUDE.md**\n\nReplace the CI paragraph under \"Layout and commands\", from \"CI runs test/lint/types\" to \"stops all three.\":\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory, on pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. One Cloudflare workflow deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: it applies `common/`'s migrations to D1, then deploys the pipeline Worker from the built `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, then the API Worker; a job runs only when no job before it failed, so a failed check in `common/` stops all three.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"66de954b-959a-42b5-a84b-c3717c241990","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"8cb1003d-18aa-40f9-9918-1ec41f35dc7c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"nested_memory","path":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/CLAUDE.md","content":{"path":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# Nagara\n\n**Nagara (ながら)**: from *ながら聞き*, consuming content *while doing something else*. A private,\nAPI-first **audio read-later queue**: `enqueue(url, voice?) → generate eagerly → private item with\na listen link`. The TTS pipeline is proven; the open question is demand: see\n[`docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md`](docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md).\n\n## Layout and commands\n\n| Tree | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `api/` | The API Worker: the only routes clients reach (enqueue, poll, retry, audio, images, health), behind a single API key; it writes the item row at enqueue and creates and restarts the item Workflow |\n| `tts/` | The GPU text-to-speech service: a separate Modal deployable the item Workflow calls through two web endpoints |\n| `pipeline/` | The pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, with no public routes: the item Workflow that does all the work for one item, a DomainQueue Durable Object per domain, the recipe runtime, the Flue author and revision agents |\n| `common/` | The package both Workers build from: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, the item row's reads and writes, the SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library. Only the migrations are deployed |\n| `web/` | The web surfaces: not yet built on `main`. The concluded read-along player spike is preserved on the `idea/read-along-player` branch with its tree relocated to `web/`, so checking that branch out is how you see what the spike settled; it is reference material, not a starting point |\n| `docs/` | The documentation. **Read [`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md) before touching anything under it**, not only before adding a note, but before editing, renaming, or moving one. `technical-design/` is how the code works, `product-design/` is what nagara is, and a note explains how a part works, mechanism first, with reasoning in callouts; it is not a decision record. Work in flight lives in Linear, never in `docs/`; see \"Work tracking\" below. |\n\n```\ntts/       uv run modal serve app.py (dev) · uv run modal deploy app.py (prod)\n           uv run pytest · uv run ruff check · uv run ty check\npipeline/  pnpm install · pnpm dev (vite dev) · pnpm deploy (prod, via wrangler)\n           pnpm test · pnpm lint · pnpm types\nweb/       pnpm dev · pnpm test · pnpm build · pnpm biome check      (not yet built)\n```\n\nLocally both Workers share one state folder, with D1 and R2 on disk, and Modal uploads audio through a\nquick tunnel and a local proxy: read\n[`docs/technical-design/local-development.md`](docs/technical-design/local-development.md) before\nrunning them together.\n\n`tts/` is pinned to **Python 3.12** (Kokoro / `modal`-client constraints; the system Python is\nnewer). `tts/` carries the Modal image's runtime deps (kokoro/torch-cpu/numpy/soundfile) as\n**dev** dependencies, not runtime ones, so `ty` can type-check all of `app.py` locally: do not \"clean\nup\" those dependencies out of the dev group.\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory,\non pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. The\nCloudflare side deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: `common/`'s workflow applies its migrations to\nD1, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for it before deploying, the pipeline Worker from the built\n`dist/nagara/wrangler.json` and then the API Worker; a failed check in `common/`'s workflow stops all\nthree. `tts` adds a deploy job to Modal gated on its own three checks. Read\n[`docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md`](docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md) before\ntouching deploy configuration.\n\n## Invariants that must not drift\n\nThese are decisions, not preferences. Each has a note in `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`\nexplaining what it buys; if the two disagree, that note is the explanation and this is the summary:\nfix both.\n\n1. **One extraction is the source of truth.** The spoken form never reaches a client and the display\n   form is never synthesized: both come from one markdown segmentation and ride on the same typed unit.\n2. **Display, spoken, and timing ride on one typed unit, never matched by text.** A dropped unit leaves\n   the one list and its window goes with it; a length mismatch at finalize fails the item.\n3. **Timing windows are contiguous and the last `end` equals the audio duration.** The inter-paragraph\n   pause is folded into the preceding window.\n4. **Every route that touches an item requires the key**: enqueue, poll, and audio alike. `/health` is\n   the only unauthenticated route.\n5. **No Worker imports the TTS code.** `tts/` is an image definition uploaded to Modal; the item\n   Workflow reaches it over HTTP through two web endpoints behind Modal's proxy auth: no broker, no\n   background sweeper. Deferred work runs as the item Workflow's steps, each retried on its own; the\n   retry route restarts a failed item from the step that failed, and the API queues an item again\n   when it reads one that has been queued for more than 15 minutes.\n6. **Which backend is a question about configuration, never an environment name.** No `if production`,\n   no `if testing` in runtime code.\n7. **A schema change is a migration.** Hand-written SQL migrations in `common/` own the D1 schema,\n   applied before either Worker deploys; the Workers' tests build their schema from the same\n   migrations.\n8. **The three deployables ship separately, and no CI pipeline runs on another's tree except\n   `common/`'s.** A change to `common/` runs both Workers' type checks; the Cloudflare side deploys\n   `common/`'s migrations, then the pipeline Worker, then the API Worker.\n9. **Read-along highlight sync is `requestAnimationFrame`, never `timeupdate`.** (Applies to `web/`,\n   which does not exist yet.)\n10. **A settled recipe always runs through the deterministic runtime.** The agent judges, the runtime\n    extracts: an authored or revised script is handed back as source and run like any other recipe, so\n    a fresh recipe and a years-old one extract through the same deterministic path.\n\n## Code style\n\nFollows the global style guide, plus:\n\n- **Payloads crossing a service boundary are typed**: what the Workers share (the item row, the unit\n  union, the item Workflow's params) is a TypeScript type in `common/`, and the TTS service's request\n  and return shapes are pydantic models in `tts/`. The Workers' types are checked when the code builds,\n  never at runtime: a value read back from D1 or received from Modal is trusted to match its type.\n- Everything more than one module uses lives in `common/`; each module inside `pipeline/` is as\n  independent of the others as it can be.\n- Comments explain **why**, never what; do not add narration alongside a comment that is already there\n  for a non-obvious reason.\n- A new or changed note is created from `docs/_templates/note.md`; rationale goes in a `> [!NOTE]`\n  callout beside the mechanism it justifies, never as the note's spine. No hard wrapping: one line per\n  paragraph.\n\n## Docs\n\n- **Docs**: `docs/`. Read its `README.md` before adding, editing, renaming or moving anything under it.\n- **Folders**: `technical-design/`, how the code works; `product-design/`, what nagara is.\n- **Run**: see \"Layout and commands\" above.\n- **Checks**: `pnpm test` · `pnpm lint` · `pnpm types` in whichever of `api/`, `pipeline/` and\n  `common/` a change touches (both Workers' as well when it touches `common/`); `uv run pytest` ·\n  `uv run ruff check` · `uv run ty check` in `tts/`.\n- **Seeing it work**: two failure modes pass every check and still don't work. **Extraction can succeed\n  on the wrong thing**: a URL serving a 200-status error page extracts cleanly, generates audio, and\n  reaches `ready`; the status is never `failed`, so a green pipeline is not evidence the item is the\n  article. **And you have to listen to it**: a strip regression is invisible in a diff and inaudible in\n  a test summary, and unmistakable in one second of audio; a leaked `**` is only ever caught by\n  playing the file.\n\n### When adding something\n\n- **A new route** → a route in the API Worker + its request and response types + a section in\n  `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`.\n- **A new rule for what a page yields** → the pipeline's runtime or the authoring prompts, plus a\n  section or callout in `docs/technical-design/recipes.md`.\n- **A new rule for how a job's units become the item's** (the spoken form, the drop) → a function\n  at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step in `pipeline/`, beside the spoken-form derivation, and a\n  test case, plus a section or callout in\n  `docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md`.\n- **A change to the item JSON** → `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`, **and a SQL migration in\n  `common/` when the persisted shape needs a backfill**, because the display list is persisted across\n  the async gap and an in-flight item is read back by a later request than the one that wrote it. **A\n  new member of the unit union needs neither**: the item row's `units` is a JSON column, so the union\n  is not schema and there is no DDL to write, and rows persisted before the new kind existed carry none\n  of it and stay readable unchanged.\n- **A new cross-environment capability** → an interface and a factory that reads configuration, never a\n  branch (invariant 6), plus a paragraph in `docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md`.\n- **Anything more than one module uses** → `common/`, described in the note of the part that uses it,\n  with a link to `docs/technical-design/common-package.md`.\n- **Anything that changes what a listener hears or sees** → a note or section in `docs/product-design/`.\n- **A new hard-to-reverse decision** → an entry in \"Invariants that must not drift\" above and in\n  `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`; anything smaller is a `> [!NOTE]` callout beside the mechanism\n  it justifies.\n\n## Work tracking\n\n- **Linear** holds the backlog: workspace `asermax`, team Asermax (key `ASE`), project **nagara**. A\n  milestone is a feature; an issue is one task under it, or a loose task with no milestone. Labels are\n  `Bug`, `Feature`, `Improvement` and `Spike` (throwaway code written to answer a question). Priority is\n  High, Medium or Low. `Backlog` is the default status and `Todo` marks what is about to be worked on.\n- **A feature is shaped and designed before it is built.** `mahou:shape` and `mahou:design` settle it\n  with the user, the design lands as a note under `docs/`, and only then does a milestone get its\n  implementation tasks. A spike precedes that when a question needs code to answer, and it is\n  throwaway: it never merges.\n- **A note never links to an issue.** An issue may cite a note by path. The issue closes and the link\n  would rot; the note is maintained for as long as the part exists.\n- **Branches** are `feat/<feature-name>`, one per feature.\n","contentDiffersFromDisk":false},"displayPath":".scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/CLAUDE.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"bb9c11f2-38dd-493f-988f-a26943e6bc9d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:22:56.965Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nContents of /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/docs-drafts/CLAUDE.md:\n\n# Nagara\n\n**Nagara (ながら)**: from *ながら聞き*, consuming content *while doing something else*. A private,\nAPI-first **audio read-later queue**: `enqueue(url, voice?) → generate eagerly → private item with\na listen link`. The TTS pipeline is proven; the open question is demand: see\n[`docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md`](docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md).\n\n## Layout and commands\n\n| Tree | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `api/` | The API Worker: the only routes clients reach (enqueue, poll, retry, audio, images, health), behind a single API key; it writes the item row at enqueue and creates and restarts the item Workflow |\n| `tts/` | The GPU text-to-speech service: a separate Modal deployable the item Workflow calls through two web endpoints |\n| `pipeline/` | The pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, with no public routes: the item Workflow that does all the work for one item, a DomainQueue Durable Object per domain, the recipe runtime, the Flue author and revision agents |\n| `common/` | The package both Workers build from: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, the item row's reads and writes, the SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library. Only the migrations are deployed |\n| `web/` | The web surfaces: not yet built on `main`. The concluded read-along player spike is preserved on the `idea/read-along-player` branch with its tree relocated to `web/`, so checking that branch out is how you see what the spike settled; it is reference material, not a starting point |\n| `docs/` | The documentation. **Read [`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md) before touching anything under it**, not only before adding a note, but before editing, renaming, or moving one. `technical-design/` is how the code works, `product-design/` is what nagara is, and a note explains how a part works, mechanism first, with reasoning in callouts; it is not a decision record. Work in flight lives in Linear, never in `docs/`; see \"Work tracking\" below. |\n\n```\ntts/       uv run modal serve app.py (dev) · uv run modal deploy app.py (prod)\n           uv run pytest · uv run ruff check · uv run ty check\npipeline/  pnpm install · pnpm dev (vite dev) · pnpm deploy (prod, via wrangler)\n           pnpm test · pnpm lint · pnpm types\nweb/       pnpm dev · pnpm test · pnpm build · pnpm biome check      (not yet built)\n```\n\nLocally both Workers share one state folder, with D1 and R2 on disk, and Modal uploads audio through a\nquick tunnel and a local proxy: read\n[`docs/technical-design/local-development.md`](docs/technical-design/local-development.md) before\nrunning them together.\n\n`tts/` is pinned to **Python 3.12** (Kokoro / `modal`-client constraints; the system Python is\nnewer). `tts/` carries the Modal image's runtime deps (kokoro/torch-cpu/numpy/soundfile) as\n**dev** dependencies, not runtime ones, so `ty` can type-check all of `app.py` locally: do not \"clean\nup\" those dependencies out of the dev group.\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory,\non pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. The\nCloudflare side deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: `common/`'s workflow applies its migrations to\nD1, and the pipeline and API workflows wait for it before deploying, the pipeline Worker from the built\n`dist/nagara/wrangler.json` and then the API Worker; a failed check in `common/`'s workflow stops all\nthree. `tts` adds a deploy job to Modal gated on its own three checks. Read\n[`docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md`](docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md) before\ntouching deploy configuration.\n\n## Invariants that must not drift\n\nThese are decisions, not preferences. Each has a note in `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`\nexplaining what it buys; if the two disagree, that note is the explanation and this is the summary:\nfix both.\n\n1. **One extraction is the source of truth.** The spoken form never reaches a client and the display\n   form is never synthesized: both come from one markdown segmentation and ride on the same typed unit.\n2. **Display, spoken, and timing ride on one typed unit, never matched by text.** A dropped unit leaves\n   the one list and its window goes with it; a length mismatch at finalize fails the item.\n3. **Timing windows are contiguous and the last `end` equals the audio duration.** The inter-paragraph\n   pause is folded into the preceding window.\n4. **Every route that touches an item requires the key**: enqueue, poll, and audio alike. `/health` is\n   the only unauthenticated route.\n5. **No Worker imports the TTS code.** `tts/` is an image definition uploaded to Modal; the item\n   Workflow reaches it over HTTP through two web endpoints behind Modal's proxy auth: no broker, no\n   background sweeper. Deferred work runs as the item Workflow's steps, each retried on its own; the\n   retry route restarts a failed item from the step that failed, and the API queues an item again\n   when it reads one that has been queued for more than 15 minutes.\n6. **Which backend is a question about configuration, never an environment name.** No `if production`,\n   no `if testing` in runtime code.\n7. **A schema change is a migration.** Hand-written SQL migrations in `common/` own the D1 schema,\n   applied before either Worker deploys; the Workers' tests build their schema from the same\n   migrations.\n8. **The three deployables ship separately, and no CI pipeline runs on another's tree except\n   `common/`'s.** A change to `common/` runs both Workers' type checks; the Cloudflare side deploys\n   `common/`'s migrations, then the pipeline Worker, then the API Worker.\n9. **Read-along highlight sync is `requestAnimationFrame`, never `timeupdate`.** (Applies to `web/`,\n   which does not exist yet.)\n10. **A settled recipe always runs through the deterministic runtime.** The agent judges, the runtime\n    extracts: an authored or revised script is handed back as source and run like any other recipe, so\n    a fresh recipe and a years-old one extract through the same deterministic path.\n\n## Code style\n\nFollows the global style guide, plus:\n\n- **Payloads crossing a service boundary are typed**: what the Workers share (the item row, the unit\n  union, the item Workflow's params) is a TypeScript type in `common/`, and the TTS service's request\n  and return shapes are pydantic models in `tts/`. The Workers' types are checked when the code builds,\n  never at runtime: a value read back from D1 or received from Modal is trusted to match its type.\n- Everything more than one module uses lives in `common/`; each module inside `pipeline/` is as\n  independent of the others as it can be.\n- Comments explain **why**, never what; do not add narration alongside a comment that is already there\n  for a non-obvious reason.\n- A new or changed note is created from `docs/_templates/note.md`; rationale goes in a `> [!NOTE]`\n  callout beside the mechanism it justifies, never as the note's spine. No hard wrapping: one line per\n  paragraph.\n\n## Docs\n\n- **Docs**: `docs/`. Read its `README.md` before adding, editing, renaming or moving anything under it.\n- **Folders**: `technical-design/`, how the code works; `product-design/`, what nagara is.\n- **Run**: see \"Layout and commands\" above.\n- **Checks**: `pnpm test` · `pnpm lint` · `pnpm types` in whichever of `api/`, `pipeline/` and\n  `common/` a change touches (both Workers' as well when it touches `common/`); `uv run pytest` ·\n  `uv run ruff check` · `uv run ty check` in `tts/`.\n- **Seeing it work**: two failure modes pass every check and still don't work. **Extraction can succeed\n  on the wrong thing**: a URL serving a 200-status error page extracts cleanly, generates audio, and\n  reaches `ready`; the status is never `failed`, so a green pipeline is not evidence the item is the\n  article. **And you have to listen to it**: a strip regression is invisible in a diff and inaudible in\n  a test summary, and unmistakable in one second of audio; a leaked `**` is only ever caught by\n  playing the file.\n\n### When adding something\n\n- **A new route** → a route in the API Worker + its request and response types + a section in\n  `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`.\n- **A new rule for what a page yields** → the pipeline's runtime or the authoring prompts, plus a\n  section or callout in `docs/technical-design/recipes.md`.\n- **A new rule for how a job's units become the item's** (the spoken form, the drop) → a function\n  at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step in `pipeline/`, beside the spoken-form derivation, and a\n  test case, plus a section or callout in\n  `docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md`.\n- **A change to the item JSON** → `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`, **and a SQL migration in\n  `common/` when the persisted shape needs a backfill**, because the display list is persisted across\n  the async gap and an in-flight item is read back by a later request than the one that wrote it. **A\n  new member of the unit union needs neither**: the item row's `units` is a JSON column, so the union\n  is not schema and there is no DDL to write, and rows persisted before the new kind existed carry none\n  of it and stay readable unchanged.\n- **A new cross-environment capability** → an interface and a factory that reads configuration, never a\n  branch (invariant 6), plus a paragraph in `docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md`.\n- **Anything more than one module uses** → `common/`, described in the note of the part that uses it,\n  with a link to `docs/technical-design/common-package.md`.\n- **Anything that changes what a listener hears or sees** → a note or section in `docs/product-design/`.\n- **A new hard-to-reverse decision** → an entry in \"Invariants that must not drift\" above and in\n  `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`; anything smaller is a `> [!NOTE]` callout beside the mechanism\n  it justifies.\n\n## Work tracking\n\n- **Linear** holds the backlog: workspace `asermax`, team Asermax (key `ASE`), project **nagara**. A\n  milestone is a feature; an issue is one task under it, or a loose task with no milestone. Labels are\n  `Bug`, `Feature`, `Improvement` and `Spike` (throwaway code written to answer a question). Priority is\n  High, Medium or Low. `Backlog` is the default status and `Todo` marks what is about to be worked on.\n- **A feature is shaped and designed before it is built.** `mahou:shape` and `mahou:design` settle it\n  with the user, the design lands as a note under `docs/`, and only then does a milestone get its\n  implementation tasks. A spike precedes that when a question needs code to answer, and it is\n  throwaway: it never merges.\n- **A note never links to an issue.** An issue may cite a note by path. The issue closes and the link\n  would rot; the note is maintained for as long as the part exists.\n- **Branches** are `feat/<feature-name>`, one per feature.\n\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
