{"parentUuid":"848d21e5-838a-4984-827f-532b24c3f112","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfutouG2om1oM934RReoE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6964,"cache_read_input_tokens":329606,"output_tokens":6654,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":59},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6964,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6654,"cache_read_input_tokens":329606,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6964,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6964},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":6112,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"99c46194-75a4-4141-8759-a42688272df8","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfutotsiQwK5uYFDzyBhb","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"a0963c67-7fba-4f6f-84a5-0b431fa5673d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:25:52.025Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"a0963c67-7fba-4f6f-84a5-0b431fa5673d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfutouG2om1oM934RReoE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These edits bring the docs in line with the settled program design. The main changes:\n\n- **Synthesis step:** step 8 becomes \"wait for synthesis\".\n- **Lease waits:** 5 minutes each, up to 3.\n- **Status write:** the step wrapper writes the status at the start of each attempt.\n- **Re-queue:** it claims the row first and doesn't count toward the retry cap.\n- **Failed instance creation:** the item fails at enqueue.\n- **Declared images:** they pass from the recipe step to the images step as its step result. Because of that, the images step is now where an item with no surviving units fails.\n- **CI:** one Cloudflare workflow.\n- **common/:** holds what both Workers use.\n- **tts-service:** the result endpoint's status codes.\n- **Local development:** gets its launch steps.\n\nThe draft went through two rounds of prose cleanup and the review against the docs rules, and I applied what both reported. One reviewer point is about content, not form, so it's your call. It considers the numbered \"How to run both Workers locally\" list a runbook, while the charter says a note explains how a part works. It's in local-development below for now. The alternative is to move those commands into CLAUDE.md's commands block.\n\n**docs/technical-design/item-lifecycle.md**\n\n\"How an item moves through the system\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n      s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for<br/>synthesis\" }\n\nReplace the last sentence of the third paragraph under \"Modeling\", after the field table:\n\nOnly the values the Workflow needs and the row does not hold pass from one step to the next as step results: the fetched HTML, the images the recipe declared, Modal's call id and the synthesis result.\n\nAppend to the \"Enqueue\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with the error the binding returned, `current_step` stays empty, and a retry creates the instance.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the \"Poll\" paragraph under \"States\":\n\nWhen the row is `queued` and its `queued_at` is more than 15 minutes old, the API queues the item again when it reads the row. It first claims the row with one conditional write. The write rewrites `queued_at` only if the row is still `queued` and `queued_at` is still more than 15 minutes old, so two polls that arrive together queue the item once. Then it restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, the same restart point a retry uses, or creates an instance when the row holds no instance id.\n\n\"States\" diagram, a transition added after `[*] --> queued`:\n\n    [*] --> failed : creating the Workflow instance fails\n\nNew callout after the \"Poll\" paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why a re-queue does not count toward the retry cap\n> The cap bounds how often a client spends again on an item that failed. A re-queue recovers an item that never failed, waiting on a Workflow that has not moved, so counting it would let a slow start use up a client's retries.\n\n\"The item Workflow's steps\" diagram, node `s8`:\n\n    s8@{ shape: rect, label: \"8 wait for synthesis\" }\n\nSteps table, row lease, both columns after the step:\n\n| lease | waits for the domain's lease from DomainQueue through `waitForEvent`, asking again after each wait that times out | 5 min per wait, up to 3 waits |\n\nSteps table, row poll, replaced:\n\n| wait for synthesis | asks Modal for the result every 5 seconds until it is ready | 15 min |\n\nNew callouts after the \"Why the units live on the row and the HTML in a step result\" callout:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why waiting for synthesis is one step\n> One step that asks Modal every 5 seconds inside its own body keeps one name in `current_step`, and a retry restarts it without starting a second synthesis.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: a loop of polling steps\n> The Workflow replays a step's stored result by the step's name, so each round of a polling loop needs a step name of its own, and `current_step` would carry a round number instead of the step's name.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the first paragraph under \"The step wrapper\", keeping the sentence after it:\n\nAt the start of each attempt, inside the step, the wrapper writes `current_step` and the status the step is given, and clears the error, so a restart from that step writes them again.\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph after the DomainQueue sequence diagram:\n\nDomainQueue grants the lease to an instance id that asks for it again, so a retry that restarts from the lease step gets the lease even while DomainQueue still records the earlier attempt as holding it. An instance id that asks again while it waits in the FIFO keeps its place, so a re-queue of an item waiting for the lease does not send it to the back.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the first paragraph under \"Retry restarts from the step that failed\":\n\nThe route writes `queued`, rewrites `queued_at` and clears the error. Through the same Workflow binding enqueue uses, it restarts the item's instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates an instance when the row holds no instance id. It returns `202`.\n\n\"What a failure names\", replace the first sentence of the paragraph under the heading:\n\nEvery `failed` item carries the failed step's own error, with no phase prefix, and its `current_step` names the step that failed, so a reader can place a failure without a stack trace; `current_step` is empty only when the instance was never created.\n\n\"What a failure names\", replace the \"Synthesis that fails every time\" and \"Result expired\" bullets, and add the \"Instance not created\" bullet between \"Result expired\" and \"Storage failure on finalize\":\n\n- **Synthesis that fails every time**: the wait for synthesis step uses its attempts on a crashed synthesis and fails the item.\n- **Result expired**: Modal holds a synthesis result for 7 days; the wait for synthesis step then fails the item without retrying.\n- **Instance not created**: creating the Workflow instance at enqueue fails; the API writes the item as `failed` with that error.\n\nReplace the second sentence of the paragraph after the failure bullets:\n\nAn item that waits for its domain's lease through 3 waits of 5 minutes each fails like any step out of attempts.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/item-contract.md**\n\nRoute table, `POST /items`, \"Does\" column, append:\n\nWhen creating the instance fails, the API writes the item as `failed` with that error and returns it; a retry creates the instance.\n\nRoute table, `GET /items/{id}`, replace the third sentence of the \"Does\" column:\n\nQueues again an item that has been `queued` for more than 15 minutes. One conditional write claims the row and rewrites `queued_at`, so concurrent polls queue the item once. The API then restarts the item's instance from the step `current_step` names, or creates one when the row holds no instance id. The re-queue does not count toward the retry cap.\n\nReplace \"then restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`\" in the first paragraph under \"Retry\" with:\n\nthen restarts the item's existing Workflow instance from the step named in `current_step`, or creates one when the row holds no instance id\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md**\n\nReplace the units flowchart:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    start([\"The recipe returns<br/>a title and<br/>display units\"]) --> loop\n    subgraph loop [\"Recipe step: each recipe<br/>unit, in document order\"]\n        direction TB\n        kind{\"Image unit?\"}\n        kind -->|No| spoken[\"1 Derive the spoken<br/>form from the display\"]\n        spoken --> test{\"2 Drop rule<br/>matches?\"}\n        test -->|Yes| drop[\"3 Drop: no typed unit\"]\n        test -->|No| keep[\"4 Keep as a typed<br/>paragraph or code unit\"]\n        kind -->|Yes| stash[\"5 Hold it after the<br/>last kept text unit\"]\n    end\n    subgraph images [\"Images step\"]\n        direction TB\n        acquire[\"6 Acquire held images,<br/>interleave with kept units\"]\n        acquire --> any{\"7 Any unit survives?\"}\n        any -->|Yes| write[\"8 Write the units<br/>into the item row\"]\n    end\n    loop --> acquire\n    any -. no .-> fail([\"Fail the item:<br/>no surviving units\"])\n    write --> next([\"Describe, synthesis,<br/>store: survivors only\"])\n```\n\nReplace the sentences from \"Step 6, the images step, acquires\" to \"the later steps read them from there.\" in the paragraph after the units flowchart:\n\nThe recipe step writes the kept text units into the item row and hands the held images to the images step as its step result, each with its src, its alt and the kept unit it follows, so the row holds only finished units. Steps 6 to 8 run in the images step. It acquires the held images, places each after its unit and drops any that will not acquire (see below). It fails the item when no unit survives. It writes the full list and the degradations into the item row, and the later steps read them from there.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/tts-service.md**\n\n\"What it exposes\", endpoint table, row fetch result, \"Answers\" column:\n\n`202` while the synthesis runs; `200` with the result below once it is done; `500` when the synthesis raised; `410` once Modal no longer holds the result\n\nReplace the third, fourth and fifth sentences of the paragraph after the sequence diagram:\n\nThe wait for synthesis step asks the result endpoint for the call id every 5 seconds until the result is ready (4, 5). The store step then writes the finished state into the item row together with the tts ledger row, priced by the audio's length (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md) and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)). The call id and the result pass from step to step as step results; the item row holds neither. Each step gets 3 attempts. Start synthesis has 2 minutes per attempt, and wait for synthesis has 15 minutes.\n\nReplace the paragraph after it:\n\nA synthesis that fails every time fails the item once the wait for synthesis step has used its attempts. Modal discards a result 7 days after the call. After that the result endpoint answers `410`, and the item fails at once.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md**\n\nReplace the last sentence of the paragraph on `common/` under \"How the Workers deploy\":\n\n`common/` deploys nothing but its SQL migrations, which the Cloudflare workflow's migrate job applies to D1 with wrangler; see [common-package](common-package.md) for the rest of the package.\n\nReplace the heading \"🔁 CI: one path-filtered workflow per tree, deploys in a fixed order\" and the diagram under it:\n\n## 🔁 CI: path-filtered checks per tree, deploys in a fixed order\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    push([\"push to main\"]) --> ch[\"changes:<br/>paths-filter for<br/>common, pipeline, api\"]\n    ch -->|\"common/ changed\"| cc[\"common checks:<br/>test, lint, types,<br/>api and pipeline types\"]\n    ch -->|\"pipeline/ changed\"| pc[\"pipeline checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    ch -->|\"api/ changed\"| ac[\"api checks:<br/>test, lint, types\"]\n    subgraph chain [\"deploy chain: a job runs only when no job before it failed\"]\n        direction TB\n        mig[\"1 migrate: wrangler d1<br/>migrations apply --remote\"]\n        pd[\"2 deploy pipeline:<br/>pnpm build,<br/>deploy dist config\"]\n        ad[\"3 deploy api\"]\n        mig --> pd --> ad\n    end\n    cc --> mig\n    pc --> pd\n    ac --> ad\n    cc -. \"fails\" .-> s1([\"1, 2 and 3 do not run:<br/>no migration, no<br/>Worker deploys\"])\n    pc -. \"fails\" .-> s2([\"2 and 3 do not run\"])\n    ac -. \"fails\" .-> s3([\"3 does not run\"])\n    tw[\"tts workflow: test, lint,<br/>types, then modal deploy\"]\n    push -->|\"tts/ changed\"| tw\n    classDef step fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a\n    classDef broken fill:#fee2e2,stroke:#dc2626\n    classDef person fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#4b5563\n    class ch,cc,pc,ac,mig,pd,ad,tw step\n    class s1,s2,s3 broken\n    class push person\n```\n\nReplace \"inside `common/`'s workflow\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nin `common/`'s checks\n\nReplace the paragraph beginning \"Three workflows ship the Cloudflare side\":\n\nOne workflow ships the Cloudflare side with `cloudflare/wrangler-action`. A paths filter decides which of `common/`, `pipeline/` and `api/` changed, and each changed tree's checks run. The deploy chain runs in a fixed order: the migrate job applies `common/`'s SQL migrations to D1, the pipeline job runs `pnpm build` and deploys `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, and the API job deploys the API Worker. A job in the chain runs only when no job before it failed. A job skipped because its tree did not change does not stop the jobs after it, so a push that changes only `pipeline/` deploys the pipeline without a migration. A failed `common/` check stops all three: no migration, no pipeline deploy and no API deploy. A failed `pipeline/` check stops both Worker deploys, and a failed `api/` check stops the API deploy. The jobs authenticate with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`. `tts` keeps its own workflow, with a **`deploy` job that depends on all three of its checks**, runs `modal deploy` and authenticates with `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` / `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. All four are GitHub Actions secrets.\n\nNew callouts after that paragraph:\n\n> [!NOTE] Why one workflow for the Cloudflare side\n> The deploys depend on each other: the migrations go first and the API Worker last. Jobs in one workflow wait on each other within a push.\n\n> [!TIP] Rejected: one workflow per tree\n> Separate workflows cannot wait on each other within a push without polling one another, so the pipeline and API deploys could not wait for the migrations.\n\nReplace the first sentence of the \"Two migration runs at once are not guarded\" warning:\n\nNothing serializes two runs of the Cloudflare workflow, so two pushes to `main` in quick succession can apply `common/`'s migrations twice at the same time.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/common-package.md**\n\nReplace the first sentence of \"Overview\":\n\ncommon/ is the TypeScript package that api/ and pipeline/ both build from, and it holds everything both Workers use: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, item row reads and writes, the hand-written SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library the integration tests use. Code that only the pipeline's own modules share stays in pipeline/.\n\nReplace \"by common/'s own CI workflow\" in the second sentence of \"Overview\" with:\n\nby the Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job\n\nArchitecture diagram, edge from `mig`:\n\n  mig -->|\"applied by the migrate job\"| d1\n\nReplace \"common/'s CI workflow applies the SQL migrations to D1\" in the paragraph after the diagram with:\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies the SQL migrations to D1\n\n\"What it holds\" table, row SQL migrations, \"Used by\" column:\n\nthe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job, both Workers' tests, local development\n\nReplace the second and third sentences of the second paragraph under \"Checking the shared types at build time\":\n\nWhen either check fails, the whole deploy stops: the migrate job applies no migration, and neither Worker deploys. When both pass, the migrate job applies the migrations to D1 with wrangler, then the pipeline Worker deploys and then the API Worker, all in one CI workflow.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md**\n\nReplace the second sentence of \"How the schema changes\":\n\nThe Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies them to D1 with wrangler before the pipeline Worker and the API Worker deploy, in the same workflow, so neither Worker ever runs against a schema older than its code (see [deployment-and-ci](deployment-and-ci.md)).\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/local-development.md**\n\nNew section after \"Overview\":\n\n## 🚀 How to run both Workers locally\n\n1. Apply common/'s migrations to the shared state folder: `pnpm migrate:local` in `common/`.\n2. Start the pipeline Worker: `pnpm dev` in `pipeline/`.\n3. Start the Node proxy: `pnpm dev:proxy` in `pipeline/`.\n4. Start a quick tunnel to the proxy's port with `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:<port>`, and write `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT=https://<hostname>/s3` into `pipeline/.dev.vars`, with the tunnel's hostname in place of `<hostname>`.\n5. Start the API Worker: `pnpm dev` in `api/`.\n\nReplace step 2 under \"How Modal's audio reaches the local bucket\":\n\n2. It hands that URL to Modal with localhost's endpoint replaced by the one in `R2_UPLOAD_ENDPOINT`. The URL then holds the quick tunnel's hostname and the path /s3/<bucket>/<key>. In production both endpoints are R2's, so the same code runs in both places.\n\nReplace the paragraph under \"Re-queuing a stuck item\":\n\nAn item enqueued while the pipeline Worker is not running cannot start. When the API fails to create its Workflow instance, the item fails at enqueue, and a retry once the pipeline Worker is running creates the instance. When the instance is created but never starts, or ends in error, the item stays queued, and the API re-queues the item the way it re-queues any item queued for more than 15 minutes. When the API reads the item, it restarts the item's instance and rewrites queued_at (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The developer polls the item once the pipeline Worker is running, and the item starts again.\n\n---\n\n**docs/technical-design/invariants.md**\n\nTable, row 7, replace \"and `common/`'s CI workflow applies them with wrangler before either Worker deploys\" with:\n\nand the Cloudflare CI workflow's migrate job applies them with wrangler before either Worker deploys\n\nTable, row 8, replace the last sentence of the invariant column:\n\nA change to `common/` runs the type checks of both Workers, and one Cloudflare workflow deploys in a fixed order: `common/`'s migrations, then the pipeline Worker, then the API Worker.\n\n---\n\n**CLAUDE.md**\n\nReplace the CI paragraph under \"Layout and commands\", from \"CI runs test/lint/types\" to \"stops all three.\":\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory, on pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. One Cloudflare workflow deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: it applies `common/`'s migrations to D1, then deploys the pipeline Worker from the built `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, then the API Worker; a job runs only when no job before it failed, so a failed check in `common/` stops all three."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6964,"cache_read_input_tokens":329606,"output_tokens":6654,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":59},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6964,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6654,"cache_read_input_tokens":329606,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6964,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6964},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"99c46194-75a4-4141-8759-a42688272df8","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfutotsiQwK5uYFDzyBhb","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"d8b6d7f2-e39c-40c1-b5f3-134d848df83f","timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:26:29.084Z","advisorModel":"claude-