{"parentUuid":"21b96ae7-7658-4b0a-8baf-bb2c48f0c8ef","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuwkpBx4PuMgiZLmrZHi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S9wuLTcrXcQt2tDoN5QZD2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"P=/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins; grep -rln \"program-design-with-canvas\" $P --include=\"*.md\" | grep -v \"skills/program-design-with-canvas/\"; sed -n 30,42p $P/mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md; grep -n \"Working with the user\" -A3 $P/mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md | head; grep -n \"loop\\|counter\\|name\" $P/mahou/skills/wiki/references/cloudflare/workflows.md | head -20"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3066,"cache_read_input_tokens":498211,"output_tokens":229,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3066,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":229,"cache_read_input_tokens":498211,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3066},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01S9wuLTcrXcQt2tDoN5QZD2":{"command":"P=/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins; grep -rln \"program-design-with-canvas\" $P --include=\"*.md\" | grep -v \"skills/program-design-with-canvas/\"; sed -n 30,42p $P/mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md; grep -n \"Working with the user\" -A3 $P/mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md | head; grep -n \"loop\\|counter\\|name\" $P/mahou/skills/wiki/references/cloudflare/workflows.md | head -20"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"ac7a35af-5aec-4374-abb8-fa7885a2fdd6","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfuwkokQwye1j9EjgZyfs","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:learn","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"3b695a81-4f7b-4f91-84bd-94ea91ed9800","timestamp":"2026-10-11T06:04:25.758Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"3b695a81-4f7b-4f91-84bd-94ea91ed9800","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f7b2e221-dce1-4cb7-9994-688970cad1ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S9wuLTcrXcQt2tDoN5QZD2","type":"tool_result","content":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/CLAUDE.md\n/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou/README.md\nEdits to the app under `scripts/app/` reach the running canvas only after a restart, because `start` copies the app into the cache. Run `stop` and then `start`, and reload the open tabs.\n\nBuild and edit the canvas from scripts run with `agent-browser` in your own tab, opened once on `<URL>?user=Claude` and kept open, so the user sees your cursor under that name. Every tab edits the same document live: your changes appear in the user's tab as you make them, and theirs in yours. The page exposes `window.editor`, `window.tldraw` with `createShapeId` and `toRichText`, and `window.canvas` with `notes()` and `step(at, change, pause?)`.\n\nDraw in steps the user can follow. A script's changes reach the other tabs together, so a script that draws everything at once shows the user only the finished result. Run each element as an awaited `canvas.step`: it moves your cursor to `at` (a shape id or a page point, or `null` to stay), applies `change`, and waits `pause` milliseconds, 500 by default. One step per shape, arrow or move, in the order a person would draw them: follow the flow the drawing explains, and join each element to what it connects to as soon as both are on the canvas, so the drawing grows as one connected piece and never as all the boxes first and all the arrows last.\n\n- An area goes first, before what it holds.\n- After the first element, each new element is followed right away by the arrow that joins it to what is already drawn, then by that arrow's label if it has one.\n- A note goes right after the element it describes, and its arrow right after the note.\n\nCheck every change with a screenshot, saved under a new file name each time, since reading a path you read before can show the earlier image.\n\n## Mockups\n70:## Working with the user\n71-\n72-- **Your tab holds the current state.** Read what you change from `window.editor` right before changing it, since the user may have moved it. Once the user has edited the canvas, never rebuild it; change only the shapes the change touches.\n73-- **Talking in the chat.** Say what changed and ask in the reply. The user answers in the chat, by voice, or with sticky notes.\n12:- `instance.status()` from the binding returns only `status`, `error` (`name`, `message`), `output` and `rollback`; it does not name the step that failed. The REST GET-instance endpoint lists every step with its name, success, output and attempts, each attempt with its error. `status()` does not tell a parked `waitForEvent` waiter from a running one: observed as `running` in most cases and `waiting` in some.\n15:- Steps run once durably via `step.do`; the return value is persisted and replayed on resume instead of re-executing. A repeated step name replays the cached result, so a step inside a loop needs a counter in its name (`poll 7`). Branching is plain JS control flow inside `run()`.\n20:- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results; `from` takes `name`, plus `count` for the n-th step of that name and `type` (`do`, `sleep`, `waitForEvent`), and throws when no step matches. `restart()` with no argument erases the instance's state and runs every step again. No option restarts from the failed step without naming that step, and no binding member returns its name. Only the REST get-instance endpoint returns it, and that endpoint needs an API token. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n21:- `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts the restart, before any step runs. Through a cross-script binding it took 0.4 to 0.6 s, and right after it `status()` read `queued` with `error` null. The restarted step started 3.1 to 3.8 s later, with a median of 3.4 s, whether it restarted from a named step or from the start, so the delay is scheduling, not replay. A caller that wants the step's own effect, such as a status write, waits for it after `restart()` returns.\n22:- Deploys: a deploy fails every step executing at that moment (`WorkflowInternalError`) and runs the body again even if it had finished, so step bodies must be idempotent; the failed attempt counts against the step's retry limit. Workflows sometimes records the failure only when the step's timeout fires, and twice a `waitForEvent` across a deploy resolved exactly 5 minutes late. Sleeping instances wake on the new code and keep the results of steps already run; a step added before the sleep point runs on wake, and a renamed sleep step sleeps again. An instance created within seconds of a deploy can start on the old code. `wrangler delete` on the Worker leaves its Workflow in place; `wrangler workflows delete <name>` removes it.\n23:- Cross-script binding: a Worker in another script binds the Workflow with `script_name`. That Worker deploys even when the target script does not exist, and wrangler gives no warning; its calls throw `workflow.not_found` until the target deploys, then work within about 3 s with no redeploy. Locally, see `local-dev`.\n27:- A step is ordinary Worker code, so it reaches code in other languages through what it can call: a service binding to another Worker, a Container through its Durable Object, or plain HTTP. Whether a `script_name` binding works across languages is undocumented.\n28:- Steps per instance: 10,000 by default on Paid, configurable to 25,000 (1,024 on Free); `step.sleep` does not count as a step. A sleep-and-poll loop at 5 s used about 31 steps per 3 minutes of waiting. State per instance is up to 1 GB on Paid.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9d5ffdbe-5079-459a-8aad-275538a68eac","timestamp":"2026-10-11T06:04:26.206Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/CLAUDE.md\n/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou/README.md\nEdits to the app under `scripts/app/` reach the running canvas only after a restart, because `start` copies the app into the cache. Run `stop` and then `start`, and reload the open tabs.\n\nBuild and edit the canvas from scripts run with `agent-browser` in your own tab, opened once on `<URL>?user=Claude` and kept open, so the user sees your cursor under that name. Every tab edits the same document live: your changes appear in the user's tab as you make them, and theirs in yours. The page exposes `window.editor`, `window.tldraw` with `createShapeId` and `toRichText`, and `window.canvas` with `notes()` and `step(at, change, pause?)`.\n\nDraw in steps the user can follow. A script's changes reach the other tabs together, so a script that draws everything at once shows the user only the finished result. Run each element as an awaited `canvas.step`: it moves your cursor to `at` (a shape id or a page point, or `null` to stay), applies `change`, and waits `pause` milliseconds, 500 by default. One step per shape, arrow or move, in the order a person would draw them: follow the flow the drawing explains, and join each element to what it connects to as soon as both are on the canvas, so the drawing grows as one connected piece and never as all the boxes first and all the arrows last.\n\n- An area goes first, before what it holds.\n- After the first element, each new element is followed right away by the arrow that joins it to what is already drawn, then by that arrow's label if it has one.\n- A note goes right after the element it describes, and its arrow right after the note.\n\nCheck every change with a screenshot, saved under a new file name each time, since reading a path you read before can show the earlier image.\n\n## Mockups\n70:## Working with the user\n71-\n72-- **Your tab holds the current state.** Read what you change from `window.editor` right before changing it, since the user may have moved it. Once the user has edited the canvas, never rebuild it; change only the shapes the change touches.\n73-- **Talking in the chat.** Say what changed and ask in the reply. The user answers in the chat, by voice, or with sticky notes.\n12:- `instance.status()` from the binding returns only `status`, `error` (`name`, `message`), `output` and `rollback`; it does not name the step that failed. The REST GET-instance endpoint lists every step with its name, success, output and attempts, each attempt with its error. `status()` does not tell a parked `waitForEvent` waiter from a running one: observed as `running` in most cases and `waiting` in some.\n15:- Steps run once durably via `step.do`; the return value is persisted and replayed on resume instead of re-executing. A repeated step name replays the cached result, so a step inside a loop needs a counter in its name (`poll 7`). Branching is plain JS control flow inside `run()`.\n20:- Recovery: `instance.restart({ from: { name: \"<step>\" } })` on an errored instance re-runs from that step and keeps earlier results; `from` takes `name`, plus `count` for the n-th step of that name and `type` (`do`, `sleep`, `waitForEvent`), and throws when no step matches. `restart()` with no argument erases the instance's state and runs every step again. No option restarts from the failed step without naming that step, and no binding member returns its name. Only the REST get-instance endpoint returns it, and that endpoint needs an API token. Rollback handlers on a step run on failure and on `terminate({ rollback: true })`; a plain `terminate()` runs no code in the instance.\n21:- `restart()` resolves once Cloudflare accepts the restart, before any step runs. Through a cross-script binding it took 0.4 to 0.6 s, and right after it `status()` read `queued` with `error` null. The restarted step started 3.1 to 3.8 s later, with a median of 3.4 s, whether it restarted from a named step or from the start, so the delay is scheduling, not replay. A caller that wants the step's own effect, such as a status write, waits for it after `restart()` returns.\n22:- Deploys: a deploy fails every step executing at that moment (`WorkflowInternalError`) and runs the body again even if it had finished, so step bodies must be idempotent; the failed attempt counts against the step's retry limit. Workflows sometimes records the failure only when the step's timeout fires, and twice a `waitForEvent` across a deploy resolved exactly 5 minutes late. Sleeping instances wake on the new code and keep the results of steps already run; a step added before the sleep point runs on wake, and a renamed sleep step sleeps again. An instance created within seconds of a deploy can start on the old code. `wrangler delete` on the Worker leaves its Workflow in place; `wrangler workflows delete <name>` removes it.\n23:- Cross-script binding: a Worker in another script binds the Workflow with `script_name`. That Worker deploys even when the target script does not exist, and wrangler gives no warning; its calls throw `workflow.not_found` until the target deploys, then work within about 3 s with no redeploy. Locally, see `local-dev`.\n27:- A step is ordinary Worker code, so it reaches code in other languages through what it can call: a service binding to another Worker, a Container through its Durable Object, or plain HTTP. Whether a `script_name` binding works across languages is undocumented.\n28:- Steps per instance: 10,000 by default on Paid, configurable to 25,000 (1,024 on Free); `step.sleep` does not count as a step. A sleep-and-poll loop at 5 s used about 31 steps per 3 minutes of waiting. State per instance is up to 1 GB on Paid.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ac7a35af-5aec-4374-abb8-fa7885a2fdd6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3b695a81-4f7b-4f91-84bd-94ea91ed9800","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
