{"parentUuid":"371bf5bb-0ff5-45c4-b45f-cddae6e9ce99","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round3/drafts.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round3/drafts.md","content":"# Learn round 3: drafts\n\n## 1. New skill: test-design-with-canvas\n\nLands in `mahou/skills/test-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`.\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: test-design-with-canvas\ndescription: Runs the test-design skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The candidate roots are drawn on a map of the system for the user to pick, and each branch is drawn as a classification tree with its combination matrix under it; the user corrects with sticky notes or by voice. Use when the user wants to design the test cases and manual validations of a settled change on a canvas.\nargument-hint: <the settled design to prove>\n---\n\nLoad mahou:test-design first, then mahou:canvas. Then read `.mahou/test-design-with-canvas.md` if present.\n\n# Test design with canvas\n\n`test-design` and the skills it loads keep their procedure: roots, branches, classifications, partitions, matrices, facts, dropped rows, recording and ending.\n\nThe document is `canvas/test-design.tldr.json` in the change's scratch folder. The record stays `test-design/<repo>.md`; write a tree's change into it whenever the canvas changes, and draw the canvas from it.\n\n## Roots on a map of the system\n\nThe candidate roots go on the canvas as a map of the system, under a title naming the change and the order the trees are worked in.\n\n- **Each tree is an `area`**, labelled with its letter, its repository or deployable, and what it is to the rest of the system: `D api/: what a client calls`. Lay the areas out in the order work flows through them, whoever starts it on the left; put the tree that every other one imports along the bottom.\n- **Each candidate root is a blue card** inside its tree's area: `☐ <id> <entry point>`, then `e.g. <scenario>`, one or two scenarios through that entry point, an error case among them.\n- **A candidate that is not a root is a grey dashed card** with the reason, such as a piece that runs only inside another entry point, or a migration validated through a read.\n- **Arrows join the cards that hand work to each other**, labelled with the action: create, restart, start.\n\nTell the user to tick or cross out cards with sticky notes or by voice.\n\n## Each branch is a classification tree\n\nEach branch is an `area` labelled `<id> <root> · <status>`, inside its tree's area. Branches stack down their tree's area, and the trees sit side by side.\n\n- **The root is a blue box** at the top, with the entry point and what it is given.\n- **Each classification is a violet box** under the root, joined to it by a line with no arrowheads.\n- **Each partition is a green box** under its classification, joined the same way, with a short label.\n- **The matrix goes under the tree.** A header line reads `row · what the test does` on the left and `expected · kind · setup` on the right, both in grey. Each row is one line: its id and summary on the left, a filled dot under the centre of each partition it picks, and its expected outcome, kind and setup on the right. Dotted grey lines run between the rows and down each partition's column.\n- **A row run by hand is orange**, its text and its dots, whether it is `test + manual` or `manual`. Its expected outcome adds what the check observes by hand.\n- **Dropped rows get a grey line under the matrix**, `Dropped: <reason>`.\n- **A branch with one row** draws its root and that row, with no classifications.\n\n## Messages from Claude\n\nWrite what you would say in the chat as one Claude note at the top of the canvas: how to read the drawing, the open questions, and the implementation tasks the trees surfaced. Replace its text each round.\n\n## Ending\n\nWhen every tree is settled, relabel its areas `settled`, list the implementation tasks in the Claude note, continue with `test-design`'s Recording and Ending in the chat, and stop the canvas as `canvas` says.\n```\n\nPlus, in `mahou/README.md`, the with-canvas line:\n\nOld: \"`mahou:explore-with-canvas`, `mahou:shape-with-canvas`, `mahou:design-with-canvas`, `mahou:program-design-with-canvas`. Run the skill they name ... while the record file keeps the text form.\"\n\nNew: \"`mahou:explore-with-canvas`, `mahou:shape-with-canvas`, `mahou:design-with-canvas`, `mahou:program-design-with-canvas`, `mahou:test-design-with-canvas`. Run the skill they name ... while the record file keeps the text form. Test design draws the candidate roots on a map of the system for you to pick, then each branch as a classification tree with its matrix under it, a dot under each partition that a row picks.\"\n\nAnd in the claude-plugins `CLAUDE.md`, after the `program-design-with-canvas` parenthesis:\n\n\", `test-design-with-canvas` (added 2026-10-11: runs test-design on a canvas; the candidate roots as ☐ cards on a map of the system, one area per tree, grey dashed cards for what is not a root; each branch a classification tree, root blue, classifications violet, partitions green, with the matrix under it as dots under the partitions and orange rows for those run by hand; `test-design/<repo>.md` keeps the record)\"\n\n## 2. Canvas: stop it when the work is done\n\nLands in `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, a new section before \"What this skill is not\":\n\n```markdown\n## Ending\n\nWhen the loading skill's work is done, or the user moves on to other work, stop the canvas: run `canvas.sh stop` on the document, stop the monitor running `canvas.sh watch`, and close your `agent-browser` tab.\n```\n\n## 3. Canvas: draw a large batch in one script\n\nLands in `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, \"Running it\", after the paragraph beginning \"Draw in steps the user can follow\":\n\n```markdown\nDraw a large batch in one script instead, without `canvas.step`, when a change creates or redraws many shapes at once, such as every tree rebuilt from a revised record. Stepping through hundreds of shapes makes the user wait for a drawing they will read only once it is finished.\n```\n\n## 4. Program design: diagrams as the representations\n\nThe base representations change, and the canvas adds only how to draw them. Lands in `mahou/skills/program-design/` and `mahou/skills/program-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`.\n\n`program-design/SKILL.md`, \"The level\", second sentence:\n\nOld: \"No function bodies, no declarations in the language's syntax, no snippet that would compile. A shape is a JSON Schema, a call path is a callstack, a value is a line in a derivation ledger, a file is a path in the module list.\"\n\nNew: \"No function bodies, no declarations in the language's syntax, no snippet that would compile. A shape is a class diagram, a call path is a flowchart, a value is a line in a derivation ledger, a file is a box in the module list.\"\n\n`references/representations/package-layout.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n\n```markdown\nA mermaid flowchart of the packages. Each package is a node labelled with its folder, its package name and how it is built or run; the workspace is a document node joined to the packages it holds; a target a package deploys to is a store or a cloud. Edges between packages are labelled with what one imports from the other. Colour each package by the deployable it belongs to, with the palette in superpowers:mermaid.\n\nThe prose under it says what the diagram cannot: why each package is separate, what is published, what is built, what runs it, which runtime version it needs and why, and the dependencies the change adds.\n```\n\n`references/representations/interfaces.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n\n```markdown\nTwo mermaid diagrams. A class diagram of the shapes that cross between parts: one class per shape, its members with `?` on the optional ones, a stereotype for a union, and an edge for each shape that holds another. A flowchart of who calls what: the parts the branch adds or changes as nodes grouped by module, each labelled with the operations it exposes, and an edge from each caller to the part it calls, labelled when the call carries a particular direction or mode.\n\nThe prose under them says what they cannot: what an operation answers, which callers change with a part, and what the boundary does with an absent member, a present value and an explicit null when that differs between paths.\n```\n\n`references/representations/callstack.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n\n```markdown\nA top-to-bottom mermaid flowchart per entry, from the request and what it is given to every response it can give. A check is a diamond, an action a rectangle coloured by the deployable that runs it, a refusal a red terminal with its status, a success a green one. A failure the entry absorbs is a dotted edge labelled with what went wrong.\n\nThe prose under it names the other entries that reach the same parts and states the rule the flowchart implements, once.\n```\n\n`references/representations/module-list.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n\n```markdown\nA mermaid flowchart with three subgraphs: new, touched and deleted. Each node is a file or a set of files in one folder, labelled with the path and, when the path alone does not say it, why it changes. New nodes are green, touched amber and deleted red.\n\nUnder it, the placement decisions and only those, each citing the standard it follows when a standard covers it.\n```\n\n`references/representations/standards.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n\n```markdown\nA mermaid flowchart: each source of conventions is a document node, joined to a node listing what it governs in this root, stated in terms of the root's settled branches.\n```\n\n`program-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, appended to \"The canvas\":\n\n```markdown\n- **Draw a part whose representation is a mermaid diagram as a `mermaid` shape** holding the record's source, sized to the diagram's own `viewBox`, with a caption of one or two lines under it.\n```\n\n## 5. Test design: manual picks and partitions\n\nLands in `mahou/skills/test-design/SKILL.md`.\n\n\"Working a branch\", step 2, Old: \"Two partitions that the code treats identically are one partition.\"\n\nNew: \"Two partitions that the code treats identically are one partition. When every input takes the code down the same path, the root is one row.\"\n\nThe paragraph under the steps, Old: \"The manual picks on a service always include the most common flows, error cases included. Schema rejections are not manual picks.\"\n\nNew: \"Pick the manual checks from the test rows: a picked row's kind becomes `test + manual`, and its expected outcome adds what the check observes by hand. On a service they always include the most common flows and the most common error cases. A row is `manual` alone only when no automated test can check it. Schema rejections are not manual picks.\"\n\n## 6. Mermaid: no crossing edges\n\nLands in `superpowers/skills/mermaid/references/flowchart.md`, appended to \"Styling guidance\":\n\n```markdown\n- **No two edges cross.** When the layout makes edges cross, reorder the nodes or restructure the diagram until none do.\n```\n\n## 7. Canvas: delete the arrows of deleted shapes\n\nLands in `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, \"Drawing\":\n\n```markdown\n- **Delete the arrows of the shapes you delete.** An arrow bound to a deleted shape stays on the canvas where it was drawn. After deleting shapes, delete every arrow with fewer than two bindings.\n```\n","numLines":163,"startLine":1,"totalLines":163}},"displayPath":".scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round3/drafts.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7714fef0-7638-48c3-befb-894b05098aea","timestamp":"2026-10-11T06:25:13.771Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/learn/round3/drafts.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Learn round 3: drafts\n2\t\n3\t## 1. New skill: test-design-with-canvas\n4\t\n5\tLands in `mahou/skills/test-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`.\n6\t\n7\t```markdown\n8\t---\n9\tname: test-design-with-canvas\n10\tdescription: Runs the test-design skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The candidate roots are drawn on a map of the system for the user to pick, and each branch is drawn as a classification tree with its combination matrix under it; the user corrects with sticky notes or by voice. Use when the user wants to design the test cases and manual validations of a settled change on a canvas.\n11\targument-hint: <the settled design to prove>\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\tLoad mahou:test-design first, then mahou:canvas. Then read `.mahou/test-design-with-canvas.md` if present.\n15\t\n16\t# Test design with canvas\n17\t\n18\t`test-design` and the skills it loads keep their procedure: roots, branches, classifications, partitions, matrices, facts, dropped rows, recording and ending.\n19\t\n20\tThe document is `canvas/test-design.tldr.json` in the change's scratch folder. The record stays `test-design/<repo>.md`; write a tree's change into it whenever the canvas changes, and draw the canvas from it.\n21\t\n22\t## Roots on a map of the system\n23\t\n24\tThe candidate roots go on the canvas as a map of the system, under a title naming the change and the order the trees are worked in.\n25\t\n26\t- **Each tree is an `area`**, labelled with its letter, its repository or deployable, and what it is to the rest of the system: `D api/: what a client calls`. Lay the areas out in the order work flows through them, whoever starts it on the left; put the tree that every other one imports along the bottom.\n27\t- **Each candidate root is a blue card** inside its tree's area: `☐ <id> <entry point>`, then `e.g. <scenario>`, one or two scenarios through that entry point, an error case among them.\n28\t- **A candidate that is not a root is a grey dashed card** with the reason, such as a piece that runs only inside another entry point, or a migration validated through a read.\n29\t- **Arrows join the cards that hand work to each other**, labelled with the action: create, restart, start.\n30\t\n31\tTell the user to tick or cross out cards with sticky notes or by voice.\n32\t\n33\t## Each branch is a classification tree\n34\t\n35\tEach branch is an `area` labelled `<id> <root> · <status>`, inside its tree's area. Branches stack down their tree's area, and the trees sit side by side.\n36\t\n37\t- **The root is a blue box** at the top, with the entry point and what it is given.\n38\t- **Each classification is a violet box** under the root, joined to it by a line with no arrowheads.\n39\t- **Each partition is a green box** under its classification, joined the same way, with a short label.\n40\t- **The matrix goes under the tree.** A header line reads `row · what the test does` on the left and `expected · kind · setup` on the right, both in grey. Each row is one line: its id and summary on the left, a filled dot under the centre of each partition it picks, and its expected outcome, kind and setup on the right. Dotted grey lines run between the rows and down each partition's column.\n41\t- **A row run by hand is orange**, its text and its dots, whether it is `test + manual` or `manual`. Its expected outcome adds what the check observes by hand.\n42\t- **Dropped rows get a grey line under the matrix**, `Dropped: <reason>`.\n43\t- **A branch with one row** draws its root and that row, with no classifications.\n44\t\n45\t## Messages from Claude\n46\t\n47\tWrite what you would say in the chat as one Claude note at the top of the canvas: how to read the drawing, the open questions, and the implementation tasks the trees surfaced. Replace its text each round.\n48\t\n49\t## Ending\n50\t\n51\tWhen every tree is settled, relabel its areas `settled`, list the implementation tasks in the Claude note, continue with `test-design`'s Recording and Ending in the chat, and stop the canvas as `canvas` says.\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tPlus, in `mahou/README.md`, the with-canvas line:\n55\t\n56\tOld: \"`mahou:explore-with-canvas`, `mahou:shape-with-canvas`, `mahou:design-with-canvas`, `mahou:program-design-with-canvas`. Run the skill they name ... while the record file keeps the text form.\"\n57\t\n58\tNew: \"`mahou:explore-with-canvas`, `mahou:shape-with-canvas`, `mahou:design-with-canvas`, `mahou:program-design-with-canvas`, `mahou:test-design-with-canvas`. Run the skill they name ... while the record file keeps the text form. Test design draws the candidate roots on a map of the system for you to pick, then each branch as a classification tree with its matrix under it, a dot under each partition that a row picks.\"\n59\t\n60\tAnd in the claude-plugins `CLAUDE.md`, after the `program-design-with-canvas` parenthesis:\n61\t\n62\t\", `test-design-with-canvas` (added 2026-10-11: runs test-design on a canvas; the candidate roots as ☐ cards on a map of the system, one area per tree, grey dashed cards for what is not a root; each branch a classification tree, root blue, classifications violet, partitions green, with the matrix under it as dots under the partitions and orange rows for those run by hand; `test-design/<repo>.md` keeps the record)\"\n63\t\n64\t## 2. Canvas: stop it when the work is done\n65\t\n66\tLands in `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, a new section before \"What this skill is not\":\n67\t\n68\t```markdown\n69\t## Ending\n70\t\n71\tWhen the loading skill's work is done, or the user moves on to other work, stop the canvas: run `canvas.sh stop` on the document, stop the monitor running `canvas.sh watch`, and close your `agent-browser` tab.\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\t## 3. Canvas: draw a large batch in one script\n75\t\n76\tLands in `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, \"Running it\", after the paragraph beginning \"Draw in steps the user can follow\":\n77\t\n78\t```markdown\n79\tDraw a large batch in one script instead, without `canvas.step`, when a change creates or redraws many shapes at once, such as every tree rebuilt from a revised record. Stepping through hundreds of shapes makes the user wait for a drawing they will read only once it is finished.\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\t## 4. Program design: diagrams as the representations\n83\t\n84\tThe base representations change, and the canvas adds only how to draw them. Lands in `mahou/skills/program-design/` and `mahou/skills/program-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`.\n85\t\n86\t`program-design/SKILL.md`, \"The level\", second sentence:\n87\t\n88\tOld: \"No function bodies, no declarations in the language's syntax, no snippet that would compile. A shape is a JSON Schema, a call path is a callstack, a value is a line in a derivation ledger, a file is a path in the module list.\"\n89\t\n90\tNew: \"No function bodies, no declarations in the language's syntax, no snippet that would compile. A shape is a class diagram, a call path is a flowchart, a value is a line in a derivation ledger, a file is a box in the module list.\"\n91\t\n92\t`references/representations/package-layout.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n93\t\n94\t```markdown\n95\tA mermaid flowchart of the packages. Each package is a node labelled with its folder, its package name and how it is built or run; the workspace is a document node joined to the packages it holds; a target a package deploys to is a store or a cloud. Edges between packages are labelled with what one imports from the other. Colour each package by the deployable it belongs to, with the palette in superpowers:mermaid.\n96\t\n97\tThe prose under it says what the diagram cannot: why each package is separate, what is published, what is built, what runs it, which runtime version it needs and why, and the dependencies the change adds.\n98\t```\n99\t\n100\t`references/representations/interfaces.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n101\t\n102\t```markdown\n103\tTwo mermaid diagrams. A class diagram of the shapes that cross between parts: one class per shape, its members with `?` on the optional ones, a stereotype for a union, and an edge for each shape that holds another. A flowchart of who calls what: the parts the branch adds or changes as nodes grouped by module, each labelled with the operations it exposes, and an edge from each caller to the part it calls, labelled when the call carries a particular direction or mode.\n104\t\n105\tThe prose under them says what they cannot: what an operation answers, which callers change with a part, and what the boundary does with an absent member, a present value and an explicit null when that differs between paths.\n106\t```\n107\t\n108\t`references/representations/callstack.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n109\t\n110\t```markdown\n111\tA top-to-bottom mermaid flowchart per entry, from the request and what it is given to every response it can give. A check is a diamond, an action a rectangle coloured by the deployable that runs it, a refusal a red terminal with its status, a success a green one. A failure the entry absorbs is a dotted edge labelled with what went wrong.\n112\t\n113\tThe prose under it names the other entries that reach the same parts and states the rule the flowchart implements, once.\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\t`references/representations/module-list.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n117\t\n118\t```markdown\n119\tA mermaid flowchart with three subgraphs: new, touched and deleted. Each node is a file or a set of files in one folder, labelled with the path and, when the path alone does not say it, why it changes. New nodes are green, touched amber and deleted red.\n120\t\n121\tUnder it, the placement decisions and only those, each citing the standard it follows when a standard covers it.\n122\t```\n123\t\n124\t`references/representations/standards.md`, \"Representation\", replaced:\n125\t\n126\t```markdown\n127\tA mermaid flowchart: each source of conventions is a document node, joined to a node listing what it governs in this root, stated in terms of the root's settled branches.\n128\t```\n129\t\n130\t`program-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md`, appended to \"The canvas\":\n131\t\n132\t```markdown\n133\t- **Draw a part whose representation is a mermaid diagram as a `mermaid` shape** holding the record's source, sized to the diagram's own `viewBox`, with a caption of one or two lines under it.\n134\t```\n135\t\n136\t## 5. Test design: manual picks and partitions\n137\t\n138\tLands in `mahou/skills/test-design/SKILL.md`.\n139\t\n140\t\"Working a branch\", step 2, Old: \"Two partitions that the code treats identically are one partition.\"\n141\t\n142\tNew: \"Two partitions that the code treats identically are one partition. When every input takes the code down the same path, the root is one row.\"\n143\t\n144\tThe paragraph under the steps, Old: \"The manual picks on a service always include the most common flows, error cases included. Schema rejections are not manual picks.\"\n145\t\n146\tNew: \"Pick the manual checks from the test rows: a picked row's kind becomes `test + manual`, and its expected outcome adds what the check observes by hand. On a service they always include the most common flows and the most common error cases. A row is `manual` alone only when no automated test can check it. Schema rejections are not manual picks.\"\n147\t\n148\t## 6. Mermaid: no crossing edges\n149\t\n150\tLands in `superpowers/skills/mermaid/references/flowchart.md`, appended to \"Styling guidance\":\n151\t\n152\t```markdown\n153\t- **No two edges cross.** When the layout makes edges cross, reorder the nodes or restructure the diagram until none do.\n154\t```\n155\t\n156\t## 7. Canvas: delete the arrows of deleted shapes\n157\t\n158\tLands in `mahou/skills/canvas/SKILL.md`, \"Drawing\":\n159\t\n160\t```markdown\n161\t- **Delete the arrows of the shapes you delete.** An arrow bound to a deleted shape stays on the canvas where it was drawn. After deleting shapes, delete every arrow with fewer than two bindings.\n162\t```\n163\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
