{"parentUuid":"0c1f041a-78e2-420b-b80a-5e0735016696","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/home/agus/.claude/CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# General Coding Style Guide\n\n* Use conventional commits\n* I like an \"airy\" code style, grouping code in blocks of related instructions\n* Leave spaces before and after control structures like ifs and fors\n* Avoid creating intermediate variables when possible; only case it might make sense is when the intermediate variable makes the code easier to understand and follow\n* Do not write obvious, narrative comments (e.g. explaining what a variable holds or restating what the next line does). Good naming and types already convey the *what*. Only add a comment when it explains the **WHY** behind a non-obvious decision (a hidden constraint, a workaround, a subtle invariant), or to disambiguate a confusing **block** of code whose intent isn't clear from reading it. If removing the comment wouldn't leave a future reader confused, don't write it.\n\n# TypeScript Coding Style Guide\n\n- When checking for a null or undefined value in any language, use the explicit syntax `value == null`.\n- When creating new types, use existing types as base if available (e.g. extending interfaces or referencing their properties).\n- Never use TypeScript's enum, instead use a constant map and derive the type from it.\n- By default, assume each function or component must be defined in its own module, unless specified otherwise or it has high cohesion with another element and it might make sense to keep them together.\n\n# React Coding Style Guide\n\n## General\n\n- Always use named exports.\n- Prefer assigning arrow functions to a variable over using a function declaration.\n- When trying to install node dependencies, check the available lock file on the root folder to determine which tool to use.\n\n## Imports and Component Structure\n\n- Always import React internals (e.g. `useState`, `useEffect`) by name from the React module, never use the default import.\n- Colocate related code (state, effects, functions) within a component to improve readability. Place common code above the blocks where it is used.\n- Each function or component should be defined in its own module by default, unless it has high cohesion with another element.\n\n## Component Definition\n\n- Always use arrow functions for React components; do not use the `React.FC` type.\n- Destructure props directly in the function arguments when defining a component.\n- When generating a component's props interface, always use the `Props` name.\n\n## JSX and Rendering\n\n- Within JSX, always use ternary operators for conditional rendering.\n- Avoid nested ternaries in JSX.\n- Use Panda-css syntax for styling: `className={css({ color: \"red\" })}`.\n- Always add a comma after the last item in an object or array.\n\n## React Query\n\n- Unless the project is following a different pattern, always aim to use react-query for remote data fetching and manipulation\n- Avoid destructuring queries and mutations.\n\n# Python Coding Style Guide\n\n- **Dependency management**: I use poetry for dependency management, so make sure to use it to\n    install dependencies and run commands.\n- **Explicit Null Checks**: When checking for a null value, always use explicit syntax: `value is None`.\n- **Optional Types**: Use the newer `| None` syntax for optional types instead of `Optional[]`.\n- **Type Hints**: Use type hints throughout your code for clarity and type safety.\n- **Testing**: Write tests for all new code, using pytest and relevant fixtures.\n\n# Writing style\nMannered prose substitutes metaphor and flourish for direct statement. Instead of \"a parameter worth varying,\" the mannered writer produces \"a dial worth turning.\" Instead of \"this point still matters,\" they write \"this point earns its keep.\" The phrases exist to display the writer, not to convey the idea, and readers can tell. That is why mannered prose irritates: it makes the reader work harder so the writer can perform. It is also imprecise. Metaphors drag in connotations the writer did not choose and cannot control.\nNever ever use mannered prose or metaphors on your writing, always say what you mean directly, no need to embellish or use complicated prose to impress the reader.\n\nDo not describe the message inside the message. Some of your words refer to the subject you are writing about; others refer to the text itself, to how many parts it has, what order they come in, that something is about to arrive, or that you are about to say it. That second kind tells the reader nothing, because the thing being announced arrives in the very next words anyway. Delete it, then check that every fact survived the deletion.\n\nThese spans hide anywhere in a sentence, not only in front of a colon. \"Two things to take into account:\" and \"One thing worth doing now:\" and \"Processing X, the next step is:\" sit at the front. \"The config is fine, but there is one thing worth flagging here\" buries it mid-sentence. \"I should mention that the build fails on ARM\" becomes \"the build fails on ARM\". \"Which brings us to the next point\" just goes. The same applies to headings: name what is in the section (\"Issues found\", \"Considerations\"), do not count it or trail it (\"Two things to consider\", \"What follows\"). A count also goes stale as soon as an item is added or removed.\n\nDo not say the same thing twice. A second pass over an idea, in different words at the same level of detail, gives a reader who understood the first pass nothing. It shows up as a closing sentence that recaps the paragraph above it, an \"in other words\" that paraphrases instead of sharpening, or a trailing clause that restates the sentence it hangs off. Before writing one, ask what the reader can do or know after the second version that they could not after the first. If the answer is nothing, cut it. Keep the restatement only when it adds precision, gives a concrete example, or translates a term the first version assumed the reader knew.\n\nMake every pronoun resolve once, to one thing. The same pronoun standing for two different referents in one sentence (\"the unit before it\" where \"it\" is the unit, \"any text it says was left behind\" where \"it\" is the tool from the previous sentence) makes the reader hold both candidates and test each against what follows. A dropped relative pronoun does the same: \"any text it says was left behind\" has lost its \"that\", so \"it says\" reads as the verb until \"was\" breaks the parse. The trap is sharpest when the referent sits in an earlier sentence while a nearer candidate of the same kind sits in this one. Name the referent (\"inside the previous unit\", \"any text the tool says was left behind\") or restore the \"that\". A short sentence can still make the reader resolve one word twice; length is not what makes this readable.\n\nWrite actions as verbs, not as nouns. An event turned into a noun phrase (\"the server failing to start\", \"the removal of the file\", \"validation of the input\") and hung on a weak verb (\"goes in the chat\", \"happens\", \"takes place\") makes the reader unpack who does what. Write the clause with its actor and its verb: \"Only the server failing to start goes in the chat\" becomes \"When the server fails to start, tell the user in the chat\", and \"Validation of the input happens in the parser\" becomes \"The parser validates the input\". A noun stays when it names something the text keeps referring to, such as a defined term.\n\nGive the content, not a pointer to it. A clause like \"what a thing is\", \"what flows\" or \"what the arrows cannot show\" points at information without giving it, and the reader has to guess what belongs there; write the information or a concrete example instead (\"label each arrow with the payload or action it carries\"). An artifact that talks does the same: a shape, label or test that \"says\" or \"shows\" something is a rule hidden behind the artifact, so write the rule as an instruction (\"draw each module as a rectangle\"). A bold lead-in is a label naming the item or the instruction itself, never a slogan such as \"**Shapes say what a thing is.**\"\n\n# Other notes\n\n## Executing root restricted actions\n\nIf you ever need to execute a root restricted action (e.g. using `sudo` to call a CLI tool), you can use `pkexec` to provide the user a way to give your action the adequate permissions to run."},{"path":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# Nagara\n\n**Nagara (ながら)**: from *ながら聞き*, consuming content *while doing something else*. A private,\nAPI-first **audio read-later queue**: `enqueue(url, voice?) → generate eagerly → private item with\na listen link`. The TTS pipeline is proven; the open question is demand: see\n[`docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md`](docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md).\n\n## Layout and commands\n\n| Tree | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `api/` | The API Worker: the only routes clients reach (enqueue, poll, retry, audio, images, health), behind a single API key; it writes the item row at enqueue and creates and restarts the item Workflow |\n| `tts/` | The GPU text-to-speech service: a separate Modal deployable the item Workflow calls through two web endpoints |\n| `pipeline/` | The pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, with no public routes: the item Workflow that does all the work for one item, a DomainQueue Durable Object per domain, the recipe runtime, the Flue author and revision agents |\n| `common/` | The package both Workers build from: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, the item row's reads and writes, the SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library. Only the migrations are deployed |\n| `web/` | The web surfaces: not yet built on `main`. The concluded read-along player spike is preserved on the `idea/read-along-player` branch with its tree relocated to `web/`, so checking that branch out is how you see what the spike settled; it is reference material, not a starting point |\n| `docs/` | The documentation. **Read [`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md) before touching anything under it**, not only before adding a note, but before editing, renaming, or moving one. `technical-design/` is how the code works, `product-design/` is what nagara is, and a note explains how a part works, mechanism first, with reasoning in callouts; it is not a decision record. Work in flight lives in Linear, never in `docs/`; see \"Work tracking\" below. |\n\n```\ntts/       uv run modal serve app.py (dev) · uv run modal deploy app.py (prod)\n           uv run pytest · uv run ruff check · uv run ty check\npipeline/  pnpm install · pnpm dev (vite dev) · pnpm deploy (prod, via wrangler)\n           pnpm test · pnpm lint · pnpm types\nweb/       pnpm dev · pnpm test · pnpm build · pnpm biome check      (not yet built)\n```\n\nLocally both Workers share one state folder, with D1 and R2 on disk, and Modal uploads audio through a\nquick tunnel and a local proxy: read\n[`docs/technical-design/local-development.md`](docs/technical-design/local-development.md) before\nrunning them together.\n\n`tts/` is pinned to **Python 3.12** (Kokoro / `modal`-client constraints; the system Python is\nnewer). `tts/` carries the Modal image's runtime deps (kokoro/torch-cpu/numpy/soundfile) as\n**dev** dependencies, not runtime ones, so `ty` can type-check all of `app.py` locally: do not \"clean\nup\" those dependencies out of the dev group.\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory,\non pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. One\nCloudflare workflow deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: it applies `common/`'s migrations to D1, then\ndeploys the pipeline Worker from the built `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, then the API Worker; a job runs\nonly when no job before it failed, so a failed check in `common/` stops all three. `tts` adds a deploy job to Modal gated on its own three checks. Read\n[`docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md`](docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md) before\ntouching deploy configuration.\n\n## Invariants that must not drift\n\nThese are decisions, not preferences. Each has a note in `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`\nexplaining what it buys; if the two disagree, that note is the explanation and this is the summary:\nfix both.\n\n1. **One extraction is the source of truth.** The spoken form never reaches a client and the display\n   form is never synthesized: both come from one markdown segmentation and ride on the same typed unit.\n2. **Display, spoken, and timing ride on one typed unit, never matched by text.** A dropped unit leaves\n   the one list and its window goes with it; a length mismatch at finalize fails the item.\n3. **Timing windows are contiguous and the last `end` equals the audio duration.** The inter-paragraph\n   pause is folded into the preceding window.\n4. **Every route that touches an item requires the key**: enqueue, poll, and audio alike. `/health` is\n   the only unauthenticated route.\n5. **No Worker imports the TTS code.** `tts/` is an image definition uploaded to Modal; the item\n   Workflow reaches it over HTTP through two web endpoints behind Modal's proxy auth: no broker, no\n   background sweeper. Deferred work runs as the item Workflow's steps, each retried on its own; the\n   retry route restarts a failed item from the step that failed, and the API queues an item again\n   when it reads one that has been queued for more than 15 minutes.\n6. **Which backend is a question about configuration, never an environment name.** No `if production`,\n   no `if testing` in runtime code.\n7. **A schema change is a migration.** Hand-written SQL migrations in `common/` own the D1 schema,\n   applied before either Worker deploys; the Workers' tests build their schema from the same\n   migrations.\n8. **The three deployables ship separately, and no CI pipeline runs on another's tree except\n   `common/`'s.** A change to `common/` runs both Workers' type checks; the Cloudflare side deploys\n   `common/`'s migrations, then the pipeline Worker, then the API Worker.\n9. **Read-along highlight sync is `requestAnimationFrame`, never `timeupdate`.** (Applies to `web/`,\n   which does not exist yet.)\n10. **A settled recipe always runs through the deterministic runtime.** The agent judges, the runtime\n    extracts: an authored or revised script is handed back as source and run like any other recipe, so\n    a fresh recipe and a years-old one extract through the same deterministic path.\n\n## Code style\n\nFollows the global style guide, plus:\n\n- **Payloads crossing a service boundary are typed**: what the Workers share (the item row, the unit\n  union, the item Workflow's params) is a TypeScript type in `common/`, and the TTS service's request\n  and return shapes are pydantic models in `tts/`. The Workers' types are checked when the code builds,\n  never at runtime: a value read back from D1 or received from Modal is trusted to match its type.\n- Everything both Workers use lives in `common/`; code that only `pipeline/` modules share stays in\n  `pipeline/`, in the module it belongs to, and the others import it from there. Each module inside\n  `pipeline/` is as independent of the others as it can be.\n- Comments explain **why**, never what; do not add narration alongside a comment that is already there\n  for a non-obvious reason.\n- A new or changed note is created from `docs/_templates/note.md`; rationale goes in a `> [!NOTE]`\n  callout beside the mechanism it justifies, never as the note's spine. No hard wrapping: one line per\n  paragraph.\n\n## Docs\n\n- **Docs**: `docs/`. Read its `README.md` before adding, editing, renaming or moving anything under it.\n- **Folders**: `technical-design/`, how the code works; `product-design/`, what nagara is.\n- **Run**: see \"Layout and commands\" above.\n- **Checks**: `pnpm test` · `pnpm lint` · `pnpm types` in whichever of `api/`, `pipeline/` and\n  `common/` a change touches (both Workers' as well when it touches `common/`); `uv run pytest` ·\n  `uv run ruff check` · `uv run ty check` in `tts/`.\n- **Seeing it work**: two failure modes pass every check and still don't work. **Extraction can succeed\n  on the wrong thing**: a URL serving a 200-status error page extracts cleanly, generates audio, and\n  reaches `ready`; the status is never `failed`, so a green pipeline is not evidence the item is the\n  article. **And you have to listen to it**: a strip regression is invisible in a diff and inaudible in\n  a test summary, and unmistakable in one second of audio; a leaked `**` is only ever caught by\n  playing the file.\n\n### When adding something\n\n- **A new route** → a route in the API Worker + its request and response types + a section in\n  `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`.\n- **A new rule for what a page yields** → the pipeline's runtime or the authoring prompts, plus a\n  section or callout in `docs/technical-design/recipes.md`.\n- **A new rule for how a job's units become the item's** (the spoken form, the drop) → a function\n  at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step in `pipeline/`, beside the spoken-form derivation, and a\n  test case, plus a section or callout in\n  `docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md`.\n- **A change to the item JSON** → `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`, **and a SQL migration in\n  `common/` when the persisted shape needs a backfill**, because the display list is persisted across\n  the async gap and an in-flight item is read back by a later request than the one that wrote it. **A\n  new member of the unit union needs neither**: the item row's `units` is a JSON column, so the union\n  is not schema and there is no DDL to write, and rows persisted before the new kind existed carry none\n  of it and stay readable unchanged.\n- **A new cross-environment capability** → an interface and a factory that reads configuration, never a\n  branch (invariant 6), plus a paragraph in `docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md`.\n- **Anything both Workers use** → `common/`, described in the note of the part that uses it,\n  with a link to `docs/technical-design/common-package.md`.\n- **Anything that changes what a listener hears or sees** → a note or section in `docs/product-design/`.\n- **A new hard-to-reverse decision** → an entry in \"Invariants that must not drift\" above and in\n  `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`; anything smaller is a `> [!NOTE]` callout beside the mechanism\n  it justifies.\n\n## Work tracking\n\n- **Linear** holds the backlog: workspace `asermax`, team Asermax (key `ASE`), project **nagara**. A\n  milestone is a feature; an issue is one task under it, or a loose task with no milestone. Labels are\n  `Bug`, `Feature`, `Improvement` and `Spike` (throwaway code written to answer a question). Priority is\n  High, Medium or Low. `Backlog` is the default status and `Todo` marks what is about to be worked on.\n- **A feature is shaped and designed before it is built.** `mahou:shape` and `mahou:design` settle it\n  with the user, the design lands as a note under `docs/`, and only then does a milestone get its\n  implementation tasks. A spike precedes that when a question needs code to answer, and it is\n  throwaway: it never merges.\n- **A note never links to an issue.** An issue may cite a note by path. The issue closes and the link\n  would rot; the note is maintained for as long as the part exists.\n- **Branches** are `feat/<feature-name>`, one per feature."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"57569d87-d34a-457d-9fe2-5df6286395a8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T06:26:33.488Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of /home/agus/.claude/CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# General Coding Style Guide\n\n* Use conventional commits\n* I like an \"airy\" code style, grouping code in blocks of related instructions\n* Leave spaces before and after control structures like ifs and fors\n* Avoid creating intermediate variables when possible; only case it might make sense is when the intermediate variable makes the code easier to understand and follow\n* Do not write obvious, narrative comments (e.g. explaining what a variable holds or restating what the next line does). Good naming and types already convey the *what*. Only add a comment when it explains the **WHY** behind a non-obvious decision (a hidden constraint, a workaround, a subtle invariant), or to disambiguate a confusing **block** of code whose intent isn't clear from reading it. If removing the comment wouldn't leave a future reader confused, don't write it.\n\n# TypeScript Coding Style Guide\n\n- When checking for a null or undefined value in any language, use the explicit syntax `value == null`.\n- When creating new types, use existing types as base if available (e.g. extending interfaces or referencing their properties).\n- Never use TypeScript's enum, instead use a constant map and derive the type from it.\n- By default, assume each function or component must be defined in its own module, unless specified otherwise or it has high cohesion with another element and it might make sense to keep them together.\n\n# React Coding Style Guide\n\n## General\n\n- Always use named exports.\n- Prefer assigning arrow functions to a variable over using a function declaration.\n- When trying to install node dependencies, check the available lock file on the root folder to determine which tool to use.\n\n## Imports and Component Structure\n\n- Always import React internals (e.g. `useState`, `useEffect`) by name from the React module, never use the default import.\n- Colocate related code (state, effects, functions) within a component to improve readability. Place common code above the blocks where it is used.\n- Each function or component should be defined in its own module by default, unless it has high cohesion with another element.\n\n## Component Definition\n\n- Always use arrow functions for React components; do not use the `React.FC` type.\n- Destructure props directly in the function arguments when defining a component.\n- When generating a component's props interface, always use the `Props` name.\n\n## JSX and Rendering\n\n- Within JSX, always use ternary operators for conditional rendering.\n- Avoid nested ternaries in JSX.\n- Use Panda-css syntax for styling: `className={css({ color: \"red\" })}`.\n- Always add a comma after the last item in an object or array.\n\n## React Query\n\n- Unless the project is following a different pattern, always aim to use react-query for remote data fetching and manipulation\n- Avoid destructuring queries and mutations.\n\n# Python Coding Style Guide\n\n- **Dependency management**: I use poetry for dependency management, so make sure to use it to\n    install dependencies and run commands.\n- **Explicit Null Checks**: When checking for a null value, always use explicit syntax: `value is None`.\n- **Optional Types**: Use the newer `| None` syntax for optional types instead of `Optional[]`.\n- **Type Hints**: Use type hints throughout your code for clarity and type safety.\n- **Testing**: Write tests for all new code, using pytest and relevant fixtures.\n\n# Writing style\nMannered prose substitutes metaphor and flourish for direct statement. Instead of \"a parameter worth varying,\" the mannered writer produces \"a dial worth turning.\" Instead of \"this point still matters,\" they write \"this point earns its keep.\" The phrases exist to display the writer, not to convey the idea, and readers can tell. That is why mannered prose irritates: it makes the reader work harder so the writer can perform. It is also imprecise. Metaphors drag in connotations the writer did not choose and cannot control.\nNever ever use mannered prose or metaphors on your writing, always say what you mean directly, no need to embellish or use complicated prose to impress the reader.\n\nDo not describe the message inside the message. Some of your words refer to the subject you are writing about; others refer to the text itself, to how many parts it has, what order they come in, that something is about to arrive, or that you are about to say it. That second kind tells the reader nothing, because the thing being announced arrives in the very next words anyway. Delete it, then check that every fact survived the deletion.\n\nThese spans hide anywhere in a sentence, not only in front of a colon. \"Two things to take into account:\" and \"One thing worth doing now:\" and \"Processing X, the next step is:\" sit at the front. \"The config is fine, but there is one thing worth flagging here\" buries it mid-sentence. \"I should mention that the build fails on ARM\" becomes \"the build fails on ARM\". \"Which brings us to the next point\" just goes. The same applies to headings: name what is in the section (\"Issues found\", \"Considerations\"), do not count it or trail it (\"Two things to consider\", \"What follows\"). A count also goes stale as soon as an item is added or removed.\n\nDo not say the same thing twice. A second pass over an idea, in different words at the same level of detail, gives a reader who understood the first pass nothing. It shows up as a closing sentence that recaps the paragraph above it, an \"in other words\" that paraphrases instead of sharpening, or a trailing clause that restates the sentence it hangs off. Before writing one, ask what the reader can do or know after the second version that they could not after the first. If the answer is nothing, cut it. Keep the restatement only when it adds precision, gives a concrete example, or translates a term the first version assumed the reader knew.\n\nMake every pronoun resolve once, to one thing. The same pronoun standing for two different referents in one sentence (\"the unit before it\" where \"it\" is the unit, \"any text it says was left behind\" where \"it\" is the tool from the previous sentence) makes the reader hold both candidates and test each against what follows. A dropped relative pronoun does the same: \"any text it says was left behind\" has lost its \"that\", so \"it says\" reads as the verb until \"was\" breaks the parse. The trap is sharpest when the referent sits in an earlier sentence while a nearer candidate of the same kind sits in this one. Name the referent (\"inside the previous unit\", \"any text the tool says was left behind\") or restore the \"that\". A short sentence can still make the reader resolve one word twice; length is not what makes this readable.\n\nWrite actions as verbs, not as nouns. An event turned into a noun phrase (\"the server failing to start\", \"the removal of the file\", \"validation of the input\") and hung on a weak verb (\"goes in the chat\", \"happens\", \"takes place\") makes the reader unpack who does what. Write the clause with its actor and its verb: \"Only the server failing to start goes in the chat\" becomes \"When the server fails to start, tell the user in the chat\", and \"Validation of the input happens in the parser\" becomes \"The parser validates the input\". A noun stays when it names something the text keeps referring to, such as a defined term.\n\nGive the content, not a pointer to it. A clause like \"what a thing is\", \"what flows\" or \"what the arrows cannot show\" points at information without giving it, and the reader has to guess what belongs there; write the information or a concrete example instead (\"label each arrow with the payload or action it carries\"). An artifact that talks does the same: a shape, label or test that \"says\" or \"shows\" something is a rule hidden behind the artifact, so write the rule as an instruction (\"draw each module as a rectangle\"). A bold lead-in is a label naming the item or the instruction itself, never a slogan such as \"**Shapes say what a thing is.**\"\n\n# Other notes\n\n## Executing root restricted actions\n\nIf you ever need to execute a root restricted action (e.g. using `sudo` to call a CLI tool), you can use `pkexec` to provide the user a way to give your action the adequate permissions to run.\n\nContents of /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Nagara\n\n**Nagara (ながら)**: from *ながら聞き*, consuming content *while doing something else*. A private,\nAPI-first **audio read-later queue**: `enqueue(url, voice?) → generate eagerly → private item with\na listen link`. The TTS pipeline is proven; the open question is demand: see\n[`docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md`](docs/product-design/what-nagara-is.md).\n\n## Layout and commands\n\n| Tree | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `api/` | The API Worker: the only routes clients reach (enqueue, poll, retry, audio, images, health), behind a single API key; it writes the item row at enqueue and creates and restarts the item Workflow |\n| `tts/` | The GPU text-to-speech service: a separate Modal deployable the item Workflow calls through two web endpoints |\n| `pipeline/` | The pipeline Worker on Cloudflare, with no public routes: the item Workflow that does all the work for one item, a DomainQueue Durable Object per domain, the recipe runtime, the Flue author and revision agents |\n| `common/` | The package both Workers build from: the shared types, the R2 key layout, presigning, the item row's reads and writes, the SQL migrations for D1 and the cassette replay library. Only the migrations are deployed |\n| `web/` | The web surfaces: not yet built on `main`. The concluded read-along player spike is preserved on the `idea/read-along-player` branch with its tree relocated to `web/`, so checking that branch out is how you see what the spike settled; it is reference material, not a starting point |\n| `docs/` | The documentation. **Read [`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md) before touching anything under it**, not only before adding a note, but before editing, renaming, or moving one. `technical-design/` is how the code works, `product-design/` is what nagara is, and a note explains how a part works, mechanism first, with reasoning in callouts; it is not a decision record. Work in flight lives in Linear, never in `docs/`; see \"Work tracking\" below. |\n\n```\ntts/       uv run modal serve app.py (dev) · uv run modal deploy app.py (prod)\n           uv run pytest · uv run ruff check · uv run ty check\npipeline/  pnpm install · pnpm dev (vite dev) · pnpm deploy (prod, via wrangler)\n           pnpm test · pnpm lint · pnpm types\nweb/       pnpm dev · pnpm test · pnpm build · pnpm biome check      (not yet built)\n```\n\nLocally both Workers share one state folder, with D1 and R2 on disk, and Modal uploads audio through a\nquick tunnel and a local proxy: read\n[`docs/technical-design/local-development.md`](docs/technical-design/local-development.md) before\nrunning them together.\n\n`tts/` is pinned to **Python 3.12** (Kokoro / `modal`-client constraints; the system Python is\nnewer). `tts/` carries the Modal image's runtime deps (kokoro/torch-cpu/numpy/soundfile) as\n**dev** dependencies, not runtime ones, so `ty` can type-check all of `app.py` locally: do not \"clean\nup\" those dependencies out of the dev group.\n\nCI runs test/lint/types as three parallel jobs per subproject, each path-filtered to its own directory,\non pushes to `main`; a change to `common/` also runs the type checks of `api/` and `pipeline/`. One\nCloudflare workflow deploys with wrangler in a fixed order: it applies `common/`'s migrations to D1, then\ndeploys the pipeline Worker from the built `dist/nagara/wrangler.json`, then the API Worker; a job runs\nonly when no job before it failed, so a failed check in `common/` stops all three. `tts` adds a deploy job to Modal gated on its own three checks. Read\n[`docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md`](docs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md) before\ntouching deploy configuration.\n\n## Invariants that must not drift\n\nThese are decisions, not preferences. Each has a note in `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`\nexplaining what it buys; if the two disagree, that note is the explanation and this is the summary:\nfix both.\n\n1. **One extraction is the source of truth.** The spoken form never reaches a client and the display\n   form is never synthesized: both come from one markdown segmentation and ride on the same typed unit.\n2. **Display, spoken, and timing ride on one typed unit, never matched by text.** A dropped unit leaves\n   the one list and its window goes with it; a length mismatch at finalize fails the item.\n3. **Timing windows are contiguous and the last `end` equals the audio duration.** The inter-paragraph\n   pause is folded into the preceding window.\n4. **Every route that touches an item requires the key**: enqueue, poll, and audio alike. `/health` is\n   the only unauthenticated route.\n5. **No Worker imports the TTS code.** `tts/` is an image definition uploaded to Modal; the item\n   Workflow reaches it over HTTP through two web endpoints behind Modal's proxy auth: no broker, no\n   background sweeper. Deferred work runs as the item Workflow's steps, each retried on its own; the\n   retry route restarts a failed item from the step that failed, and the API queues an item again\n   when it reads one that has been queued for more than 15 minutes.\n6. **Which backend is a question about configuration, never an environment name.** No `if production`,\n   no `if testing` in runtime code.\n7. **A schema change is a migration.** Hand-written SQL migrations in `common/` own the D1 schema,\n   applied before either Worker deploys; the Workers' tests build their schema from the same\n   migrations.\n8. **The three deployables ship separately, and no CI pipeline runs on another's tree except\n   `common/`'s.** A change to `common/` runs both Workers' type checks; the Cloudflare side deploys\n   `common/`'s migrations, then the pipeline Worker, then the API Worker.\n9. **Read-along highlight sync is `requestAnimationFrame`, never `timeupdate`.** (Applies to `web/`,\n   which does not exist yet.)\n10. **A settled recipe always runs through the deterministic runtime.** The agent judges, the runtime\n    extracts: an authored or revised script is handed back as source and run like any other recipe, so\n    a fresh recipe and a years-old one extract through the same deterministic path.\n\n## Code style\n\nFollows the global style guide, plus:\n\n- **Payloads crossing a service boundary are typed**: what the Workers share (the item row, the unit\n  union, the item Workflow's params) is a TypeScript type in `common/`, and the TTS service's request\n  and return shapes are pydantic models in `tts/`. The Workers' types are checked when the code builds,\n  never at runtime: a value read back from D1 or received from Modal is trusted to match its type.\n- Everything both Workers use lives in `common/`; code that only `pipeline/` modules share stays in\n  `pipeline/`, in the module it belongs to, and the others import it from there. Each module inside\n  `pipeline/` is as independent of the others as it can be.\n- Comments explain **why**, never what; do not add narration alongside a comment that is already there\n  for a non-obvious reason.\n- A new or changed note is created from `docs/_templates/note.md`; rationale goes in a `> [!NOTE]`\n  callout beside the mechanism it justifies, never as the note's spine. No hard wrapping: one line per\n  paragraph.\n\n## Docs\n\n- **Docs**: `docs/`. Read its `README.md` before adding, editing, renaming or moving anything under it.\n- **Folders**: `technical-design/`, how the code works; `product-design/`, what nagara is.\n- **Run**: see \"Layout and commands\" above.\n- **Checks**: `pnpm test` · `pnpm lint` · `pnpm types` in whichever of `api/`, `pipeline/` and\n  `common/` a change touches (both Workers' as well when it touches `common/`); `uv run pytest` ·\n  `uv run ruff check` · `uv run ty check` in `tts/`.\n- **Seeing it work**: two failure modes pass every check and still don't work. **Extraction can succeed\n  on the wrong thing**: a URL serving a 200-status error page extracts cleanly, generates audio, and\n  reaches `ready`; the status is never `failed`, so a green pipeline is not evidence the item is the\n  article. **And you have to listen to it**: a strip regression is invisible in a diff and inaudible in\n  a test summary, and unmistakable in one second of audio; a leaked `**` is only ever caught by\n  playing the file.\n\n### When adding something\n\n- **A new route** → a route in the API Worker + its request and response types + a section in\n  `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`.\n- **A new rule for what a page yields** → the pipeline's runtime or the authoring prompts, plus a\n  section or callout in `docs/technical-design/recipes.md`.\n- **A new rule for how a job's units become the item's** (the spoken form, the drop) → a function\n  at the end of the item Workflow's recipe step in `pipeline/`, beside the spoken-form derivation, and a\n  test case, plus a section or callout in\n  `docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md`.\n- **A change to the item JSON** → `docs/technical-design/item-contract.md`, **and a SQL migration in\n  `common/` when the persisted shape needs a backfill**, because the display list is persisted across\n  the async gap and an in-flight item is read back by a later request than the one that wrote it. **A\n  new member of the unit union needs neither**: the item row's `units` is a JSON column, so the union\n  is not schema and there is no DDL to write, and rows persisted before the new kind existed carry none\n  of it and stay readable unchanged.\n- **A new cross-environment capability** → an interface and a factory that reads configuration, never a\n  branch (invariant 6), plus a paragraph in `docs/technical-design/persistence-and-storage.md`.\n- **Anything both Workers use** → `common/`, described in the note of the part that uses it,\n  with a link to `docs/technical-design/common-package.md`.\n- **Anything that changes what a listener hears or sees** → a note or section in `docs/product-design/`.\n- **A new hard-to-reverse decision** → an entry in \"Invariants that must not drift\" above and in\n  `docs/technical-design/invariants.md`; anything smaller is a `> [!NOTE]` callout beside the mechanism\n  it justifies.\n\n## Work tracking\n\n- **Linear** holds the backlog: workspace `asermax`, team Asermax (key `ASE`), project **nagara**. A\n  milestone is a feature; an issue is one task under it, or a loose task with no milestone. Labels are\n  `Bug`, `Feature`, `Improvement` and `Spike` (throwaway code written to answer a question). Priority is\n  High, Medium or Low. `Backlog` is the default status and `Todo` marks what is about to be worked on.\n- **A feature is shaped and designed before it is built.** `mahou:shape` and `mahou:design` settle it\n  with the user, the design lands as a note under `docs/`, and only then does a milestone get its\n  implementation tasks. A spike precedes that when a question needs code to answer, and it is\n  throwaway: it never merges.\n- **A note never links to an issue.** An issue may cite a note by path. The issue closes and the link\n  would rot; the note is maintained for as long as the part exists.\n- **Branches** are `feat/<feature-name>`, one per feature.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"user","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"57569d87-d34a-457d-9fe2-5df6286395a8","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"session_context","context":{"userEmail":"The user's email address is acarrasco@filadd.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.","gitStatus":"This is the git status at the start of the conversation. Note that this status is a snapshot in time, and will not update during the conversation.\n\nCurrent branch: feat/cloudflare-migration\n\nMain branch (you will usually use this for PRs): main\n\nGit user: Agustin Carrasco\n\nStatus:\n(clean)\n\nRecent commits:\nfdc0c1a docs: record what the Cloudflare migration test design settled\n4930ea1 docs: record the program design of the Cloudflare migration\n54cb15d chore: add local spike rules\nc0dbdba docs: describe nagara on Cloudflare\na27a255 chore: add a local skill for syncing nagara with the shin-sekai vault"}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"aa7059f5-4922-4a1a-9032-49a4c12ac39c","timestamp":"2026-10-11T06:26:33.488Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# userEmail\nThe user's email address is acarrasco@filadd.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n# gitStatus\nThis is the git status at the start of the conversation. Note that this status is a snapshot in time, and will not update during the conversation.\n\nCurrent branch: feat/cloudflare-migration\n\nMain branch (you will usually use this for PRs): main\n\nGit user: Agustin Carrasco\n\nStatus:\n(clean)\n\nRecent commits:\nfdc0c1a docs: record what the Cloudflare migration test design settled\n4930ea1 docs: record the program design of the Cloudflare migration\n54cb15d chore: add local spike rules\nc0dbdba docs: describe nagara on Cloudflare\na27a255 chore: add a local skill for syncing nagara with the shin-sekai vault\n\nClaude Code attached this context automatically; it isn't part of the user's message. It describes the user's own account and workspace, so they don't need it reported back.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"user","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
