ons, the cassette replay library exported from `./testing` as msw handlers). One root pnpm workspace holds `common/`, `api/`, `pipeline/`.\n\nRead before touching code, in this order (they are the source of truth; this summary does not replace them):\n\n1. `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/CLAUDE.md` and `docs/README.md`.\n2. The notes under `docs/technical-design/` for your part: `item-lifecycle.md`, `item-contract.md`, `article-extraction.md`, `tts-service.md`, `common-package.md`, `persistence-and-storage.md`, `deployment-and-ci.md`, `local-development.md`, `invariants.md`, `recipes.md`.\n3. The program design record: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/program-design/nagara.md` (every part: package layout, callstacks, interfaces, module list, standards). It is settled; follow it.\n4. The test design: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/test-design/nagara.md`. Your trees' rows are the tests you write.\n5. Spike findings when your part touches what a spike measured: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes/` (`REPORT.md` first; `local-audio-upload/`, `local-audio-tunnel/`, `restart-resolve/`, `workflow-testing/`, `cross-script-binding/`, `shared-d1/`, `modal-http/`, `image-processing/`, `svg-describe/`, `firecrawl-gemini/`). Spike code is throwaway reference: never copy its shortcuts, build clean.\n6. The mahou wiki entries for the technologies you touch (Cloudflare Workers, Workflows, D1, R2, vitest pool workers, msw, Modal, Flue): read `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou/skills/wiki/references/INDEX.md` and `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.mahou/wiki/` indexes and follow them.\n7. The user's style guide: `/home/agus/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n\nWork with medium reasoning effort.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe `test` and `test + manual` rows of your trees are the automated tests you write, through the entry point, at the level the program design's standards and the repository conventions name. Name each test after what it proves. No row id (A1, C5…), Linear id or scratch path appears in a test name, comment or docstring. Keep the row-to-test mapping only in your report. The \"by hand\" part of `test + manual` rows is for the later manual validation; do not run it now. The \"Implementation tasks\" at the end of the test design are yours when they fall in your part.\n\n## Repository rules\n\n- Repository: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara`. Integration branch: `feat/cloudflare-migration` (already holds the design docs commits). Your part says whether you work in the main checkout on that branch or in a worktree branch.\n- Follow the project `CLAUDE.md`, `.mahou/basics.md`, the wiki entries and the user's style guide. Keep changes minimal and consistent with the surrounding code. Touch only the trees your part names; if you must touch another tree (for example the root lockfile when you add a dependency), keep it to that.\n- Run the linters, type checks and full suites of every tree you touch (`pnpm test`, `pnpm lint`, `pnpm types`; `uv run pytest`, `uv run ruff check`, `uv run ty check` in `tts/`). Everything must pass.\n- Commit on your branch with conventional commits (scopes in use: `common`, `pipeline`, `api`, `tts`, `ci`, `docs`). Do not push. Do not open a pull request. Do not merge other branches.\n- Never edit files under `docs/`. When the code has to differ from a note, flag it in your report as design drift.\n\n## Safety rules\n\n- Never print secrets. Read them from the files that hold them (`api/.env`, `pipeline/.dev.vars`, `.scratch/cloudflare-migration/modal-proxy.env`) and copy values by key name with a script, never echoing values.\n- Never deploy. Never run `wrangler deploy`, `wrangler d1 ... --remote`, `modal deploy`, or anything that changes a remote resource. Never touch the production `nagara` Worker, its Workflows or Durable Objects, the `nagara` AI Gateway's settings, or the deployed `nagara-tts` Modal app. Local dev servers and `modal serve` (an ephemeral copy) are allowed only where your part says so.\n- Stop only processes you started, by the PID you recorded. Never `pkill`, `killall` or kill by pattern.\n- Any spike-like scratch resource you create is prefixed `nagara-spike-` and deleted when done.\n\n## Linear\n\nTasks live in Linear (workspace `asermax`, team Asermax, project nagara, milestone \"Cloudflare migration\"), through the `linear-server` MCP tools. Read each of your tasks in full with `get_issue` before starting it. Move a task to `In Progress` with `save_issue` when you start it and to `Done` when its change is implemented, tested and committed. Work the task list doesn't cover becomes a new task in the same milestone, created and worked like the rest; a task that dissolves mid-work is set `Canceled` with a comment saying why. Name each in your report.\n\n## Questions and the user\n\nThe user is away and answers through the Plannotator Inbox (`mcp__plannotator__plannotator_inbox`, load it with ToolSearch). When a design ambiguity changes the outcome, or you need something only the user can give:\n\n1. Send it with `action: send_message`, `thread: \"cloudflare-migration: <your part>\"`, written so it reads on its own (context in two or three sentences, then a `:::question` block with choices and your recommendation).\n2. Do not wait idle. Continue with the work that does not depend on the answer. The reply arrives in your session by itself when you are idle.\n3. If everything left depends on the answer, take the choice that is most consistent with the settled design and easiest to reverse, mark it in the code's commit message body and in your report as \"decided without the user, pending: <question>\", and continue.\n\nDo not ask about what the notes, the program design or the code already answer.\n\n## How to answer\n\nWhen your part is done, your final message is a report: the branch and its commits (hash and subject), a summary per change, the row-to-test mapping, the tail of the lint/types/test output for each tree you touched, every design drift (where the code differs from a note or the program design, and why), the decisions taken without the user, the Linear tasks you moved, created or canceled, and what you could not resolve.\n"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"aefbedd4-3fdc-4005-935f-1de2d59cc020","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cfuz6dYJFYEJVJk418r5Y","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:implement","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"5f587ec3-51d3-4764-a382-0849457880fb","timestamp":"2026-10-11T06:35:35.161Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"5f587ec3-51d3-4764-a382-0849457880fb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"fa24657c-a4b7-4b9d-9c50-2e639d9c0f75","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKuQiwdnG246WBXT5jdu2R","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/briefs/implement-common.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ab315dee-424c-4e35-83f8-8f0a8619070a","timestamp":"2026-10-11T06:35:35.239Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/briefs/implement-common.md","content":"# Implementation brief: nagara on Cloudflare (shared part)\n\nYou are one of several subagents implementing the Cloudflare migration of nagara. The section at the end, \"Your part\", says what is yours. Everything above it is shared by all subagents.\n\n## The change\n\nnagara moves off Railway onto Cloudflare. The Python FastAPI `api/` (Postgres, Railway) becomes a TypeScript API Worker `nagara-api` (D1 + R2). The pipeline Worker `nagara` (today the extraction service: POST /jobs, DomainQueue creating extraction Workflow instances) becomes the item pipeline: one `ItemWorkflow` per item does everything (fetch through Firecrawl, lease from a per-domain DomainQueue DO, recipe through the deterministic runtime and the Flue agents, images, describe, start synthesis on Modal, wait for synthesis, store). `tts/` on Modal gains two proxy-auth web endpoints (start, result) instead of being called by Python. A new source-only package `@nagara/common` in `common/` holds what both Workers share (types, R2 key layout, presign with aws4fetch, the item row's reads and writes including the two-form `writeStatus`, `readItem`, `restartPoint`, the D1 SQL migrations, the cassette replay library exported from `./testing` as msw handlers). One root pnpm workspace holds `common/`, `api/`, `pipeline/`.\n\nRead before touching code, in this order (they are the source of truth; this summary does not replace them):\n\n1. `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/CLAUDE.md` and `docs/README.md`.\n2. The notes under `docs/technical-design/` for your part: `item-lifecycle.md`, `item-contract.md`, `article-extraction.md`, `tts-service.md`, `common-package.md`, `persistence-and-storage.md`, `deployment-and-ci.md`, `local-development.md`, `invariants.md`, `recipes.md`.\n3. The program design record: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/program-design/nagara.md` (every part: package layout, callstacks, interfaces, module list, standards). It is settled; follow it.\n4. The test design: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/test-design/nagara.md`. Your trees' rows are the tests you write.\n5. Spike findings when your part touches what a spike measured: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/spikes/` (`REPORT.md` first; `local-audio-upload/`, `local-audio-tunnel/`, `restart-resolve/`, `workflow-testing/`, `cross-script-binding/`, `shared-d1/`, `modal-http/`, `image-processing/`, `svg-describe/`, `firecrawl-gemini/`). Spike code is throwaway reference: never copy its shortcuts, build clean.\n6. The mahou wiki entries for the technologies you touch (Cloudflare Workers, Workflows, D1, R2, vitest pool workers, msw, Modal, Flue): read `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou/skills/wiki/references/INDEX.md` and `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.mahou/wiki/` indexes and follow them.\n7. The user's style guide: `/home/agus/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n\nWork with medium reasoning effort.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe `test` and `test + manual` rows of your trees are the automated tests you write, through the entry point, at the level the program design's standards and the repository conventions name. Name each test after what it proves. No row id (A1, C5…), Linear id or scratch path appears in a test name, comment or docstring. Keep the row-to-test mapping only in your report. The \"by hand\" part of `test + manual` rows is for the later manual validation; do not run it now. The \"Implementation tasks\" at the end of the test design are yours when they fall in your part.\n\n## Repository rules\n\n- Repository: `/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara`. Integration branch: `feat/cloudflare-migration` (already holds the design docs commits). Your part says whether you work in the main checkout on that branch or in a worktree branch.\n- Follow the project `CLAUDE.md`, `.mahou/basics.md`, the wiki entries and the user's style guide. Keep changes minimal and consistent with the surrounding code. Touch only the trees your part names; if you must touch another tree (for example the root lockfile when you add a dependency), keep it to that.\n- Run the linters, type checks and full suites of every tree you touch (`pnpm test`, `pnpm lint`, `pnpm types`; `uv run pytest`, `uv run ruff check`, `uv run ty check` in `tts/`). Everything must pass.\n- Commit on your branch with conventional commits (scopes in use: `common`, `pipeline`, `api`, `tts`, `ci`, `docs`). Do not push. Do not open a pull request. Do not merge other branches.\n- Never edit files under `docs/`. When the code has to differ from a note, flag it in your report as design drift.\n\n## Safety rules\n\n- Never print secrets. Read them from the files that hold them (`api/.env`, `pipeline/.dev.vars`, `.scratch/cloudflare-migration/modal-proxy.env`) and copy values by key name with a script, never echoing values.\n- Never deploy. Never run `wrangler deploy`, `wrangler d1 ... --remote`, `modal deploy`, or anything that changes a remote resource. Never touch the production `nagara` Worker, its Workflows or Durable Objects, the `nagara` AI Gateway's settings, or the deployed `nagara-tts` Modal app. Local dev servers and `modal serve` (an ephemeral copy) are allowed only where your part says so.\n- Stop only processes you started, by the PID you recorded. Never `pkill`, `killall` or kill by pattern.\n- Any spike-like scratch resource you create is prefixed `nagara-spike-` and deleted when done.\n\n## Linear\n\nTasks live in Linear (workspace `asermax`, team Asermax, project nagara, milestone \"Cloudflare migration\"), through the `linear-server` MCP tools. Read each of your tasks in full with `get_issue` before starting it. Move a task to `In Progress` with `save_issue` when you start it and to `Done` when its change is implemented, tested and committed. Work the task list doesn't cover becomes a new task in the same milestone, created and worked like the rest; a task that dissolves mid-work is set `Canceled` with a comment saying why. Name each in your report.\n\n## Questions and the user\n\nThe user is away and answers through the Plannotator Inbox (`mcp__plannotator__plannotator_inbox`, load it with ToolSearch). When a design ambiguity changes the outcome, or you need something only the user can give:\n\n1. Send it with `action: send_message`, `thread: \"cloudflare-migration: <your part>\"`, written so it reads on its own (context in two or three sentences, then a `:::question` block with choices and your recommendation).\n2. Do not wait idle. Continue with the work that does not depend on the answer. The reply arrives in your session by itself when you are idle.\n3. If everything left depends on the answer, take the choice that is most consistent with the settled design and easiest to reverse, mark it in the code's commit message body and in your report as \"decided without the user, pending: <question>\", and continue.\n\nDo not ask about what the notes, the program design or the code already answer.\n\n## How to answer\n\nWhen your part is done, your final message is a report: the branch and its commits (hash and subject), a summary per change, the row-to-test mapping, the tail of the lint/types/test output for each tree you touched, every design drift (where the code differs from a note or the program design, and why), the decisions taken without the user, the Linear tasks you moved, created or canceled, and what you could not resolve.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5f587ec3-51d3-4764-a382-0849457880fb","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
