trypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"9f187ccd-8c65-4c06-8679-4a01e79c15b4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9185824c-72c2-4513-9524-1b50125adfd3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SkQhkhpJPhBc8DFKXY362t","type":"tool_result","content":"40 briefs/validate.md\n{\"pane_id\":\"w46:p2\",\"sent\":true,\"status\":\"working\"}","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dcef596a-8178-40c3-9150-5022a6b505c6","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:28.777Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"40 briefs/validate.md\n{\"pane_id\":\"w46:p2\",\"sent\":true,\"status\":\"working\"}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design/recipes.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":112,"oldLines":7,"newStart":112,"newLines":7,"lines":["     end"," ```"," ","-Revision triggers when an article fails validation under the current version. The iteration prompt receives the current script, stored whole, and the validator's report. The standing instruction is additive: keep the recipe's previous assumptions and add what this article needs. The exception is a wholesale markup change, where the agent judges the domain was redesigned and may rewrite the recipe outright. Validation is against the triggering article only. The item that triggered the revision holds while the revision runs, then retries with the new version; the listener sees a slow item and nothing else. Authoring and revision both run inside the item Workflow's recipe step, while the item holds its domain's lease (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). Each attempt of the recipe step has 15 minutes; one measured authoring run took 97 s, and the hundred-turn bound is stated in the prompt alone. A revision that gives up writes no version, so the older one stays current and the next article from the domain triggers revision again. Each attempt is independently bounded.","+Revision triggers when an article fails validation under the current version. The iteration prompt receives the current script, stored whole, and the validator's report. The standing instruction is additive: keep the recipe's previous assumptions and add what this article needs. The exception is a wholesale markup change, where the agent judges the domain was redesigned and may rewrite the recipe outright. Validation is against the triggering article only. The item that triggered the revision holds while the revision runs, then retries with the new version; the listener sees a slow item and nothing else. Authoring and revision both run inside the item Workflow's recipe step, while the item holds its domain's lease (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). Each attempt of the recipe step has 15 minutes; one measured authoring run took 97 s, and the hundred-turn bound is stated in the prompt alone. A revision that gives up writes no version, so the older one stays current and the next article from the domain triggers revision again. An authoring or revision run that gives up fails the item at once, like a not-article verdict, without retrying the recipe step, so the runs' own retry bound is the only one that applies. Each attempt is independently bounded."," "," > [!WARNING] A failed revision leaves the domain exposed"," > Every new article from a permanently broken domain triggers one bounded revision attempt before failing, and nothing counts these consecutive attempts: the failures are visible, but the agent spend is unbounded."]}]},{"filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" The item Workflow's fetch step scrapes the page through Firecrawl with its own fetch-based client. Its raw HTML goes to the recipe step unsegmented. The domain of the final URL, after redirects, picks the recipe. A scrape that cannot reach Firecrawl, or returns no HTML, fails the step; once the step has used its 3 attempts the item fails, with `current_step` naming fetch (see [item-lifecycle](item-lifecycle.md)). The fetch step writes what each scrape bills, from Firecrawl's `creditsUsed`, as a ledger row (see [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md)), so a failed item still records the credit it spent."," "," > [!NOTE] What firecrawl is called with, and why rawHtml","-> The scrape asks for `rawHtml` and `markdown` at `proxy: \"auto\"`. The fetch step asks for `rawHtml` rather than firecrawl's cleaned HTML because the cleaning throws away images (9 against 5 on one corpus article), and the recipe declares the article's images from the page's own markup. `markdown` costs no extra credit, so the fetch step requests it as evidence; nothing reads it. `proxy: \"auto\"` bills 1 credit when a basic proxy suffices and 5 on a stealth escalation.","+> The scrape asks for `rawHtml` and `markdown` at `proxy: \"auto\"`. The fetch step asks for `rawHtml` rather than firecrawl's cleaned HTML because the cleaning throws away images (9 against 5 on one corpus article), and the recipe declares the article's images from the page's own markup. `markdown` costs no extra credit, so the fetch step requests it as evidence; nothing reads it. The ledger row records the `creditsUsed` each response reports, whichever proxy `auto` settled on."," "," > [!WARNING] firecrawl's output is non-deterministic"," > The same URL scraped minutes apart returned a 5x spread in size, and `rawHtml` is post-JavaScript, so it can carry hydrated page chrome a plain fetch never had. The recipe's container and ignores are what keep that chrome out of the units. The spread affects re-recording a test cassette, never a replay, which returns recorded bytes verbatim."]},{"oldStart":93,"oldLines":14,"newStart":93,"newLines":14,"lines":[" "," ### ⬇️ Acquisition: download, validate, re-encode, store"," ","-A declared image is downloaded under a per-host semaphore (default 2) beneath a global one (default 10), with a 10-second timeout and a 10 MB streamed size cap. The per-host bound is the one that matters: one article's many same-host images must never open that many connections to a server nagara has no relationship with. A Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections at a time, so at most 6 downloads run at once whatever the global bound.","+A declared image's `src` is resolved against the item's URL, so a relative path reaches the article's own host, and a `data:` URI is decoded in place without a download. The image is downloaded under a per-host semaphore (default 2) beneath a global one (default 10), with a 10-second timeout and a 10 MB streamed size cap. The per-host bound is the one that matters: one article's many same-host images must never open that many connections to a server nagara has no relationship with. A Workers instance holds 6 outbound connections at a time, so at most 6 downloads run at once whatever the global bound."," ","-Validation runs on the **decoded bytes**, never on HTTP or HTML metadata. The bytes are sniffed for an `<svg` root. A raster image goes through Cloudflare's Images binding, which decodes it outside the Worker, and is **kept when `min(width, height) >= 200`**, \\1 A smaller raster is dropped and records no degradation. What survives is re-encoded to WebP through the same binding. Each kept image is stored once per content hash, taken over the stored bytes, under the images prefix of the R2 bucket, with its content type on the object, and the hash becomes the unit's image reference, so no origin URL leaks into persisted markdown. common/'s image key function builds the object's key from the hash (see [common-package](common-package.md), and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) for the bucket layout).","+Validation runs on the **decoded bytes**, never on HTTP or HTML metadata. The bytes are sniffed for an `<svg` root. A raster image goes through Cloudflare's Images binding, which decodes it outside the Worker, and is **kept when `min(width, height) >= 200`**, measured on the decoded file. A smaller raster is dropped and records no degradation. What survives is re-encoded to WebP through the same binding. Each kept image is stored once per content hash, taken over the stored bytes, under the images prefix of the R2 bucket, with its content type on the object, and the hash becomes the unit's image reference, so no origin URL leaks into persisted markdown. common/'s image key function builds the object's key from the hash (see [common-package](common-package.md), and [persistence-and-storage](persistence-and-storage.md) for the bucket layout)."," "," > [!NOTE] Why validate by decoding, not by trusting the attributes or the Content-Type"," > Most of the corpus omits `width`/`height` entirely (all four New Yorker contact sheets carry neither), and a Content-Type header can lie, so the only trustworthy size and format come from decoding the file itself. The 200 px floor sits in a gap in the corpus: avatars and tracking pixels cluster around 40 px and every legitimate figure is 305 px or larger. Two square brand logos survive it, accepted rather than chased, because a square-ratio rule has no legitimate square image in the corpus to test against and a false positive costs only one cheap describe call."," ","-An SVG stays an SVG. Its scripts are stripped before it is stored, and it is stored under the content hash of the stripped markup, with the SVG content type on the object. It has no pixel size to measure, so the 200 px floor does not apply to it. It is describable from its markup (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)) and is served like any other image; a client shows it only through an `<img>` element, where its scripts would not run (see [item-contract](item-contract.md)).","+An SVG stays an SVG. Its `<script>` elements and every `on*` event-handler attribute are stripped before it is stored, since the describer reads the stored markup, and it is stored under the content hash of the stripped markup, with the SVG content type on the object. It has no pixel size to measure, so the 200 px floor does not apply to it. It is describable from its markup (see [image-and-code-describer](image-and-code-describer.md)) and is served like any other image; a client shows it only through an `<img>` element, where its scripts would not run (see [item-contract](item-contract.md))."," "," > [!TIP] Rejected: an image library inside the Worker"," > The Images binding rejects every SVG, so rasterising one would take a WebAssembly rasteriser in the Worker: about 1.5 MiB gzipped added to the bundle, and every text element in the SVG disappears unless a font is bundled with it. Decoding a raster above about 10 MP inside the Worker exceeds its 128 MB of memory and ends the invocation where no code can catch it. The binding decodes rasters outside the Worker's memory, and a browser renders an SVG natively, so keeping the SVG needs no rasteriser at all."]}]},{"filePath":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design/tts-service.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":20,"oldLines":7,"newStart":20,"newLines":7,"lines":[" | Endpoint | Takes | Answers |"," |---|---|---|"," | start synthesis | the spoken forms, one string per read-along unit in unit order; the Kokoro voice, defaulting to `af_heart`; the destination URL, a presigned R2 PUT URL for the item's audio key | Modal's call id for this synthesis, at once |","-| fetch result | the call id | `202` while the synthesis runs; `200` with the result below once it is done; `500` when the synthesis raised; `410` once Modal no longer holds the result; `422` for an id Modal never issued or one that is not a call id |","+| fetch result | the call id | `202` while the synthesis runs; `200` with the result below once it is done; `500` with `{error}` when the synthesis raised; `410` with `{error}` once Modal no longer holds the result; `422` with `{error}` for a call id Modal never issued, and `422` with the web framework's validation body for an id that is not a call id |"," "," | Result field | Answers |"," |---|---|"]},{"oldStart":93,"oldLines":7,"newStart":93,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," The execution layer is coupled to Modal as a result: moving off it means replacing both the compute host and the async layer, and the GPU with them."," ","-In local development Modal still runs in the cloud and uploads into the local bucket through a Cloudflare quick tunnel; [local-development](local-development.md) explains how the PUT URL reaches it.","+In local development the item Workflow calls an ephemeral copy of the app that `modal serve` runs in the cloud, and that copy uploads into the local bucket through a Cloudflare quick tunnel; [local-development](local-development.md) explains how the PUT URL reaches it."," "," ## ⏩ What is not built yet"," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design/recipes.md","/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design/article-extraction.md","/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/docs/technical-design/tts-service.md"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"b6ae7193-7065-4e5c-b2e6-11cb9bde2dc3","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":4,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9f187ccd-8c65-4c06-8679-4a01e79c15b4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
