{"parentUuid":"c5197996-f390-4ed6-95a9-521e85ef1427","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"769a7d9a-4c76-4466-ad6f-542f4a0e9fa9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017suTX2jmc7tbxzXFoYPsh1","type":"tool_result","content":"== explore-with-canvas\n3:description: Runs the explore skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The user asks with sticky notes, the skill lays out the answers freely on the canvas with their facts and diagrams, and a decision log grows in one place. Use when the user wants to build their understanding of a problem on a canvas.\n11:`explore` keeps its opening, how each question is answered and its decision rules.\n19:- **Lay the answers out freely on the canvas.** Draw related topics close together, and join them with an arrow when the relation needs saying. Give the answer first, then the facts that support it, then the decisions it raises. Write each decision as a violet card titled `❓ <title>`. Keep verified facts apart from what a source asserts.\n25:Everything you would say in the chat goes on the canvas, as a Claude note: a grey dashed rectangle labelled `Claude`, next to the answer or shape the note refers to. Replace the note's text when the message changes instead of adding another note.\n== shape-with-canvas\n3:description: Runs the shape skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The canvas is the shape tree, one section per branch with its leaves and its prose; questions, findings and readings are written on it, and the user answers with sticky notes. Use when the user wants to settle the open decisions of a problem and solution definition on a canvas.\n11:`shape` and the skills it loads keep their procedure, question rules and tree rules, the Out of scope branch included.\n22:- **Shape draws no representations.** A diagram goes on the canvas only inside a question, and is deleted with the question's card.\n27:The opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with the ✓ leaves the user brought under their areas and no prose yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles. When a branch opens, its statement of what is settled goes on the canvas as its prose. When a branch settles, ask the user to approve it there.\n31:## Questions\n33:Questions go in the open branch, on its right side, under its prose. The branch's Claude note goes under the questions.\n35:- **A question is a violet card**: a rectangle with violet border and fill, titled `❓ Q<n> · <title>`, then the body, which gives the situation and ends with the question. Add a diagram, drawn with tldraw shapes, when the question is about the order things happen in, who sees what, or which part of the definition a situation touches.\n36:- **Follow-ups stay inside the base question's card**, one at a time, until the question branch closes. The card holds, top to bottom: the base question and its diagram, a summary box, one Claude note, and the current follow-up as a violet card of its own.\n37:- **The summary box**, a solid grey rectangle labelled `Summary · Q<n> so far`, holds what the question branch has settled and the findings that bear on it. Rewrite it each round; never add a line per answer.\n38:- **When the question branch closes**, delete the card, update the branch's leaves on the left, and update its prose.\n39:- **Keep two questions open for the user to answer.** A question waiting on a lookup does not count toward the two, so there are three while one waits. Open the next question from the branch's open leaves as soon as one closes.\n43:Everything you would say in the chat goes on the canvas: how you read an answer, lookup results, status, and requests to approve a branch. Write it as a Claude note: a grey dashed rectangle labelled `Claude`, in the branch or the question it is about. Keep one Claude note per place and replace its text each round. When a branch is done, delete its note.\n== design-with-canvas\n3:description: Runs the design skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The canvas is the design tree, one section per branch with its leaves, its representation and its prose; questions, findings and readings are written on it, and the user answers with sticky notes. The recording at the end stays in the chat. Use when the user wants to design a system on a canvas.\n11:`design` and the skills it loads keep their procedure, branch types, question rules and tree rules.\n34:The opening tree goes on the canvas as the branch areas, each one pending, with no leaves yet. Above the areas, a Claude note says in one line per branch what that branch settles. A branch's opening statement goes on the canvas as its leaves and its prose. When a branch settles, draw its representation and prose in its section and ask the user to approve it there.\n36:## Questions\n38:Questions go in the open branch, on its right side, under its write-up. The branch's Claude note goes under the questions.\n40:- **A question is a violet card**: a rectangle with violet border and fill, titled `❓ Q<n> · <title>`, then the body, which gives the situation and ends with the question. Add a diagram, drawn with tldraw shapes, when the question is about where something runs, in what order or across which boundary.\n41:- **Follow-ups stay inside the base question's card**, one at a time, until the question branch closes. The card holds, top to bottom: the base question and its diagram, a summary box, one Claude note, and the current follow-up as a violet card of its own.\n42:- **The summary box**, a solid grey rectangle labelled `Summary · Q<n> so far`, holds what the question branch has settled and the findings that bear on it. Rewrite it each round; never add a line per answer.\n43:- **When the question branch closes**, delete the card, update the branch's leaves on the left, and update its write-up.\n44:- **Keep two questions open for the user to answer.** A question waiting on a spike, a prototype or a lookup does not count toward the two, so there are three while one waits. Open the next question from the frontier as soon as one closes.\n48:Everything you would say in the chat goes on the canvas: how you read an answer, research and spike results, status, and requests to approve a branch. Write it as a Claude note: a grey dashed rectangle labelled `Claude`, in the branch or the question it is about. Keep one Claude note per place and replace its text each round. When a branch is done, delete its note.\n52:When the tree is complete, stop the canvas and continue with `design`'s Recording section in the chat.\n== program-design-with-canvas\n== test-design-with-canvas\n3:description: Runs the test-design skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The candidate roots are drawn on a map of the system for the user to pick, and each branch is drawn as a classification tree with its combination matrix under it; the user corrects with sticky notes or by voice. Use when the user wants to design the test cases and manual validations of a settled change on a canvas.\n31:- **Each classification is a violet box** under the root, joined to it by a line with no arrowheads.\n40:Write what you would say in the chat as one Claude note at the top of the canvas: how to read the drawing, the open questions, and the implementation tasks the trees surfaced. Replace its text each round.\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"85af358c-7a05-4db8-8c87-535501ed0f01","timestamp":"2026-10-11T22:43:08.942Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"== explore-with-canvas\n3:description: Runs the explore skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The user asks with sticky notes, the skill lays out the answers freely on the canvas with their facts and diagrams, and a decision log grows in one place. Use when the user wants to build their understanding of a problem on a canvas.\n11:`explore` keeps its opening, how each question is answered and its decision rules.\n19:- **Lay the answers out fr