{"parentUuid":"4838e650-374d-44f1-be6a-d9ac6a176dcf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwG6WEczQve9qT9LA7Eo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Gmx63zV5VWmwcdrh4DzsGq","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou; sed -n 1,50p skills/test-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md; git -C /home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins status --short | wc -l; grep -n \"consistency\\|Design reconciliation\\|Manual validation\" skills/implement/SKILL.md README.md ../CLAUDE.md 2>/dev/null | head","description":"Read test-design-with-canvas and implement section anchors"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1306,"cache_read_input_tokens":429092,"output_tokens":232,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1306,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":232,"cache_read_input_tokens":429092,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1306,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1306},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Gmx63zV5VWmwcdrh4DzsGq":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou; sed -n 1,50p skills/test-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md; git -C /home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins status --short | wc -l; grep -n \"consistency\\|Design reconciliation\\|Manual validation\" skills/implement/SKILL.md README.md ../CLAUDE.md 2>/dev/null | head","description":"Read test-design-with-canvas and implement section anchors"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"1ea95b45-3ba7-46d7-ad29-be3e81e6ebdf","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfwG6Vnq9qPe8kb56CdPF","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"6dd92762-1a02-467e-83c1-94151ea621e7","timestamp":"2026-10-11T22:45:30.950Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"6dd92762-1a02-467e-83c1-94151ea621e7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a494df3b-a977-4017-be9c-129fddc17163","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gmx63zV5VWmwcdrh4DzsGq","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: test-design-with-canvas\ndescription: Runs the test-design skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The candidate roots are drawn on a map of the system for the user to pick, and each branch is drawn as a classification tree with its combination matrix under it; the user corrects with sticky notes or by voice. Use when the user wants to design the test cases and manual validations of a settled change on a canvas.\nargument-hint: <the settled design to prove>\n---\n\nLoad mahou:test-design first, then mahou:canvas. Then read `.mahou/test-design-with-canvas.md` if present.\n\n# Test design with canvas\n\n`test-design` and the skills it loads keep their procedure: roots, branches, classifications, partitions, matrices, facts, dropped rows, recording and ending.\n\nThe document is `canvas/test-design.tldr.json` in the change's scratch folder. The record stays `test-design/<repo>.md`; write a tree's change into it whenever the canvas changes, and draw the canvas from it.\n\n## Roots on a map of the system\n\nThe candidate roots go on the canvas as a map of the system, under a title naming the change and the order the trees are worked in.\n\n- **Each tree is an `area`**, labelled with its letter, its repository or deployable, and what it is to the rest of the system, such as `B <service>/: what a client calls`. Lay the areas out in the order work flows through them, whoever starts it on the left; put the tree that every other one imports along the bottom.\n- **Each candidate root is a blue card** inside its tree's area. Its first line is an empty checkbox, `☐`, for the user to tick, then the root's id and its entry point; its second line is `e.g. <scenario>`, one or two scenarios through that entry point, an error case among them.\n- **A candidate that is not a root is a grey dashed card** with the reason, such as a piece that runs only inside another entry point, or a migration validated through a read.\n- **Arrows join the cards that hand work to each other**, each labelled with the action one card asks of the other, such as `create` or `start`.\n\nTell the user to tick or cross out cards with sticky notes or by voice.\n\n## Each branch is a classification tree\n\nEach branch is an `area` labelled `<id> <root> · <status>`, inside its tree's area. Branches stack down their tree's area, and the trees sit side by side.\n\n- **The root is a blue box** at the top, with the entry point and what it is given.\n- **Each classification is a violet box** under the root, joined to it by a line with no arrowheads.\n- **Each partition is a green box** under its classification, joined the same way, with a short label.\n- **The matrix goes under the tree.** A header line reads `row · what the test does` on the left and `expected · kind · setup` on the right, both in grey. Each row is one line: its id and summary on the left, a filled dot under the centre of each partition it picks, and its expected outcome, kind and setup on the right. Dotted grey lines run between the rows and down each partition's column.\n- **A row run by hand is orange**, its text and its dots, whether it is `test + manual` or `manual`. Its expected outcome adds what the check observes by hand.\n- **Dropped rows get a grey line under the matrix**, `Dropped: <reason>`.\n- **A branch with one row** draws its root and that row, with no classifications.\n\n## Messages from Claude\n\nWrite what you would say in the chat as one Claude note at the top of the canvas: how to read the drawing, the open questions, and the implementation tasks the trees surfaced. Replace its text each round.\n0\nskills/implement/SKILL.md:40:## Design reconciliation\nskills/implement/SKILL.md:44:## Manual validation\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b16cbb33-af83-4026-a2c1-5e5972820edb","timestamp":"2026-10-11T22:45:31.622Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nname: test-design-with-canvas\ndescription: Runs the test-design skill on a canvas instead of in the chat. The candidate roots are drawn on a map of the system for the user to pick, and each branch is drawn as a classification tree with its combination matrix under it; the user corrects with sticky notes or by voice. Use when the user wants to design the test cases and manual validations of a settled change on a canvas.\nargument-hint: <the settled design to prove>\n---\n\nLoad mahou:test-design first, then mahou:canvas. Then read `.mahou/test-design-with-canvas.md` if present.\n\n# Test design with canvas\n\n`test-design` and the skills it loads keep their procedure: roots, branches, classifications, partitions, matrices, facts, dropped rows, recording and ending.\n\nThe document is `canvas/test-design.tldr.json` in the change's scratch folder. The record stays `test-design/<repo>.md`; write a tree's change into it whenever the canvas changes, and draw the canvas from it.\n\n## Roots on a map of the system\n\nThe candidate roots go on the canvas as a map of the system, under a title naming the change and the order the trees are worked in.\n\n- **Each tree is an `area`**, labelled with its letter, its repository or deployable, and what it is to the rest of the system, such as `B <service>/: what a client calls`. Lay the areas out in the order work flows through them, whoever starts it on the left; put the tree that every other one imports along the bottom.\n- **Each candidate root is a blue card** inside its tree's area. Its first line is an empty checkbox, `☐`, for the user to tick, then the root's id and its entry point; its second line is `e.g. <scenario>`, one or two scenarios through that entry point, an error case among them.\n- **A candidate that is not a root is a grey dashed card** with the reason, such as a piece that runs only inside another entry point, or a migration validated through a read.\n- **Arrows join the cards that hand work to each other**, each labelled with the action one card asks of the other, such as `create` or `start`.\n\nTell the user to tick or cross out cards with sticky notes or by voice.\n\n## Each branch is a classification tree\n\nEach branch is an `area` labelled `<id> <root> · <status>`, inside its tree's area. Branches stack down their tree's area, and the trees sit side by side.\n\n- **The root is a blue box** at the top, with the entry point and what it is given.\n- **Each classification is a violet box** under the root, joined to it by a line with no arrowheads.\n- **Each partition is a green box** under its classification, joined the same way, with a short label.\n- **The matrix goes under the tree.** A header line reads `row · what the test does` on the left and `expected · kind · setup` on the right, both in grey. Each row is one line: its id and summary on the left, a filled dot under the centre of each partition it picks, and its expected outcome, kind and setup on the right. Dotted grey lines run between the rows and down each partition's column.\n- **A row run by hand is orange**, its text and its dots, whether it is `test + manual` or `manual`. Its expected outcome adds what the check observes by hand.\n- **Dropped rows get a grey line under the matrix**, `Dropped: <reason>`.\n- **A branch with one row** draws its root and that row, with no classifications.\n\n## Messages from Claude\n\nWrite what you would say in the chat as one Claude note at the top of the canvas: how to read the drawing, the open questions, and the implementation tasks the trees surfaced. Replace its text each round.\n0\nskills/implement/SKILL.md:40:## Design reconciliation\nskills/implement/SKILL.md:44:## Manual validation","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutput