e-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"5a3f53ad-4a2d-4f16-a464-49db545281e2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b6e0d8ff-2a57-44cc-926d-07c3009f144b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012usom1pY4BaKsV4gBGuv3a","type":"tool_result","content":"294:- **Process lives in skills, knowledge lives in the wiki, the project's own documentation lives in the project.** The wiki (`skills/wiki/references/`, one `INDEX.md` per folder listing only its own level, entries carrying a `verified` date) is the standard the `conventions-reviewer` measures a change against; it is what dev-standards was in the Filadd plugin. The project's `docs/` folder is what the Notion docs database was: a `README.md` charter saying what each folder holds and how a note is written, one `README.md` per folder as its index, `_templates/` optional. `docs` (loaded, never typed, by `write-documentation`, `explore`, `design`, `init` and the `docs-scout`/`documentation-reviewer` agents) carries the resolution order (`.mahou/basics.md` → a `CLAUDE.md` section → `docs/README.md`), the parts a skill may assume exist (charter, per-folder index, templates, notes vs records), how the seeded note template is meant to be used (an overview first, then a **menu** of optional sections that is neither required nor closed, ordered for the reader rather than as listed, emoji on every heading, reasoning and edge cases in callouts or inline prose and never in a section) and the rule that no skill assumes the structure or carries a template body; the charter wins over anything a skill sketches. `basics` only points at it. A vault seeded by `zenku:init` (nagara's, for instance) already has this shape and is read as is; the mahou side never names zenku, since the structure is the project's whatever seeded it.\n299:*Process, in the order work moves:* `explore` (user-steered investigation, one question at a time, facts fetched through `lookup` so the reading stays out of context, a question about a system's parts goes to `architecture-exploration` and one only running code can answer to `spike`, writes nothing) → `shape` (settle behavior-level decisions as a design tree; opens by placing the decisions the user brings as ✓ leaves under pending areas, then works one area at a time) → `design` (system design one area at a time, type references under `references/types/` for data model, schema (a structured value a schema validates rather than a table: JSON column contents, cross-system payloads, tool arguments), data flow, rules, lifecycle, scheduled process (added 2026-10-10: a job on a schedule or a wait that ends with the system acting), component architecture, contract and UI, hands the tree to `write-documentation`; the branch list is shown as the tree itself, the first branch opens with its approval and each later one with the approval of the previous branch's rendering; a branch opens by stating what is already settled as ✓ leaves and waiting for corrections before the frontier is asked; the reference's representation is rendered directly, asked only when no reference matches; sub-branches describing one thing may render as one representation once settled, and a behaviour another note or branch already settles is referenced in one sentence rather than redrawn) → `program-design` (renamed from `program-architecture` 2026-10: turns a settled design, or a change proposed in the conversation and confirmed as the source, into a program design, one repository at a time, a branch per package, screen or entry point; since 2026-10-10 a host app and the library a surface is built from may be one root, with the module list marking each file's repository and the record named after the surface; the one skill that proposes rather than asks, named as the exception in `basics`' rule against recommending, because the design already made the decisions and the proposals only work out the details; every part is a representation grounded in the code and the conventions and never code: package layout, screen layout, component tree, derivation ledger, callstack, control flow, interfaces as JSON Schema, class diagram, module list, plus a standards list closing each root; each reference under `references/representations/` has What it covers, What to check before proposing, Representation and When it fits; the record is `program-design/<repo>.md` in the scratch folder, and departures from the design notes go through `write-documentation` at the end) → `spike` (answer a design question that reading and asking cannot settle, by building the smallest working version of the real thing and running it against reality; throwaway code that never merges, one question per spike, rounds under a strategy from `references/strategies/`, code and findings in the change's scratch folder at `spikes/<question-slug>/`) → `test-design` (Classification Tree Method on the settled design, before any code: one tree per repository, entry points or flows as roots, classifications, partitions, then a combination matrix per branch with rows marked `test`, `manual` or both; candidates derive only from settled leaves and facts read from the code, the user settles every level; test level and naming come from the wiki's testing entries and the repository's own conventions, whether a surface has a runner is a fact read from it; settled trees and matrices written to the scratch folder at `test-design/<repo>.md` so implement reads them across sessions; ported from `filadd-experimental-dev` 2026-09, the Filadd stack ordering replaced by \"from the repository others depend on outward\") → `implement` (one subagent per repository with a full brief: design notes and summary, tasks, its matrix rows verbatim (read from the scratch folder at `test-design/<repo>.md` when test-design wrote them there), repository rules, the `report` shape; relays results and flags as decisions, then a subagent runs `agentic-review` per repository as it lands and `validate` one repository at a time; task tracker generalized, the tracker, its tool and its states come from `.mahou/implement.md` or the user, and the brief names them concretely; ported 2026-09 with the Notion Board states and the `local-services` load removed) or `implement-with-tdd` (added 2026-10: `implement` as written, except each repository gets a tester and an implementer subagent that never talk directly; the tester writes failing tests for the `test` rows and checks the reason each fails, the implementer makes them pass without touching test files, the tester validates against the design's rules and contracts, and the session relays every exchange and commits through `commit-changes` once the tester approves) or code written by hand or another tool → `validate` (the manual rows from `test-design`, or user-named scenarios, driven through a browser via `agent-browser`, over HTTP via `curl`, or on an Android device via `adb` and the `scripts/android/` helpers, references under `references/`; how the system runs comes from `.mahou/validate.md` or the project's own setup; capture-mutate-revert discipline for local data, an HTML report artifact in the change's scratch folder at `reports/` built as rows run, **fixes nothing**, every failure a decision; renamed from filadd-dev's `validate-feature` 2026-09 with the rubric dropped because `test-design` already covers it, the three Filadd surface references collapsed to two generic ones (plus `android.md`, generalized from filadd-dev's mobile reference 2026-09), and the \"fix and re-run until clean\" rule inverted) → `code-blast-radius` (one `usage-tracer` per changed element, reports only where behavior changes for someone) → `agentic-review` (typed or invoked by `implement`, `disable-model-invocation` dropped 2026-09 for that; four facet reviewers per repo plus one `conventions-reviewer` per wiki entry or project rule file, the list passed by the caller or picked from the wiki indexes, fixes within the change's scope, up to three rounds; **the one skill that edits without asking**, bounded to changeset files, uncommitted, every fix and skip reported) → `human-review` (typed only; `plannotator` annotations verified and settled before any edit) → `address-pr-review` (the review on an open PR: one GraphQL query for reviews and threads, each finding verified with a verdict and its premise checked, settled with the user, applied, then committed through `commit-changes`, pushed and the ruled threads resolved; never posts to the PR; typed or run by `land` on a review; ported from `filadd-experimental-dev` 2026-09 with `dev-standards` replaced by the wiki and the project's rule files) → `write-documentation` (overview, then the template's sections the subject needs, `superpowers:unslop` pass, a `documentation-reviewer` pass against the charter, shown before written; a stripping rule set — name roles not products, link dependencies, no library names, no current-implementation description, no ownership, no prototype controls, no comparison with today — keeps session artifacts out of the shared note) → `commit-changes` (model-invocable since 2026-10 so `address-pr-review` and `implement-with-tdd` can invoke it; conventional commits, grouping confirmed first, never pushes, no co-author trailers rule) → `release-check` (what breaks when the change's repositories are released in a given order, and the order that breaks nothing; boundaries come from the diff plus the user since mahou has no dependency map to read, one `compat-checker` per boundary and `code-blast-radius` alongside, findings verified against their cited lines before reporting; ported 2026-09 with project-knowledge's dependency script, `prepare-repo` and the filadd org replaced by diff-derived boundaries, inline repo prep and the remote's owner) → `land` (from branches to merged pull requests in the safe order: runs `release-check`, opens every PR through `create-pull-request`, babysits them in parallel with one poll loop each — `address-pr-review` on a review, `rebase` when behind, stop on a failed check, record on merge — then walks the confirmed order, watching each merge's CI run and stopping for the user's production confirmation before the next, the release following each project's own process; never merges a PR itself; proposes skipping `release-check` for a one-repository change that crosses no service boundary, and ends by switching each checkout to its base, pulling and deleting the merged branch with `branch -d`, never forced; ported 2026-09 with `release-repo` dropped because generic projects release from their own CI).\n305:**Agents** (all write findings and edit nothing, `model: sonnet`): the four generic facet reviewers plus `conventions-reviewer` (one per source: a wiki entry or a project rule file, cites it, flags stale `verified` dates on entries; split per source 2026-09 after the Filadd standards reviewer went one per guide); `usage-tracer`; `code-scout` and `docs-scout` (one question, one repo or the docs folder, report under forty lines); `compat-checker` (one service boundary, new side against deployed other side in both directions, migrations against instances on the old code, a client still running an old build; dispatched by `release-check`, ported near-verbatim 2026-09); `documentation-reviewer` (a draft against the charter, folder README and template, judged the way `docs` says the template is used; a menu section left out is never a finding).\n mahou/README.md                                  |  5 ++++-\n mahou/skills/implement/SKILL.md                  | 20 +++++++++++++-------\n mahou/skills/program-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md | 13 +++++++++++++\n mahou/skills/test-design-with-canvas/SKILL.md    | 11 ++++++++++-\n mahou/skills/validate/SKILL.md                   |  6 ++++++\n 5 files changed, 46 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)\nAs each implementation subagent reports, dispatch the agentic review for that repository without waiting for the others: a subagent invokes mahou:agentic-review on that repository's changeset, the bra\n\n## Manual validation\n\nThe manual rows run one repository at a time against the shared local system, from the repository the others depend on outward, so two subagents never mutate the same system at once. For each reposito\n\n## Design reconciliation\n\nThe code that lands is what was actually built, and the notes drift from it the moment a child deviates, simplifies, or meets a platform fact. When the manual validation has reported, collect the drif\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e3b94bff-6e48-48ea-a40a-244a7fc236f2","timestamp":"2026-10-11T22:52:46.408Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"294:- **Process lives in skills, knowledge lives in the wiki, the project's own documentation lives in the project.** The wiki (`skills/wiki/references/`, one `INDEX.md` per folder listing only its own level, entries carrying a `verified` date) is the standard the `conventions-reviewer` measures a change against; it is what dev-standards was in the Filadd plugin. The project's `docs/` folder is what the Notion docs database was: a `README.md` charter saying what each folder holds and how a note is written, one `README.md` per folder as its index, `_templates/` optional. `docs` (loaded, never typed, by `write-documentation`, `explore`, `design`, `init` and the `docs-scout`/`documentation-reviewer` agents) carries the resolution order (`.mahou/basics.md` → a `CLAUDE.md` section → `docs/README.md`), the parts a skill may assume exist (charter, per-folder index, templates, notes vs records), how the seeded note template is meant to be used (an overview first, then a **menu** of optional sections that is neither required nor closed, ordered for the reader rather than as listed, emoji on every heading, reasoning and edge cases in callouts or inline prose and never in a section) and the rule that no skill assumes the structure or carries a template body; the charter wins over anything a skill sketches. `basics` only points at it. A vault seeded by `zenku:init` (nagara's, for instance) already has this shape and is read as is; the mahou side never names zenku, since the structure is the project's whatever seeded it.\n299:*Process, in the order work moves:* `explore` (user-steered investigation, one question at a time, facts fetched through `lookup` so the reading stays out of context, a question about a system's parts goes to `architecture-exploration` and one only running code can answer to `spike`, writes nothing) → `shape` (settle behavior-level decisions as a design tree; opens by placing the decisions the user brings as ✓ leaves under pending areas, then works one area at a time) → `design` (system design one area at a time, type references under `references/types/` for data model, schema (a structured value a schema validates rather than a table: JSON column contents, cross-system payloads, tool arguments), data flow, rules, lifecycle, scheduled process (added 2026-10-10: a job on a schedule or a wait that ends with the system acting), component architecture, contract and UI, hands the tree to `write-documentation`; the branch list is shown as the tree itself, the first branch opens with its approval and each later one with the approval of the previous branch's rendering; a branch opens by stating what is already settled as ✓ leaves and waiting for corrections before the frontier is asked; the reference's representation is rendered directly, asked only when no reference matches; sub-branches describing one thing may render as one representation once settled, and a behaviour another note or branch already settles is referenced in one sentence rather than redrawn) → `program-design` (renamed from `program-architecture` 2026-10: turns a settled design, or a change proposed in the conversation and confirmed as the source, into a program design, one repository at a time, a branch per package, screen or entry point; since 2026-10-10 a host app and the library a surface is built from may be one root, with the module list marking each file's repository and the record named after the surface; the one skill that proposes rather than asks, named as the exception in `basics`' rule against recommending, because the design already made the decisions and the proposals only work out the details; every part is a representation grounded in the code and the conventions and never code: package layout, screen layout, component tree, derivation ledger, callstack, control flow, interfaces as JSON Schema, class diagram, module list, plus a standards list closing each root; each reference under `references/representations/` has What it covers, What to check before proposing, Representation and When it fits; the record is `program-design/<repo>.md` in the scratch folder, and departures from the design notes go through `write-documentation` at the end) → `spike` (answer a design question that reading and asking cannot settle, by building the smallest working version of the real thing and running it against reality; throwaway code that never merges, one question per spike, rounds under a strategy from `references/strategies/`, code and findings in the change's scratch folder at `spikes/<question-slug>/`) → `test-design` (Classification Tree Method on the settled design, before any code: one tree per repository, entry points or flows as roots, classifications, partitions, then a combination matrix per branch with rows marked `test`, `manual` or both; candidates derive only from settled leaves and facts read from the code, the user settles every level; test level and naming come from the wiki's testing entries and the repository's own conventions, whether a surface has a runner is a fact read from it; settled trees and matrices written to the scratch folder at `test-design/<repo>.md` so implement reads them across sessions; ported from `filadd-experimental-dev` 2026-09, the Filadd stack ordering replaced by \"from the repository others depend on outward\") → `implement` (one subagent per repository with a full brief: design notes and summary, tasks, its matrix rows verbatim (read from the scratch folder at `test-design/<repo>.md` when test-design wrote them there), repository rules, the `report` shape; relays results and flags as decisions, then a subagent runs `agentic-review` per repository as it lands and `validate` one repository at a time; task tracker generalized, the tracker, its tool and its states come from `.mahou/implement.md` or the user, and the brief names them concretely; ported 2026-09 with the Notion Board states and the `local-services` load removed) or `implement-with-tdd` (added 2026-10: `implement` as written, except each repository gets a tester and an implementer subagent that never talk directly; the tester writes failing tests for the `test` rows and checks the reason each fails, the implementer makes them pass without touching test files, the tester validates against the design's rules and contracts, and the session relays every exchange and commits through `commit-changes` once the tester approves) or code written by hand or another tool → `validate` (the manual rows from `test-design`, or user-named scenarios, driven through a browser via `agent-browser`, over HTTP via `curl`, or on an Android device via `adb` and the `scripts/android/` helpers, references under `references/`; how the system runs comes from `.mahou/validate.md` or the project's own setup; capture-mutate-revert discipline for local data, an HTML report artifact in the change's scratch folder at `reports/` built as rows run, **fixes nothing**, every failure a decision; renamed from filadd-dev's `validate-feature` 2026-09 with the rubric dropped because `test-design` already covers it, the three Filadd surface references collapsed to two generic ones (plus `android.md`, generalized from filadd-dev's mobile reference 2026-09), and the \"fix and re-run until clean\" rule inverted) → `code-blast-radius` (one `usage-tracer` per changed element, reports only where behavior changes for someone) → `agentic-review` (typed or invoked by `implement`, `disable-model-invocation` dropped 2026-09 for that; four facet reviewers per repo plus one `conventions-reviewer` per wiki entry or project rule file, the list passed by the caller or picked from the wiki indexes, fixes within the change's scope, up to three rounds; **the one skill that edits without asking**, bounded to changeset files, uncommitted, every fix and skip reported) → `human-review` (typed only; `plannotator` annotations verified and settled before any edit) → `address-pr-review` (the review on an open PR: one GraphQL query for reviews and threads, each finding verified with a verdict and its premise checked, settled with the user, applied, then committed through `commit-changes`, pushed and the ruled threads resolved; never posts to the PR; typed or run by `land` on a review; ported from `filadd-experimental-dev` 2026-09 with `dev-standards` replaced by the wiki and the project's rule files) → `write-documentation` (overview, then the template's sections the subject needs, `superpowers:unslop` pass, a `documentation-reviewer` pass against the charter, shown before written; a stripping rule set — name roles not products, link dependencies, no library names, no current-implementation description, no ownership, no prototype controls, no comparison with today — keeps session artifacts out of the shared note) → `commit-changes` (model-invocable since 2026-10 so `address-pr-review` and `implement-with-tdd` can invoke it; conventional commits, grouping confirmed first, never pushes, no co-author trailers rule) → `release-check` (what breaks when the change's repositories are released in a given order, and the order that breaks nothing; boundaries come from the diff plus the user since mahou has no dependency map to read, one `compat-checker` per boundary and `code-blast-radius` alongside, findings verified against their cited lines before reporting; ported 2026-09 with project-knowledge's dependency script, `prepare-repo` and the filadd org replaced by diff-derived boundaries, inline repo prep and the remote's owner) → `land` (from branches to merged pull requests in the safe order: runs `release-check`, opens every PR through `create-pull-request`, babysits them in parallel with one poll loop each — `address-pr-review` on a review, `rebase` when behind, stop on a failed check, record on merge — then walks the confirmed order, watching each merge's CI run and stopping for the user's production confirmation before the next, the release following each project's own process; never merges a PR itself; proposes skipping `release-check` for a one-repository change that crosses no service boundary, and ends by switching each checkout to its base, pulling and deleting the merged branch with `branch -d`, never forced; ported 2026-09 with `release-repo` dropped because generic projects release from their own CI).\n305:**Agents** (all write findings and edit nothing, `model: sonnet`): the four generic facet reviewers plus `conventions-reviewer` (one per source: a wiki entry or a project rule file, cites it, flags stale `verified` dates on entries; split per source 2026-09 after the Filadd standards reviewer went one per guide); `usage-tracer`; `code-scout` and `docs-scout` (one question, one repo or the docs folder, report under forty lines); `compat-checker` (one service boundary, new side against deployed other side in both directions, migrations against instances on the old code, a client still running an old build; dispatched by `release-check`, ported near-verbatim 2026-09); `documentation-reviewer` (a draft against the charter, folder README and template, judged the way `docs` says the template is used; a menu section left out is never a finding).\n mahou/README.md                                  |  5 ++++-\n mahou/skills/implement/SKILL.md                  | 20 +++++++++++++-------\n mahou/skills/program-design-with-canv