{"parentUuid":"705e0efe-37ad-48b7-be8c-67d746dc57d2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwHXfqsoB2VnaaqCc1cJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1126,"cache_read_input_tokens":476556,"output_tokens":2236,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":425},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1126,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2236,"cache_read_input_tokens":476556,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1126,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1126},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":4763,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"cf96228e-2dd3-4986-a542-423d5b612fb9","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfwHXf9xkiHr8CWhjPZEE","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"2138f9f4-1292-41a0-ad3d-de747f7dec51","timestamp":"2026-10-11T23:04:22.479Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/briefs","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"2138f9f4-1292-41a0-ad3d-de747f7dec51","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwHXfqsoB2VnaaqCc1cJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B2VuTxoape96uabZh8YvBt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/cloudflare-migration/briefs; cat > ase84-orm-followups.md <<'EOF'\n# ASE-84 follow-ups: the ORM shape the user settled\n\nMain checkout, `feat/cloudflare-migration` (HEAD 71b23b0). Same rules as your ASE-84 brief. Your final message holds the full report.\n\nThe user's intent: the Workers never use the drizzle client for anything but passing it to `common/src/db` functions; every read and write, single or atomic, is an ORM function there. The functions are reusable and generic, not shaped around batching.\n\n## Code\n\n1. **Creates and updates run and return the row.** `createItem`, `createRecipeVersion`, `createCostEntry` and `updateItem` execute and return the created or updated row through `.returning()`. `createItem` returns the item, not `{ id, query }`. No function returns an unexecuted query.\n2. **Atomic writes are their own functions, holding the `db.batch` inside.** The three places that batch today become functions in `common/src/db/` named for what they record, each taking the client and the values and running one batch: the fetch step's instance id and domain with its Firecrawl cost entry; the describe step's units and degradations with one cost entry per description; the store step's ready write with its TTS cost entry. They live in a folder for writes that span tables (for example `common/src/db/operations/`, one function per module); name them for what they do and say the names in your report. The steps call them and no longer call `db.batch`.\n3. **Claims return the claimed row.** `claimRetry` and `claimRequeue` return the updated row via `.returning()`, or null when the call lost. The retry route then reads at most twice: read, claim; on a win it answers 202 with the claimed row; on a loss it reads again for the 409 message. The `?? read` fallback goes.\n4. **Ids and timestamps are schema defaults.** `id` (per table, with its prefix), `created_at` and `queued_at` get `$defaultFn`; `updated_at` gets `$defaultFn` and `$onUpdate`. The create and update functions stop setting them. `mint-id.ts` stays only if the defaults use it. Re-run `pnpm db:check` (runtime defaults emit no SQL, so the migration should not change; if it does, regenerate and say why).\n5. **CHECK helpers.** `valuesOf`, `oneOf` and `jsonOrNull` stay in `schema.ts`, with a short comment that they are kept there because only the schema's CHECKs use them.\n6. The three seeding helpers stay separate.\n\nTests: adjust the `common/` tests to the new return shapes, add a test per atomic function (both writes land; a failing second write rolls back the first, if D1 under the vitest plugin lets you provoke one, otherwise say so), and keep the API and pipeline suites' assertions unchanged. Run test, lint, types in `common/`, `api/`, `pipeline/`, the pipeline build and `pnpm db:check`. Commit as `refactor(common): …` / `refactor(api): …` / `refactor(pipeline): …`.\n\n## Docs\n\nThen run `/mahou:write-documentation` once for this change, with the user's approval to write directly after the unslop and documentation-reviewer passes: the CLAUDE.md code-style database bullet (per-table functions plus functions for atomic writes across tables, all executing and returning rows; the Workers only pass the client), `common-package.md`'s \"Reading and writing D1\" section and its functions table (no unexecuted queries; the atomic functions and which step calls each), and anywhere else that says writes return unawaited queries or that the steps call `db.batch` (`persistence-and-storage.md`, `item-lifecycle.md`, the local wiki entry `.mahou/wiki/cloudflare/drizzle-on-d1.md`). Commit as `docs: …` (and `chore: …` for `.mahou/`).\nEOF\nH=/home/ag