00-0000\\|placeholder\" common/wrangler.jsonc api/wrangler.jsonc pipeline/wrangler.jsonc docs .github 2>/dev/null | head","description":"Fill in D1 id in wrangler configs"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"10c28fec-f2d1-4682-af8d-6e44284d84c1","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfwcP65rx84YbsPt4d12v","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"239e1c1a-82a9-4d7a-af9a-f33481c789a7","timestamp":"2026-10-12T03:11:31.958Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"feat/cloudflare-migration","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"239e1c1a-82a9-4d7a-af9a-f33481c789a7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e0a05216-e8a7-4e4f-b612-b047b5b1686e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RVfGwB2ABoTRp5XuZXwpqT","type":"tool_result","content":" api/wrangler.jsonc      | 3 +--\n common/wrangler.jsonc   | 3 +--\n pipeline/wrangler.jsonc | 3 +--\n 3 files changed, 3 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)\ndiff --git a/common/wrangler.jsonc b/common/wrangler.jsonc\nindex c1f4ba2..426c310 100644\n--- a/common/wrangler.jsonc\n+++ b/common/wrangler.jsonc\n@@ -2,12 +2,11 @@\n   \"$schema\": \"node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json\",\n   \"name\": \"nagara-common\",\n   \"compatibility_date\": \"2026-09-01\",\n-  // The all-zero database_id is a placeholder until the production D1 database exists.\n   \"d1_databases\": [\n     {\n       \"binding\": \"DB\",\n       \"database_name\": \"nagara\",\n-      \"database_id\": \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\",\n+      \"database_id\": \"bdc5b6b3-724f-47df-8733-640fd263cd2f\",\n       \"migrations_dir\": \"migrations\"\n     }\n   ]\ndocs/README.md:15:Which folder a note belongs in is a question about its subject, not about who wrote it. Code reading as a short placeholder rather than being read aloud is product design: it is a statement about what a listener experiences. The spoken-form derivation that produces that placeholder is technical design. The 100 ms inter-paragraph pause is product design, a number about how listening feels; folding it into the preceding timing window so there is no dead highlight zone is technical design. A note that needs both goes wherever it is mostly about and links across.\ndocs/technical-design/image-and-code-describer.md:67:A **code** unit's spoken form becomes `Code: <sentence>`, overwriting the interim `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder from the spoken-form derivation.\ndocs/product-design/README.md:25:- **The read-along player's visual design.** [The listening experience](listening-experience.md) describes the player's shape (two reading modes, scroll-decoupled navigation), settled against a deliberately placeholder aesthetic. The design language (typography, colour, motion, brand feel) is unbuilt; it is the Read-along visual design milestone in Linear.\ndocs/product-design/what-gets-read-aloud.md:27:- **An XML-like tagged word** an author wrote about (`<software>`, `<your-api-key>`, `<T>`) reads as the words inside it, brackets dropped, so the listener hears \"software\" and \"your API key\". A reader still sees the brackets on the page, because they are the author's own notation for a placeholder or a generic.\ndocs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md:17:> [!WARNING] The D1 database id is a placeholder until the production database exists\ndocs/technical-design/deployment-and-ci.md:18:> `pipeline/wrangler.jsonc`, `api/wrangler.jsonc` and `common/wrangler.jsonc` carry the all-zero `database_id` `00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000`, which local development and the tests use as is. No D1 database has that id, so the migrate job and both deploys fail against the real account until the production database exists and its id replaces the placeholder in all three files.\ndocs/technical-design/article-extraction.md:59:The spoken form is derived from the unit's display markdown by walking its CommonMark token tree, so the display and spoken forms come from one extraction (invariant 1). A fenced code block becomes the fixed placeholder `\"Code sample.\"`. A table is spoken as its body rows only, each row as its own line of header-aware prose (`\"Feature: Extraction, Status: done.\"`) and never as pipe characters. The header row gets no line of its own. A table with no body rows is read as one line of its header cells. A row gets a full stop only when it does not already end in `.`, `!` or `?`. A cell goes through the same inline walk as a paragraph, so a code span keeps its text, and then through the sanitize tail, described below. A heading and a quote are read as their words, without their markers. Every block inside a unit becomes its own line, so a unit carrying several paragraphs or list items never runs the last word of one into the first word of the next. A list item gets no full stop. An ordered item keeps its `1.` at the start of its line, and the spoken form does not announce nesting depth. A code block or an image nested inside a list unit is silent in the spoken form. It stays inside the list's own display and is never described or acquired on its own (see [markdown-units](markdown-units.md)). A list item that holds only a code block, an image or a nested list gets no line. The derivation strips a task-list checkbox and reads struck-through text as its words, with no sign of the strike. Emphasis markers and link destinations are dropped throughout.\ndocs/technical-design/article-extraction.md:68:The `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder is the **interim** spoken form of a code block. During the describe step the describer overwrites a code unit's spoken form with `Code: <one sentence>` and a describable image's with `Image: <one sentence>`; a code block keeps the floor `\"Code with no description.\"` only when the describer fails or the per-item budget is spent.\ndocs/technical-design/article-extraction.md:84:What the rule catches is a section-break ornament (`❦`, `* * *`, `§`) or bare punctuation that a recipe emitted as its own paragraph. A code unit always passes, because its spoken form is the `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8d154c26-33a6-42eb-aca6-575436aee182","timestamp":"2026-10-12T03:11:32.474Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":" api/wrangler.jsonc      | 3 +--\n common/wrangler.jsonc   | 3 +--\n pipeline/wrangler.jsonc | 3 +--\n 3 files changed, 3 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)\ndiff --git a/common/wrangler.jsonc b/common/wrangler.jsonc\nindex c1f4ba2..426c310 100644\n--- a/common/wrangler.jsonc\n+++ b/common/wrangler.jsonc\n@@ -2,12 +2,11 @@\n   \"$schema\": \"node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json\",\n   \"name\": \"nagara-common\",\n   \"compatibility_date\": \"2026-09-01\",\n-  // The all-zero database_id is a placeholder until the production D1 database exists.\n   \"d1_databases\": [\n     {\n       \"binding\": \"DB\",\n       \"database_name\": \"nagara\",\n-      \"database_id\": \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\",\n+      \"database_id\": \"bdc5b6b3-724f-47df-8733-640fd263cd2f\",\n       \"migrations_dir\": \"migrations\"\n     }\n   ]\ndocs/README.md:15:Which folder a note belongs in is a question about its subject, not about who wrote it. Code reading as a short placeholder rather than being read aloud is product design: it is a statement about what a listener experiences. The spoken-form derivation that produces that placeholder is technical design. The 100 ms inter-paragraph pause is product design, a number about how listening feels; folding it into the preceding timing window so there is no dead highlight zone is technical design. A note that needs both goes wherever it is mostly about and links across.\ndocs/technical-design/image-and-code-describer.md:67:A **code** unit's spoken form becomes `Code: <sentence>`, overwriting the interim `\"Code sample.\"` placeholder from the spoken-form derivation.\ndocs/product-design/README.md:25:- **The read-along player's visual design.** [The listening experience](listening-experience.md) describes the player's shape (two reading modes, scroll-decoupled navigation), settled against a deliberately placeholder aesthetic. The design language (typography, colour, motion, brand feel) is unbuilt; it is the Read-along visual design milestone in Linear.\ndocs/product-design/what-gets-read-aloud.md:27:- **An XML-like tagged word** an author wrote about (`<software>`, `<your-api-key>`, `<T>`) reads as the words inside it, brackets dropped, so the listener hears \"software\" and \"your API key\". A reader still sees the brackets on the page, because they are the author's own notation for