{"parentUuid":"88d10d96-fce1-48ba-8bed-a674b390b36c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwfRNbwxQQyNZL5bZqYv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`nagara.asermax.com` now serves the new Cloudflare API.\n\nThe GitHub repo already has all four CI secrets: `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN`, `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`, `MODAL_TOKEN_ID` and `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`. The Cloudflare token dates from 2026-09-22, from the extraction-service CI. The new workflow also applies D1 migrations and deploys the API onto the custom domain, and I can't read what that token is allowed to do. If the first CI run fails on permissions, the token needs D1 Edit, Workers Scripts Edit, and Workers Routes Edit on the `asermax.com` zone.\n\nWhat's left of the cutover needs the dashboards, which I can't reach:\n1. **Cloudflare Access:** under **Zero Trust → Access → Applications**, delete the app that guarded the old `nagara` pipeline. Then delete its service token under **Access → Service credentials → Service Tokens**.\n2. **Railway:** delete the API service, Postgres and the bucket. Railway has been serving no traffic since the DNS switch, and Modal's new TTS app already broke its synthesis.\n3. **Merge:** once the branch is pushed and merged to `main`, `cloudflare.yml` and `tts.yml` run for the first time on CI. Should I push `feat/cloudflare-migration` and open the PR? The branch has 3 local commits on top of the remote: the D1 id, the provider-key change and its docs."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":955,"cache_read_input_tokens":176035,"output_tokens":609,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":86},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":955,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_token