stedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionSkill":"mahou:learn","attributionPlugin":"mahou","type":"assistant","uuid":"074871f5-8aef-4d50-af87-acb782b20431","timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:05:54.311Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"074871f5-8aef-4d50-af87-acb782b20431","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e4762885-ae3e-48b4-ba27-b94d78ca74a0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015jLyDnFJhNo9YzxcDMyUcY","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# AI Gateway: calling a provider through it from a Worker\n5:Answers what changes when a Worker calls a model provider through Cloudflare AI Gateway instead of directly: authentication, caching, what the caller learns about cost, and what can be managed from the CLI. Distilled from a spike that called Gemini through a gateway from a deployed Worker, and from the AI Gateway docs.\n9:- A provider is reached at `https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account>/<gateway>/<provider>/...` (for Gemini, the `google-ai-studio` provider, then the provider's own path such as `v1/models/<model>:generateContent`). The provider's SDK works with its base URL pointed there, and so does plain `fetch`; both sent byte-identical bodies, and the gateway did not change the body.\n10:- A gateway with authentication on requires a `cf-aig-authorization` header on every request.\n11:- With response caching on, an identical request returns the stored response without calling the provider (0.1 s against 1.6–2.7 s for a miss). The `cf-aig-cache-status` response header says `HIT` or `MISS`.\n12:- The docs describe no cost or token figures returned to the caller, in headers or body. Cost is an estimate the gateway computes from token data for its own observability. `cf-aig-custom-cost` is a request header that overrides the price used; it returns nothing.\n13:- wrangler has no gateway command, and the OAuth token wrangler uses cannot create gateways (`10000 Authentication error`); create gateways in the dashboard or with an API token that has AI Gateway Edit.\n20:- Google AI Studio provider: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/usage/providers/google-ai-studio/\nr2.md:11:- Presigned URLs support GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE, expire from 1 second to 7 days, and work only on the `*.r2.cloudflarestorage.com` host, not on a custom domain. A presigned PUT needs no other authentication header, so a plain HTTP client can upload with the presigned URL alone; when `ContentType` is signed, the upload must send the same value or R2 answers `403 SignatureDoesNotMatch`. A single-part upload goes up to about 5 GiB.\nr2.md:17:- A custom domain gives caching and access control through Access or WAF token authentication. `r2.dev` is rate-limited and meant for development only. From a Worker, an R2 binding is also available. Local development: see `local-dev`.\nlocal-dev.md:13:- D1, R2 and Durable Object state are shared only when every process uses the same state folder: `--persist-to <dir>` for wrangler, `persistState: { path }` in the Vite plugin. Otherwise each config directory gets its own `.wrangler/state` and the other Worker sees empty tables (`no such table`). Migrations apply locally with `wrangler d1 migrations apply <db> --local --persist-to <dir>`; `database_id` can be a placeholder.\nlocal-dev.md:17:- Local R2 over the S3 API (experimental, undocumented): `local_dev.experimental_s3_credentials` (`accessKeyId`, `secretAccessKey`) on an `r2_buckets` entry serves the local bucket at `/cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/<bucket-id>` on the dev server, checking SigV4 signatures in headers and in presigned query strings. Added in workers-sdk PR #14280, merged 2026-07-27. A process with the same state folder and no credentials reads the objects through its binding.\nd1.md:1:# D1: SQLite on Cloudflare, migrations and drivers\nd1.md:7:## Knowledge\nd1.md:17:## Sources\nai-gateway.md:1:# AI Gateway: calling a provider through it from a Worker\nai-gateway.md:7:## Knowledge\nai-gateway.md:16:## Sources\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"36092b76-1631-434f-8bbb-36382d0ca2d1","timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:05:54.734Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1:# AI Gateway: calling a provider through it from a Worker\n5:Answers what changes when a Worker calls a model provider through Cloudflare AI Gateway instead of directly: authentication, caching, what the caller learns about cost, and what can be managed from the CLI. Distilled from a spike that called Gemini through a gateway from a deployed Worker, and from the AI Gateway docs.\n9:- A provider is reached at `https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account>/<gateway>/<provider>/...` (for Gemini, the `google-ai-studio` provider, then the provider's own path such as `v1/models/<model>:generateContent`). The provider's SDK works with its base URL pointed there, and so does plain `fetch`; both sent byte-identical bodies, and the gateway did not change the body.\n10:- A gateway with authentication on requires a `cf-aig-authorization` header on every request.\n11:- With response caching on, an identical request returns the stored response without calling the provider (0.1 s against 1.6–2.7 s for a miss). The `cf-aig-cache-status` response header says `HIT` or `MISS`.\n12:- The docs describe no cost or token figures returned to the caller, in headers or body. Cost is an estimate the gateway computes from token data for its own observability. `cf-aig-custom-cost` is a request header that overrides the price used; it returns nothing.\n13:- wrangler has no gateway command, and the OAuth token wrangler uses cannot create gateways (`10000 Authentication error`); create gateways in the dashboard or with an API token that has AI Gateway Edit.\n20:- Google AI Studio provider: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/usage/providers/google-ai-studio/\nr2.md:11:- Presigned URLs support GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE, expire from 1 second to 7 days, and work only on the `*.r2.cloudflarestorage.com` host, not on a custom domain. A presigned PUT needs no other authentication header, so a plain HTTP client can upload with the presigned URL alone; when `ContentType` is signed, the upload must send the same value or R2 answers `403 SignatureDoesNotMatch`. A single-part upload goes up to about 5 GiB.\nr2.md:17:- A custom domain gives caching and access control through Access or WAF token authentication. `r2.dev` is rate-limited and meant for development only. From a Worker, an R2 binding is also available. Local development: see `local-dev`.\nlocal-dev.md:13:- D1, R2 and Durable Object state are shared only when every process uses the same state folder: `--persist-to <dir>` for wrangler, `persistState: { path }` in the Vite plugin. Otherwise each config directory gets its own `.wrangler/state` and the other Worker sees empty tables (`no such table`). Migrations apply locally with `wrangler d1 migrations apply <db> --local --persist-to <dir>`; `database_id` can be a placeholder.\nlocal-dev.md:17:- Local R2 over the S3 API (experimental, undocumented): `local_dev.experimental_s3_credentials` (`accessKeyId`, `secretAccessKey`) on an `r2_buckets` entry serves the local bucket at `/cdn-cgi/local/r2/s3/<bucket-id>` on the dev server, checking SigV4 signatures in headers and in presigned query strings. Added in workers-sdk PR #14280, merged 2026-07-27. A process with the same state folder and no credentials reads the objects through its binding.\nd1.md:1:# D1: SQLite on Cloudflare, migrations and drivers\nd1.md:7:## Knowledge\nd1.md:17:## Sources\nai-gateway.md:1:# AI Gateway: calling a provider through it from a Worker\nai-gateway.md:7:## Knowledge\nai-gateway.md:16:## Sources","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"91605e50-3428-4bc2-81c2-b13a7f0ca938","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"074871f5-8aef-4d50-af87-acb782b20431","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
