{"parentUuid":"0c225bc5-214f-4209-9c3e-05f00da3e8ca","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfwge8ikBLabPxzkRGHpW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QnWhLeQqSHKPKLw7hHVTHr","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou/skills && cat wiki/references/cloudflare/ai-gateway.md; echo ----; cat wiki/references/cloudflare/d1.md | head -20; echo ----; cat learn/references/discover.md; grep -rn -i \"directed\\|discovery\\|no arguments\\|arguments given\" --include=*.md .. | grep -v \"learn/references\" | head","description":"Read entries and learn references to edit"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":104,"cache_read_input_tokens":203729,"output_tokens":323,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":98},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":104,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":323,"cache_read_input_tokens":203729,"cache_creation_input_tokens":104,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":104},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01QnWhLeQqSHKPKLw7hHVTHr":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/claude-plugins/mahou/skills && cat wiki/references/cloudflare/ai-gateway.md; echo ----; cat wiki/references/cloudflare/d1.md | head -20; echo ----; cat learn/references/discover.md; grep -rn -i \"directed\\|discovery\\|no arguments\\|arguments given\" --include=*.md .. | grep -v \"learn/references\" | head","description":"Read entries and learn references to edit"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"1044fd6e-ab82-40a1-b082-56dc2019e75b","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cfwge7ixzyq8m7dRqeE9E","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"b5ee8645-639f-4237-a6af-3897ae007ea0","timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:07:23.281Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","sessionId":"f14bad90-e61e-4c27-a38f-bf7cbc76465e","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"atomic-stirring-rossum"}
{"parentUuid":"b5ee8645-639f-4237-a6af-3897ae007ea0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ceba04d9-a0e6-47f8-b87e-eaa86737ebf8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QnWhLeQqSHKPKLw7hHVTHr","type":"tool_result","content":"# AI Gateway: calling a provider through it from a Worker\n\nverified: 2026-10-10\n\nAnswers what changes when a Worker calls a model provider through Cloudflare AI Gateway instead of directly: authentication, caching, what the caller learns about cost, and what can be managed from the CLI. Distilled from a spike that called Gemini through a gateway from a deployed Worker, and from the AI Gateway docs.\n\n## Knowledge\n\n- A provider is reached at `https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account>/<gateway>/<provider>/...` (for Gemini, the `google-ai-studio` provider, then the provider's own path such as `v1/models/<model>:generateContent`). The provider's SDK works with its base URL pointed there, and so does plain `fetch`; both sent byte-identical bodies, and the gateway did not change the body.\n- A gateway with authentication on requires a `cf-aig-authorization` header on every request.\n- With response caching on, an identical request returns the stored response without calling the provider (0.1 s against 1.6–2.7 s for a miss). The `cf-aig-cache-status` response header says `HIT` or `MISS`.\n- The docs describe no cost or token figures returned to the caller, in headers or body. Cost is an estimate the gateway computes from token data for its own observability. `cf-aig-custom-cost` is a request header that overrides the price used; it returns nothing.\n- wrangler has no gateway command, and the OAuth token wrangler uses cannot create gateways (`10000 Authentication error`); create gateways in the dashboard or with an API token that has AI Gateway Edit.\n- The gateway logs every request sent through it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Caching: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/configuration/caching/\n- Costs: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/observability/costs/\n- Google AI Studio provider: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/usage/providers/google-ai-studio/\n- Spike, 2026-10-04: Gemini image description from a deployed Worker through a gateway, with the SDK and with plain `fetch`\n----\n# D1: SQLite on Cloudflare, migrations and drivers\n\nverified: 2026-10-04\n\nAnswers what D1 offers as a database, how it is reached, how its schema is migrated, and whether SQLAlchemy and Alembic can drive it. Distilled from documentation and package inspection while weighing a Postgres-to-D1 move; nothing was run against D1.\n\n## Knowledge\n\n- D1 runs on SQLite's engine with the JSON extension, FTS5 and math functions. `PRAGMA foreign_keys` and `defer_foreign_keys` are available. `run()` returns `meta.changes`, so a conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE status = ...` can be checked by its row count. `batch()` runs its statements as one transaction.\n- Limits on Paid: 10 GB per database (500 MB on Free), 2 MB per row or value, 100 bound parameters per query, 1,000 queries per Worker invocation. Each database is single-threaded.\n- Pricing on Paid includes 25 billion rows read, 50 million rows written and 5 GB a month; beyond that $0.001 per million reads, $1.00 per million writes and $0.75 per GB-month. No egress charges.\n- From a Worker it is a binding. From anything else, such as a container, the only documented route is the REST API.\n- Migrations are hand-written `.sql` files in a `migrations/` folder, created with `wrangler d1 migrations create`, applied with `--local` or `--remote`, and tracked in a `d1_migrations` table. There is no down migration: a failing migration rolls back itself and the earlier ones stay. Time Travel keeps 30 days of point-in-time restore on Paid (7 on Free).\n- Read replication is GA at no cost; without the Sessions API every query goes to the primary, and with it reads are sequentially consistent.\n- `sqlalchemy-cloudflare-d1` (0.4.1, 2026-10-02) connects over the REST API or a Python Worker binding. Its async engine is unavailable inside Workers, its `commit` and `rollback` are no-ops because every statement auto-commits, and its package contains no Alembic support. No other package connects Alembic to D1; Alembic's offline `--sql` output written into D1's migration files is an untested route.\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/pricing/\n- https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/limits/\n----\n# Discovery mode\n\nUsed when `learn` is invoked without arguments.\n\n1. **Scan the conversation for friction.** Read it end to end before judging any part of it. Then collect only the points where the flow's behavior changed. Most of these are the user's own interventions: a request to change the format of an output, a request for something outside the flow, a correction of something the agent had put together or researched, a reversal of an earlier decision, anything the user had to ask for more than once. Where the agent itself redid an output or retracted a reading, that counts too. The stretches where the flow ran without the user steering it are evidence the skills worked, not findings. Where the conversation has been compacted, work from what remains and say so: friction a compaction dropped is gone, and a plausible reconstruction of it is a guess.\n\n2. **Report each friction point, and stop there.** This is a deliverable, not a preamble. One short block of plain prose per point, in the order the points happened: what the flow was doing, what the user asked for instead, and what that says when compared with the plugin's existing skills and wiki. Read the skills and entries each point touches before writing this. What it says is an observation, not a proposal: a format a skill prescribes that the user overrides, a step that exists only in session memory, a need the flow had no answer for and the user improvised around, a guardrail nothing enforced, a fact about a tool the agent had to rediscover. Where the same correction shows up more than once, say so; that is the strongest signal in the session.\n\n   Ask whether the points match what the user remembers. They were there. You are reading their interventions back to them. Iterate until the points match.\n\n3. **The user proposes what to keep.** Do not present a candidate list, and do not turn the points into proposals. Answer questions about the points, then wait.\n\nContinue with **Drafting** in `SKILL.md`.\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.md\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2aff98fe-de8f-4ef3-85cc-05e67d3c47e5","timestamp":"2026-10-12T04:07:23.747Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# AI Gateway: calling a provider through it from a Worker\n\nverified: 2026-10-10\n\nAnswers what changes when a Worker calls a model provider through Cloudflare AI Gateway instead of directly: authentication, caching, what the caller learns about cost, and what can be managed from the CLI. Distilled from a spike that called Gemini through a gateway from a deployed Worker, and from the AI Gateway docs.\n\n## Knowledge\n\n- A provider is reached at `https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account>/<gateway>/<provider>/...` (for Gemini, the `google-ai-studio` provider, then the provider's own path such as `v1/models/<model>:generateContent`). The provider's SDK works with its base URL pointed there, and so does plain `fetch`; both sent byte-identical bodies, and the gateway did not change the body.\n- A gateway with authentication on requires a `cf-aig-authorization` header on every request.\n- With response caching on, an identical request returns the stored response without calling the provider (0.1 s against 1.6–2.7 s for a miss). The `cf-aig-cache-status` response header says `HIT` or `MISS`.\n- The docs describe no cost or token figures returned to the caller, in headers or body. Cost is an estimate the gateway computes from token data for its own observability. `cf-aig-custom-cost` is a request header that overrides the price used; it returns nothing.\n- wrangler has no gateway command, and the OAuth token wrangler uses cannot create gateways (`10000 Authentication error`); create gateways in the dashboard or with an API token that has AI Gateway Edit.\n- The gateway logs every request sent through it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Caching: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/configuration/caching/\n- Costs: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/observability/costs/\n- Google AI Studio provider: https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/usage/providers/google-ai-studio/\n- Spike, 2026-10-04: Gemini image description from a deployed Worker through a gateway, with the SDK and with plain `fetch`\n----\n# D1: SQLite on Cloudflare, migrations and drivers\n\nverified: 2026-10-04\n\nAnswers what D1 offers as a database, how it is reached, how its schema is migrated, and whether SQLAlchemy and Alembic can drive it. Distilled from documentation and package inspection while weighing a Postgres-to-D1 move; nothing was run against D1.\n\n## Knowledge\n\n- D1 runs on SQLite's engine with the JSON extension, FTS5 and math functions. `PRAGMA foreign_keys` and `defer_foreign_keys` are available. `run()` returns `meta.changes`, so a conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE status = ...` can be checked by its row count. `batch()` runs its statements as one transaction.\n- Limits on Paid: 10 GB per database (500 MB on Free), 2 MB per row or value, 100 bound parameters per query, 1,000 queries per Worker invocation. Each database is single-threaded.\n- Pricing on Paid includes 25 billion rows read, 50 million rows written and 5 GB a month; beyond that $0.001 per million reads, $1.00 per million writes and $0.75 per GB-month. No egress charges.\n- From a Worker it is a binding. From anything else, such as a container, the only documented route is the REST API.\n- Migrations are hand-written `.sql` files in a `migrations/` folder, created with `wrangler d1 migrations create`, applied with `--local` or `--remote`, and tracked in a `d1_migrations` table. There is no down migration: a failing migration rolls back itself and the earlier ones stay. Time Travel keeps 30 days of point-in-time restore on Paid (7 on Free).\n- Read replication is GA at no cost; without the Sessions API every query goes to the primary, and with it reads are sequentially consistent.\n- `sqlalchemy-cloudflare-d1` (0.4.1, 2026-10-02) connects over the REST API or a Python Worker binding. Its async engine is unavailable inside Workers, its `commit` and `rollback` are no-ops because every statement auto-commits, and its package contains no Alembic support. No other package connects Alembic to D1; Alembic's offline `--sql` output written into D1's migration files is an untested route.\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/pricing/\n- https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/limits/\n----\n# Discovery mode\n\nUsed when `learn` is invoked without arguments.\n\n1. **Scan the conversation for friction.** Read it end to end before judging any part of it. Then collect only the points where the flow's behavior changed. Most of these are the user's own interventions: a request to change the format of an output, a request for something outside the flow, a correction of something the agent had put together or researched, a reversal of an earlier decision, anything the user had to ask for more than once. Where the agent itself redid an output or retracted a reading, that counts too. The stretches where the flow ran without the user steering it are evidence the skills worked, not findings. Where the conversation has been compacted, work from what remains and say so: friction a compaction dropped is gone, and a plausible reconstruction of it is a guess.\n\n2. **Report each friction point, and stop there.** This is a deliverable, not a preamble. One short block of plain prose per point, in the order the points happened: what the flow was doing, what the user asked for instead, and what that says when compared with the plugin's existing skills and wiki. Read the skills and entries each point touches before writing this. What it says is an observation, not a proposal: a format a skill prescribes that the user overrides, a step that exists only in session memory, a need the flow had no answer for and the user improvised around, a guardrail nothing enforced, a fact about a tool the agent had to rediscover. Where the same correction shows up more than once, say so; that is the strongest signal in the session.\n\n   Ask whether the points match what the user remembers. They were there. You are reading their interventions back to them. Iterate until the points match.\n\n3. **The user proposes what to keep.** Do not present a candidate list, and do not turn the points into proposals. Answer questions about the points, then wait.\n\nContinue with **Drafting** in `SKILL.md`.\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.md","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1044fd6e-ab82-40a1-b082-56dc2019e75b","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","root":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"asermax/nagara","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"filadd/cc-plugin-marketplace","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara","platform":"linux"}},"sourceToolAssista